window.CCI_STARTER_DECKS = "DECK: Discipleship Experience (Section 1) Soteriology\r\n\r\nQ1. Why does the lesson use the example of the criminal on the cross?\r\nA. To show that everyone receives equal earthly rewards.\r\nB. To teach that good deeds after death determine salvation\r\nC. To demonstrate that repentance always removes earthly consequences.\r\nD. To illustrate that justification is a legal declaration made on the basis of Christ's substitution.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The criminal on the cross illustrates justification as a legal declaration of acquittal on the basis of Christ's substitution.\r\nREF: Justification — 05:03–06:07; 07:06–08:16\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 8:33–34 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A33%E2%80%9334/>\r\n\r\nQ2. According to the lesson, what is the strongest biblical basis for concluding that condemnation comes after a person knowingly rejects the gospel?\r\nA. Genesis 3: 11\r\nB. John 3:18–19\r\nC. Romans 7: 1\r\nD. Psalm 51: 2\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson uses John 3:18–19 to explain that condemnation applies to those who hear and understand the gospel but choose not to receive it.\r\nREF: Original Sin — 08:41–09:41\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 3:18–19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A18%E2%80%9319/>\r\n\r\nQ3. Which argument involving Jesus does Michael Heiser use to challenge the view that every person inherits Adam's personal guilt?\r\nA. Jesus obeyed the Law perfectly.\r\nB. If everyone born through Adam is automatically guilty, Jesus would also have been guilty.\r\nC. Jesus' miracles prove He was sinless.\r\nD. Jesus never discussed original sin.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Michael Heiser's argument is that if everyone born through Adam automatically inherited personal guilt, then Jesus would also be guilty.\r\nREF: Original Sin — 03:22–04:13\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A12/> | Added from online research: Hebrews 4:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+4%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ4. In the salvation lesson, why are both Nicodemus and Cornelius discussed?\r\nA. To demonstrate that both moral and religious people still needed salvation through faith in Christ.\r\nB. To compare Jewish and Roman cultures.\r\nC. To explain the history of evangelism.\r\nD. To illustrate different spiritual gifts.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Nicodemus and Cornelius illustrate that moral or religious standing does not replace the need to receive the gospel and be saved through faith in Christ.\r\nREF: Born Again — 06:39–08:30\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 3:1–10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A1%E2%80%9310/>; Acts 10:34–48 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10%3A34%E2%80%9348/>\r\n\r\nQ5. Which statement best summarizes the lesson's definition of being born again?\r\nA. Becoming more disciplined in religious practice.\r\nB. Joining a local church.\r\nC. Living a morally upright life.\r\nD. Receiving the life of God through faith in Christ.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Being born again is receiving God's supernatural life through faith in Jesus, rather than earning it by behavior.\r\nREF: Born Again — 08:30–10:04\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 3:3–8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A3%E2%80%938/>\r\n\r\nQ6. According to the lesson on righteousness, believers are described as \"accepted in the Beloved\" because:\r\nA. They consistently obey God's commands.\r\nB. They no longer require repentance.\r\nC. Christ's work secures their acceptance before God.\r\nD. They possess greater spiritual gifts than others.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Believers are accepted in the Beloved because Christ took their place and secured the benefits of His work.\r\nREF: Righteousness — 04:26–05:11\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 1:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ7. Which statement best reflects the lesson's explanation of right standing with God?\r\nA. It depends on maintaining perfect obedience.\r\nB. It depends on sincere intentions.\r\nC. It is grounded in faith in Christ rather than personal behaviour.\r\nD. It is earned through consistent repentance.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says right standing with God rests on faith in Christ and what He has done, not on how well a person behaves.\r\nREF: Righteousness — 06:12–06:53\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 3:22–24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A22%E2%80%9324/>\r\n\r\nQ8. Why does the lesson distinguish between metamelomai and metanoia?\r\nA. To argue that sorrow alone is insufficient because it should lead to repentance.\r\nB. To show they can be used similarly.\r\nC. To teach that repentance belongs only to the Old Testament.\r\nD. To show the role of emotions in repentance.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The distinction shows that sorrow is not itself repentance; the lesson says godly sorrow leads to repentance.\r\nREF: Repentance — 04:41–05:47\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+7/>\r\n\r\nQ9. What role does the pornography illustration serve in the lesson?\r\nA. To explain discipline and it's role in a believer.\r\nB. To show that behavioural change alone is not the same as gospel repentance.\r\nC. To teach that addiction can be overcome just by making changes.\r\nD. To argue against moral discipline.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The example shows that stopping an outward behavior alone is not gospel repentance; repentance turns toward Christ.\r\nREF: Repentance — 01:54–02:46\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Mark 1:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+1%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ10. Why does the lesson distinguish redemption from justification?\r\nA. Redemption describes Christ's saving work, while justification describes God's legal declaration concerning the believer.\r\nB. Redemption applies to Old Testament, while justification applies to the Church now.\r\nC. Redemption concerns forgiveness, while justification concerns rewards.\r\nD. They are presented as completely unrelated doctrines.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Redemption describes Christ's saving provision through His death and resurrection, while justification is the legal declaration that the believer is not guilty.\r\nREF: Justification — 09:24–10:31\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 8:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ11. Apostle's final position on original sin is that humanity inherits:\r\nA. Adam's guilt alone.\r\nB. Mortality together with an irresistible tendency to sin.\r\nC. Physical death without any inclination toward sin.\r\nD. Immediate spiritual condemnation at birth.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says humanity receives mortality from Adam and describes an irresistible tendency for the natural person to sin.\r\nREF: Original Sin — 02:19–03:22; 10:47–11:43\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A12/>; Romans 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+7/>; Romans 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8/>\r\n\r\nQ12. Which statement best reflects the lesson's teaching about the moment a person is saved?\r\nA. At water baptism.\r\nB. At the altar call.\r\nC. At the point of genuine faith in Christ.\r\nD. After completing discipleship classes.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says a person receives Christ's life and is saved at the point of placing genuine faith in Jesus.\r\nREF: Born Again — 10:04–11:39\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16/>; Acts 10:43 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10%3A43/>\r\n\r\nQ13. According to the lesson, why does Paul repeatedly affirm believers' identity before correcting their conduct?\r\nA. Because identity provides the foundation from which lasting transformation occurs.\r\nB. Because behaviour is unimportant.\r\nC. Because discipline belongs only to church leaders.\r\nD. Because sin disappears after conversion.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Paul emphasizes believers' identity in Christ by grace as a basis for realigning their conduct.\r\nREF: Righteousness — 01:48–03:34\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Corinthians 1:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+1%3A4/>; 1 Corinthians 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+5/>; 1 Corinthians 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+6/>; 1 Corinthians 14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+14/>; 1 Corinthians 11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+11/>\r\n\r\nQ14. What is the significance of Luke 24:47 in the repentance lesson?\r\nA. It limits repentance to the nation of Israel.\r\nB. It shows repentance and forgiveness are to be proclaimed to all nations.\r\nC. It teaches repentance is unnecessary after Christ's resurrection.\r\nD. It defines repentance as sorrow alone.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson cites Luke 24:47 to show that repentance and remission of sins should be preached to all nations.\r\nREF: Repentance — 10:09–11:08\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Luke 24:47 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A47/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+2/>; Acts 11:18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+11%3A18/>\r\n\r\nQ15. The lesson explains that once God declares a believer justified:\r\nA. Other accusations remain equally authoritative.\r\nB. The believer must continually earn that verdict.\r\nC. No other opinion overrides God's verdict.\r\nD. The believer no longer faces any earthly consequences.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Once the judge declares someone not guilty, the lesson says no other opinion overrides that verdict.\r\nREF: Justification — 07:06–08:16\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 8:33–34 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A33%E2%80%9334/>\r\n\r\nQ16. Which statement best integrates the teaching across the salvation and righteousness lessons?\r\nA. Spiritual life begins through faith in Christ, and that new identity becomes the basis for transformed living.\r\nB. Spiritual growth must precede salvation.\r\nC. Good behaviour produces spiritual life.\r\nD. Identity in Christ replaces the need for obedience.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The salvation lesson says faith in Jesus brings Christ's life; the righteousness lesson says identity in Christ by grace influences behavior.\r\nREF: Born Again — 09:15–10:04; Righteousness — 01:48–03:34\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16/>; 1 Corinthians 1:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+1%3A4/>; Ephesians 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A3/>\r\n\r\nQ17. Across the week's lessons, which recurring pattern best describes how God transforms people?\r\nA. Behaviour changes first, then God grants acceptance.\r\nB. God grants acceptance in Christ, and that reality shapes a transformed life.\r\nC. Fear of judgment produces lasting holiness.\r\nD. Religious discipline creates spiritual life.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents acceptance in Christ by grace as the foundation for realigning believers' conduct.\r\nREF: Righteousness — 01:48–03:34; 04:26–05:11\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Corinthians 1:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+1%3A4/>; 1 Corinthians 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+5/>; 1 Corinthians 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+6/>; 1 Corinthians 14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+14/>; 1 Corinthians 11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+11/>; Ephesians 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A3/>\r\n\r\nQ18. Which statement best captures the relationship between faith and repentance as presented throughout the lessons?\r\nA. Faith removes any expectation of changed living.\r\nB. Repentance earns salvation before faith.\r\nC. Faith in Christ brings salvation, while genuine repentance accompanies that faith and produces its fruit.\r\nD. Repentance and faith are unrelated ideas.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lessons connect salvation with faith in Jesus and describe genuine repentance as turning toward Christ, with behavior change as its fruit.\r\nREF: Born Again — 10:04–10:54; Repentance — 06:53–08:04; 13:36–14:40\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16/>; Romans 10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+10/>; Matthew 3:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+3%3A8/>; Luke 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+3/>; Acts 26:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+26%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ19. Looking across all five lessons, which statement most accurately summarizes the central message?\r\nA. Christianity is primarily about becoming morally disciplined.\r\nB. Salvation is God's gracious work in Christ, received through faith, resulting in a new identity that produces transformed living.\r\nC. The Christian life is sustained mainly through religious activities.\r\nD. The gospel is chiefly a call to improve personal behaviour.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Across the lessons, salvation is presented as God's work in Christ received through faith, with acceptance in Christ leading to transformed conduct.\r\nREF: Born Again — 08:30–10:54; Original Sin — 08:41–09:41; Repentance — 06:53–08:04; 13:36–14:40; Justification — 05:03–06:07; 09:24–10:31; Righteousness — 01:48–03:34; 05:11–06:53\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16/>; Romans 8:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A1/>; 1 Corinthians 1:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+1%3A4/>; Acts 26:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+26%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ20. Which statement best explains why the lesson begins by discussing God's role as Judge before defining justification?\r\nA. To establish that justification should be understood as a legal declaration rather than merely a feeling of forgiveness.\r\nB. To show that believers should fear God's judgment every day.\r\nC. To demonstrate that Old Testament believers understood salvation exactly as Christians do today.\r\nD. To explain why every Christian will receive earthly rewards.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The introduction establishes the legal setting by identifying God as Judge and describing justification and condemnation in legal terms.\r\nREF: Justification — 01:16–04:06\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 18:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+18%3A25/>; 1 Samuel 2:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Samuel+2%3A10/>; Psalm 7:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+7%3A11/>; Romans 14:10, 12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+14%3A10%2C+12/>; 2 Corinthians 5:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A10/>; Romans 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8/>\r\n\r\nQ21. In the lesson on original sin, what distinction is presented as central to interpreting Romans 5:12?\r\nA. Humanity inherited Adam's personal guilt.\r\nB. Humanity inherited death and an eventual tendency to sin rather than Adam's personal guilt.\r\nC. Humanity inherited neither death nor sin from Adam.\r\nD. Humanity only inherited physical weakness from Adam.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker distinguishes mortality received from Adam from Adam's personal guilt, then describes an irresistible tendency to sin.\r\nREF: Original Sin — 02:19–03:22; 10:47–11:43\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 5:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A12/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+7/>; Romans 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8/>\r\n\r\nQ22. The CNDP framework introduced in the salvation lesson is primarily intended to help believers:\r\nA. Memorize the order of the New Testament books.\r\nB. Explain salvation in a complete and coherent way.\r\nC. Compare different theological traditions.\r\nD. Study biblical Greek more effectively.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: CNDP organizes the lesson around the context, necessity, definition, and provision of salvation.\r\nREF: Born Again — 02:06–03:06\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3/>\r\n\r\nQ23. Paul's approach to correcting the Corinthians' moral failures was to:\r\nA. First question whether they were truly saved.\r\nB. Focus primarily on punishment as motivation for holiness.\r\nC. Emphasize their identity in Christ before addressing their conduct.\r\nD. Remove members involved in every form of sin before teaching them.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Paul emphasizes the Corinthians' identity in Christ by grace and uses it as a basis to realign their conduct.\r\nREF: Righteousness — 01:48–03:34\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Corinthians 1:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+1%3A4/>; 1 Corinthians 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+5/>; 1 Corinthians 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+6/>; 1 Corinthians 14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+14/>; 1 Corinthians 11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+11/>\r\n\r\nQ24. Which statement best captures the lesson's understanding of genuine repentance?\r\nA. It is turning to Christ in faith, producing an inner change that naturally bears fruit in conduct.\r\nB. It is behavioural reform that earns God's acceptance.\r\nC. It is simply feeling remorse for past actions.\r\nD. It is abandoning sinful habits before believing the gospel.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Repentance is turning toward Christ with an inner resolve that eventually bears fruit in conduct.\r\nREF: Repentance — 06:53–08:04; 13:36–14:40\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+10/>; Matthew 3:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+3%3A8/>; Luke 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+3/>; Acts 26:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+26%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ25. The courtroom illustration used in the justification lesson primarily emphasizes that:\r\nA. Every believer will eventually stand trial again.\r\nB. Justification changes a person's legal standing before God because of Christ's substitution.\r\nC. Christians must defend themselves before God.\r\nD. Good works overturn past guilt.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The courtroom illustration presents justification as a legal verdict of acquittal on the basis of Christ's substitution.\r\nREF: Justification — 05:03–06:07; 07:06–08:16\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 8:33–34 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A33%E2%80%9334/>\r\n\r\nQ26. Why does Apostle disagree, at least in part, with Michael Heiser's final conclusion?\r\nA. He believes Adam's guilt alone explains human sinfulness.\r\nB. He believes children should be considered guilty from birth.\r\nC. He believes Romans 5 should not be used when discussing original sin.\r\nD. He believes mortality alone cannot explain why every human eventually sins.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker disagrees in part because mortality alone does not explain why humans eventually sin; he also argues for an irresistible tendency to sin.\r\nREF: Original Sin — 10:47–11:43\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5/>; Romans 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+7/>; Romans 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8/>\r\n\r\nQ27. Which illustration is used to explain that a person may appear physically alive while lacking spiritual life?\r\nA. A courtroom verdict\r\nB. A ledger balancing debts\r\nC. A funeral where the body looked unchanged\r\nD. A child learning to walk\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The funeral story illustrates that a body may appear unchanged after life has departed, introducing the lesson's explanation of spiritual life.\r\nREF: Born Again — 04:46–05:40\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+3/>; Ephesians 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+2/>\r\n\r\nQ28. According to the lesson on righteousness, why does Paul remind believers that their bodies are temples of the Holy Spirit?\r\nA. To convince them they have permanently lost God's presence.\r\nB. To use their identity in Christ as the basis for transformed behaviour.\r\nC. To teach that holiness depends on human effort.\r\nD. To discourage participation in public worship.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Paul reminds believers that their bodies are temples of the Holy Spirit and uses their identity and relationship with God to encourage changed behavior.\r\nREF: Righteousness — 02:47–03:34\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Corinthians 6:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+6%3A19/>\r\n\r\nQ29. Why does the lesson distinguish between repentance and its fruit?\r\nA. Because repentance is an inward turning to God that should eventually produce outward obedience.\r\nB. Because behavioural change has no place in the Christian life.\r\nC. Because repentance only applies before salvation.\r\nD. Because obedience and repentance always mean the same thing.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson distinguishes repentance from its fruit by describing repentance as an inward turning to God, while changed behavior is its fruit.\r\nREF: Repentance — 13:36–14:40\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+10/>; Matthew 3:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+3%3A8/>; Luke 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+3/>; Acts 26:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+26%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ30. The Greek word dikaio, as explained in the lesson, most accurately means to:\r\nA. Become morally perfect over time.\r\nB. Be declared righteous or acquitted.\r\nC. Earn righteousness through obedience.\r\nD. Receive power for holy living.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson defines the term as to declare righteous, acquit, or vindicate.\r\nREF: Justification — 05:03–06:07\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ31. What does the speaker say the term \"soteriology\" refers to?\r\nA. The study of human sin and its effects on the soul\r\nB. The study of salvation, from the Greek soteria and logi\r\nC. The study of final judgment and the end of the age\r\nD. The study of the church and how it carries out its mission\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker opens the series by explaining that soteria is the Greek word for salvation and logi means study, so soteriology is the study of salvation.\r\nREF: ORIGINAL SIN — [00:16]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ32. How did some people interpret Romans 5:14, which says death reigned even over those who had not sinned in the way Adam did?\r\nA. As showing that death comes only to those who personally commit sin as Adam did\r\nB. As showing that Adam's sin affected only the first generation after the fall\r\nC. As showing that infants are spared death until they reach an age to choose\r\nD. As showing that Adam's guilt has passed to every person, even before sin\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the idea of death passing to those who had not sinned like Adam led people to conclude that Adam's guilt was passed on to all, so a person is born a sinner before sinning.\r\nREF: ORIGINAL SIN — [01:14]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 5:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A14/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ33. On the view that Adam's guilt is passed on to everyone at birth, what does the speaker say follows for infants and for people unable to hear and believe the gospel?\r\nA. They would be counted as sinners and go where sinners go\r\nB. They would be saved automatically because they cannot understand the gospel\r\nC. They would be held in a separate category until they are old enough to choose\r\nD. They would be judged only by the sins their parents committed\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that on this understanding the answer is yes: infants who die are sinners who go where sinners go, and mentally challenged people who cannot hear and believe the gospel go to hell. He then challenges this view.\r\nREF: ORIGINAL SIN — [01:14; 02:19]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ34. According to the scholars the speaker cites, what does Romans 5:12 actually say spread to all men?\r\nA. Sin along with Adam's guilt\r\nB. A fully formed sinful nature\r\nC. Death, rather than sin itself\r\nD. Condemnation for every newborn\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Scholars note that in Romans 5:12 it is death that passed to all men, not sin; the speaker repeats that death spread to all men because all have sinned.\r\nREF: ORIGINAL SIN — [02:19; 03:22]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 5:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ35. How does the speaker describe the view that humanity received no consequence at all from Adam's sin?\r\nA. As the view that best fits Romans 5:14 on its own terms\r\nB. As a sound middle ground between the two positions he describes\r\nC. As a view he accepts for infants but not for adults\r\nD. As the opposite extreme, which he calls a theological heresy\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: After describing the guilt view, the speaker says some go to the other extreme, a theological heresy that denies any consequence of Adam's sin; what is actually received is death and a propensity to eventually sin.\r\nREF: ORIGINAL SIN — [02:19]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ36. How does Michael Heiser, as the speaker reports it, respond to the claim that the virgin birth shields Jesus from inherited sin?\r\nA. He asks why sin would pass only through the father when Mary also contributed biologically\r\nB. He argues that the virgin birth is not clearly recorded in the Gospel accounts of Jesus' birth\r\nC. He argues that Joseph's seed was needed for Jesus to fully share in human nature\r\nD. He argues that Jesus avoided sin because Mary was herself sinless from birth\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker recounts that Heiser asked who says sin is transmitted only through the man, since a woman carried Jesus and he shares a woman's attributes, which undermines the virgin-birth defense.\r\nREF: ORIGINAL SIN — [04:13]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ37. What problem does Michael Heiser raise for the view that everyone born through Adam's lineage is a sinner?\r\nA. It would mean Jesus never faced temptation.\r\nB. It would mean Jesus, even if he never committed sin, would also be a sinner.\r\nC. It would mean Adam was never guilty.\r\nD. It would mean Romans 5 is not Scripture.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Heiser argues that if birth through Adam's lineage alone makes someone a sinner, Jesus would be a sinner too.\r\nREF: Original Sin — 03:22–04:13\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A12/> | Added from online research: Hebrews 4:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+4%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ38. What does the speaker give as the simple answer for why Jesus did not commit sin?\r\nA. Because he was born of a virgin\r\nB. Because Jesus is God himself\r\nC. Because Mary was sinless\r\nD. Because he kept the Law\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the simple answer is that Jesus is God. He adds that the Bible does not categorically say the virgin birth is the reason, and that the claim that Mary was sinless is not biblical.\r\nREF: ORIGINAL SIN — [05:14]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1/>; Hebrews 4:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+4%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ39. What point does the speaker attribute to William Lane Craig about Jesus' use of children as illustrations of the kingdom?\r\nA. Children illustrate the kingdom only as a picture of humility, with no deeper meaning\r\nB. Jesus used children to teach that guilt is inherited but can be set aside by humility\r\nC. Jesus would not have used them as he did if he thought they carried Adam's guilt\r\nD. Jesus used children to show that infants must be baptized before entering the kingdom\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reports Craig's argument, citing Mark 10:14 and Matthew 18:3-6, that Jesus placed a premium on little ones and would not set them forth as models for entering the kingdom if they carried Adam's guilt.\r\nREF: ORIGINAL SIN — [06:03; 06:56; 07:46]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Mark 10:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+10%3A14/>; Matthew 18:3–6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+18%3A3%E2%80%936/>\r\n\r\nQ40. How does the speaker describe the \"age of accountability\"?\r\nA. The age at which someone can hear the gospel, understand it, and accept or reject it\r\nB. The age at which a person is first held responsible for the sin Adam committed\r\nC. The age at which a person is first able to sin and is therefore called a sinner\r\nD. The age at which a person becomes eligible for baptism and church membership\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Reading John 3:18-19, the speaker says condemnation falls on those who hear and understand the gospel but choose not to receive it, which requires reaching what theologians call the age of accountability.\r\nREF: ORIGINAL SIN — [08:41]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 3:18–19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A18%E2%80%9319/>\r\n\r\nQ41. What does the speaker say Michael Heiser claims about Augustine's explanation of original sin?\r\nA. Augustine rejected Romans 5:12 as unreliable and built his view mainly on the Gospel of John\r\nB. Augustine read the original Greek carefully and found Adam's guilt clearly taught there\r\nC. Augustine taught only that death, and never guilt, was passed on from Adam to his children\r\nD. Augustine used a Latin translation that mistranslated a word, which led to the guilt view\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reports Heiser's claim that Augustine, a Latin speaker, worked from a Latin translation that mistranslated a particular word, which led to several centuries of the view that guilt passed on.\r\nREF: ORIGINAL SIN — [09:41]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5/>\r\n\r\nQ42. What does the speaker say about when a person becomes a sinner, given that all people will eventually sin?\r\nA. They are sinners from conception, though their guilt is counted only later\r\nB. They become sinners when they first hear and reject the gospel\r\nC. They are not sinners until they themselves actually commit sin\r\nD. They become sinners at the moment they are born into Adam's line\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that even though all men will eventually sin, they are not sinners until they do.\r\nREF: ORIGINAL SIN — [10:47]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5/>; Romans 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+7/>; Romans 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8/>\r\n\r\nQ43. How does the speaker himself define original sin?\r\nA. Adam's personal guilt, which makes every person a sinner from the moment of birth\r\nB. An irresistible tendency to sin that means every man, though born innocent, will eventually sin\r\nC. Mortality inherited from Adam, with no real tendency toward sin attached to it at any stage of life\r\nD. A spiritual condition that makes it impossible for infants who die to be saved\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says mortality from Adam is not enough to explain why everyone sins (he points to Romans 7 and 8) and defines original sin as an irresistible tendency in the natural man to sin, though born innocent.\r\nREF: ORIGINAL SIN — [10:47]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+7/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5/>; Romans 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8/>\r\n\r\nQ44. How does the speaker say people who cannot receive or understand the gospel, such as infants, are judged?\r\nA. By the same standard that applies to everyone who has been born into Adam's line of descent\r\nB. By the response their parents gave when they heard the gospel\r\nC. By whether they were dedicated to God or baptized as young children\r\nD. By a different metric from those able to hear, receive, or reject the gospel\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that as long as someone is not in a position to hear, receive, or reject the gospel, God has a different rule for them; people in infancy or other states where they cannot understand it are judged by a different metric.\r\nREF: ORIGINAL SIN — [09:41; 11:43]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5/>; Romans 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+7/>; Romans 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8/>\r\n\r\nQ45. What do the four letters of the speaker's CNDP framework stand for?\r\nA. Conviction, New birth, Discipline, Prayer\r\nB. Calling, Newness, Devotion, Perseverance\r\nC. Context, Necessity, Definition, Provision\r\nD. Christ, Need, Discipleship, Promise\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says CNDP is not even an acronym, just key letters to remember: C for context, N for necessity, D for definition, and P for provision.\r\nREF: BORN AGAIN — [02:06]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3/>\r\n\r\nQ46. What does the speaker draw from Jesus' contrast between being born of the flesh and born of the Spirit in John 3?\r\nA. Just as biological life has an origin, spiritual life in Christ must have a beginning\r\nB. Spiritual life is simply a continuation of biological life, sustained by religious practice\r\nC. Spiritual birth happens gradually as a person matures in good behavior over time\r\nD. Physical birth is sinful, while spiritual birth is a holy event given to the faithful\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says there is an origin to biological life and Jesus is saying there must be an origin to spiritual life in Christ: life in Christ will have no end, but it will have a beginning.\r\nREF: BORN AGAIN — [03:06; 03:55]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+3/>; Ephesians 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+2/>\r\n\r\nQ47. When Adam ate the fruit, the speaker says he was \"still walking, still talking, but he was dead.\" What is his point?\r\nA. God's warning about death was postponed until the time of Adam's physical death\r\nB. The spiritual part of man died in Genesis 3 even though Adam stayed physically alive\r\nC. Adam's body began dying right away, while his spirit stayed fully alive in God\r\nD. Adam lost his ability to hear God clearly but kept his spiritual life intact\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says something fundamentally wrong happened in Genesis 3 when the spiritual part of man died, which is why God said Adam would surely die; Ephesians 2 then speaks of believers dead in trespasses and sins who were made alive.\r\nREF: BORN AGAIN — [03:55; 04:46]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+3/>; Ephesians 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+2/>\r\n\r\nQ48. What does the speaker conclude from the first burial he attended as a young boy?\r\nA. Kind and generous people do not truly die.\r\nB. A dead person can be revived by good behaviour.\r\nC. The body is just a casing, and something inside the man was missing even though he looked the same.\r\nD. Burials are the best setting in which to preach salvation.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The man looked much the same in the coffin, but something inside him was missing. The speaker realised the body is just a casing and that spiritual life can be imparted.\r\nREF: Born Again — 04:46–05:40\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+3/>; Ephesians 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+2/>\r\n\r\nQ49. Which part of a person is born again, according to the lesson, and how far does its effect reach?\r\nA. The body, with no effect on the soul or spirit\r\nB. The soul, with no effect on the spirit or body\r\nC. The spirit, with its efficacy extending to the soul and eventually the body\r\nD. The mind, with its effect limited to what a person believes\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The spiritual part is what is born again, though its efficacy goes beyond the spirit to the soul and eventually the body.\r\nREF: Born Again — 05:40\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 3:5–6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A5%E2%80%936/>\r\n\r\nQ50. Why does the speaker contrast a rowdy \"gangster\" type with a calm, successful, non-religious person?\r\nA. Wealthy, successful people are harder to reach with the gospel than anyone else, because they feel no need\r\nB. Visible vices, rather than unbelief, are what bring a person under condemnation\r\nC. Rowdy people are more open to the gospel than people who are morally upright\r\nD. We feel less urgency for well-behaved unbelievers, though they also need faith in Jesus\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says we are moved with compassion for people with visible vices, but feel less incentive to see the futility of a well-behaved person's efforts if he does not place faith in Jesus; Jesus told even a morally excellent Nicodemus he must be born again.\r\nREF: BORN AGAIN — [05:40; 06:39]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 3:1–10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A1%E2%80%9310/>; Acts 10:34–48 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10%3A34%E2%80%9348/>\r\n\r\nQ51. What does the lesson conclude from Jesus telling the morally excellent Nicodemus \"you must be born again\" and from Cornelius having to send for Peter?\r\nA. Salvation is reserved for people of high moral excellence.\r\nB. Religious leaders must be saved before ordinary people.\r\nC. Attending church is enough for those who behave well.\r\nD. Salvation is not about good behaviour but about faith in Jesus.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Both men were well behaved yet needed salvation. The speaker adds that being in a garage does not make you a car, so going to church does not make you born again.\r\nREF: Born Again — 07:36\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 3:1–10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A1%E2%80%9310/>; Acts 10:34–48 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10%3A34%E2%80%9348/>\r\n\r\nQ52. Which other biblical term does the lesson give for being born again, described as a translation from the kingdom of darkness into the kingdom of light?\r\nA. Consecration\r\nB. Redemption\r\nC. Repentance\r\nD. Regeneration\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the Bible also calls being born again regeneration.\r\nREF: Born Again — 08:30\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Titus 3:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Titus+3%3A5/>; Colossians 1:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+1%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ53. What does the lesson say the transfer from darkness into light requires, and how does it support this with Paul?\r\nA. Behaviour; Paul says the gospel is a standard believers must keep.\r\nB. Knowledge; Paul says the gospel is wisdom reserved for the learned.\r\nC. Power, not behaviour; Paul says he is not ashamed of the gospel because it is the power of God unto salvation to those who believe.\r\nD. Discipline; Paul says the gospel rewards those who are faithful.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says this transfer takes power, not behaviour, and cites Paul on the gospel as the power of God unto salvation.\r\nREF: Born Again — 08:30\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 1:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+1%3A16/>; Colossians 1:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+1%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ54. Why does the speaker quote \"If the Lord should count iniquities, who shall stand?\" when introducing provision?\r\nA. To show that no one can meet the requirements for salvation by works, so God's provision is needed\r\nB. To teach believers to fear judgment every day\r\nC. To show that sin is less serious for religious people\r\nD. To show that confession must come before faith\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says none of us can meet the requirements for salvation by works. John 3:16 is then presented as God's provision.\r\nREF: Born Again — 09:15–10:04\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+9/>\r\n\r\nQ55. The lesson presents John 3:16 as God's provision. What does it say a person must specifically place faith in, and how does it describe the roles of the heart and the mouth?\r\nA. That Jesus died for his sins and rose again for his justification; with the heart man believes unto righteousness, and with the mouth confession is made unto salvation\r\nB. That Jesus was a great moral teacher; the mouth alone saves and belief in the heart is optional\r\nC. That Jesus will reward good behaviour; belief in the heart saves and confession is unnecessary\r\nD. That God overlooks the sins of religious people; the heart produces good works and the mouth produces righteousness\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Faith is placed in Jesus who died for our sins and rose for our justification. The speaker cites the verse that heart belief is unto righteousness and mouth confession is unto salvation.\r\nREF: Born Again — 10:04–10:54\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 10:9–10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+10%3A9%E2%80%9310/>\r\n\r\nQ56. What does the speaker say the Acts 10 account of Cornelius's household shows about when people receive the Holy Spirit and are saved?\r\nA. The Spirit came only after Peter finished his sermon and gave an invitation to receive\r\nB. The Spirit fell while Peter was still speaking, when they placed their faith in Jesus\r\nC. The Spirit came only after the household was baptized in water as Peter instructed\r\nD. The Spirit came only after they had proved their devotion to God over a period of time\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites Acts 10: while Peter was still speaking, the Holy Spirit fell on all who heard, because faith was in their hearts before he even reached the point of an invitation.\r\nREF: BORN AGAIN — [11:39]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10/>\r\n\r\nQ57. How does the speaker describe the relationship between receiving Christ's life and giving one's life to Christ?\r\nA. Receiving Christ's life comes first; consecration follows because the life lived is his\r\nB. Giving one's life to Christ comes first, and he then responds by granting new life\r\nC. Both happen only after a believer completes a period of formal discipleship training\r\nD. They are one and the same act, so they cannot be meaningfully told apart\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says salvation is receiving the life of Christ and the Holy Spirit; only then can a person give his life in consecration and service, because the life he now lives is Christ's (Galatians 2:20).\r\nREF: BORN AGAIN — [12:24]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Galatians 2:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+2%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ58. If someone says, \"I'm a good person and I even behave better than many people in your church,\" what does the speaker want a believer to be able to do?\r\nA. Agree that good behavior is enough to please God and invite the person to attend church\r\nB. Compare the person's behavior with that of church members to settle who is better\r\nC. Explain that being born again is receiving Christ's life by faith, not being well behaved\r\nD. Wait until the person has felt enough guilt about sin before speaking about Christ at all\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says a believer must be able to articulate that, as biological life has an origin, being born again means placing faith in Jesus and receiving the life of Christ and the Holy Spirit, rather than being well behaved.\r\nREF: BORN AGAIN — [09:15]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 3:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A3/>; Ephesians 2:8–10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+2%3A8%E2%80%9310/>\r\n\r\nQ59. What problem does the speaker identify in preaching that treats behavioral modification as the finality of repentance?\r\nA. It overemphasizes forgiveness while ignoring the place of guilt over past sins entirely\r\nB. It treats a mere change of mind as sufficient and denies any place for outward change\r\nC. It teaches that repentance was meant only for the Jews who had rejected Christ\r\nD. It says almost nothing about what Christ did and mainly aims to make people feel guilty\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says some preachers slip works-consciousness into salvation by making behavioral modification the finality of repentance, with almost no mention of what Christ did and a goal of making people feel guilty about their past.\r\nREF: REPENTANCE — [00:16; 00:55]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Titus 3:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Titus+3%3A5/>\r\n\r\nQ60. According to the speaker's reading of Mark 1:15, what makes repentance gospel-centered?\r\nA. How deep a person's sorrow about their past sins happens to be\r\nB. Who a person turns to, not merely what they turn away from\r\nC. How completely a person abandons one particular sinful habit\r\nD. How publicly a person announces the decision to change\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Citing Mark 1:15 (repent and believe in the gospel), the speaker says repentance is turning away from something, but what makes it gospel-centric is what, and more specifically who, a person turns to.\r\nREF: REPENTANCE — [02:46]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Mark 1:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+1%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ61. What careful claim does the speaker make against those who say repentance is only a change of mind with no behavioral modification?\r\nA. Behavioral change is not required for salvation but is a necessary fruit of genuine salvation\r\nB. Behavioral change is required for salvation alongside faith, since faith alone is incomplete\r\nC. Behavioral change is optional for believers and matters mainly for church leaders\r\nD. A change of mind comes only after the person's behavior has already changed first\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says he is choosing his words carefully: he is not saying behavioral change is required for salvation, but that it is a necessary fruit of genuine salvation.\r\nREF: REPENTANCE — [03:39]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 3:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+3%3A8/>; Acts 26:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+26%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ62. What does the speaker say people who use 2 Corinthians 7 to separate metamelomai from metanoia overlook?\r\nA. Paul used the two Greek words as exact synonyms throughout the passage\r\nB. Paul never mentions sorrow anywhere in the passage\r\nC. Paul himself said that godly sorrow leads to repentance\r\nD. Paul taught that sorrow itself is what saves a person\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: In 2 Corinthians 7 the contrast is between being made sorry (metamelomai) and repentance (metanoia), but the speaker notes Paul said godly sorrow leads to repentance, even though feeling bad is not what saves; the blood of Jesus saves.\r\nREF: REPENTANCE — [04:41; 05:47]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 2 Corinthians 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+7/>\r\n\r\nQ63. In the speaker's view, what saves a person in relation to a change of lifestyle?\r\nA. The lifestyle change itself, provided that it is sincere and lasting over time\r\nB. The faith that made the person turn away, not the lifestyle change itself\r\nC. Faith alone, with no need for any resolve to turn away from sin\r\nD. A feeling of sorrow that is deep enough to produce a real change\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says it is not the change of lifestyle but the faith that made you turn away that saves, yet that faith is not genuine without a genuine resolve to turn away from those things.\r\nREF: REPENTANCE — [08:04]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 2:8–10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+2%3A8%E2%80%9310/>; Mark 1:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+1%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ64. How does the speaker respond to the claim that preaching \"just believe in Jesus\" is enough?\r\nA. It is incorrect because it leaves out repentance and so misleads those who hear it\r\nB. It is complete as it stands and needs no further qualification from other texts\r\nC. It is correct only when preached to Gentiles, for whom repentance is not required\r\nD. It is not wrong, just incomplete, because real faith includes a resolve to turn from sin\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that telling people to just believe in Jesus is not incorrect, just incomplete, and he warns of a thin line between grace and lasciviousness.\r\nREF: REPENTANCE — [08:04]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Mark 1:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+1%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ65. What does the speaker's example of resolving never again to give money to an ungrateful person show about repentance?\r\nA. Repentance is not work but an internal posture change that will influence behavior\r\nB. Repentance is the first outward act of refusal, which others can observe and verify\r\nC. Repentance must first be proven through works before it can count as genuine\r\nD. Repentance depends mainly on how badly other people have treated you in the past\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the resolve is not yet works but a changed internal posture that influences future behavior, and that repentance is likewise not work but a posture change that will influence works.\r\nREF: REPENTANCE — [09:04]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Corinthians 7:10–11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+7%3A10%E2%80%9311/>\r\n\r\nQ66. What does the speaker conclude from Acts 11:18, where the Gentiles are granted \"repentance to life\"?\r\nA. Repentance was offered to the Gentiles only after they had first changed their behavior\r\nB. Repentance is mainly remorse over having been part of the crucifixion of Christ\r\nC. Repentance is a change of mind focused on eternal life, which if genuine will affect lifestyle\r\nD. Repentance to life is meant only for Jews who had earlier rejected Christ\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Acts 11:18 shows repentance for Gentiles is a change of mind focused on receiving eternal life, not first of all a change of behavior, though a genuine change of mind will influence a change of lifestyle.\r\nREF: REPENTANCE — [11:08]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 11:18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+11%3A18/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+2/>; Luke 24:47 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A47/>\r\n\r\nQ67. How does the speaker interpret confessing \"the Lord Jesus\" in Romans 10:9?\r\nA. It means repeating a confession that secures salvation regardless of one's lifestyle\r\nB. It means receiving his lordship and living by his commands in response to his grace\r\nC. It means earning one's salvation by faithfully obeying his commands each day\r\nD. It means keeping one's salvation intact by avoiding sin and its consequences\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says confessing Jesus as Lord means receiving his lordship and living by his commands; faith brings responsibilities, not in order to keep salvation but in response to what he has freely done.\r\nREF: REPENTANCE — [12:15]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 10:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+10%3A9/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 11:18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+11%3A18/>\r\n\r\nQ68. How does the speaker respond to the extreme that treats obedience as synonymous with repentance?\r\nA. Repentance consists of the works that show that a person has turned from sin\r\nB. Works consistent with repentance are optional for those who genuinely believe\r\nC. Repentance and behavior change have no necessary connection to each other at all\r\nD. Repentance is an inner resolve to serve the Lord, and behavior change is only its fruit\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Citing Matthew 3:8 and Acts 26:20, the speaker says repentance is a posture change, an inner resolve to serve the Lord, and that the behavior change is the fruit of repentance, not repentance in itself.\r\nREF: REPENTANCE — [13:36]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Matthew 3:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+3%3A8/>; Acts 26:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+26%3A20/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+10/>; Luke 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+3/>\r\n\r\nQ69. According to the speaker, does a believer's ongoing struggle with sin show that their salvation is not genuine?\r\nA. No; like a baby learning to walk, a genuine believer can struggle yet resolve to turn\r\nB. Yes; any continued struggle with sin shows that the repentance was never genuine\r\nC. Yes, unless the struggle ends within a short time after the person first believes\r\nD. No, because ongoing struggle has no connection to whether someone has repented\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says a person who genuinely believes and repents may still be struggling like a baby learning to walk, and that does not invalidate his salvation; he points to Jesus' teaching on forgiving seven times a day.\r\nREF: REPENTANCE — [14:40]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Matthew 3:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+3%3A8/>; Luke 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+3/>; Acts 26:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+26%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ70. What does the speaker say is one way to show you are truly saved and have divine life in you?\r\nA. A complete absence of any struggle with the sins of one's former life\r\nB. A strong sense of guilt that never lifts, however much one has repented\r\nC. A sensitivity to sin that breaks your heart and prompts a resolve to turn\r\nD. A visible record of moral reform that others can point to and confirm\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says one sign of real divine life is a sensitivity to sin: it breaks your heart that you, having received so much love, would still indulge in it, and there should be a resolve to turn away because you are free in Christ.\r\nREF: REPENTANCE — [15:41]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Corinthians 7:10–11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+7%3A10%E2%80%9311/>\r\n\r\nQ71. What image does the speaker use for the right knowledge of God, and which two extremes does it warn against?\r\nA. A razor blade, risking a fall into lasciviousness or works consciousness\r\nB. A narrow bridge over a deep gorge, risking a fall into either pride or despair\r\nC. A steep mountain path, risking a fall into doubt or fanaticism\r\nD. A narrow road at night, risking a fall into unbelief or hypocrisy\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the right knowledge of God is like working on a razor blade, easy to fall to either extreme: lasciviousness, with no incentive to grow in consecration, or works consciousness.\r\nREF: RIGHTEOUSNESS — [00:15]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Titus 2:11-12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Titus+2%3A11-12/>; Romans 6:1-2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6%3A1-2/>\r\n\r\nQ72. How does the speaker explain why Paul affirmed the Corinthians' standing in Christ in the opening of 1 Corinthians?\r\nA. Paul did not yet know about their problems at the time he wrote the opening lines\r\nB. Paul postponed any discussion of their conduct until he could write a later letter\r\nC. Paul used who they are in Christ by grace as a yardstick to realign unstable believers\r\nD. Paul considered their problems minor compared with the gifts they were exercising\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker contrasts two possibilities, that Paul did not know their problems or that he knew and understood the force of righteousness; he takes the second: Paul used who they are in Christ by grace to realign those who were unstable.\r\nREF: RIGHTEOUSNESS — [01:04; 01:48]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Corinthians 1:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+1%3A4/>\r\n\r\nQ73. Which pairing matches what the speaker says Paul knew about the Corinthians when he affirmed their identity in Christ?\r\nA. Lawsuits among believers, in 1 Corinthians 14\r\nB. Sexual immorality, in 1 Corinthians 5\r\nC. Misuse of spiritual gifts, in 1 Corinthians 6\r\nD. Disorder at the Lord's table, in 1 Corinthians 5\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker lists what Paul knew: sexual immorality (1 Corinthians 5), lawsuits (6), disorder at the Lord's table (11), and misuse of gifts (14), and yet he still emphasized their identity in Christ.\r\nREF: RIGHTEOUSNESS — [02:47]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Corinthians 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+5/> | Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Corinthians 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+6/>; 1 Corinthians 14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+14/>; 1 Corinthians 11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+11/>\r\n\r\nQ74. Why does the speaker say Paul and the other apostles called it \"repentance towards God\"?\r\nA. Repentance is mainly an awareness of guilt over the past, which then moves a person to change course\r\nB. Repentance is measured by how fully and how quickly a person's conduct has changed\r\nC. Repentance is directed to God alone and never involves the people one has wronged\r\nD. There must be something a person shifts to, not just from, because repentance responds to light\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says there must be something you are shifting to, not just something you are shifting from, and not just guilt: repentance is a response to light.\r\nREF: RIGHTEOUSNESS — [03:34]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Corinthians 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+5/>; 1 Corinthians 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+6/>; 1 Corinthians 14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+14/>; 1 Corinthians 11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+11/>\r\n\r\nQ75. How does the speaker interpret Ephesians 1's statement that believers are chosen to be \"holy and without blame\"?\r\nA. They are a status in Christ by grace, settled before people did anything right or wrong\r\nB. They are a standard that believers must eventually reach through growth in consecration\r\nC. They are a reward that God gives after a believer's repentance has been completed\r\nD. They are an ideal that applies fully only to believers after they have died\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Reading Ephesians 1:3-6, the speaker says holiness and blamelessness are a status in Christ by grace, determined before we did right or wrong, with righteousness reckoned by faith in Jesus.\r\nREF: RIGHTEOUSNESS — [04:26]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Ephesians 1:3–6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A3%E2%80%936/>\r\n\r\nQ76. How does the speaker define righteousness?\r\nA. A record of conduct that God finds acceptable over a lifetime of obedience\r\nB. The inner discipline that lets a believer resist sin consistently over time\r\nC. The absence of any sin in a believer's life after the moment of conversion\r\nD. Standing before God as full beneficiaries of all that Christ has done, by grace\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker defines righteousness as standing before God as full beneficiaries of all that Christ has done, so that by grace believers are worthy of Christ's love and of a relationship with God forever.\r\nREF: RIGHTEOUSNESS — [05:11]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A3/>\r\n\r\nQ77. When a believer sins, how does the speaker say they should approach God in prayer?\r\nA. After first assessing their own behavior to be sure that they qualify to pray\r\nB. By faith in Christ their advocate, not by first assessing how well they have behaved\r\nC. Only after they have fully stopped the sin and shown that the change is lasting\r\nD. Only by asking for forgiveness and for nothing else until the sin is dealt with\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says it is not that every time we pray we first assess how well we have behaved; we have an advocate with the Father, and faith in what Christ has done gives access, while right standing comes from faith alone, not behavior.\r\nREF: RIGHTEOUSNESS — [06:12; 06:53]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 John 2:1–2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+2%3A1%E2%80%932/>\r\n\r\nQ78. In the church drama the speaker describes, what happens just as the accused sinner is about to be condemned?\r\nA. An angel steps forward and reads out the sinner's good deeds in his defense\r\nB. The judge delays the sentence to give the sinner time to repent first\r\nC. Jesus appears and says, in effect, \"see my blood, I died for him\"\r\nD. The sinner pleads for mercy and the judge agrees to a lighter sentence\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker recalls a court-setting drama in which, just as the sinner was about to be condemned, Jesus came out and said in effect, see my blood, I died for him.\r\nREF: JUSTIFICATION — [00:00]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Peter 2:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+2%3A24/>\r\n\r\nQ79. What does the lesson say the Greek word often used for \"justify\" means?\r\nA. To make morally perfect over time\r\nB. To declare righteous, acquit or vindicate\r\nC. To empower for holy living\r\nD. To forgive and forget\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says it means to declare righteous, or to acquit or vindicate. It is like a judge saying \"discharged and acquitted.\"\r\nREF: Justification — 05:03\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ80. Why does the speaker say Jesus' words to the criminal on the cross show \"a judge talking\"?\r\nA. Because Jesus sentenced the criminal for his crimes\r\nB. Because the criminal had earned paradise by confessing his crimes\r\nC. Because Jesus' declaration that the man would be with him in paradise that day was a verdict of not guilty\r\nD. Because Jesus postponed the criminal's verdict to a later date\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Jesus said \"today you shall be with me in paradise.\" The speaker says this is a judge declaring the man not guilty.\r\nREF: Justification — 07:06\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 23:39–43 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+23%3A39%E2%80%9343/>\r\n\r\nQ81. What does the speaker take from Abraham's appeal in Genesis 18:25 over Sodom and Gomorrah?\r\nA. Abraham knew God as the judge of the whole earth who does right\r\nB. Abraham regarded God's coming judgment on the cities as unfair\r\nC. Abraham saw God mainly as a merciful father rather than a judge\r\nD. Abraham believed that only the nation of Israel would ever be judged\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that in asking whether the judge of all the earth shall not do right, Abraham showed he knew God was, among other things, the judge of the whole earth.\r\nREF: JUSTIFICATION — [01:16]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 18:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+18%3A25/> | Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Samuel 2:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Samuel+2%3A10/>\r\n\r\nQ82. What does the speaker point out about 2 Corinthians 5:10 compared with Romans 14:10-12?\r\nA. It limits appearing before the judgment seat to unbelievers alone\r\nB. It says that Christ will judge only entire nations at the end and never individual people\r\nC. It says that believers will not be called on to give any account\r\nD. It names Christ's judgment seat, where Romans 14 names the judgment seat of God\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reads Romans 14 (all will stand before the judgment seat of God and give account) and 2 Corinthians 5:10 (all must appear before the judgment seat of Christ), noting that this one says specifically Christ.\r\nREF: JUSTIFICATION — [02:10]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 14:10–12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+14%3A10%E2%80%9312/>; 2 Corinthians 5:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A10/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 18:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+18%3A25/>; 1 Samuel 2:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Samuel+2%3A10/>; Psalm 7:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+7%3A11/>; Romans 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8/>\r\n\r\nQ83. How does the speaker describe the relationship between justification and condemnation, drawing on Romans 8?\r\nA. They are two different names for one and the same verdict from the judge\r\nB. They are opposite legal terms: a person is either justified or condemned\r\nC. They are successive stages of one legal process that ends in acquittal\r\nD. They are unrelated ideas, since one is legal and the other is spiritual\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker first calls them synonyms, then corrects himself to antonyms, opposite words, so a person is either justified or condemned, and both are legal terms.\r\nREF: JUSTIFICATION — [03:11]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Psalm 7:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+7%3A11/>; Romans 14:10, 12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+14%3A10%2C+12/>; 2 Corinthians 5:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A10/>\r\n\r\nQ84. What does the speaker say \"no condemnation\" in Romans 8:1 means for those in Christ Jesus?\r\nA. They no longer commit any wrongdoing once they have come to be in Christ\r\nB. They are spared every earthly consequence of what they did before believing\r\nC. They can no longer be sentenced for their wrongdoings, since they are in Christ\r\nD. They are still sentenced but the penalty is reduced to a lighter one\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker treats condemnation as legal language, like a court sentencing the accused; so no condemnation means those in Christ can no longer be sentenced for their wrongdoings.\r\nREF: JUSTIFICATION — [04:06; 09:24]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 8:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ85. Why does the speaker say that, in evangelism, understanding condemnation is needed to appreciate justification?\r\nA. Fear of condemnation is what produces lasting holiness in the lives of believers\r\nB. Condemnation remains a believer's status until their good works over many years have proved otherwise\r\nC. Justification applies only to people who have already been sentenced by a human court\r\nD. People must see they deserve death, the wages of sin, before eternal life in Christ makes sense\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that to appreciate justification you must understand condemnation: you deserve to die, the wages of sin is death, but the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord.\r\nREF: JUSTIFICATION — [05:03]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 6:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6%3A23/>; Romans 8:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ86. How does the speaker connect John 3:16 to a person being found not guilty?\r\nA. It shows that God acquits people whose faith is greater than the weight of their sin\r\nB. It shows the Father's love in giving his Son, who as substitute died the person's death\r\nC. It shows that God simply overlooks sin because he loves the whole world so much\r\nD. It shows that the Son's death makes forgiveness automatic whether or not a person believes\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says a person is found not guilty on one basis alone, that Christ is his substitute, and calls this the love of the Father in John 3:16: he let his Son die for you.\r\nREF: JUSTIFICATION — [05:03; 06:07]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ87. What does the speaker's ledger analogy teach?\r\nA. Wrongdoings are a debt on one side; the blood of Jesus on the other is enough to blot out every one\r\nB. Good deeds on one side can be weighed against wrongdoings on the other to reach a balance\r\nC. God keeps a running record of a person's conduct that is carefully reviewed at the end of life, and weighs it\r\nD. Each separate sin must be answered by its own offering before it can be cleared\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker describes a ledger with all of a person's wrongdoing on one side and the precious blood of Jesus on the other; he says one drop of that blood is sufficient and efficacious to blot out every wrongdoing.\r\nREF: JUSTIFICATION — [06:07; 07:06]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ88. How does the speaker characterize justification?\r\nA. An inner change in a person's very nature, like a change of essence\r\nB. A reward that is granted after a believer has lived righteously for a time\r\nC. A legal status that results once the judge declares a person not guilty\r\nD. A feeling of relief that depends on how confident a believer happens to feel\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says justification is a legal term and a status, not quite an essence; once the judge has declared you not guilty, that has become your status.\r\nREF: JUSTIFICATION — [07:06]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 8:33–34 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A33%E2%80%9334/>\r\n\r\nQ89. Someone keeps reminding a believer of his past and calling him guilty. Which response best reflects the lesson on justification?\r\nA. He should assume his accusers may be right until he proves himself.\r\nB. Once the judge has declared him not guilty, no other opinion holds sway, regardless of where he has been or what he has done.\r\nC. He should defend himself before every accuser.\r\nD. He should wait for his behaviour to confirm the verdict.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that when the judge declares you not guilty, nobody can call you guilty and no other opinion holds sway.\r\nREF: Justification — 07:06–08:16\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 8:33–34 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A33%E2%80%9334/>\r\n\r\nQ90. How does the lesson distinguish redemption from justification?\r\nA. Redemption applies only to Old Testament believers, while justification applies to the church.\r\nB. Redemption is the believer's response, while justification is God's gift.\r\nC. Redemption describes the provision of salvation (Christ dying and rising again), while justification is the legal declaration that the believer is not guilty.\r\nD. They are the same thing described in different words.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Redemption describes the provision or execution of salvation. Justification is the legal declaration.\r\nREF: Justification — 08:16–09:24\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 8:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ91. In the speaker's stolen-car illustration, which element corresponds to justification?\r\nA. The kinsman redeemer going and paying what was owed for the stolen car\r\nB. The clearing of the record: the declaration that the person is henceforth not guilty\r\nC. The person being sent to prison for the theft until the penalty is served\r\nD. The person's sincere promise to the judge that he will never steal anything again\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: In the illustration, paying what is owed corresponds to redemption, the provision of salvation, while the clearing of the record, the declaration that the person is henceforth not guilty, is justification.\r\nREF: JUSTIFICATION — [09:24]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 8:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A1/>\n\nDECK: Discipleship Experience (Section 2) Soteriology II\r\n\r\nQ1. Isaiah 32:17 is used to show the result of righteousness in the life of a believer. What does it produce?\r\nA. Boldness and authority\r\nB. Peace, quietness, and assurance forever\r\nC. Spiritual gifts and power\r\nD. Holiness through personal effort\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Isaiah 32:17 is quoted as saying righteousness produces peace, quietness, and assurance.\r\nREF: ASSURANCE — [00:16]–[01:14] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Isaiah 32:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+32%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ2. Which statement best describes propitiation as taught in the lesson?\r\nA. Christ's death satisfied the demands of God's justice.\r\nB. Christ's resurrection replaced His sacrifice.\r\nC. God overlooked humanity's sin.\r\nD. Believers now satisfy God's justice through obedience.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Christ’s redemptive work appeased God’s justice; the lesson describes propitiation as satisfaction.\r\nREF: PROPITIATION — [00:16]–[01:13] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Isaiah 53 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53/> | Added from online research: 1 John 2:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+2%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ3. The teaching describes reconciliation as God's work through Christ to:\r\nA. reward righteous living.\r\nB. remove the hostility between God and humanity.\r\nC. restore Israel's kingdom.\r\nD. unite all nations politically.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Reconciliation is described as Christ’s work removing the hostility between God and humanity.\r\nREF: ATONEMENT — [02:09]–[04:11] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5/>; 2 Corinthians 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5/>\r\n\r\nQ4. What does it mean to redeem someone?\r\nA. Restore a broken relationship.\r\nB. Ignore an offence.\r\nC. Free someone from bondage by paying a ransom.\r\nD. Remove every earthly consequence of sin.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: To redeem is to free someone from bondage by paying ransom.\r\nREF: REDEMPTION — [04:49]–[05:50] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+2/>; 1 Peter 1:18–19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+1%3A18%E2%80%9319/>; Ephesians 1:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ5. According to the teaching, why are people \"without excuse\" before God?\r\nA. They all possess the written Law.\r\nB. Creation reveals the existence of God.\r\nC. Every person has heard the gospel.\r\nD. Every nation received the prophets.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says creation makes God’s existence knowable and therefore leaves people without excuse.\r\nREF: THOSE WHO NEVER HEARD THE GOSPEL — [05:42]–[06:36] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 1:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+1%3A20/>; Acts 17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17/>\r\n\r\nQ6. Believers sometimes struggle with assurance because:\r\nA. God deliberately withholds assurance.\r\nB. They are conditioned to think acceptance must always be earned.\r\nC. Salvation depends on daily performance.\r\nD. Faith naturally produces uncertainty.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says believers may doubt assurance because they are conditioned to think in terms of what must be earned.\r\nREF: ASSURANCE — [02:15]–[03:32] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Philippians 1:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+1%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ7. The comparison between Noah's ark and the blood of Jesus illustrates that:\r\nA. Christ's atoning work provides the covering that secures believers.\r\nB. Noah earned God's favour through obedience.\r\nC. Judgment is greater under grace.\r\nD. The ark represents heaven.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson compares the ark’s protective covering with the covering provided by Jesus’ blood.\r\nREF: ATONEMENT — [05:04]–[07:03] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Peter 3:20–21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+3%3A20%E2%80%9321/>\r\n\r\nQ8. Which responsibility was specifically associated with a kinsman-redeemer?\r\nA. Leading Israel in worship.\r\nB. Buying back a relative's inheritance.\r\nC. Interpreting the Law.\r\nD. Serving as high priest.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: A kinsman-redeemer could buy back land or inheritance sold because of poverty.\r\nREF: REDEMPTION — [00:55]–[01:53] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Leviticus 25:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Leviticus+25%3A25/>\r\n\r\nQ9. Why was Cain mentioned in the teaching?\r\nA. To explain the beginning of sacrifice.\r\nB. To show people instinctively recognize wrongdoing.\r\nC. To introduce God's covenant.\r\nD. To explain the origin of death.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Cain is used to illustrate that people can recognize wrongdoing without a specific written command.\r\nREF: THOSE WHO NEVER HEARD THE GOSPEL — [02:12]–[03:07] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 2:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+2%3A14/>; Romans 2:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+2%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ10. Paul expressed confidence that God would:\r\nA. prevent believers from facing trials.\r\nB. complete the work He began in believers.\r\nC. remove every weakness immediately.\r\nD. reward only mature Christians.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson quotes Philippians 1:6 as Paul’s confidence that God will complete the good work He began in believers.\r\nREF: ASSURANCE — [03:32]–[04:29] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Philippians 1:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+1%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ11. Propitiation is best understood as:\r\nA. regaining favour where favour had been lost.\r\nB. becoming spiritually mature.\r\nC. earning God's acceptance.\r\nD. receiving spiritual gifts.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson explains propitiation as appeasement or satisfaction and describes it as regaining favor where it was lost.\r\nREF: PROPITIATION — [00:16]–[01:13] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 John 2:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+2%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ12. Who initiated humanity's reconciliation with God?\r\nA. Humanity\r\nB. The Church\r\nC. God\r\nD. The Prophets\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson explicitly identifies God as the initiator of reconciliation.\r\nREF: ATONEMENT — [08:05]–[08:56] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 5:18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A18/>\r\n\r\nQ13. Why does redemption and forgiveness go hand in hand?\r\nA. Forgiveness came at the cost of Christ's blood.\r\nB. Forgiveness replaced redemption.\r\nC. Redemption only applies to Israel.\r\nD. Forgiveness is earned through obedience.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson connects redemption and forgiveness, saying forgiveness came at the cost of Christ’s blood.\r\nREF: REDEMPTION — [06:49]–[07:57] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 1:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ14. Romans 2 teaches that even those without God's written law possess:\r\nA. complete knowledge of salvation.\r\nB. an instinctive awareness of right and wrong.\r\nC. perfect obedience.\r\nD. the Abrahamic covenant.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says people without the written law still have an instinctive moral awareness.\r\nREF: THOSE WHO NEVER HEARD THE GOSPEL — [01:09]–[02:12] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 2:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+2%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ15. The illustration of protecting a candle from the wind emphasizes:\r\nA. self-preservation.\r\nB. perseverance through discipline.\r\nC. the Holy Spirit's preserving work.\r\nD. the importance of church leadership.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The candle illustration describes the Holy Spirit preserving believers.\r\nREF: ASSURANCE — [06:14]–[07:19] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Jude 24–25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jude+24%E2%80%9325/>\r\n\r\nQ16. Isaiah 53:11 supports propitiation by stating that the Lord would:\r\nA. remember sin forever.\r\nB. see the suffering of His Servant and be satisfied.\r\nC. establish a new covenant.\r\nD. judge Israel's enemies.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson connects Isaiah 53:11’s ‘be satisfied’ with propitiation as satisfaction.\r\nREF: PROPITIATION — [00:16]–[01:13] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Isaiah 53:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ17. The Greek word katalaso helps explain reconciliation by emphasizing:\r\nA. an exchange made possible through Christ.\r\nB. forgiveness without sacrifice.\r\nC. moral improvement.\r\nD. religious devotion.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson describes reconciliation as involving an exchange made possible through Christ’s substitution.\r\nREF: ATONEMENT — [07:03]–[08:05] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Leviticus 16:30 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Leviticus+16%3A30/>\r\n\r\nQ18. The statement, \"The judge is the one who paid the price,\" highlights that:\r\nA. Christ fully met the requirements needed for believers' freedom.\r\nB. everyone pays for personal sin.\r\nC. justice was cancelled.\r\nD. believers no longer face temptation.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says Christ, who knows the requirements, paid the price in full, freeing believers from guilt.\r\nREF: REDEMPTION — [10:40]–[11:29] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 1:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ19. The story of Cornelius illustrates that:\r\nA. good works save.\r\nB. God responds to sincere seekers and brings them further light.\r\nC. evangelism is unnecessary.\r\nD. prayer alone brings salvation.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The Apostle says God responds to those seeking Him and describes God sending Peter to Cornelius with further information.\r\nREF: THOSE WHO NEVER HEARD THE GOSPEL — [06:36]–[07:33] and [16:38]–[17:33] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 10:1–48 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10%3A1%E2%80%9348/>\r\n\r\nQ20. According to Jude 24, believers can be confident because God is able to:\r\nA. make them wealthy.\r\nB. keep them from stumbling.\r\nC. remove all suffering.\r\nD. prevent every temptation.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson quotes Jude 24 as assurance that God is able to keep believers from stumbling.\r\nREF: ASSURANCE — [08:07]–[09:14] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Jude 24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jude+24/>\r\n\r\nQ21. Romans 3:25 teaches that Christ became our propitiation through:\r\nA. His teachings.\r\nB. His blood.\r\nC. His miracles.\r\nD. His resurrection.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson explicitly says Christ was set forth as propitiation by His blood.\r\nREF: PROPITIATION — [02:14]–[03:11] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 3:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A25/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Zephaniah 3:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Zephaniah+3%3A17/>; Romans 3:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A24/>; 1 John 2:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+2%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ22. The Holy Spirit's role in reconciliation includes:\r\nA. replacing Christ's work.\r\nB. applying the benefits of reconciliation to believers.\r\nC. judging unbelievers.\r\nD. establishing the Law.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson identifies the Holy Spirit as the applier of reconciliation.\r\nREF: ATONEMENT — [08:05]–[08:56] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 5:18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A18/>; Romans 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8/>\r\n\r\nQ23. Christ became our kinsman-redeemer by:\r\nA. becoming human.\r\nB. being born into Israel.\r\nC. fulfilling the Law.\r\nD. After completing discipleship classes.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says Christ became human and thereby became our kinsman.\r\nREF: REDEMPTION — [03:52]–[04:49] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+2/>; 1 Peter 1:18–19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+1%3A18%E2%80%9319/>\r\n\r\nQ24. What assumption does the teaching challenge about people who have never heard the gospel?\r\nA. That they are automatically innocent before God.\r\nB. That they are all saved.\r\nC. That they lived before Christ.\r\nD. That they rejected the gospel.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson challenges the assumption that people are justified simply because they have not heard the gospel.\r\nREF: THOSE WHO NEVER HEARD THE GOSPEL — [00:00]–[01:09] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 2:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+2%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ25. According to the teaching, assurance is strongest when it is based on:\r\nA. personal consistency.\r\nB. spiritual discipline.\r\nC. God's preserving work.\r\nD. church accountability.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson grounds assurance in God and the Spirit’s preserving work rather than in self-confidence.\r\nREF: ASSURANCE — [06:14]–[07:19] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 8:31–39 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A31%E2%80%9339/>\r\n\r\nQ26. Rahab's example shows that genuine faith is:\r\nA. based on complete theological knowledge.\r\nB. demonstrated through corresponding actions.\r\nC. limited to Israel.\r\nD. always rewarded immediately.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Rahab’s actions are presented as a demonstration of her faith.\r\nREF: THOSE WHO NEVER HEARD THE GOSPEL — [12:40]–[13:39] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Galatians 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+3/>; Hebrews 11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+11/>; Joshua 2:9–11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Joshua+2%3A9%E2%80%9311/>; James 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+2/>\r\n\r\nQ27. According to the second view presented, people are judged based on:\r\nA. their nationality.\r\nB. the light they received and how they responded to it.\r\nC. the era in which they lived.\r\nD. the number of good works they performed.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The second view is described as judgment according to the information a person received and their response to it.\r\nREF: THOSE WHO NEVER HEARD THE GOSPEL — [13:39]–[14:50] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 2:12–16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+2%3A12%E2%80%9316/>\r\n\r\nQ28. What enables believers to call God \"Abba, Father\"?\r\nA. Spiritual maturity.\r\nB. The spirit of adoption received through the Holy Spirit.\r\nC. Faithful church attendance.\r\nD. Keeping the commandments.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson links the Spirit of adoption with believers crying ‘Abba, Father.’\r\nREF: ATONEMENT — [08:05]–[08:56] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 8:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ29. One result of Christ's propitiatory work emphasized in the teaching is that believers:\r\nA. stand without guilt before God.\r\nB. no longer need repentance.\r\nC. become incapable of sin.\r\nD. immediately become spiritually mature.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says propitiation means believers stand without guilt before God.\r\nREF: PROPITIATION — [03:11]–end of transcript (approximate boundary)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 3:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A24/>; Romans 3:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A25/>; 1 John 2:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+2%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ30. Which statement best reflects the Apostle's own conclusion regarding those who have never heard the gospel?\r\nA. They are automatically saved.\r\nB. They are automatically condemned.\r\nC. He leans toward the view that God judges people according to the light they have received.\r\nD. Scripture provides no basis for any conclusion.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The Apostle says he leans toward the view based on the information a person received and their receptiveness to it.\r\nREF: THOSE WHO NEVER HEARD THE GOSPEL — [18:40]–end of transcript (approximate boundary); see also [13:39]–[14:50] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Joshua 2:9–11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Joshua+2%3A9%E2%80%9311/>; James 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+2/>; John 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ31. In the lesson, what was a kinsman redeemer?\r\nA. A priest who offered sacrifices on behalf of the whole nation\r\nB. A judge who settled disputes between relatives\r\nC. A relative who had the responsibility of rescuing or redeeming someone in loss or bondage\r\nD. A prophet who announced the cancellation of debts\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker describes a kinsman redeemer as a relative responsible for rescuing or redeeming a person in loss or bondage.\r\nREF: Redemption — 00:16\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ruth 4:1–10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ruth+4%3A1%E2%80%9310/>\r\n\r\nQ32. Which list gives the four situations in which the lesson says the kinsman redeemer law took effect?\r\nA. Redeeming land sold through poverty, freeing a relative sold into slavery, providing an heir through levirate marriage, and avenging blood\r\nB. Redeeming land sold through poverty, collecting debts owed to the temple, providing an heir through levirate marriage, and avenging blood\r\nC. Redeeming land sold through poverty, freeing a relative sold into slavery, leading the family in worship, and avenging blood\r\nD. Freeing a relative sold into slavery, providing an heir through levirate marriage, interpreting the law, and avenging blood\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The four situations are redeeming sold land (Leviticus 25:25), redeeming a relative sold into slavery, providing an heir through levirate marriage, and avenging blood.\r\nREF: Redemption — 00:16–03:52\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Leviticus 25:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Leviticus+25%3A25/>\r\n\r\nQ33. Why does the lesson say God created the kinsman redeemer system for people who had sold themselves into slavery?\r\nA. To punish people who fell into debt\r\nB. To increase the number of servants in Jewish households\r\nC. To make sure poor people stayed in service permanently\r\nD. Because God did not want any of his people to be slaves, so relatives could redeem them as a system of grace\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker calls the system a panacea for people in financial trouble who sold themselves into slavery. God did not want any of his people to be slaves.\r\nREF: Redemption — 01:53\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Leviticus 25:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Leviticus+25%3A25/>\r\n\r\nQ34. What does the lesson say about levirate marriage?\r\nA. A widow's land passed to the king when her husband died without an heir.\r\nB. When a man died without an heir, one of his brothers could marry his widow to continue his legacy, and it was seen as a graceful law, not a forced one.\r\nC. A widow was required to leave her husband's family when he had no heir.\r\nD. A man had to marry the widow of any relative who had been killed.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: A brother could marry the widow so the man's lineage was not extinguished. The speaker, using Ruth and Naomi, says it was a graceful law the women often wanted.\r\nREF: Redemption — 02:47\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Deuteronomy 25:5–10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+25%3A5%E2%80%9310/>; Ruth 4:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ruth+4%3A5/>\r\n\r\nQ35. Why does the lesson say it is fitting to call Christ a kinsman redeemer, and which texts does it cite?\r\nA. Because he was born into the priestly tribe (Hebrews 2:14) and offered animal sacrifices (1 Peter 1:18–19)\r\nB. Because he became human, a kinsman (Hebrews 2:14), and paid ransom with his blood (1 Peter 1:18–19)\r\nC. Because he inherited land in Israel (Hebrews 2:14) and bought it back (1 Peter 1:18–19)\r\nD. Because he avenged Israel's enemies (Hebrews 2:14) and defeated death by his teaching (1 Peter 1:18–19)\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: In Hebrews 2 he became human, meaning a kinsman. He paid ransom with his blood (1 Peter 1:18–19).\r\nREF: Redemption — 03:52\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 2:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+2%3A14/>; 1 Peter 1:18–19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+1%3A18%E2%80%9319/>\r\n\r\nQ36. What does it mean to redeem someone, and how does the lesson apply this to salvation?\r\nA. To restore a broken relationship; God removes the hostility between himself and humanity\r\nB. To declare someone not guilty; God acquits the sinner in court\r\nC. To cover something so that nothing can get in; Jesus' blood covers us\r\nD. To free someone in bondage by paying a ransom; God gave Jesus as the ransom for our sins so we can be free from bondage\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Redeeming is freeing someone in bondage by paying ransom. The speaker says redeem and ransom go hand in hand.\r\nREF: Redemption — 04:49–05:50\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+2/>; 1 Peter 1:18–19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+1%3A18%E2%80%9319/>; Ephesians 1:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ37. What does the lesson say the Greek word for \"forgiveness\" in Ephesians 1:7 means?\r\nA. To release, an expression used of someone kept in bondage who was set free\r\nB. To forget, as when a record is wiped from memory\r\nC. To pardon, as when a judge cancels a sentence\r\nD. To cover, as when a roof is sealed against rain\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The Greek word means to release, an old expression for someone kept in bondage who was released. Christ's forgiveness, his ransom, freed us from the bondage of sin.\r\nREF: Redemption — 05:50–06:49\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Ephesians 1:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ38. What does the lesson say about the relationship between redemption and forgiveness?\r\nA. Forgiveness replaced the need for redemption.\r\nB. Redemption is a reward given after forgiveness has been earned.\r\nC. They go hand in hand: forgiveness came at a cost, since God did not simply say \"I forgive you\" and Christ's blood was the ransom.\r\nD. They are separate gifts, with forgiveness free and redemption paid for by believers.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says redemption and forgiveness go hand in hand and that forgiveness came at a cost. His blood was the ransom.\r\nREF: Redemption — 06:49\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 1:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ39. What point does the Titanic illustration make about ransom?\r\nA. Ransom can mean someone giving his life at his own expense to save another, so one need not ask to whom the ransom was paid.\r\nB. Ransom always requires payment to a specific person.\r\nC. Christ's death was identical in every respect to the romantic sacrifice in the film.\r\nD. Ransom is meaningful only to people who have been kidnapped.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: After noting the long debate over who the ransom was paid to, the speaker says someone living at your expense is still ransom, whether or not it is paid to anybody. He adds that the film's character \"is not Jesus Christ anyway.\"\r\nREF: Redemption — 06:49–08:49\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 1:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A7/>; Colossians 1:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+1%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ40. How does the lesson compare Colossians 1:14 with Ephesians 1:7?\r\nA. Colossians speaks of ransom and Ephesians speaks of reconciliation.\r\nB. They disagree about how redemption comes.\r\nC. They say almost exactly the same thing; Ephesians 1:7 adds \"according to the riches of his grace.\"\r\nD. They say almost exactly the same thing; Colossians 1:14 adds \"according to the riches of his grace.\"\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Colossians 1:14 says almost exactly what Ephesians 1:7 says, and the only difference is that Ephesians adds \"according to riches of his grace.\"\r\nREF: Redemption — 09:44\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Ephesians 1:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A7/>; Colossians 1:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+1%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ41. What does the lesson highlight from Colossians 1:15–16 about the Redeemer?\r\nA. He was a human representative who acted apart from the Creator.\r\nB. He was the Creator of the whole world, through whom all things were created, yet he took our place and died our death.\r\nC. He was an angelic being of great rank.\r\nD. He was one of the principalities and powers.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The passage shows the kinsman redeemer was in fact the creator of the whole world, who took our place and died our death.\r\nREF: Redemption — 09:44\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Colossians 1:15–16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+1%3A15%E2%80%9316/>\r\n\r\nQ42. What does the lesson conclude from Paul asking \"Who is it that condemns?\" and answering that it is Christ who died?\r\nA. Christ will still condemn believers who sin after salvation.\r\nB. Christ's death cancelled the requirements of justice.\r\nC. Believers must still pay part of the price.\r\nD. The judge is the one who paid the price in full, so believers in Christ are free from the guilt of sin.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the judge knows the requirements and paid them in full, so those in Christ are free from guilt.\r\nREF: Redemption — 10:40\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 8:33–34 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A33%E2%80%9334/>\r\n\r\nQ43. A believer is troubled by flashbacks of past mistakes and still feels guilt and shame. Which response reflects the lesson?\r\nA. The lingering shame shows the ransom was only partly paid.\r\nB. He should make up for his past through acts of penance.\r\nC. He should wait until his memories fade before trusting that he is free.\r\nD. In Christ the guilt of his sin has been washed away forever through redemption by his blood, so he should walk in newness of life and liberty with thanksgiving.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker acknowledges flashbacks and shame, then says the guilt has been washed away and the ransom paid in full. Believers should walk in that liberty with thanksgiving.\r\nREF: Redemption — 10:40–11:29\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 1:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ44. How does the lesson relate substitutionary atonement to propitiation?\r\nA. They are two names for exactly the same thing.\r\nB. They are cause and effect: Christ took our place and died our death, and as a result the justice demands of God were appeased.\r\nC. Propitiation is the cause, and substitutionary atonement is its effect.\r\nD. They are unrelated doctrines.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker calls them cause and effect: Christ's redemptive work in our place appeased the justice demands of God.\r\nREF: Propitiation — 00:16\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Isaiah 53 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53/> | Added from online research: 1 John 2:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+2%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ45. How does the lesson define propitiation in simple terms?\r\nA. To free someone from bondage by paying a ransom\r\nB. To declare someone righteous or acquitted\r\nC. To regain the favour of someone with whom favour was lost; to appease\r\nD. To cover something so that nothing can seep in\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Propitiation means to regain favour with someone with whom you lost favour, that is, to appease.\r\nREF: Propitiation — 00:16\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 John 2:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+2%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ46. Which other word does the lesson give for propitiation?\r\nA. Satisfaction\r\nB. Substitution\r\nC. Release\r\nD. Adoption\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says another word for propitiation is satisfaction, when the demands of something have been wholly met.\r\nREF: Propitiation — 01:13\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 John 2:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+2%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ47. How does the lesson use Isaiah 53:10–11?\r\nA. Verse 10 portrays propitiation and verse 11 portrays redemption.\r\nB. Verse 10 portrays reconciliation and verse 11 portrays justification.\r\nC. Verse 10 explains substitutionary atonement (he makes his soul an offering for sin), while verse 11 shows propitiation: he sees the travail of his soul and is satisfied, meaning the atonement was enough.\r\nD. Both verses portray only the suffering of Christ, with no idea of satisfaction.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Verse 10 passes as an explanation of substitutionary atonement. Verse 11 shows satisfaction, meaning the sacrifice was provided, fulfilled and sufficient, like \"it is finished.\"\r\nREF: Propitiation — 00:16–01:13\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Isaiah 53:10–11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53%3A10%E2%80%9311/>\r\n\r\nQ48. What does the lesson say Zephaniah 3:17 pictures?\r\nA. God's anger being poured out on the nations\r\nB. The Messiah's birth in Bethlehem\r\nC. The believer's duty to rejoice\r\nD. A prophetic picture that, when the demands of justice have been met, God rests in his love and holds nothing against us\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker calls it a prophetic declaration of redemption in Christ. When the demands of justice are met, God rests in his love and holds nothing against us.\r\nREF: Propitiation — 02:14\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Zephaniah 3:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Zephaniah+3%3A17/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 3:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A24/>; Romans 3:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A25/>\r\n\r\nQ49. Which pairing correctly matches the two New Testament texts the lesson cites for propitiation with their wording?\r\nA. Romans 3:25 — \"he himself is the propitiation for our sins, and for the sins of the world\"; 1 John 2:2 — God set forth Jesus as propitiation by his blood through faith\r\nB. Romans 3:25 — God set forth Jesus as propitiation by his blood through faith; 1 John 2:2 — he himself is the propitiation for our sins and for the sins of the world\r\nC. Romans 3:25 — he shall see the travail of his soul and be satisfied; 1 John 2:2 — God set forth Jesus by his blood\r\nD. Romans 3:25 and 1 John 2:2 both say Jesus is the propitiation for believers only\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Romans 3:25 says God set Jesus forth as propitiation by his blood through faith. 1 John 2:2 says he himself is the propitiation for our sins and for the sins of the world.\r\nREF: Propitiation — 02:14–03:11\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 3:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A25/>; 1 John 2:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+2%3A2/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Zephaniah 3:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Zephaniah+3%3A17/>; Romans 3:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A24/>\r\n\r\nQ50. According to the lesson, what is the personal result for believers of the propitiation in Christ Jesus?\r\nA. They stand without guilt, unblamable in the sight of God.\r\nB. They become incapable of sinning.\r\nC. They are exempt from all earthly trials.\r\nD. They no longer need faith.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says believers now stand without guilt, unblamable in God's sight, because of the propitiation in Christ Jesus.\r\nREF: Propitiation — 03:11\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 3:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A24/>; Romans 3:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A25/>; 1 John 2:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+2%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ51. Which sequence reflects the lesson's logic from the cross to the believer's standing before God?\r\nA. God's justice is appeased → Christ takes our place and dies our death → God holds nothing against us\r\nB. Christ takes our place and dies our death → the demands of God's justice are appeased → God rests in his love and holds nothing against us\r\nC. God holds nothing against us → Christ takes our place → justice is appeased\r\nD. Christ dies our death → God rests in his love → justice is appeased\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Substitution is the cause and propitiation the effect. Once justice's demands are met, God rests in his love and holds nothing against us.\r\nREF: Propitiation — 00:16–02:14\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Isaiah 53 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53/>; Zephaniah 3:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Zephaniah+3%3A17/>; Romans 3:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A24/>; Romans 3:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A25/>\r\n\r\nQ52. How does the lesson connect broken human relationships to the Bible's narrative?\r\nA. The Bible begins with a beautiful relationship between God and mankind in Eden that was broken when Adam disobeyed, and reconciliation is God removing the hostility through Christ.\r\nB. Broken human relationships show that God also refuses to speak to those who offend him.\r\nC. Broken human relationships are repaired when both people apologise, which is how God and man were reconciled.\r\nD. The Bible begins with hostility between God and mankind that Adam had to remove.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: After the friendship story, the speaker says the Bible starts with a beautiful relationship in Eden, broken when Adam disobeyed. Reconciliation is God removing the hostility through Christ.\r\nREF: Atonement — 02:09\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 3:8–24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+3%3A8%E2%80%9324/>; 2 Corinthians 5:18–19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A18%E2%80%9319/>\r\n\r\nQ53. How does the lesson define reconciliation?\r\nA. Regaining the favour of someone with whom favour was lost\r\nB. Freeing someone in bondage by paying a ransom\r\nC. The work of God through Christ in which he removes the hostility between himself and humanity\r\nD. A legal declaration that a person is not guilty\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Reconciliation is the work of God through Christ removing the hostility between himself and humanity. It is the fourth term after propitiation, justification and redemption.\r\nREF: Atonement — 02:09–03:13\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5/>; 2 Corinthians 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5/>\r\n\r\nQ54. What contrast does the lesson draw from Romans 5:10–11?\r\nA. Jews were reconciled by the law, but Gentiles were reconciled by faith.\r\nB. Believers are reconciled by Christ's life, and saved by his death.\r\nC. Believers were friends of God who became enemies by their own choice.\r\nD. While we were enemies we were reconciled to God by the death of his Son; much more, having been reconciled, we shall be saved by his life.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker stresses \"were enemies\" and \"were reconciled,\" with reconciliation by Christ's death and being saved by his life.\r\nREF: Atonement — 03:13\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 5:10–11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A10%E2%80%9311/> | Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5/>\r\n\r\nQ55. Why does the speaker stress that God was the initiator of reconciliation?\r\nA. Because man had to call out to God first before God would act\r\nB. Because the Holy Spirit began the plan after Christ's death\r\nC. Because God waited until man repented before preparing salvation\r\nD. Because the provision was already available when man realised he was a sinner: God planned it through foreknowledge before the foundation of the world and began as soon as man fell (the seed of the woman who would bruise the serpent's head)\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says God took the initiative, planning salvation before the foundation of the world and beginning as soon as man fell.\r\nREF: Atonement — 08:05–08:56\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 5:18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A18/>\r\n\r\nQ56. In the lesson's account of reconciliation, which roles belong to God, Christ's death and the Holy Spirit?\r\nA. God is the means, Christ's death is the applier, the Holy Spirit is the initiator\r\nB. God is the applier, Christ's death is the initiator, the Holy Spirit is the means\r\nC. God is the initiator, Christ's death is the means, the Holy Spirit is the applier\r\nD. God is the initiator, Christ's death is the applier, the Holy Spirit is the means\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says God is the initiator (2 Corinthians 5:18), Christ's death is the means, and the Holy Spirit is the applier.\r\nREF: Atonement — 08:56\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 2 Corinthians 5:18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A18/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8/>\r\n\r\nQ57. In the story of Noah, what was kapha, according to the lesson?\r\nA. The covering God told Noah to put on the ark so that rain could not seep in\r\nB. The wood from which the ark was built\r\nC. The sign of God's covenant after the flood\r\nD. The offering Noah sacrificed after leaving the ark\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: God told Noah to cover the ark with kapha so rain would not seep in. The speaker likens it to bitumen or tar.\r\nREF: Atonement — 05:04–06:02\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 6:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+6%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ58. What does the lesson conclude from the same word being used for atonement in Leviticus 16:30?\r\nA. Atonement means to exchange, so Christ replaced us in judgment.\r\nB. To atone means to cover; as the covering kept the ark from sinking, the blood of Jesus covers us so our ship of salvation will not sink.\r\nC. Atonement means to appease, so God's justice was satisfied.\r\nD. Atonement means to release, so we are free from bondage.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: To atone means to cover. The blood of Jesus covers us, and that is why the ship of salvation will not sink.\r\nREF: Atonement — 06:02–07:03\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Leviticus 16:30 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Leviticus+16%3A30/>\r\n\r\nQ59. What does the lesson say the Greek word katalasso means, and what does this suggest?\r\nA. To reconcile and also to exchange; reconciliation came by someone standing in your place and dying your death\r\nB. To cover; reconciliation came by a covering over our sin\r\nC. To release; reconciliation came by freeing us from slavery\r\nD. To appease; reconciliation came by satisfying justice\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Katalasso means to reconcile but also to exchange, so reconciliation came by somebody standing in your place and dying your death.\r\nREF: Atonement — 07:03\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Leviticus 16:30 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Leviticus+16%3A30/>\r\n\r\nQ60. According to the lesson, why was reconciliation needed?\r\nA. Because humans had not yet been taught the law\r\nB. Because there was hostility between God and mankind, with the mind that is enmity against God\r\nC. Because Israel had broken the covenant of circumcision\r\nD. Because creation itself had been destroyed\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says reconciliation was needed because there was hostility between God and mankind, which the Bible calls enmity against God.\r\nREF: Atonement — 07:03–08:05\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Leviticus 16:30 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Leviticus+16%3A30/>\r\n\r\nQ61. According to the lesson, what enables believers to cry \"Abba, Father\"?\r\nA. Their spiritual maturity\r\nB. Their faithful keeping of the commandments\r\nC. Their membership in the covenant people\r\nD. Having received the Spirit of adoption, the Holy Spirit, who bears witness with our spirit that we are children of God\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Citing Romans 8:15–16, the speaker says receiving the Holy Spirit means receiving the Spirit of adoption, the seal of adoption.\r\nREF: Atonement — 08:56\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 8:15–16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A15%E2%80%9316/> | Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 5:18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A18/>\r\n\r\nQ62. How does Ephesians 2:11 onward, as read in the lesson, describe the change Christ brought for Gentiles?\r\nA. They were once under the law, but now they are free from it.\r\nB. They were once strangers to Israel, but are now citizens of its commonwealth by circumcision.\r\nC. They were once without Christ, aliens from the commonwealth of Israel and strangers from the covenant of promise, having no hope and without God in the world, but those far off have been brought near by the blood of Christ.\r\nD. They were once under judgment, but now they are given a new law.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reads the Gentiles' former condition and then says those once far off have been brought near by the blood of Christ. That is reconciliation by blood.\r\nREF: Atonement — 09:48–10:41\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Ephesians 2:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+2%3A11/> | Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 5:18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A18/>; Romans 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8/>\r\n\r\nQ63. What does 2 Corinthians 5, as used in the lesson, say about God's work of reconciliation?\r\nA. God reconciled us to himself by Jesus Christ, and the ministry of reconciliation belongs only to church leaders.\r\nB. All things are of God, who reconciled us to himself by Jesus Christ and gave us the ministry of reconciliation.\r\nC. We reconcile ourselves to God and then receive the ministry of reconciliation.\r\nD. Christ reconciled us to himself apart from the Father and gave us the ministry of reconciliation.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reads that all things are of God, who reconciled us to himself by Jesus Christ, and says Paul describes being given the ministry of reconciliation.\r\nREF: Atonement — 03:13–04:11, 07:03\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 2 Corinthians 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5/>; Leviticus 16:30 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Leviticus+16%3A30/>\r\n\r\nQ64. How does the lesson relate atonement to reconciliation?\r\nA. Reconciliation made atonement necessary.\r\nB. Atonement and reconciliation happened apart from each other.\r\nC. Reconciliation came by the sacrifice of atonement of Jesus: his blood covers us, and by that blood we have reconciliation.\r\nD. Atonement came after reconciliation.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the blood of Jesus covers us, which is our atonement, and by that blood we have reconciliation. Reconciliation came by the sacrifice of atonement.\r\nREF: Atonement — 06:02–07:03\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 5:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A10/>\r\n\r\nQ65. According to Isaiah 32:17 as used in the lesson, what will be the effect of righteousness?\r\nA. Boldness and authority\r\nB. Spiritual gifts and power\r\nC. Holiness through personal effort\r\nD. Quietness and assurance forever (the work of righteousness being peace)\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The work of righteousness will be peace, and the effect of righteousness will be quietness and assurance forever. The Bible says what righteousness produces in the believer.\r\nREF: Assurance — 00:16–01:14\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Isaiah 32:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+32%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ66. How does the lesson contrast God with people who give you something and then make you doubt the agreement?\r\nA. God does not pander to man's antics and politics; he does not keep you petrified and doubting, since the product of righteousness is peace and quietness forever.\r\nB. God sometimes withholds assurance to keep believers grateful.\r\nC. God, like people, expects regular proof before confirming what he gave.\r\nD. God gives assurance only after a believer has shown a consistent life.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says man sometimes rubs a gift in to make you unsure, but God does not need to. The Bible is definite that righteousness produces peace and quietness forever.\r\nREF: Assurance — 01:14\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Isaiah 32:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+32%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ67. According to the lesson, where do a believer's occasional doubts about assurance come from?\r\nA. From God's deliberate withholding of certainty\r\nB. From sin the believer is secretly committing\r\nC. From lack of discipline in devotion\r\nD. From ignorance, since we grow up in a culture in which everything is earned\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Even the most sanctified might have doubts, but those doubts stem from ignorance. We are conditioned to think everything is earned.\r\nREF: Assurance — 02:15\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 2:8-9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+2%3A8-9/>\r\n\r\nQ68. A believer has a period of inconsistent devotion and wonders whether God still loves him and whether he still has assurance. According to the lesson, why does he reason this way, and what does God say instead?\r\nA. He reasons from human relationships, where a boss would respond with a threat, but God says the effect of righteousness is quietness and peace forever.\r\nB. He reasons from Scripture, which says assurance is lost during inconsistency, but God restores it later.\r\nC. He reasons from the Holy Spirit's conviction, which warns of lost salvation.\r\nD. He reasons from the law, which says assurance requires perfect obedience.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: We think of human relationships, where a boss would follow absence with a threat. God says the effect of righteousness is quietness and peace forever, and fruits come as a result of faith.\r\nREF: Assurance — 02:15–03:32\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Isaiah 32:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+32%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ69. Why does the speaker single out Philippians 1:6 as a favourite text for assurance?\r\nA. It shows that only mature believers can be sure of salvation.\r\nB. It shows assurance about others, not just personal assurance: a pastor confident that he who began a good work in them will complete it.\r\nC. It shows that God finishes his work only in those who keep the law.\r\nD. It shows that assurance is a private matter that cannot be shared with others.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says having assurance about others is a new level beyond personal assurance. Paul shows confidence that God will complete the good work, which means he had assurance of their salvation.\r\nREF: Assurance — 03:32\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Philippians 1:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+1%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ70. The lesson says faith is inward. What does it say helped Paul know that the believers he addressed were saved?\r\nA. Their length of service and church titles\r\nB. Their emotional experiences at conversion\r\nC. The profession of their faith with corresponding action, which was sufficient for him\r\nD. A private revelation about each believer\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Faith is known inwardly, so others know it by the profession of faith and corresponding action (\"by the fruits\"). That was sufficient for Paul.\r\nREF: Assurance — 04:29\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 7:16–20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+7%3A16%E2%80%9320/>\r\n\r\nQ71. How does the lesson contrast Paul's confidence in the believers with the approach of some pastors?\r\nA. Paul warned believers they might end up in hell, as some pastors rightly do.\r\nB. Both Paul and these pastors express confidence that God will complete the work.\r\nC. Some pastors think scaring people with the possibility of not making it, regardless of title or years of service, is teaching on holiness, while Paul's confidence that God will complete the good work stirs the Holy Spirit's efficacy to drop inconsistencies.\r\nD. Paul avoided addressing inconsistencies so as not to unsettle believers.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says some think warning people they may end up in hell is good preaching. Paul's confident approach, like \"know ye not that your body is the temple,\" stirs the Spirit's efficacy to drop inconsistencies.\r\nREF: Assurance — 05:24–06:14\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Philippians 1:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+1%3A6/>; 1 Corinthians 6:19–20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+6%3A19%E2%80%9320/>\r\n\r\nQ72. According to the lesson, why do many people struggle with assurance, and what changes it?\r\nA. They trust God too little, and basing assurance on feelings changes it.\r\nB. They do not trust themselves, and rightly so, because trusting themselves would put them in trouble; basing assurance on God changes things.\r\nC. They trust church leaders too little, and basing assurance on the church changes it.\r\nD. They have not read the Old Testament, and reading it changes it.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says we struggle because we do not trust ourselves, rightly so. When conviction is based on God, it changes the ball game.\r\nREF: Assurance — 06:14\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 8:31–39 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A31%E2%80%9339/>\r\n\r\nQ73. What does the candle-in-a-windy-place illustration emphasise?\r\nA. The Holy Spirit preserves, guides and guards the work he started in the believer.\r\nB. Believers must protect their own faith from outside influence by discipline.\r\nC. Church leaders must shield young believers from false teaching.\r\nD. Faith naturally becomes weaker without effort.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the Holy Spirit will preserve, guide and guard what he started, as you would protect a flame in a windy place.\r\nREF: Assurance — 07:19\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Jude 24–25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jude+24%E2%80%9325/>\r\n\r\nQ74. In the lesson, to whom is Jude 24 addressed in context, and what comes before it?\r\nA. To false teachers who had crept in, after Jude described true believers\r\nB. To the Jewish nation, after Jude described Israel's failures\r\nC. To church leaders only, after Jude described their duties\r\nD. To believers, after Jude described people who crept in unawares and pretended to be part of the believing flock but were not\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Jude first talks about those who crept in pretending to be part of the flock. Then he addresses the believers, saying God is able to keep them from stumbling and present them faultless.\r\nREF: Assurance — 08:07\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Jude 24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jude+24/>\r\n\r\nQ75. Why does the speaker say Jude 24 transformed him as a young believer?\r\nA. It told him that if keeping depended on his own power he would be afraid, but God is able to keep him from stumbling and present him faultless with exceeding joy.\r\nB. It told him that his own effort would eventually keep him from stumbling.\r\nC. It told him that he would never need to outgrow his weaknesses.\r\nD. It told him that ignorance would end his ministry unless corrected quickly.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says if it were his power he would be afraid, but God is able to keep him. He also says ignorance will not end his life or ministry.\r\nREF: Assurance — 08:07–09:14\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Jude 24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jude+24/>\r\n\r\nQ76. What common assumption does the lesson challenge about people who never heard the gospel?\r\nA. That they will all be condemned immediately\r\nB. That they have no access to any revelation\r\nC. That they lived only before Christ\r\nD. That because no one preached to them, they are justified or did not know any better\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says people usually assume that because no one preached to them they are justified or knew no better. He calls this lopsided and says the Bible differs.\r\nREF: Those Who Never Heard the Gospel — 00:00\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 2:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+2%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ77. According to Romans 2:14 in the lesson, what do Gentiles without God's written law show?\r\nA. They have no moral awareness at all.\r\nB. They are saved by their conscience.\r\nC. They show they know his law when they instinctively obey it.\r\nD. They know God's name and nature fully.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Romans 2:14 says Gentiles without the written law show they know it when they instinctively obey it. The speaker calls this an instinctive moral law.\r\nREF: Those Who Never Heard the Gospel — 01:09\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 2:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+2%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ78. What does the \"I helped you when it was your turn\" illustration show?\r\nA. Reciprocity is the highest form of morality.\r\nB. Even unbelievers who follow no holy book appeal to a universal, instinctive law, believing others will understand it.\r\nC. People who refuse to help others will be judged by the Law.\r\nD. Helping others earns salvation.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Even unbelievers who follow no holy book appeal to a universal agreed law, because they believe the other person will understand it.\r\nREF: Those Who Never Heard the Gospel — 01:09–02:12\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 2:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+2%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ79. Why does the lesson mention Cain?\r\nA. To show that sacrifice began with the first generation\r\nB. To show people instinctively know wrongdoing: he was the first killer with no precedent, yet he knew enough to run away and try to lie\r\nC. To show God's covenant with the first family\r\nD. To show that murder was first condemned in the written law\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Cain had no precedent for murder yet knew he had done wrong, running away and trying to lie.\r\nREF: Those Who Never Heard the Gospel — 02:12–03:07\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 4:8–16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+4%3A8%E2%80%9316/>\r\n\r\nQ80. How does Romans 2:12, as read in the lesson, say Jews and Gentiles are judged?\r\nA. Both are judged only by the written law.\r\nB. Only Jews are judged, since Gentiles had no law.\r\nC. Jews who have the law will be judged by that law, and Gentiles without it are judged because they know instinctively what God expects.\r\nD. Gentiles are judged by the Jews' law, and Jews by instinct.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The Jews will be judged by the law they failed to obey. The Gentiles, who lack the law, will be judged because they know instinctively what God expects.\r\nREF: Those Who Never Heard the Gospel — 03:07–03:53\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 2:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+2%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ81. What conclusion does the lesson draw from Paul's argument for those who lived before Jesus, with or without the law?\r\nA. It all ended the same way: everyone needed salvation by grace through faith, because all have sinned.\r\nB. Those with the law were saved by it, while those without it were saved by instinct.\r\nC. Those without the law were excused from judgment.\r\nD. Those with the law needed grace, but those without did not.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Paul's point is that all ended in the same place, needing salvation by grace through faith. All have sinned and come short of the glory of God.\r\nREF: Those Who Never Heard the Gospel — 03:53\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 2:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+2%3A12/> | Added from online research: Romans 3:23–24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A23%E2%80%9324/>\r\n\r\nQ82. How does the lesson answer the argument that some who never heard the gospel are good people?\r\nA. Good people who never heard are saved by their goodness.\r\nB. God must give every good person a second chance after death.\r\nC. Good works are enough for those who lacked the written law.\r\nD. If God judged by works he would be just, since all have sinned; letting people live, hear the gospel and sending a Messiah to die their death is grace.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says judging every man by works would be just because all have sinned. If God had killed Adam when he sinned, he would have been just, and giving people time and a Messiah is grace.\r\nREF: Those Who Never Heard the Gospel — 03:53–04:42\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 2:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+2%3A12/> | Added from online research: Romans 3:23–24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A23%E2%80%9324/>\r\n\r\nQ83. What does Romans 1:20 teach, according to the lesson, about general and specific revelation?\r\nA. Specific revelation in the Word is the only way anyone can know God exists.\r\nB. General revelation reaches only believers who have already read Scripture.\r\nC. General revelation is enough to save without the gospel.\r\nD. Creation gives general revelation, accessible to anyone open and objective, that there is a God, so no one is without excuse; those who deny it suppress it.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker distinguishes specific revelation in God's word from general revelation in creation. Everyone is without excuse, and those who do not believe in God suppressed it.\r\nREF: Those Who Never Heard the Gospel — 05:42\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 1:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+1%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ84. What does the speaker conclude from the story of Cornelius about God and people who are openly seeking him?\r\nA. God reaches out to those who openly seek him, and the speaker trusts that no genuine seeker has failed to find him.\r\nB. God reaches only those who already know Christ's name.\r\nC. Seeking God earns salvation apart from the gospel.\r\nD. God ignores sincere seekers until they join Israel.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says God reaches out to people openly seeking him, in both testaments. He trusts God's providence that no genuine seeker has failed to find him.\r\nREF: Those Who Never Heard the Gospel — 06:36\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 10:1–8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10%3A1%E2%80%938/>; Acts 10:30–33 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10%3A30%E2%80%9333/>\r\n\r\nQ85. How does the lesson use the Athenians' altar to the unknown God in Acts 17?\r\nA. To show that worshipping unknown gods is acceptable\r\nB. To show that people with an inner awareness of God who worship as they know how are met by God, who causes their paths to cross with Paul, who declares the one they ignorantly worship\r\nC. To show that the Greeks were condemned for idolatry\r\nD. To show that Paul avoided preaching in Athens\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The Athenians had an inner awareness of God and labelled him unknown. The speaker says God will cause their paths to cross with Paul, who declares the one they ignorantly worship.\r\nREF: Those Who Never Heard the Gospel — 06:36–07:33\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 1:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+1%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ86. What view does the lesson attribute to Frank Turek about those who never heard the gospel?\r\nA. That all who never heard will be saved\r\nB. That all who never heard will be condemned immediately\r\nC. That God's providence placed people in the era and place of their birth so that, had they been in another era, they would not have believed anyway, vindicating his justice\r\nD. That Acts 17:26 teaches nations are saved at appointed times\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker presents this as a view held by sound people like Turek. It is supported by Acts 17:26, which says God determined pre-appointed times and boundaries.\r\nREF: Those Who Never Heard the Gospel — 07:33–08:30\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 17:26 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17%3A26/>\r\n\r\nQ87. How does the speaker respond to Turek's conclusion?\r\nA. He fully endorses it as the best reading of Acts 17:26.\r\nB. He rejects the idea that God's providence influences when and where people are born.\r\nC. He says it is a convenient position you would have to already be a believer to hold, so he does not subscribe to it.\r\nD. He says the question of those who never heard cannot be addressed from Scripture.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: After citing Turek's survey (97% said the unbelievers they know are not actively seeking truth), the speaker says he does not subscribe to the conclusion. He calls it a convenient position.\r\nREF: Those Who Never Heard the Gospel — 09:34–10:35\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17/>\r\n\r\nQ88. What does the lesson mean by saying Christ's sacrifice has a \"retroactive action\"?\r\nA. It undid sins that Old Testament people committed before the cross.\r\nB. It applied only to those who lived after the cross.\r\nC. It was effective only for Israel.\r\nD. Christ's sacrifice was efficacious for those who believed before and after it: Old Testament believers trusted in what Christ would do, and those today are saved by believing in what he has done.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the sacrifice was efficacious for those who believed before and after it. Old Testament believers believed in what Christ would do, and we believe in what he has done.\r\nREF: Those Who Never Heard the Gospel — 10:35\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 9:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+9%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ89. Which examples does the lesson use to ask how much information Old Testament believers had about the Messiah?\r\nA. Abraham, who believed the gospel according to Galatians 3, and Rahab, a hero of faith in Hebrews 11\r\nB. Cain and Abel\r\nC. Cornelius and the woman at the well\r\nD. Noah and Moses\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker asks how much information Abraham had when Galatians 3 says he believed the gospel. He then asks the same of Rahab, who appears in Hebrews 11 as a hero of faith.\r\nREF: Those Who Never Heard the Gospel — 11:39\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Galatians 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+3/>; Hebrews 11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+11/>\r\n\r\nQ90. What does the lesson say Rahab acknowledged and did?\r\nA. She knew the Messiah's name and that he would die and rise.\r\nB. She acknowledged that the God of Israel is God in heaven above and earth below, and she acted on her faith by lodging the spies and sending them off safely, which James 2:25 counts as righteous.\r\nC. She converted to Israel's law before helping the spies.\r\nD. She helped the spies out of fear alone, with no knowledge of God.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: In Joshua 2:9–11 she acknowledged God in heaven above and earth below. She may not have known his name or nature, but she acted on her faith, which James 2:25 calls righteous.\r\nREF: Those Who Never Heard the Gospel — 12:40–13:39\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Joshua 2:9–11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Joshua+2%3A9%E2%80%9311/>; James 2:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+2%3A25/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Galatians 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+3/>; Hebrews 11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+11/>; John 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ91. What is the second view the lesson presents, and which text anchors it?\r\nA. God judges people according to the extent of information they had, primarily for rejection of the gospel, anchored by John 3:18–19, where condemnation comes because light came and men loved darkness.\r\nB. God judges by the era in which people were born, anchored by Acts 17:26.\r\nC. God judges everyone by works, anchored by Romans 2:12.\r\nD. God saves everyone who never heard, anchored by Romans 1:20.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The second view is that God judges by the extent of information received, primarily for rejecting the gospel. John 3:18–19 is read alongside Athens (\"whom you ignorantly worship\") and the days of ignorance God overlooked.\r\nREF: Those Who Never Heard the Gospel — 13:39–15:36\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 3:18–19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A18%E2%80%9319/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Joshua 2:9–11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Joshua+2%3A9%E2%80%9311/>; James 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+2/>; John 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ92. What does the lesson conclude from the way God responded to Cornelius's prayers before he was saved?\r\nA. Prayer to an unknown God secures salvation without the gospel.\r\nB. God responds to the degree of information a person has: an angel told Cornelius his alms had gone up as a memorial though he did not yet know Jesus' name, while the story still shows God places a premium on proper information.\r\nC. Cornelius was saved before Peter came.\r\nD. Cornelius's example shows God sends evangelists to Gentiles in the Old Testament.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Cornelius had not yet placed faith in Jesus' name, yet his devotional attempts were received. The speaker adds there is no Old Testament example of God sending someone to evangelise a Gentile.\r\nREF: Those Who Never Heard the Gospel — 16:38–17:33\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 10:1–48 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10%3A1%E2%80%9348/>\r\n\r\nQ93. According to the second view, where do children who die before the age of accountability and people who never had the consciousness to hear and believe go, and what is the speaker's own position?\r\nA. Both go to hell, and the speaker holds the first view.\r\nB. Both go to heaven, and the speaker rejects the second view.\r\nC. Both are judged by Adam's guilt, and the speaker is undecided.\r\nD. Both go to heaven, and the speaker tilts toward the second opinion as his conviction while allowing others to believe as persuaded.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: By the second view such children and people go to heaven; the speaker says that by the first opinion they would go to hell. He tilts toward the second.\r\nREF: Those Who Never Heard the Gospel — 17:33–18:40\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 17:30 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17%3A30/>; John 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16/>; John 3:18–19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A18%E2%80%9319/>\r\n\r\nQ94. According to the lesson's reading of Romans 3:25, what did God set Christ forth as a propitiation to demonstrate?\r\nA. That previously committed sins did not require Christ's sacrifice.\r\nB. That all people were declared righteous apart from faith.\r\nC. That God's forbearance meant sin had no consequences.\r\nD. God's righteousness despite his forbearance toward past sins.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reads Romans 3:25 as saying Christ was set forth as a propitiation by his blood through faith to demonstrate God's righteousness, because God had passed over previously committed sins in his forbearance.\r\nREF: Propitiation — 03:11\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 3:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A25/>\n\nDECK: Discipleship Experience (Section 3) Predestination & Trinity\r\n\r\nQ1. Why does Apostle point to \"Spirit of Christ,\" \"Spirit of God,\" and \"Spirit of His Son\"?\r\nA. To show that the Spirit carries different identities in different ages.\r\nB. To demonstrate that Scripture speaks of the same Spirit in relation to the Father and the Son.\r\nC. To distinguish the ministry of Jesus from that of the Spirit.\r\nD. To prove Paul preferred figurative language.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Scripture refers to the Spirit as the Spirit of Christ, the Spirit of God, and the Spirit of the Son.\r\nREF: TRINITY II — [09:36]–[10:43] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 8:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A9/>; Philippians 1:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+1%3A19/>; Galatians 4:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+4%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ2. If a believer says, \"I lost my job because God specifically willed it.\" What will be the most consistent response according to the lesson?\r\nA. Every painful event is individually ordained by God.\r\nB. God may have known it would happen, but not every painful event is His predetermined will.\r\nC. Painful events only happen because believers lack faith.\r\nD. Since God is sovereign, human choices have no real influence on life's events.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: God’s foreknowledge of a painful event does not mean He predetermined it.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION I — [02:47]–[03:53] and [05:45]–[06:52] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Samuel 23:9–14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Samuel+23%3A9%E2%80%9314/>\r\n\r\nQ3. Why is the word Trinity compared with words like rapture and omniscient?\r\nA. To show that theology often creates words to describe biblical truths.\r\nB. To show that every theological term came from church councils.\r\nC. To prove that every theological word appears in Scripture.\r\nD. To argue that Christians should avoid theological language.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says theological terms can describe concepts taught in Scripture even when the exact words are absent.\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [11:30]–[12:30] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1/>\r\n\r\nQ4. What is the primary danger of reading Romans 8:28–30 without continuing into Romans 9–11?\r\nA. One may misunderstand Paul's teaching on spiritual gifts.\r\nB. One may confuse justification with glorification.\r\nC. One may overlook the broader discussion concerning Jews and Gentiles.\r\nD. One may ignore the historical setting of the Roman church.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says Romans 8 should be read in the wider argument through chapters 9–11, including its discussion of Jews and Gentiles.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [12:03]–[13:39] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 8:28–30 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A28%E2%80%9330/>; Romans 9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+9/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 11:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+11%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ5. What was the role of the Council of Nicaea regarding the doctrine of the Trinity?\r\nA. It created a doctrine previously unknown.\r\nB. It introduced new Scriptures into the canon.\r\nC. It pointed to biblical evidence that already existed.\r\nD. It replaced Old Testament teaching with New Testament theology.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says the Council of Nicaea pointed to scriptural evidence that already existed rather than inventing the doctrine.\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [17:01]–[18:00] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Revelation 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+1%3A8/>; Acts 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+5/>; Isaiah 48 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+48/>\r\n\r\nQ6. Why is the illustration of a dog on a leash used?\r\nA. Human beings possess unrestricted freedom.\r\nB. God frequently overrides every human decision.\r\nC. God's sovereignty removes human responsibility.\r\nD. Human freedom operates within limits of God's ultimate purposes.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The leash illustration describes human freedom operating within the limits of God’s ultimate purposes.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION I — [04:50]–[05:45] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Samuel 23:9–13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Samuel+23%3A9%E2%80%9313/>\r\n\r\nQ7. What observation from Genesis 18 does Apostle emphasize before discussing Abraham's intercession?\r\nA. Abraham immediately recognized all three visitors as angels.\r\nB. One of the three visitors is identified in the narrative as the Lord.\r\nC. Abraham misunderstood the identity of the visitors.\r\nD. The visitors never spoke directly to Abraham.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson identifies one of the three visitors in Genesis 18 as the Lord.\r\nREF: TRINITY II — [05:50]–[06:46] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+18/>\r\n\r\nQ8. Which statement best distinguishes foreknowledge from predestination?\r\nA. Everything God foreknows must occur because He predetermined it.\r\nB. Foreknowledge and predestination describe the same aspect of God's sovereignty.\r\nC. God may know an event will occur without ordaining or causing it to happen.\r\nD. Predestination exists because human beings do not possess genuine free will.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson distinguishes God’s foreknowledge of an event from His predetermining it.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION I — [02:47]–[03:53] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Samuel 23:9–14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Samuel+23%3A9%E2%80%9314/>\r\n\r\nQ9. Why does the lesson focus on the Hebrew word echad in Deuteronomy 6:4?\r\nA. It always refers to a single individual.\r\nB. It is only used when speaking about God.\r\nC. It means exactly the same as yahid.\r\nD. It can describe a unified plurality rather than absolute singularity.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson uses Genesis 2:24 to show that echad can describe a compound unity.\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [06:10]–[07:11] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Deuteronomy 6:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+6%3A4/>; Genesis 2:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+2%3A24/>\r\n\r\nQ10. What gives believers confidence even when others exercise free will against them?\r\nA. God prevents every harmful decision before it happens.\r\nB. Human beings will eventually choose what is right.\r\nC. God's ultimate purpose cannot be frustrated by the free choices of people.\r\nD. Every difficult circumstance was specifically designed by God.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Human choices cannot frustrate God’s ultimate purpose.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION I — [04:50]–[05:45] and [06:52]–[07:36] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Job 42:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Job+42%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ11. Which conclusion best reflects the relationship between Romans 8:28 and free will?\r\nA. God works through circumstances He did not necessarily ordain to accomplish His ultimate purpose for those who love Him.\r\nB. Since all things work together for good, every event must have been individually predetermined by God.\r\nC. Human free will only exists until God decides otherwise.\r\nD. God's sovereignty eliminates the significance of human choices.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says God can work all things for good for those who love Him, including circumstances He did not necessarily will.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION I — [05:45]–[07:36] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 8:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ12. In Ephesians 1:11–13, who are \"we who first trusted in Christ\"?\r\nA. All believers everywhere.\r\nB. The apostles alone.\r\nC. The Jews.\r\nD. The Gentile believers.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson identifies ‘we who first trusted’ as the Jews in that context.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [03:53]–[05:10] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Ephesians 1:11–13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A11%E2%80%9313/>\r\n\r\nQ13. Which part of the Trinity's definition must not be omitted?\r\nA. That the three persons perform different ministries.\r\nB. That the three persons are co-equal and co-eternal.\r\nC. That the Son appears more frequently in Scripture.\r\nD. That the Father alone possesses supreme authority.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson defines the Trinity as three co-equal and co-eternal persons within one being that is God.\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [04:07]–[05:05] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 28:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+28%3A19/>; 2 Corinthians 13:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+13%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ14. What attitude should accompany discovering difficult truths about God's nature?\r\nA. Suspend belief until every question is answered.\r\nB. Accept only what appears logically consistent.\r\nC. Receive what Scripture teaches with humility.\r\nD. Avoid doctrines that seem mysterious.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says biblical truth should be embraced with humility even when it is difficult to understand.\r\nREF: TRINITY II — [14:03]–end of transcript (approximate boundary)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Deuteronomy 29:29 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+29%3A29/>\r\n\r\nQ15. Why was God able to declare that the people of Keilah would surrender David?\r\nA. His perfect knowledge of human nature made Him know how they would respond.\r\nB. Their betrayal had already become unavoidable.\r\nC. God intended to force them into betraying David.\r\nD. David's departure confirmed God's decree.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says God knew the people of Keilah would give David up because He knew they would cave under pressure.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION I — [03:53]–[04:50] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Samuel 23:9–13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Samuel+23%3A9%E2%80%9313/>\r\n\r\nQ16. What conclusion can we draw from Acts 26:5 regarding the word \"foreknew\"?\r\nA. It always refers to God's eternal knowledge of future events.\r\nB. It is a technical theological term used only by Paul.\r\nC. Its meaning depends on context and is not inherently a reference to divine omniscience.\r\nD. It always describes God's covenant relationship with Israel.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Acts 26:5 uses foreknew of people knowing Paul’s former life, so the term must be interpreted in context.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [15:16]–[16:34] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 26:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+26%3A5/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 11:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+11%3A2/>; 2 Peter 3:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Peter+3%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ17. Which two truths must both be upheld for a correct understanding of the Trinity?\r\nA. Unity and authority.\r\nB. Distinction and equality.\r\nC. Glory and power.\r\nD. Love and holiness.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents distinction and equality as the two pillars of the Trinity doctrine.\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [04:07]–[06:10] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1/> | Added from online research: Matthew 28:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+28%3A19/>\r\n\r\nQ18. What principle is reflected by the movement between Genesis, Isaiah, Psalms, Daniel, John, Hebrews, Acts, and Revelation?\r\nA. Difficult doctrines should rest on the cumulative witness of Scripture.\r\nB. Every doctrine should begin in the New Testament.\r\nC. Old Testament passages are only illustrations.\r\nD. Individual verses should be interpreted independently.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lessons support the doctrine using passages from across Scripture rather than relying on a single text.\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [05:05]–[18:00] and TRINITY II — [05:50]–[12:50] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Deuteronomy 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+6/>; Hebrews 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1/>; Daniel 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Daniel+7/>; Isaiah 9:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+9%3A6/>; Revelation 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+1%3A8/>; Acts 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+5/>; Isaiah 48 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+48/>; Genesis 18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+18/>; Isaiah 63:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+63%3A10/>; Romans 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8/>; Philippians 1:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+1%3A19/>; Galatians 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+4/>\r\n\r\nQ19. What interpretive mistake leads many readers to misunderstand predestination in Ephesians 1?\r\nA. Reading poetry as literal doctrine.\r\nB. Ignoring the original language.\r\nC. Confusing salvation with inheritance.\r\nD. Reading a corporate discussion as though it were about individuals.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson warns against treating a corporate discussion of Jews and Gentiles as though it were about individuals.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [12:03]–[14:09] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Ephesians 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 8:28–30 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A28%E2%80%9330/>; Romans 9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+9/>; Romans 11:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+11%3A2/>; Acts 26:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+26%3A5/>\r\n\r\nQ20. \"Whatever you are looking for, you will find...There is a scripture for everything you believe.\" What principle is being warned against?\r\nA. Depending too much on Bible translations.\r\nB. Ignoring the original biblical languages.\r\nC. Looking for verses that support ideas you already hold.\r\nD. Relying too heavily on cross-references.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Finding a verse that supports a belief does not by itself make that belief true.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [19:13]–[19:59] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 11:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+11%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ21. Someone says the Trinity can't be biblical since the word isn't in Scripture. What is your best response?\r\nA. Every biblical doctrine must appear by name in Scripture.\r\nB. A doctrine is biblical if Scripture teaches the concept, even if the term is not used.\r\nC. The Trinity became biblical after church councils accepted it.\r\nD. The Trinity is mainly based on Christian tradition.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says theological terms may name concepts taught in Scripture even when the exact term is absent.\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [11:30]–[12:30] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 28:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+28%3A19/>; 2 Corinthians 13:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+13%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ22. Why does Apostle repeatedly appeal to Old Testament passages when discussing the Trinity?\r\nA. To show that later Christian writers misunderstood them.\r\nB. To demonstrate that the doctrine was already present before the New Testament.\r\nC. To establish that the New Testament should be interpreted through Judaism alone.\r\nD. To prove that every Old Testament appearance of God refers only to Christ.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Old Testament passages are used to show that evidence for the Trinity predates the New Testament.\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [13:28]–[17:01] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Daniel 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Daniel+7/>; Isaiah 9:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+9%3A6/>; Isaiah 48:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+48%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ23. Which observation from Ephesians 1 forms the foundation of the interpretation of predestination?\r\nA. The inheritance belongs only to the apostles.\r\nB. God's will excludes every human response.\r\nC. Paul's repeated change in audience throughout the passage must be accounted for.\r\nD. Every occurrence of \"predestined\" always refers to salvation.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson distinguishes ‘we who first trusted’ from the ‘you also’ addressed in Ephesians 1.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [03:53]–[05:10] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Ephesians 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1/>\r\n\r\nQ24. According to the lesson, what should be the primary test of any doctrine about God?\r\nA. Whether it agrees with human reasoning.\r\nB. Whether respected scholars accept it.\r\nC. Whether it can be illustrated from nature.\r\nD. Whether it is taught by Scripture.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says a doctrine should be tested by whether the Bible teaches it, not by whether it fits human logic.\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [02:13]–[03:10] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Exodus 15:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+15%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ25. Why does Apostle repeatedly distinguish between \"we\" and \"you\" in Ephesians 1?\r\nA. To distinguish the apostles from ordinary believers.\r\nB. To show that Paul's argument distinguishes Jewish believers from Gentile believers.\r\nC. To identify mature Christians and immature Christians.\r\nD. To explain the difference between believers before and after Pentecost.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson identifies ‘we’ as Jewish believers and ‘you also’ as Gentile believers in the passage.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [03:53]–[05:10] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Ephesians 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1/>\r\n\r\nQ26. Why is it important for Christians to study doctrines such as the Trinity?\r\nA. To become more effective in theological debates.\r\nB. To answer sincere questions about their faith with biblical understanding.\r\nC. To master historical church controversies.\r\nD. To avoid reading non-Christian literature.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Believers should be ready to explain and defend their faith when asked.\r\nREF: TRINITY III — [01:00]–[01:42] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Peter 3:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+3%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ27. Why is the parable in Matthew 20 and Matthew 22 used when discussing \"called\"?\r\nA. To prove that every use of the word has exactly the same meaning.\r\nB. To establish that \"called\" always refers to believers.\r\nC. To demonstrate that the Greek word has only one possible definition.\r\nD. To let Jesus' own use of the term shape how Paul's language is understood.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson compares Jesus’ use of ‘called’ in Matthew 20 and 22 with Paul’s wording in Romans 8.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [06:53]–[12:03] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 8:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A28/>; Matthew 20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+20/>; Matthew 22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+22/>\r\n\r\nQ28. How does Genesis 2:24 support the explanation of echad?\r\nA. It uses the same word to describe two persons united as one.\r\nB. It teaches that marriage represents the Trinity.\r\nC. It shows that Adam and Eve became one person.\r\nD. It replaces the meaning of Deuteronomy 6:4.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Genesis 2:24 is cited as an example of echad describing two persons united as one.\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [06:10]–[07:11] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 2:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+2%3A24/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Deuteronomy 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+6/>\r\n\r\nQ29. Apostle challenges a student's interpretation of Romans 8:29 by opening other passages, what principle is he modeling?\r\nA. Difficult passages should only be interpreted using the original Greek.\r\nB. Every doctrine should be established from multiple Bible translations.\r\nC. Individual verses should be interpreted within the flow of the Bible's larger argument.\r\nD. New Testament doctrines should always be explained from the Old Testament first.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson models interpreting a verse in context and within the Bible’s wider argument.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [01:04]–[02:07] and [06:53]–[13:39] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 8:29 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A29/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Matthew 20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+20/>; Matthew 22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+22/>; Romans 9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+9/>; Romans 11:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+11%3A2/>; Acts 26:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+26%3A5/>\r\n\r\nQ30. Which statement best summarizes the overall philosophy of biblical interpretation in this lesson?\r\nA. Strong theological systems should determine how individual passages are read.\r\nB. Greek word studies are sufficient to settle difficult doctrines.\r\nC. Logical deductions should take priority whenever Scripture appears unclear.\r\nD. Scripture must interpret Scripture, with context governing the meaning of theological terms.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson repeatedly says to interpret passages and theological terms in their biblical context.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [01:04]–[02:07], [12:03]–[14:09], and [14:09]–[16:34] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 1:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A11/>; Romans 8:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A28/>; Romans 9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+9/>; Romans 11:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+11%3A2/>; Acts 26:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+26%3A5/>; 2 Peter 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Peter+3/>\r\n\r\nQ31. When a doctrine such as God's omniscience is used to reason about free will, what interpretive safeguard does the speaker emphasize?\r\nA. Treat logic as the final authority even when it conflicts with the biblical narrative.\r\nB. Read the doctrine and its implications in the context of the Bible's narrative.\r\nC. Assume the apostles evangelized as if some people were predestined to be saved and others were not.\r\nD. Interpret every occurrence of a theological term as having the same meaning.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says logic can help, but established doctrines and their scope must be interpreted in context and in line with the Bible's narrative.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [01:04]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Timothy 2:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+2%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ32. In the speaker's reading of Ephesians 1:11–13, whom does “we who first trusted in Christ” refer to?\r\nA. All believers everywhere, with no distinction in the passage.\r\nB. The apostles alone, contrasted with every other Christian.\r\nC. The Jews, distinguished from the Gentile believers addressed as “you also.”\r\nD. Gentile believers, while “you also” refers to Jewish believers.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker identifies “we who first trusted” as the Jews and “you also” as the Ephesians/Gentiles, emphasizing the change in audience.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [03:53]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Ephesians 1:11–13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A11%E2%80%9313/>\r\n\r\nQ33. What interpretive mistake does the speaker identify in readings of predestination in Ephesians 1?\r\nA. Taking the passage as a discussion of Jews and Gentiles.\r\nB. Noticing Paul's distinction between “we” and “you.”\r\nC. Reading the passage alongside Romans 9–11.\r\nD. Turning a corporate discussion about people groups into an individual one.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the problem is making what was corporate personal, and uses the “we”/“you” distinction to support a Jewish/Gentile reading.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [05:10]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Ephesians 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1/>\r\n\r\nQ34. In Matthew 22, how does the speaker describe the movement from the original wedding guests to the people gathered from the highways?\r\nA. The original guests accept the invitation, and the highway guests are turned away.\r\nB. The king invites only the original guests and cancels the wedding when they refuse.\r\nC. The original guests refuse the invitation and mistreat the servants; the servants then gather others from the highways, illustrating the speaker's Jews-and-Gentiles reading.\r\nD. The servants gather only the people who had already worked in the vineyard.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker identifies the original guests with the Jews who refused the invitation and mistreated the servants, then notes that the servants gather people from the highways; he connects this with the broader Jews-and-Gentiles context.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [10:33; 11:08; 11:49]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Matthew 22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+22/>\r\n\r\nQ35. In the speaker's explanation of the Matthew 20 parable, what distinguishes those who are chosen among the called?\r\nA. Being hired at the first hour rather than the eleventh.\r\nB. Faith in Christ Jesus.\r\nC. Having borne the burden and heat of the day.\r\nD. Belonging to the group that originally received the invitation.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that even among those called, few are chosen, and identifies faith in Christ Jesus as the dividing line.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [09:31]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Matthew 20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+20/>\r\n\r\nQ36. What broader context does the speaker say must be followed to understand Romans 8:28–30?\r\nA. The flow from Romans 8 through Romans 11, including the discussion of Jews and Gentiles.\r\nB. Romans 8:28–30 in isolation from the surrounding chapters.\r\nC. Only the parable in Matthew 20, without Romans 9–11.\r\nD. Only Paul's personal testimony in Acts 26.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Romans 8:28–30 cannot be understood by itself and points to the larger narrative through chapters 8–11, including Romans 9–10.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [12:03]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 8:28–30 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A28%E2%80%9330/>; Romans 11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+11/>; Romans 9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+9/>\r\n\r\nQ37. How does the speaker combine Acts 26:5 and 2 Peter 3:17 with his contextual reading of “whom he foreknew” in Romans 11:2?\r\nA. Acts and 2 Peter show that foreknowledge language can describe people who knew something beforehand; in Romans 11:2, he reads “his people” in the context of Israel.\r\nB. All three passages use “foreknew” only for God's knowledge of each individual's future salvation.\r\nC. Acts and 2 Peter show that human foreknowledge is impossible, while Romans 11:2 refers to Gentiles.\r\nD. The passages establish that foreknowledge always means that God causes the event he knows.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker points to human uses in Acts 26:5 and 2 Peter 3:17, then asks who “his people” are in Romans 11:2 and reads the passage in relation to Israel and the Gentiles grafted in.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [15:16; 16:34; 17:44]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 2 Peter 3:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Peter+3%3A17/>; Romans 11:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+11%3A2/>; Acts 26:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+26%3A5/>\r\n\r\nQ38. What caution does the speaker draw from the remark that a person can find a Scripture for anything they already believe?\r\nA. Every belief can be settled by locating one verse that sounds relevant.\r\nB. A verse should be read without regard to the wider context.\r\nC. The original-language word search makes further interpretation unnecessary.\r\nD. Finding a supporting verse does not by itself establish that the belief is true.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker warns that searching for support for a prior belief can produce a verse, but that does not make the belief true.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [19:13]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 11:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+11%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ39. What sequence in David's inquiries about Keilah illustrates why he should inquire again rather than assume an earlier instruction still applies?\r\nA. David was first told to go against the Philistines and prevailed; later he was warned Saul was coming and Keilah would hand him over, so he left.\r\nB. David was first told to stay in Keilah, then ordered to attack Saul with the people of Keilah.\r\nC. David was told Keilah would protect him, but left before asking the Lord about Saul.\r\nD. David received the same instruction both times and remained in Keilah until Saul arrived.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker recounts David's first inquiry and victory against the Philistines, then a later inquiry about Saul and the warning that Keilah would surrender him; he notes that instructions can change and David inquired again.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION I — [00:17; 01:03; 01:56; 02:47]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Samuel 23:1–14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Samuel+23%3A1%E2%80%9314/>\r\n\r\nQ40. What distinction does the speaker draw from David leaving Keilah before Saul arrived?\r\nA. God's foreknowledge that an event will happen does not mean God predetermined it.\r\nB. If God knows an event, he must have caused the people involved to choose it.\r\nC. David's departure shows that God's knowledge of the event was mistaken.\r\nD. Foreknowledge and predestination are interchangeable terms in the story.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says God knew Saul would come and the people would betray David, but this did not mean God predetermined those actions.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION I — [02:47]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Samuel 23:9–13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Samuel+23%3A9%E2%80%9313/>\r\n\r\nQ41. According to the speaker, why did God know Saul would come and Keilah's people would hand David over?\r\nA. God intended to force Saul and the people of Keilah to act that way.\r\nB. God knew Saul's hatred and persistence, and knew the people of Keilah were frail and would cave under pressure.\r\nC. Their actions had already become unavoidable because David went to Keilah.\r\nD. David's men had predicted their choices and informed God.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker attributes the knowledge to God's knowledge of Saul's hatred and of Keilah's frailty under pressure, not to God causing their choices.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION I — [03:53]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Samuel 23:9–13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Samuel+23%3A9%E2%80%9313/>\r\n\r\nQ42. How does the leash illustration explain free will alongside God's predetermined counsel concerning Christ?\r\nA. The leash means people have no choices, and Christ's coming depends on their obedience.\r\nB. People's freedom can interfere with God's plan for Christ, so the plan is not assured.\r\nC. People exercise freedom within limits, but cannot overturn God's plan that Christ would come, die, rise, and come again.\r\nD. The leash means God predetermined every painful event people experience.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the dog has freedom within the leash's scope while the owner retains control, and names Christ's coming, death, resurrection, and return as part of God's predetermined counsel that free choices cannot frustrate.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION I — [04:50]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 2:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+2%3A23/>; Acts 4:27–28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+4%3A27%E2%80%9328/>\r\n\r\nQ43. Which response best matches the speaker's qualification about painful events such as being dismissed from work or a spouse seeking divorce?\r\nA. Every painful event should be described as God's predetermined will.\r\nB. Such events happen only when believers lack faith.\r\nC. God may have known the event would happen, but not every such event was specifically willed or predetermined by God.\r\nD. Human choices cannot contribute to painful events because God controls every decision.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says people wrongly attribute every misfortune to God's will; some things happen because people are wicked, people are frail, or life is difficult.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION I — [05:45]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Samuel 23:9–14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Samuel+23%3A9%E2%80%9314/>\r\n\r\nQ44. How does the speaker connect Romans 8:28 with difficult circumstances that God did not necessarily predetermine?\r\nA. Every circumstance must therefore have been individually predetermined by God.\r\nB. Difficult circumstances prove that God has no ultimate purpose.\r\nC. The promise applies only when no one exercises free will.\r\nD. God can work through circumstances toward good for those who love him and are called according to his purpose.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites Romans 8:28 after explaining that some circumstances are caused by human actions, and presents God's sovereignty as confidence that he works through them for good.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION I — [06:52]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 8:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ45. What assurance does the speaker draw from David's words about walking through the valley of the shadow of death?\r\nA. God prevents every harmful choice before it can affect believers.\r\nB. Every valley experience is itself God's predetermined counsel.\r\nC. A believer will never encounter circumstances caused by people who hate them.\r\nD. Even when other people's free choices create unfavorable circumstances, they cannot frustrate God's ultimate purpose.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker links David's valley to unfavorable circumstances caused by people who hated him, then says their choices cannot interfere with God's ultimate plan.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION I — [07:36]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Psalm 23:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+23%3A4/>\r\n\r\nQ46. What posture does 1 Peter 3:15 call for when believers explain their hope to someone who asks?\r\nA. Avoid answering questions about faith.\r\nB. Respond with argument alone, without concern for manner.\r\nC. Wait until every theological question has been resolved.\r\nD. Be ready to give a defense with meekness and fear.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker quotes 1 Peter 3:15 as a call to be ready to explain the hope within believers, with meekness and fear.\r\nREF: TRINITY III — [01:00]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Peter 3:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+3%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ47. What point does the speaker draw from “one flesh” in Genesis 2:24 and “one in Christ” in Galatians 3:28?\r\nA. “One” must always mean one individual person.\r\nB. These passages use “one” to deny any unity among people.\r\nC. “One” can describe a unity shared by more than one person.\r\nD. The examples show that the word Trinity appears in the Bible.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker gives the marriage, the people in Christ, and Jesus' prayer that the disciples may be one as examples of unity expressed with “one.”\r\nREF: TRINITY III — [01:42]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 2:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+2%3A24/>; Galatians 3:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+3%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ48. Which combination of beliefs about Jesus does the speaker attribute to Muslims in this discussion?\r\nA. They regard Jesus as God the Son and affirm that he was crucified.\r\nB. They regard Jesus as an angel and deny that he was a prophet.\r\nC. They regard Jesus as a great prophet but do not believe he is the Son of God or that he was crucified.\r\nD. They believe Jesus was crucified in place of someone else, but deny he was a prophet.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Muslims regard Jesus as a great prophet, do not believe he is the Son of God, and do not believe he was crucified; he then describes the substitution account.\r\nREF: TRINITY III — [02:32]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 2:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+2%3A24/>; Galatians 3:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+3%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ49. What tension does the speaker identify between the substitution account of Jesus' death and an objection to Jesus dying for others?\r\nA. The account says Jesus was never taken up to God.\r\nB. The account says Jesus died, while the objection says he was a prophet.\r\nC. The account says someone else was made to die in Jesus' place, which the speaker compares with the claim that Jesus could not die for others.\r\nD. The account denies that anyone was put on a cross.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that an objection asks how Jesus could die for people, while the described account says another person died in Jesus' place.\r\nREF: TRINITY III — [03:28]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 2:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+2%3A24/>\r\n\r\nQ50. How does the speaker reason from the statement that Allah took Jesus to himself?\r\nA. He argues that the phrase proves Jesus remained on earth.\r\nB. He says the phrase establishes that Allah is not above creation.\r\nC. He treats the phrase as a denial that Jesus was raised.\r\nD. Given the stated premise that Allah is above creation, he argues that taking Jesus to himself implies Jesus is above creation.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker's stated reasoning is: if Allah is above creation and took Jesus to himself, then Jesus is implicitly above creation; he presents this as an argument for Jesus' deity.\r\nREF: TRINITY III — [04:22]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Colossians 1:15–17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+1%3A15%E2%80%9317/>\r\n\r\nQ51. What comparison does the speaker make between the Quran and Revelation 1:17?\r\nA. He contrasts the Quran's account of the crucifixion with Genesis 2:24.\r\nB. He contrasts the Quran's description of Allah as first and last with Revelation's description of Jesus as first and last.\r\nC. He says Revelation describes the Holy Spirit as first and last, unlike the Quran.\r\nD. He argues that the Quran and Revelation both call Jesus a prophet but not God.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites Quran 57:3 for Allah as first and last and Revelation 1:17 for Jesus as first and last, using the comparison to argue for Jesus' deity.\r\nREF: TRINITY III — [05:33]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Revelation 1:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+1%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ52. What further issue does the speaker raise concerning claims that the Bible has been corrupted?\r\nA. He says the claim proves the Gospels were written after the Quran.\r\nB. He says that if the Quran promises the Gospels will be preserved, claiming the Bible is corrupted creates a conflict with that promise.\r\nC. He argues that the Quran does not mention the Gospels.\r\nD. He says the claim makes Revelation 1:17 irrelevant to the discussion.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says he plans to discuss the matter further, but states that if the Quran says the Gospels will be preserved, claims of corruption conflict with that promise.\r\nREF: TRINITY III — [06:29]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Revelation 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+1/>\r\n\r\nQ53. What is the speaker's stated purpose for giving these arguments about the Quran and the Bible?\r\nA. To encourage Christians to avoid conversations about doctrine.\r\nB. To help Christians explain their beliefs to Christians who are untaught and to Muslims sincerely seeking truth.\r\nC. To replace the need to study the Bible with debate techniques.\r\nD. To show that only scholars should discuss the Trinity.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says these arguments can help in conversations and that he is addressing both Christians who have not been taught and Muslims seeking truth.\r\nREF: TRINITY III — [07:31]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Revelation 1:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+1%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ54. How does the speaker respond to the claim that the Trinity is not biblical because the word “Trinity” is absent from Scripture?\r\nA. A modern theological term can describe a concept that is rooted in Scripture even if the exact word is absent.\r\nB. Every biblical concept must appear under its later theological name.\r\nC. The absence of the word means the concept was invented by a church council.\r\nD. The word is present in the Bible only in the Old Testament.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker compares “Trinity” with “rapture” and says that modern expressions for theological concepts can be rooted in Scripture without appearing as exact words.\r\nREF: TRINITY II — [00:16]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 28:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+28%3A19/>; 2 Corinthians 13:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+13%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ55. What principle does the speaker use when answering objections that the Trinity is difficult to grasp logically?\r\nA. A doctrine is true only if it fits everyday arithmetic.\r\nB. The question is whether the doctrine is biblical, not whether it fits ordinary human intuitions about numbers.\r\nC. Human logic should replace the biblical text when the doctrine seems difficult.\r\nD. Any teaching that is hard to understand should be rejected.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says God, who created mathematics, is not bound by it, and that the question is whether the teaching is biblical.\r\nREF: TRINITY II — [01:05]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Deuteronomy 6:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+6%3A4/>; Genesis 2:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+2%3A24/>\r\n\r\nQ56. What attitude does the speaker recommend when a biblical teaching about God seems strange or difficult to grasp?\r\nA. Reject the teaching unless it fits ordinary arithmetic and human intuition.\r\nB. Treat difficulty as proof that the teaching was invented later.\r\nC. Set aside the biblical text and decide only by what seems logically simple.\r\nD. Give greater attention to studying it than arguing about it, and receive what Scripture teaches with humility.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says a student's reflex should be to study rather than argue, and that if a teaching is biblical, its strangeness is no reason to reject it.\r\nREF: TRINITY II — [14:03]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 3:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A4/>\r\n\r\nQ57. What distinction does the speaker draw between “echad” and “yahid” when explaining oneness?\r\nA. He presents yahid as compound unity and echad as absolute singularity.\r\nB. Echad is presented as compound unity, while yahid is presented as absolute singularity.\r\nC. He says the words are interchangeable names for the Father.\r\nD. He says echad is Greek and yahid is a New Testament term.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker contrasts echad, illustrated by a unified people or a bunch, with yahid, which he describes as absolute singularity.\r\nREF: TRINITY II — [03:40]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Deuteronomy 6:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+6%3A4/>; Genesis 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+2/>\r\n\r\nQ58. What does the speaker say about Old Testament appearances of an angel who speaks or acts in ways associated with God?\r\nA. Every angel is God and receives worship.\r\nB. The speaker says the Old Testament never depicts God in a human likeness.\r\nC. The word “angel” means messenger, and in some contexts the speaker identifies the figure with the Son of God rather than an ordinary angel.\r\nD. He says pastors are never described with the word “angel.”\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He explains “angel” as messenger and says that in some Old Testament contexts the Son of God can be referred to as the angel of God; he also notes pastors can be called angels in a representative sense.\r\nREF: TRINITY II — [04:47]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Joshua 5:13–15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Joshua+5%3A13%E2%80%9315/>; Genesis 18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+18/>; Genesis 19:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+19%3A24/>\r\n\r\nQ59. What detail from Genesis 18 does the speaker emphasize?\r\nA. Three men visit Abraham; the narrative identifies one of them as the Lord and the other two as angels.\r\nB. Three angels visit Abraham, and none is identified as the Lord.\r\nC. Abraham sees the Ancient of Days seated on multiple thrones.\r\nD. Two men visit Abraham, and both are identified as the Lord.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says three men passed Abraham's house, and that one was God/the Lord while the other two were angels.\r\nREF: TRINITY II — [05:50]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+18/>\r\n\r\nQ60. What connection does the speaker make between the Old Testament references to God's Spirit and the New Testament titles for the Spirit?\r\nA. Isaiah speaks of grieving God's Holy Spirit while the parallel Psalm speaks of grieving God; the New Testament also calls the Spirit the Spirit of God, Christ, and the Son.\r\nB. Isaiah and the Psalm describe unrelated events, and the New Testament gives the Spirit no relation to the Son.\r\nC. The Psalm identifies the Spirit as an ordinary angel, while the New Testament calls him a prophet.\r\nD. Both Old Testament passages deny a distinction, and the New Testament uses “Spirit of Christ” only for Jesus himself.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker compares Isaiah 63:10 with Psalm 78:40–41 and notes New Testament uses including Romans 8:9, Philippians 1:19, and Galatians 4:6 (“Spirit of his Son”).\r\nREF: TRINITY II — [08:38; 10:43]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Isaiah 63:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+63%3A10/>; Psalm 78:40–41 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+78%3A40%E2%80%9341/>; Romans 8:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A9/>; Philippians 1:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+1%3A19/>; Galatians 4:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+4%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ61. What does the speaker emphasize in Daniel 7:9–14 about the Ancient of Days and the Son of Man?\r\nA. The Ancient of Days and the Son of Man are described as one figure on one throne.\r\nB. The Son of Man gives the Ancient of Days a temporary kingdom.\r\nC. Daniel sees only one throne and no one coming to the Ancient of Days.\r\nD. The Ancient of Days is seated, and one like the Son of Man comes before him and receives dominion and an everlasting kingdom.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker points to the plural “thrones,” the Ancient of Days seated, and one like the Son of Man coming to him and receiving dominion and an everlasting kingdom.\r\nREF: TRINITY II — [11:54]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Daniel 7:9–14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Daniel+7%3A9%E2%80%9314/>\r\n\r\nQ62. How does the speaker summarize the basic definition of the Trinity?\r\nA. There are three gods who share some divine qualities.\r\nB. One person changes between the roles of Father, Son, and Spirit.\r\nC. The Father is God, while the Son and Spirit are lesser beings.\r\nD. Within the one being that is God are three co-equal and co-eternal persons: Father, Son, and Holy Spirit.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker defines one God and three persons, identifying the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit as co-equal and co-eternal within one being.\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [03:10]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Deuteronomy 6:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+6%3A4/>; Matthew 28:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+28%3A19/>; 2 Corinthians 13:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+13%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ63. Which two pillars does the speaker say must be retained in describing the Trinity?\r\nA. Different ages and different levels of authority.\r\nB. One-third shares and three separate beings.\r\nC. Distinction among the persons and equality among them.\r\nD. A single person and three temporary manifestations.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker names distinction and equality as the doctrine's two pillars and says the persons are distinct but equal. He illustrates the pillars with John 1:1: the Word was with God (distinction) and the Word was God (equality).\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [04:07]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ64. How does the speaker explain Jesus' submission to the Father alongside the Trinity's equality?\r\nA. Jesus' submission means he is eternally less than the Father.\r\nB. The Father submitted to Jesus in his human nature.\r\nC. The speaker says the Bible does not describe Jesus as submitting to the Father.\r\nD. Jesus submitted in his human nature, while in his eternity he is equal with the Father.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Jesus submitted to the Father in his human nature, yet in eternity is equal with the Father.\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [05:05]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Philippians 2:6–8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+2%3A6%E2%80%938/>; John 5:19–23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+5%3A19%E2%80%9323/>\r\n\r\nQ65. Why does the speaker reject the water analogy of solid, liquid, and gas as an explanation of the Trinity?\r\nA. It suggests the Father, Son, and Spirit are each fully God.\r\nB. It depends on the Hebrew word echad rather than yahid.\r\nC. It suggests one substance changing phases, rather than three distinct persons existing at the same time.\r\nD. It describes the Father, Son, and Spirit as separate substances that never interact.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the phase analogy depicts the same mass of water successively as solid, liquid, and gas; he calls that modalism and contrasts it with the distinct persons interacting at Jesus' baptism.\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [08:14]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 3:16–17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+3%3A16%E2%80%9317/>\r\n\r\nQ66. What paired problems does the speaker identify with the triangle and divided-circle analogies?\r\nA. A triangle's sides are not themselves triangles, while dividing a circle suggests each person is only a fraction of God; the latter is called partialism.\r\nB. Treating the one God as manifesting in different phases at different times.\r\nC. Treating the Son as equal with the Father.\r\nD. Using a theological word that does not appear in the Bible.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says each side of a triangle is not itself a triangle, and separately rejects dividing a circle into thirds because the Father, Son, and Spirit are each 100% God; he calls the fractional model partialism.\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [10:25]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 3:16–17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+3%3A16%E2%80%9317/>\r\n\r\nQ67. What principle does the speaker use to answer the objection that “Trinity” is not a word in the Bible?\r\nA. Every theological term must be spoken verbatim by Jesus.\r\nB. Theological terms can name concepts taught by Scripture even when the exact terms are not used there.\r\nC. A doctrine becomes biblical only after a church council names it.\r\nD. The absence of a word proves the associated concept is false.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker compares Trinity with terms such as omniscient and rapture and says theology uses words to describe concepts taught in the Bible.\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [11:30]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 28:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+28%3A19/>; 2 Corinthians 13:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+13%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ68. Which combination of passages does the speaker cite to support the Son's deity?\r\nA. Hebrews 1:8 addresses the Son as “O God,” Isaiah 9:6 calls the child Mighty God and Everlasting Father, and Revelation 1:8 gives the Lord God the titles Alpha and Omega.\r\nB. Matthew 20 calls the Son the Ancient of Days, and Genesis 2:24 calls him one flesh.\r\nC. Acts 5 calls the Son the Holy Spirit, and Psalm 78:40–41 calls him the Holy One of Israel.\r\nD. Romans 8:28 calls the Son first and last, and 2 Peter 3:17 describes his kingdom.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites Hebrews 1:8, where God addresses the Son as “O God”; Isaiah 9:6, where the child is called Mighty God and Everlasting Father; and Revelation 1:8, where the Lord God is called Alpha and Omega.\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [12:30; 15:01; 16:04]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A8/>; Isaiah 9:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+9%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ69. Which two passages does the speaker use to support the deity and distinction of the Holy Spirit?\r\nA. Matthew 20 and Matthew 22, where many are called but few chosen.\r\nB. Acts 5, where lying to the Holy Spirit is described as lying to God, and Isaiah 48:16, which names the Lord God, his Spirit, and “me.”\r\nC. Genesis 2:24 and Deuteronomy 6:4, both of which use “echad.”\r\nD. Romans 8:28 and Psalm 23, which describe God's care in trouble.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites Acts 5 for the Holy Spirit's deity and Isaiah 48:16 for a distinction involving the Lord God, his Spirit, and the speaker who says they sent him.\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [16:04]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+5/>; Isaiah 48:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+48%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ70. What role does the speaker assign to the Council of Nicaea in relation to the Trinity?\r\nA. It pointed to scriptural evidence that already existed rather than inventing the doctrine.\r\nB. It created the doctrine and introduced it into Scripture.\r\nC. It replaced Old Testament teaching with a new doctrine.\r\nD. It decided that biblical evidence was unnecessary for the doctrine.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the Council of Nicaea went to Scripture and pointed to evidence that had already existed, rather than inventing the message.\r\nREF: TRINITY I — [17:01]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Revelation 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+1%3A8/>; Acts 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+5/>; Isaiah 48 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+48/>\r\n\r\nQ71. What question does the speaker use to check an interpretation that some people are predestined to be saved and others are not?\r\nA. Did the apostles evangelize as though that distinction had already been settled?\r\nB. Did the apostles avoid teaching about God's foreknowledge?\r\nC. Did the apostles use the word “predestination” in every sermon?\r\nD. Did the apostles teach that human choices could frustrate God's purpose?\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker asks whether the apostles conducted evangelism as though some people were predestined to be saved and others were not, then says interpretation must follow the Bible's narrative.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [01:04]–[02:07]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Matthew 20:1–16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+20%3A1%E2%80%9316/>\r\n\r\nQ72. What point does the speaker draw from the workers hired at the eleventh hour in Matthew 20?\r\nA. Later workers earn less because they worked fewer hours.\r\nB. The first workers alone receive the reward promised by the landowner.\r\nC. Those added to the work late receive the same wage, illustrating the same reward in the speaker's salvation comparison.\r\nD. The landowner chooses workers based on how long they have waited.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that workers hired later received the same wage and uses this to illustrate that those added later to the people of God share the same salvation reward.\r\nREF: PREDESTINATION II — [09:14]–[09:31]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Matthew 20:1–16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+20%3A1%E2%80%9316/>\r\n\r\nQ73. In the Joshua example, what details does the speaker use to say the appearing messenger was no ordinary angel?\r\nA. Joshua was told to remove his sandals because the ground was holy, and Joshua worshipped the figure.\r\nB. Joshua saw the figure seated on the fiery throne described by Daniel.\r\nC. The figure arrived with Abraham's two angelic visitors and received a meal.\r\nD. Joshua recognized the figure as a human king and offered him a place to stay.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker compares the instruction to remove Joshua's sandals on holy ground with God's instruction to Moses, and notes that Joshua worshipped; he says an ordinary angel would not receive worship.\r\nREF: TRINITY II — [04:47]–[05:50]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Joshua 5:13–15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Joshua+5%3A13%E2%80%9315/>\r\n\r\nQ74. What detail from the Sodom story does the speaker use to extend his discussion of the Lord appearing to Abraham?\r\nA. Genesis says the Lord rained fire on Sodom from the Lord out of heaven; the speaker reads this as the Lord manifested on earth and the Lord transcendent in heaven.\r\nB. Genesis says Abraham's three visitors included two Lords and one angel.\r\nC. Genesis says the Lord left Abraham with the two angels and did not return.\r\nD. Genesis says Abraham asked the two angels to rain fire on Sodom.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: After identifying one of Abraham's visitors as the Lord, the speaker points to the wording in Genesis 19:24 and describes the Lord manifested as a man and the Lord transcendent in heaven as both the Lord.\r\nREF: TRINITY II — [07:39]–[08:38]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+18/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 19:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+19%3A24/>\n\nDECK: Discipleship Experience (Section 4) Christology\r\n\r\nQ1. In explaining Luke 24:25–27, how does the lesson define the theological term \"hermeneutical framework\"?\r\nA. A systematic method of memorizing Hebrew root words to translate the Old Testament accurately.\r\nB. A practical, step-by-step strategy for conducting effective soul-winning in urban centers.\r\nC. A specific lens or science of Bible interpretation used to read and understand the scriptures.\r\nD. An ancient church creed established to guard local congregations against heresies.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson defines a hermeneutical framework as a way of reading Scripture, grounded in the science of biblical interpretation.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD I — [09:27]–[10:15] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Luke 24:25–27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A25%E2%80%9327/>\r\n\r\nQ2. Why did Jesus address the two sad disciples on the road to Emmaus as \"foolish ones and slow of heart\" instead of offering immediate emotional comfort?\r\nA. Because they had abandoned the apostles in Jerusalem during a time of intense political persecution.\r\nB. Because the realm of the spirit operates on the consciousness of the Word, not on emotions\r\nC. Because they refused to believe the eyewitness testimony of the women who had visited the empty tomb that morning.\r\nD. Because their grief was causing them to walk away from their assigned geographic ministry destination.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents Jesus’ response as correcting the disciples’ understanding through Scripture rather than simply responding to their sadness.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD I — [03:29]–[04:31] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 24:25–27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A25%E2%80%9327/>\r\n\r\nQ3. What detail does the lesson highlight to prove that John 1:1 was written as a deliberate theological commentary on Genesis 1:1?\r\nA. Both books were originally transcribed in Aramaic during the exilic period in Babylon.\r\nB. The Greek Septuagint translation of Genesis 1:1 and John 1:1 open with the exact same phrasing.\r\nC. John explicitly cites Moses by name in the opening sentence of his Gospel account.\r\nD. The original Hebrew text of John's Gospel was discovered alongside the Genesis scrolls at Qumran.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says the matching opening in the Septuagint links John’s opening to Genesis.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD I — [14:49]–[15:41] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+1%3A1/>; John 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ4. How does John 1 resolve the chronological puzzle of light appearing in Genesis 1:3 days before the physical sun and moon were created?\r\nA. It explains that Genesis 1:3 refers to atmospheric lightning caused by rapid thermal expansion.\r\nB. It reveals that the light was Christ Himself, whose divine life was the creative light of men.\r\nC. It shows that Moses used metaphorical poetry rather than a literal chronological sequence.\r\nD. It clarifies that the sun was actually created in Genesis 1:1 but was hidden by heavy vapor clouds.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson identifies the light in Genesis 1 with Christ, referring to John 1’s description of life and light.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD I — [17:42]–[18:47] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+1%3A3/>; John 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1/>\r\n\r\nQ5. When Joshua encountered the man standing with a drawn sword in Joshua 5 and fell down to worship him, why was this act of worship NOT considered blasphemy?\r\nA. Because Joshua was an anointed military commander who possessed special exemption from the Second Commandment.\r\nB. Because the figure was a physical divine manifestation of Yahweh Himself.\r\nC. Because Old Testament saints were permitted to worship holy angels prior to the giving of the Mosaic Law.\r\nD. Because Joshua was actually worshipping the holy ground beneath his feet rather than the person standing before him.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson identifies the figure Joshua worshipped as a manifestation of God, not an ordinary angel.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN II — [07:21]–[08:24] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Joshua 5:13–15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Joshua+5%3A13%E2%80%9315/>\r\n\r\nQ6. According to Genesis 15:1–5 and 1 Samuel 3:10, how should we understand the phrase \"the Word of the Lord came\"?\r\nA. As an audible voice.\r\nB. As a poetic literary device used by ancient prophets to express human intuition.\r\nC. As a physical, visible Divine Person.\r\nD. As a secret message passed through angelic messengers to the high priest.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson interprets ‘the Word of the Lord came’ in these passages as a personal, visible encounter.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD II — [10:30]–[13:11] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 15:1–5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+15%3A1%E2%80%935/>; 1 Samuel 3:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Samuel+3%3A10/>\r\n\r\nQ7. How does the lesson explain Jesus' statement in John 8:58, \"Before Abraham was, I am\"?\r\nA. Jesus was referring to His pre-ordained selection in the mind of God before creation.\r\nB. Jesus was asserting His pre-existence, having personally interacted with Abraham in Genesis.\r\nC. Jesus was referring to the immortal nature that all human souls possess prior to biological birth.\r\nD. Jesus was claiming that His earthly human lineage could be traced back genealogically before Abraham.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson connects Jesus’ claim with the Word of the Lord’s earlier personal encounters with Abraham.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD II — [13:11]–[14:14] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 8:58 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+8%3A58/>\r\n\r\nQ8. According to the lesson, what is the primary doctrinal difference between Christ's Old Testament manifestations and His New Testament Incarnation?\r\nA. In the Old Testament He appeared as an angel, whereas in the New Testament He appeared as a prophet.\r\nB. In the Old Testament He assumed temporary physical forms, but in the Incarnation He was permanently born as a true man.\r\nC. In the Old Testament He possessed limited divine power, but in the New Testament He received full omnipotence.\r\nD. In the Old Testament He spoke only to Israel, but in the Incarnation He spoke exclusively to the Gentiles.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson distinguishes temporary Old Testament manifestations from Christ’s birth as a true man in the Incarnation.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD II — [14:14]–[15:28] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Isaiah 9:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+9%3A6/>; John 1:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ9. What makes Christ the ultimate standard when we are commanded to be \"imitators of Christ\"?\r\nA. He bypassed real human emotional limitations through His divine omniscience.\r\nB. He lived out God's character in human shoes, proving that divine righteousness is applicable to human existence.\r\nC. He laid down His divine attributes permanently and functioned purely as a human prophet.\r\nD. He established an easier way suited for fragile human nature compared to the Old Testament standard.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents Christ’s genuinely human, sinless life as the example believers are to imitate.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN II — [15:12]–[17:30] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 4:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+4%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ10. How does the revelation of God in Jesus Christ refine our understanding of God's nature under the Law?\r\nA. It shows that the Law were not the full expression of God's nature.\r\nB. It proves God was vengeful in the Old Testament but changed His personality in the New Testament.\r\nC. It shows that the Law was authored by angels rather than God.\r\nD. It proves that God's grace did not exist prior to the birth of Jesus in Bethlehem.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says Jesus reveals aspects of God’s nature, such as mercy and grace, not fully expressed in the Law.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN II — [17:30]–end of transcript (approximate boundary)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 1:18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A18/>\r\n\r\nQ11. How do we resolve the contradiction between Jesus \"growing in wisdom\" (Luke 2:52) and Jesus being the All-Knowing God?\r\nA. Jesus only feigned ignorance in public so His peers wouldn't feel uncomfortable.\r\nB. He is omniscient, but in His human nature He voluntarily experienced human development.\r\nC. His divine nature completely replaced His human mind once He turned thirty.\r\nD. Luke 2:52 is talking about spiritual growth rather than actual cognitive development.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson explains that Jesus is all-knowing in His divine nature and experienced growth in His human nature.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [02:31]–[04:54] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Luke 2:52 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+2%3A52/>\r\n\r\nQ12. Why is it crucial for our redemption that Jesus suffered as a genuine human rather than a divine manifestation?\r\nA. Because if He were only divine, the crucifixion would be acting rather than actual human suffering absorbing the penalty of sin.\r\nB. Because angels are incapable of entering physical blood covenants.\r\nC. Because divine beings cannot experience physical fatigue or emotional sadness.\r\nD. Because the Mosaic Law required a human priest to offer a human sacrifice to satisfy the altar's requirements.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lessons connect Christ’s humanity with real suffering and with His ability to die for humanity’s sins.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN II — [09:49]–[12:10] and JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [16:01]–[16:56] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 2:14–18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+2%3A14%E2%80%9318/>\r\n\r\nQ13. How does the reality of hematidrosis (sweating drops of blood) substantiate the doctrine of Christ’s humanity?\r\nA. It proves that Jesus contracted a physical disease right before His arrest.\r\nB. It serves as proof of extreme human stress, proving He was never insulated from human pain.\r\nC. It proves that the blood shed in Gethsemane had more redemptive power than the blood shed on the cross.\r\nD. It demonstrates that Jesus was physically weaker than most religious martyrs.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents the blood-like sweat as evidence of the extreme stress Jesus experienced as a human.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN II — [11:00]–[12:10] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 22:44 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+22%3A44/>\r\n\r\nQ14. How does the lady turned \"detective\" illustrate the believer's duty to \"love the Lord with all your mind\" (Matthew 22:37)?\r\nA. Genuine love naturally produces an intense, investigating hunger.\r\nB. True devotion requires believers to know their faith well to win debates against secular scholars.\r\nC. A loving heart must constantly cross-examine church leaders to verify whether their personal life matches their doctrine\r\nD. Loving God with the mind means experiencing emotional expressions during worship.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The illustration links love for God with a desire to know more about Him.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [02:38]–[04:30] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Matthew 22:37 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+22%3A37/>\r\n\r\nQ15. Why does Apostle state that Christianity and Buddhism are fundamentally incompatible (\"we cannot both be right\")?\r\nA. Buddhism denies the existence of moral evil, whereas Christianity teaches that evil is inherited from Adam.\r\nB. Buddhism teaches that world miseries end when we do what is right, whereas Christianity teaches we must believe what is right to be empowered to do what is right.\r\nC. Buddhism claims spiritual enlightenment is achieved through suffering, while Christianity claims suffering is always a sign of demonic influence.\r\nD. Buddhism rejects the reality of an afterlife, whereas Christianity teaches that we live eternally in heaven or hell.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The Apostle contrasts the two teachings about what ends the world’s miseries and how people are enabled to do what is right.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [07:41]–[08:41] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 1:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ16. Which scripture directly dismantles the Jehovah's Witness assertion that Jesus is merely the archangel Michael?\r\nA. John 11\r\nB. Hebrews 1\r\nC. Matthew 28\r\nD. Colossians 2\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson cites Hebrews 1 to show Jesus receives worship and does works associated with God, rather than being merely an angel.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [08:41]–[09:36] and [13:34]–[14:58] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 1:3, 5–6, 13–14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A3%2C+5%E2%80%936%2C+13%E2%80%9314/>; John 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A3/>\r\n\r\nQ17. What combination of divine Deeds (\"D\") and Seat (\"S\") in the HANDS acronym belongs exclusively to Jesus?\r\nA. Creating the universe, forgiving sins, exercising final judgment, and occupying God's eternal throne.\r\nB. Parting waters, giving divine commandments, defeating earthly armies, and ruling over angels.\r\nC. Establishing the Church, indwelling believers, authoring Scripture, and interceding for saints.\r\nD. Raising the dead, instituting communion, abolishing the Old Covenant, and establishing physical Israel.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson gives creation, forgiveness of sins, final judgment, and God’s throne as evidence of Christ’s deity.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [12:24]–[14:58] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A3/>; Mark 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+2/>; Hebrews 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A3/>; John 5:22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+5%3A22/>; Revelation 19:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+19%3A16/>; Daniel 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Daniel+7/>\r\n\r\nQ18. Why did the Sanhedrin condemn Jesus for blasphemy when He claimed the title \"Son of Man\" before Caiaphas?\r\nA. Because \"Son of Man\" was a political title reserved for the earthly King of Israel.\r\nB. Because He was invoking Daniel 7.\r\nC. Because the title implied that God had entered human existence through biological reproduction.\r\nD. Because claims of divine sonship were strictly forbidden under the traditional oral rabbinic code.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lessons connect Jesus’ words before the high priest with Daniel 7 and explain that His hearers understood the claim as one to deity.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [14:58]–[15:57] and JESUS- SON OF GOD III — [23:41]–[25:26] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Daniel 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Daniel+7/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Mark 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+2/>; Hebrews 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A3/>; John 5:22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+5%3A22/>; Revelation 19:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+19%3A16/>; Revelation 11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+11/>; Isaiah 9:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+9%3A6/>; Mark 14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+14/>\r\n\r\nQ19. How does the lesson explain the Old Testament concept of the \"Two Yahwehs\"?\r\nA. As a metaphor describing the dual nature of God's justice and God's mercy.\r\nB. As an invisible Yahweh in heaven and a physical Yahweh who appeared visibly as a man on Earth.\r\nC. As an early Hebrew misunderstanding that was later corrected by the New Testament writers.\r\nD. As a reference to the distinction between Yahweh the Creator and the Angel of Death.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson describes a Yahweh in heaven and another Yahweh manifesting visibly on earth.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD II — [08:24]–[10:30] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+15/>\r\n\r\nQ20. According to Hebrews 2:10–14, what does it mean that Christ is the Archegos (Captain/Fountainhead) of our salvation?\r\nA. He sits safely in heaven sharing instructions on how we should live.\r\nB. He paved the way through death and legally brought us into adoption as His brethren.\r\nC. He replaced angels as the chief military officer over physical earth wars.\r\nD. He serves as a legal advocate only for those who observe Levitical priesthood laws.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says Christ entered into suffering and death to bring believers into salvation and adoption as His brethren.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [15:06]–[16:56] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 2:10–14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+2%3A10%E2%80%9314/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ21. Why is the fulfillment of Psalm 22:16 (\"They pierced my hands and my feet\") powerful proof of divine prophecy?\r\nA. Because King David studied ancient execution methods used by foreign nations.\r\nB. Because when David wrote that prophecy, the execution method known as crucifixion did not even exist yet.\r\nC. Because Jesus gave the Roman soldiers written instructions on how to fulfill the prophecy.\r\nD. Because it is the only verse in the Old Testament that describes physical pain.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says Psalm 22 described pierced hands and feet centuries before crucifixion existed.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [08:42]–[09:39] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Psalm 22:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+22%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ22. According to 1 John 4:2–3, what is the ultimate litmus test for discerning whether a spiritual teacher is of God?\r\nA. Their ability to work undeniable physical miracles.\r\nB. Their confession affirming Jesus Christ both in His full humanity and deity.\r\nC. Their public commitment to living an ascetic lifestyle free from worldly riches.\r\nD. Their alignment with historical church hierarchies and ordained leadership.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents confession of Christ’s coming in the flesh as a test and emphasizes His humanity as well as His deity.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN II — [06:41]–[08:57] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 John 4:2–3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+4%3A2%E2%80%933/>; 2 John 1:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+John+1%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ23. What is the theological significance of distinguishing between \"a child is born\" and \"a son is given\" in Isaiah 9:6?\r\nA. \"Child is born\" refers to His earthly ministry; \"Son is given\" refers to after resurrection.\r\nB. \"Child is born\" points to the beginning of His humanity, while \"Son is given\" reveals His pre-existent Sonship.\r\nC. \"Child is born\" applies to Israel's national birth; \"Son is given\" refers to the spiritual adoption of believers.\r\nD. \"Child is born\" signifies His flesh; \"Son is given\" signifies the Holy Spirit coming upon Him at baptism.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lessons contrast the beginning of Jesus’ humanity with His eternal Sonship.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [16:58]–[18:00] and JESUS- SON OF GOD II — [15:28]–[16:34] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Isaiah 9:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+9%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ24. What forms the true foundation of a Christian’s bold confidence before the throne of grace?\r\nA. God looking at us through the lens of who we are in Christ and what Christ has legally accomplished for us.\r\nB. The believer’s personal record of flawless obedience and religious commitment.\r\nC. The emotional feelings of warmth experienced during church services.\r\nD. The public praise given to a believer by spiritual leaders.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson grounds confidence before the throne of grace in being reckoned in Christ and in what Christ has done.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [18:50]–[19:59] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 8:33–34 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A33%E2%80%9334/>\r\n\r\nQ25. Why do all human beings inherit a fallen nature because of Adam's sin?\r\nA. Every human being signed a spiritual contract in heaven agreeing to Adam's choice.\r\nB. Inheritance of sin is governed by demonic assignments rather than covenant representation.\r\nC. Death was introduced as an penalty rather than a structural legal reality.\r\nD. Adam was God's covenant representative for all humanity.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says Adam represented humanity and that his sin affected those who came after him.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [11:57]–[12:52] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 5:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+5%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ26. Based on Genesis 5:2, what vital detail is revealed about the word \"Adam\"?\r\nA. \"Adam\" is not merely one man's proper name, but the Hebrew word for mankind.\r\nB. \"Adam\" was a royal title given exclusively to Abraham’s spiritual lineage.\r\nC. \"Adam\" translates directly to \"sinful flesh\" in ancient Egyptian texts.\r\nD. The name \"Adam\" was invented by Moses.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says ‘Adam’ refers to mankind, not only to one individual.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [10:52]–[12:52] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 5:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+5%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ27. What vital lesson does the pastor draw from the historic confrontation between Athanasius and Arius at Nicaea?\r\nA. Theological disagreements are personal power struggles that fractured early church unity.\r\nB. The defense of precise biblical doctrine by individuals preserved the true understanding of Christ's Deity for future generations.\r\nC. Church doctrine are more authoritative than Scripture when settling arguments about the Trinity.\r\nD. Early church fathers relied on alliances rather than Scripture to establish doctrine.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson credits Athanasius’s defense of Christ’s deity with helping preserve a clear understanding of Scripture.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN II — [04:47]–[06:41] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 John 4:2–3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+4%3A2%E2%80%933/>\r\n\r\nQ28. Why is Jesus called both a \"Child [that] is born\" and \"The Everlasting Father\" within the single prophecy of Isaiah 9:6?\r\nA. Because Jesus transforms into God the Father whenever He ascends into the heavenly sanctuary.\r\nB. Because while His humanity had an origin in time, His divine identity as God is eternal, without beginning or end.\r\nC. Because Isaiah was combining two separate prophecies written by different authors into one text.\r\nD. Because \"Everlasting Father\" was a title.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lessons contrast the beginning of Jesus’ humanity with His eternal divine identity.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [16:58]–[18:00] and JESUS- SON OF GOD II — [15:28]–[16:34] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Isaiah 9:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+9%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ29. What combination of actions directed toward Jesus under the \"H\" in the HANDS acronym proves His Deity?\r\nA. Performing public miracles, receiving tithes, and appointing twelve apostles.\r\nB. Receiving direct worship, hearing prayers, and having saving faith placed explicitly in Him\r\nC. Calming the storm, rebuking religious hypocrites, and forgiving executioners on the cross.\r\nD. Reading human thoughts, fasting forty days, and promising physical thrones to His followers.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson cites worship, prayer, and faith placed in Jesus as evidence of His deity.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [08:41]–[09:36] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 1:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ30. Using the lesson's practical illustration of a phone call vs. a video call, what defines a \"mediated encounter\" with God in the Old Testament?\r\nA. Communicating through a distinct physical medium or manifestation, such as a burning bush or pillar of fire.\r\nB. Reading written prophecies inscribed on stone tablets through an ordained Levitical priest.\r\nC. Experiencing emotional conviction during high moments of congregational worship and sacrifice.\r\nD. Seeking guidance through casting physical lots like the Urim and Thummim.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson defines a mediated encounter as communication through a medium, with the burning bush and pillar of fire as examples.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD II — [05:24]–[07:21] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Exodus 33:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+33%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ31. What does the speaker say loving God with all one's mind includes?\r\nA. Limiting devotion to attending church and singing songs.\r\nB. Focusing only on receiving blessings, healing, and breakthroughs.\r\nC. Avoiding difficult sermons and books that require rereading.\r\nD. Deliberately investing effort in learning more about Jesus, including through books and repeated study.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker connects loving God with the mind to becoming interested in knowing Jesus and putting one's mind to that task.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [03:32; 04:30]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Deuteronomy 6:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+6%3A5/>; Matthew 22:37 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+22%3A37/>\r\n\r\nQ32. How does the speaker use the relationship analogy of becoming a 'detective' to describe love for God?\r\nA. A believer should investigate church leaders' private lives before worshipping.\r\nB. A Christian should gather facts only to win debates against other religions.\r\nC. Genuine commitment includes a desire to know more about the one loved, rather than receiving benefits without interest in the person.\r\nD. A person should expect God to provide gifts before learning about him.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says someone in love wants to learn details about the other person and applies this to seeking to know the Lord, not merely receiving benefits.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [02:38; 03:32]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Deuteronomy 6:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+6%3A5/>; Matthew 22:37 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+22%3A37/>\r\n\r\nQ33. Why does the speaker emphasize Jesus' question, 'Who do you say that I, the Son of Man, am?'\r\nA. Because Jesus was asking only whether his disciples knew his preferred title.\r\nB. Because what people say about Jesus matters, and believers should know and be able to explain who he is.\r\nC. Because the question was intended to establish that Jesus was only a prophet.\r\nD. Because the disciples' opinions mattered more than what Scripture says.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Jesus cares what people say about him and contrasts Christian teaching with other accounts of Jesus.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [05:26]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 16:13–16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+16%3A13%E2%80%9316/>\r\n\r\nQ34. Which contrast does the speaker draw between Christian claims about Jesus and the examples he gives of other religions' views?\r\nA. He says Muslims call Jesus a prophet, Jehovah's Witnesses a great angel, and Hindus say he has been reincarnated many times; he contrasts these with Christianity's claims about Jesus.\r\nB. He says Muslims teach repeated reincarnations, Jehovah's Witnesses call Jesus a great teacher, and Christians call him only a prophet.\r\nC. He says all these groups agree that Jesus is the eternal Son of God and fully divine.\r\nD. He says each group differs only over whether Jesus performed miracles.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker lists Muslims' view of Jesus as a prophet, the Jehovah's Witness view of him as a great angel, and Hindu claims of repeated reincarnations, then contrasts these with Christian teaching.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [06:29; 07:41]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1/>\r\n\r\nQ35. How does the speaker contrast Buddhism's account of suffering with the Christian account he presents?\r\nA. He says Buddhism teaches salvation through faith in Jesus, while Christianity teaches repeated reincarnations.\r\nB. He says Buddhism teaches that the world's miseries end when people do what is right, while Christianity teaches that people must believe what is right to be empowered to do what is right.\r\nC. He says both accounts teach that suffering ends only through physical discipline.\r\nD. He says Christianity teaches that people can do what is right without first believing what is right.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker contrasts the Buddhist claim about doing what is right with his stated Christian claim about believing what is right and being empowered to do it.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [07:41]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1/>\r\n\r\nQ36. In the speaker's HANDS acronym for defending Christ's deity, what do the letters represent?\r\nA. Humanity, Atonement, New birth, Discipleship, and Salvation.\r\nB. Holiness, Authority, Nature, Death, and Sonship.\r\nC. Honor, Attributes, Deeds, and Seat.\r\nD. Heaven, Angels, Names, Dominion, and Scripture.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker introduces HANDS as a mnemonic: honor, attributes, deeds, and seat.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [07:41]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1/>\r\n\r\nQ37. Which example does the speaker use under 'Honor' as evidence for Jesus' deity?\r\nA. Jesus refuses worship from the Magi and redirects it to the angels.\r\nB. The Father commands the angels to worship Jesus, and the Magi also worship him as a toddler.\r\nC. The angels worship Jesus only after he takes his seat at the final judgment.\r\nD. The Magi honor Jesus as a king but the speaker says they do not worship him.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites Hebrews 1:6 and the Magi's worship of the child Jesus as examples of honor belonging to God.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [08:41; 09:36]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 1:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ38. Which set of qualities does the speaker place under the 'Attributes' part of HANDS?\r\nA. Hunger, fatigue, sleep, and growth in wisdom.\r\nB. Eternity, almightiness, and divine names such as 'Mighty God' and 'Alpha and Omega.'\r\nC. The ability to teach parables and choose twelve disciples.\r\nD. The ordinary virtues of generosity, patience, and courage.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker calls eternity and almightiness incommunicable attributes and points to divine titles in John 1:1, Revelation 1:8, Isaiah 9:6, and John 8:58.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [11:04]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1/>; Revelation 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+1%3A8/>; Isaiah 9:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+9%3A6/>; John 8:58 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+8%3A58/>; John 20:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+20%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ39. Which group of actions does the speaker cite under 'Deeds' as actions of Jesus that belong to God?\r\nA. Building the temple, leading Israel out of Egypt, and writing the law on stone.\r\nB. Creating all things, forgiving sins, sustaining all things, and exercising final judgment.\r\nC. Choosing twelve apostles, instituting communion, and appointing church elders.\r\nD. Healing the sick, teaching crowds, and travelling through Galilee only.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites John 1:3, Mark 2, Hebrews 1:3, and John 5:22 when describing divine deeds attributed to Jesus.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [12:24; 13:34]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A3/>; Mark 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+2/>; Hebrews 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A3/>; John 5:22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+5%3A22/>\r\n\r\nQ40. What does the speaker mean by the 'Seat' in the HANDS acronym?\r\nA. Jesus sat in the place of a synagogue teacher during his ministry.\r\nB. Jesus occupied a temporary throne in Jerusalem but does not reign forever.\r\nC. Jesus took the seat of the high priest in the temple.\r\nD. Jesus is seated on God's throne and reigns with divine authority.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker points to Jesus being seated on God's throne, called Lord of Lords, and reigning forever.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [13:34]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Psalm 110:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+110%3A1/>; Daniel 7:13–14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Daniel+7%3A13%E2%80%9314/>; Revelation 19:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+19%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ41. Why does the speaker say Jesus' use of the title 'Son of Man' at his trial was not a denial of deity?\r\nA. He says the title meant Jesus was only a human prophet and the trial misunderstood him.\r\nB. He says Jesus used the title to deny that the Ancient of Days had a throne.\r\nC. He says the title was a political description unrelated to Daniel's vision.\r\nD. He says Jesus was invoking Daniel 7, where the Son of Man receives dominion and the peoples serve him, and the authorities understood the claim.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker links Jesus' trial statement about coming in the clouds to Daniel 7 and says the hearers understood it as a divine claim.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [14:58; 15:57]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Daniel 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Daniel+7/>\r\n\r\nQ42. How does the speaker distinguish 'a child is born' from 'a Son is given' in Isaiah 9:6?\r\nA. The child refers to Jesus' resurrection, while the Son refers to his human birth.\r\nB. The child refers to David's lineage, while the Son refers to the Holy Spirit at baptism.\r\nC. Both phrases describe the beginning of Jesus' existence at the same moment.\r\nD. The child being born points to the beginning of Jesus' humanity, while the Son being given points to his eternal Sonship.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the child had an origin in his humanity, while the Son is eternal and was given, not first made Son at the incarnation.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN I — [16:58; 18:00]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Isaiah 9:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+9%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ43. According to 1 John 4:2–3 as explained in the lesson, what confession is a test of whether a spirit is of God?\r\nA. Confessing that Jesus only appeared to have a human body.\r\nB. Confessing that Jesus was an angel who temporarily looked like a man.\r\nC. Confessing that Jesus was a great teacher but not truly human.\r\nD. Confessing that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker quotes 1 John 4:2–3 as a discernment test based on confessing Jesus Christ's coming in the flesh.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN II — [06:41]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 John 4:2–3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+4%3A2%E2%80%933/>\r\n\r\nQ44. What does the speaker say 2 John 1:7 calls those who do not confess Jesus Christ coming in the flesh?\r\nA. Faithful witnesses who protect Jesus' divine nature.\r\nB. Teachers who preserve the apostolic message.\r\nC. Deceivers and antichrists.\r\nD. People whose only error is not knowing the chronology of the Gospels.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites 2 John 1:7, which he says calls such people deceivers and antichrists.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN II — [08:57]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 2 John 1:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+John+1%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ45. What did the speaker say Athanasius defended against Arius at Nicaea?\r\nA. The claim that Jesus was only a human prophet and not the Son of God.\r\nB. The deity of Christ, standing against Arius's denial of it.\r\nC. A proposal to replace Scripture with church tradition.\r\nD. The idea that Jesus had no human nature.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker describes Arius as not believing in Christ's deity and Athanasius as defending the doctrine over the years.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN II — [04:47]\r\n\r\nQ46. What lesson does the speaker draw from Athanasius's willingness to stand against the world while defending doctrine?\r\nA. A doctrine is true whenever one church leader is willing to defend it.\r\nB. Disagreement should be avoided because councils cannot address theological questions.\r\nC. Diligent, courageous defense of biblical doctrine can help preserve a clear understanding of Scripture for later generations.\r\nD. Personal alliances are more important than careful study of Scripture.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker credits the bravery and diligence of defenders such as Athanasius with helping later people understand the Scriptures.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN II — [05:43]\r\n\r\nQ47. Why, according to the speaker, would Christ's suffering not be merely an appearance if Jesus is truly human?\r\nA. His divine nature made physical suffering impossible, so the pain was symbolic.\r\nB. The speaker says the flogging mattered only as a public sign and was not physically painful.\r\nC. His human nature could experience real pain and suffering rather than acting out pain as a divine manifestation.\r\nD. His humanity meant he could not have been divine during the crucifixion.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker argues that the humanity of Jesus makes his suffering and crucifixion real, not theatrical.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN II — [09:49]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 2:14–18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+2%3A14%E2%80%9318/>; Hebrews 4:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+4%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ48. What does the speaker identify as the significance of Jesus' hematidrosis in Gethsemane?\r\nA. It proves that Gethsemane, rather than the cross, was the only redemptive event.\r\nB. It shows that Jesus had a disease unrelated to the stress of his arrest.\r\nC. It demonstrates that Jesus did not experience human emotions.\r\nD. It illustrates extreme human stress as Jesus faced the suffering ahead.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker describes the blood-like sweat as a condition associated with extreme stress and connects it with Jesus' anguish.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN II — [11:00]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 22:44 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+22%3A44/>\r\n\r\nQ49. How does the speaker connect Christ's incarnation with the call for human moral discipline?\r\nA. Jesus avoided temptation by never living among human beings.\r\nB. Jesus' divine nature means his human example cannot apply to believers.\r\nC. Jesus experienced temptation as a human and yet was without sin, so believers cannot dismiss his example as irrelevant to human life.\r\nD. Jesus taught discipline but did not experience human temptation.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Jesus took on flesh, was tempted yet without sin, and therefore believers have no excuse for refusing to imitate him.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN II — [15:12]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 4:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+4%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ50. What specific response does the speaker draw from the example that Jesus was reviled but did not revile in return?\r\nA. Believers should retaliate only when the insult is public.\r\nB. Believers should avoid all contact with people who disagree with them.\r\nC. Believers should learn not to answer abuse with abuse and should imitate Christ.\r\nD. Believers should remain silent because Jesus never spoke during his ministry.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker applies Jesus' refusal to return abuse as a model for believers and says they must be imitators of Christ.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN II — [16:11]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Peter 2:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+2%3A23/>\r\n\r\nQ51. According to the speaker, what does Jesus do in John 1:18 as the one who has declared the Father?\r\nA. He replaces the Father's character with a different nature in the New Testament.\r\nB. He shows that God's nature can be known only through the law.\r\nC. He conceals God's character so that people must infer it from nature.\r\nD. He reveals what God is like, including God's mercy, patience, and abundant grace.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the Son came to show who God is and points to mercy, slowness to anger, and grace as seen in Christ.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN II — [17:30]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 1:18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A18/>\r\n\r\nQ52. How does the speaker distinguish the future person of the Antichrist from the message associated with the Antichrist?\r\nA. He says both the person and the message are limited to a distant future.\r\nB. He says the person of the Antichrist is coming, while the message or ideology is already in the world.\r\nC. He says the person has already come, but the message will not appear until later.\r\nD. He says the message refers only to denying that Jesus performed miracles.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the person of the Antichrist is coming but that the message or ideology is already in the world.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN II — [08:57]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 John 1:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+John+1%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ53. How does the speaker summarize the Trinity using 'what' and 'who' language?\r\nA. There are three 'whats' and one 'who,' with each person one-third of God.\r\nB. There is one 'what'—God—and three 'whos': Father, Son, and Spirit; each person is fully God.\r\nC. There is one 'who' appearing in three temporary forms, none present together.\r\nD. There are three beings who share one divine title but are not fully God.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says God is one what and three whos and emphasizes that Father, Son, and Spirit are each fully God, not thirds.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [00:17]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Deuteronomy 6:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+6%3A4/>; Matthew 28:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+28%3A19/>; 2 Corinthians 13:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+13%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ54. What does the speaker mean by saying Jesus is 'one who and two whats'?\r\nA. Jesus is one person with two natures: divine and human.\r\nB. Jesus is two persons sharing one human body.\r\nC. Jesus is one nature that changes between divine and human states.\r\nD. Jesus is one person and two separate gods.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains the phrase as one person with a divine nature and a human nature.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [01:30]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 1:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A14/>; Colossians 2:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+2%3A9/>; Hebrews 2:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+2%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ55. Why does the speaker caution against trying to explain God's nature through an example in nature?\r\nA. He says natural examples are forbidden because Scripture never uses illustrations.\r\nB. He says only mathematical models can explain the relationship among the persons.\r\nC. He says there is no natural example that fully explains God, who is unlike creation and not bound by created categories.\r\nD. He says nature proves that each person is a fraction of God.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says there is no example in nature of this and connects God's uniqueness with not being explained by created analogies.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [01:30]\r\n\r\nQ56. How does the speaker explain Jesus' growth in wisdom alongside his divine omniscience?\r\nA. He was not omniscient until after his resurrection.\r\nB. He grew and learned in his human nature, while remaining all-knowing in his divine nature.\r\nC. He only pretended to learn so that others would understand him.\r\nD. His human nature was all-knowing, while his divine nature needed instruction.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses Luke 2:52 to describe real human development while affirming omniscience in Jesus' divine nature.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [02:31]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Luke 2:52 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+2%3A52/>\r\n\r\nQ57. Which set of human experiences does the speaker ascribe to Jesus' human nature?\r\nA. Being omnipotent, all-knowing, and unable to die.\r\nB. Creating all things, sustaining the universe, and judging humanity.\r\nC. Being unable to experience weakness or physical limitations.\r\nD. Growing tired, becoming sleepy, and dying.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker contrasts the human nature, which can tire, sleep, and die, with the divine nature.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [03:41]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 2:52 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+2%3A52/>; John 11:35 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+11%3A35/>; John 19:30 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+19%3A30/>\r\n\r\nQ58. How does the speaker explain Jesus praying to God if Jesus is God?\r\nA. Jesus stopped being divine whenever he prayed.\r\nB. In the incarnation, the second person of the Trinity was human, and in his human nature he prayed as humans ought to pray.\r\nC. The prayer proves that Jesus was a separate, lesser deity.\r\nD. The speaker says the Gospel accounts do not actually describe Jesus praying.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that in his human nature Jesus prayed to God because he was a man.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [03:41]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1/>; Luke 5:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+5%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ59. When Jesus quoted 'My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?', what passage does the speaker say he was invoking?\r\nA. Psalm 22:1, whose opening line could direct hearers to the wider psalm.\r\nB. Isaiah 9:6, which begins with the child being born.\r\nC. Daniel 7:13, which describes the Son of Man coming in clouds.\r\nD. Romans 5:17, which speaks of reigning in life.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker identifies Jesus' words with Psalm 22:1 and explains that quoting the opening verse could call attention to the passage as a whole.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [04:54]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Psalm 22:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+22%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ60. Which details from Psalm 22 does the speaker connect with events at Jesus' crucifixion?\r\nA. The breaking of Jesus' legs and the tearing of the temple curtain only.\r\nB. The division of garments by casting lots, the piercing of hands and feet, and the mockery of one who trusts in God.\r\nC. The feeding of a crowd, the calming of a storm, and the raising of Lazarus.\r\nD. The transfer of Jesus' kingdom to the Ancient of Days.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker connects Psalm 22:18, 16, and 7–8 with the soldiers' actions and the taunts at the cross.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [06:42; 07:42; 08:42; 09:39]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Psalm 22:18, 16, 7–8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+22%3A18%2C+16%2C+7%E2%80%938/>\r\n\r\nQ61. Why does the speaker regard the piercing detail in Psalm 22 as especially significant?\r\nA. He says David described an execution method that Roman soldiers invented after the Gospels.\r\nB. He says crucifixion did not yet exist as an execution method when David wrote the prophecy, so the detail was not something Jesus could arrange himself.\r\nC. He says the verse is significant because it was the only biblical reference to pain.\r\nD. He says Jesus personally instructed the soldiers to fulfill the words.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker stresses that crucifixion was not yet an existing execution method when David wrote and that the soldiers' actions were not Jesus' to control.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [07:42]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Psalm 22:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+22%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ62. What does the speaker mean by calling Jesus the second and last Adam?\r\nA. Jesus comes as a new representative humanity, succeeding the first Adam and providing salvation for those who belong to him.\r\nB. Jesus is another name for the first man, who represented only Israel.\r\nC. Jesus repeats Adam's disobedience so that humanity can be released from the Law.\r\nD. Jesus is the first created angel and therefore replaces Adam.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Christ came as the second and last Adam and invites believers to choose him rather than remain under the first Adam's course.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [10:52]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 15:45 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15%3A45/>\r\n\r\nQ63. What point does the speaker make about the word 'Adam' in Genesis 5:2?\r\nA. He says Adam is the Hebrew word for mankind and that the text calls both male and female by that name.\r\nB. He says Adam was only a personal name for the first man and never referred to others.\r\nC. He says Adam was a title reserved for descendants of David.\r\nD. He says the word Adam means 'sinful flesh' in Greek.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites Genesis 5:2, where the name Adam is used for male and female, to explain that it can refer to mankind.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [11:57]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 5:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+5%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ64. According to the speaker, how does Adam's role as covenant representative explain humanity's shared fallen nature?\r\nA. Adam represented humanity, so the consequences of his disobedience affected those who came from him.\r\nB. Each person separately agreed in heaven to repeat Adam's choice.\r\nC. The curse spread only because every later person ate from the same tree.\r\nD. Adam represented only his immediate family and not humanity.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker calls Adam God's covenant man, representing humanity, and uses that representation to explain why his disobedience affected those who came after.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [11:57]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 5:12–19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A12%E2%80%9319/>\r\n\r\nQ65. What necessity for the virgin birth does the speaker infer from the first Adam's representative role?\r\nA. Jesus needed to be born without a human body so he would not experience death.\r\nB. The virgin birth was required only to fulfill a Roman legal rule.\r\nC. A new man had to be born without carrying the first Adam's implications, rather than simply repeating the same lineage under Adam.\r\nD. A second Adam had to be created as an angel rather than born as a human.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that if God was to correct the consequences of the first Adam, a man had to be born who would not carry those implications, which he calls the necessity of the virgin birth.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [12:52]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 1:18–23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+1%3A18%E2%80%9323/>; Luke 1:34–35 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+1%3A34%E2%80%9335/>\r\n\r\nQ66. How does Hebrews 2 describe Jesus as the captain or fountainhead of salvation in the speaker's explanation?\r\nA. He remained safely distant and gave instructions without entering human suffering.\r\nB. He replaced the high priest only for those who kept the Levitical law.\r\nC. He became a military commander over earthly wars and not a source of salvation.\r\nD. He entered into suffering and death to bring many sons to glory and to bring believers into adoption as his brethren.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains Archegos as captain or fountainhead: Christ entered into salvation and brought believers into adoption as brethren.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [15:06]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 2:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+2%3A10/>\r\n\r\nQ67. Why, in the speaker's account of Hebrews 2, could neither an animal nor an angel atone for human sin?\r\nA. Human beings sinned, so salvation required a human representative; Christ shared flesh and blood and was without sin.\r\nB. Animals and angels lacked enough power to defeat earthly armies.\r\nC. Only the high priest could die, and Jesus was not from the priestly tribe.\r\nD. The Law required that the sacrifice be an angel rather than a human.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says goats cannot die for human sins, angels cannot die for them, and sinful humans cannot take another's place; Christ took flesh and was without sin.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [16:01]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+2/>\r\n\r\nQ68. What assurance does the speaker draw from Romans 5:12–17 about the contrast between Adam and Christ?\r\nA. Adam's offense and Christ's gift have identical effects on every person without distinction.\r\nB. As sin and death came through Adam, grace and righteousness come through Christ; God reckons believers in Christ, so their confidence rests on what Christ has done and they reign in life.\r\nC. Christ's gift makes believers' own flawless performance the basis of their acceptance.\r\nD. Romans 5 teaches that death entered through Christ and righteousness through Adam.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker connects Adam's representative offense with Christ's grace and gift of righteousness, says God reckons believers in Christ rather than qualifying them by their own faithfulness, and cites reigning in life through Jesus.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF MAN III — [17:50; 18:50; 19:59]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 5:12–17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A12%E2%80%9317/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ69. In the Emmaus-road account, what does Jesus' rebuke of the disciples as 'slow of heart' emphasize in the speaker's application?\r\nA. They needed to believe what Scripture had said about the Messiah's suffering and entrance into glory, rather than respond only from emotion.\r\nB. They should have ignored the Scriptures and relied on eyewitness reports alone.\r\nC. Their sadness proved that they did not love Jesus.\r\nD. They were rebuked because mourning a Christian is always wrong.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says there is reason to feel grief, but believers should remember Scripture's hope and that Christ was to suffer and enter his glory.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD I — [04:31; 08:35]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 24:25–27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A25%E2%80%9327/>\r\n\r\nQ70. What qualification does the speaker make about mourning a loved one who died in Christ?\r\nA. Christians should never feel grief when a faithful believer dies.\r\nB. Only the family may mourn; other believers must remain silent.\r\nC. The dead are expected to return from heaven shortly after their death.\r\nD. Mourning is allowed, but Christians should not mourn as those without hope because departure to be with Christ is better.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explicitly permits mourning while citing the instruction not to mourn without hope and the claim that to depart and be with Christ is far better.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD I — [04:31; 07:40]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Thessalonians 4:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+4%3A13/>; Philippians 1:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+1%3A23/>\r\n\r\nQ71. How does the speaker define a 'hermeneutical framework'?\r\nA. A list of moral rules taken from Old Testament stories.\r\nB. A method for memorizing the order of the books of the Bible.\r\nC. A way or lens for reading and interpreting the Bible.\r\nD. A creed that replaces interpretation of the biblical text.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker defines hermeneutics as the science of Bible interpretation and a hermeneutical framework as a way of reading the Bible.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD I — [08:35; 09:27]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 24:25–27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A25%E2%80%9327/>\r\n\r\nQ72. What interpretive lens does the speaker say Jesus used when expounding Moses and the prophets on the Emmaus road?\r\nA. He focused only on moral examples and historical drama.\r\nB. He showed how the Scriptures concerned himself, including prophetic and messianic references to Christ.\r\nC. He treated the Old Testament as unrelated to his own mission.\r\nD. He interpreted the prophets solely as predictions about the Roman Empire.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Jesus expounded the Scriptures concerning himself and describes a Christ-centered, messianic reading of the Old Testament.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD I — [09:27]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 24:25–27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A25%E2%80%9327/>\r\n\r\nQ73. In the speaker's explanation, what does 'beginning at Moses and all the prophets' encompass?\r\nA. The Old Testament, including the first five books written by Moses and the writings of the prophets.\r\nB. Only Genesis and the Psalms.\r\nC. The New Testament Gospels and Paul's letters.\r\nD. Only books written after Israel's exile.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker identifies Moses' books as Genesis through Deuteronomy and describes the Old Testament broadly as the Law and the Prophets.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD I — [11:04; 12:17]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+1/>\r\n\r\nQ74. What detail does the speaker cite to connect the opening of Genesis 1:1 with John 1:1?\r\nA. Both passages were originally written in Aramaic by Moses.\r\nB. John explicitly names Moses in the first sentence of his Gospel.\r\nC. In the Septuagint, the two passages begin with the same words, 'In the beginning.'\r\nD. Both passages describe the creation of the sun on the first day.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the Greek Septuagint and John 1:1 open with the same phrasing, directing readers back to Genesis.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD I — [14:49]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+1%3A1/>; John 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ75. What does the speaker say John 1:1 reveals about the Word?\r\nA. The Word was created after the world and later became God.\r\nB. The Word was only a spoken message and not a person.\r\nC. The Word was another name for the Spirit and was not present at creation.\r\nD. The Word was with God and was God, indicating both distinction and deity.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that the Word was with God, was God, and was present at the beginning.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD I — [15:41]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+1%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ76. How does John 1:3 add to the Genesis creation account in the speaker's explanation?\r\nA. It says the Father created without the Son's involvement.\r\nB. It says the Son began to exist after the physical creation was complete.\r\nC. It says all things were made through the Son, showing that creation was through him.\r\nD. It says the Spirit alone created the world and the Word arrived later.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker calls John 1:3 a behind-the-scenes account showing that God created all things through his Son.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD I — [16:40]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A3/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+1%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ77. How does the speaker identify the light in Genesis 1 that appeared before the sun was made?\r\nA. He identifies it with Christ, the creative light associated with the life in the Word.\r\nB. He identifies it as sunlight temporarily hidden behind clouds.\r\nC. He says Genesis is not describing light until the sun appears.\r\nD. He identifies it as the moon, which was created before the sun.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that the sun was made later and says the light of Genesis 1 was Christ, the creative light.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD I — [17:42; 18:47]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+1/> | Also cited in lesson segment: John 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1/>\r\n\r\nQ78. What parallel does the speaker draw between the light of physical creation and salvation?\r\nA. As the Son was involved in bringing light to physical creation, salvation through the Son brings light into spiritual darkness.\r\nB. Physical light is temporary, while spiritual darkness is permanent.\r\nC. Salvation repeats the creation of the sun and moon in believers' lives.\r\nD. The speaker says spiritual darkness can be overcome only by moral effort, not by the Son.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker applies the Genesis pattern to spiritual darkness and says salvation in the Son deals with it.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD I — [18:47]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+1/>; John 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1/>\r\n\r\nQ79. How does the speaker use John 3:19 to extend the Genesis light-and-darkness pattern?\r\nA. He says John 3:19 is about the physical creation of the sun.\r\nB. He says the verse teaches that darkness is stronger than the light of Christ.\r\nC. He says spiritual darkness is present in the world and that the light has come, while people may love darkness because their deeds are evil.\r\nD. He says the verse describes only the disciples' sadness after the crucifixion.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker connects John 3:19's light coming into the world with the physical darkness and light of Genesis.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD II — [00:15]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 3:18–19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A18%E2%80%9319/>\r\n\r\nQ80. In the speaker's explanation of 2 Corinthians 4:6, what does the light shining in believers' hearts provide?\r\nA. A physical light that replaces the sun.\r\nB. A sign that believers no longer need to read Scripture.\r\nC. A promise that believers will never encounter spiritual opposition.\r\nD. The light of the knowledge of God's glory in the face of Jesus.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker quotes 2 Corinthians 4:6 and says God has shone in hearts to give knowledge of his glory in Jesus.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD II — [01:02]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 2 Corinthians 4:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+4%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ81. According to the speaker, what does Colossians 1:13 say God has done for believers?\r\nA. Delivered them from the power of darkness and conveyed them into the kingdom of his Son.\r\nB. Delivered them from the kingdom of the Son into the power of darkness.\r\nC. Given them authority to create physical light by speaking.\r\nD. Removed the need for faith by making every person automatically a believer.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites Colossians 1:13 as describing deliverance from darkness and transfer into the Son's kingdom.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD II — [01:57]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Colossians 1:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+1%3A13/> | Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 4:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+4%3A6/>; Genesis 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+1/>; Ephesians 6:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+6%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ82. How does the speaker summarize the gospel's work using the phrase 'transport business'?\r\nA. It moves people physically from one country to another for mission work.\r\nB. It transports biblical manuscripts from the Old Testament to the New Testament.\r\nC. It refers to angels carrying believers directly to heaven during worship.\r\nD. It conveys people from darkness into light and from the power of darkness into the Son's kingdom.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses the phrase to describe the gospel's movement of people from darkness to light and into the kingdom of the Son.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD II — [03:09]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 6:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+6%3A12/>; Colossians 1:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+1%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ83. What apparent tension does the speaker address between John 1:18 and passages saying Moses spoke with God 'face to face'?\r\nA. He says John 1:18 was added later and does not belong in the Bible.\r\nB. He distinguishes mediated encounters described as face-to-face from literally seeing God's face.\r\nC. He says Moses never had any encounter with God.\r\nD. He says 'face to face' means Moses saw God's face but did not speak with him.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker distinguishes seeing God face-to-face through a medium from seeing God's face, which he says no one can do and live.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD II — [04:24]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 1:18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A18/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+1/>; Exodus 33:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+33%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ84. What example does the speaker use to clarify a mediated 'face-to-face' encounter?\r\nA. A person reading a letter without knowing who wrote it.\r\nB. A phone or video call, where people speak face-to-face through a medium without literally seeing each other's face in person.\r\nC. A person meeting another in a room but refusing to look at them.\r\nD. A dream in which the speaker says no words are exchanged.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker compares mediated encounters with switching from a phone call to a video call, while still communicating through a medium.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD II — [05:24]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Exodus 33:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+33%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ85. What distinction does the speaker draw between the Word's Old Testament appearances and the incarnation?\r\nA. The Word was a human man throughout the Old Testament and became an angel at the incarnation.\r\nB. The Word appeared only as an audible voice before becoming a physical object after the incarnation.\r\nC. The Word appeared in forms such as a flame, the angel of the Lord, or a human figure, but was not a man until he was born as a human in the incarnation.\r\nD. The speaker says the Word had no Old Testament appearances and first appeared at the resurrection.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker lists Old Testament manifestations including a burning bush, the angel of the Lord, and a visible human form, then distinguishes these from becoming flesh and being born as a proper man.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD II — [06:21; 14:14]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Exodus 3:2–6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+3%3A2%E2%80%936/>; Joshua 5:13–15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Joshua+5%3A13%E2%80%9315/>; John 1:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ86. How does the speaker describe the visitor who met Abraham while the other two visitors went to Sodom?\r\nA. He says one of the three was Yahweh, while the other two were angels who went to Sodom.\r\nB. He says all three were ordinary human travelers and none was identified as God.\r\nC. He says two were Yahweh and one was an angel who stayed in Sodom.\r\nD. He says the visitor was Samuel and the other two were prophets.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says one of the three visitors was Yahweh and the two others were angels who went into Sodom.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD II — [09:23]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 18:1–3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+18%3A1%E2%80%933/>; Genesis 19:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+19%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ87. What wording in Abraham's conversation does the speaker use to illustrate two Yahwehs?\r\nA. Abraham says he will send fire from two angels who remained in Sodom.\r\nB. The two angels tell Abraham that Yahweh has no authority over Sodom.\r\nC. Abraham says that the Lord will send rain from the stars.\r\nD. The Yahweh speaking with Abraham says he will rain fire from Yahweh on high.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker points to the statement that Yahweh will rain fire from Yahweh on high as an example of one Yahweh speaking about another.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD II — [09:23]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 19:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+19%3A24/>\r\n\r\nQ88. What does the speaker argue about the Old Testament phrase 'the Word of the Lord came' in the accounts of Abraham and Samuel?\r\nA. He says the phrase always meant an audible sound with no visible encounter.\r\nB. He says it refers only to a prophet's private interpretation of events.\r\nC. He presents the Word of the Lord as a person encountered, noting that the Lord came and stood by Samuel.\r\nD. He says the phrase was used only after the incarnation.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker points to Genesis 15 and 1 Samuel 3:10, emphasizing the Word of the Lord as an encountered person and that the Lord came and stood.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD II — [10:30; 12:15; 13:11]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+15/>; 1 Samuel 3:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Samuel+3%3A10/>\r\n\r\nQ89. How does the speaker connect Jesus' words 'Before Abraham was, I am' with the Word of the Lord in earlier biblical accounts?\r\nA. He says Jesus meant that he had personally known and interacted with Abraham before Abraham existed in Jesus' earthly lifetime.\r\nB. He says Jesus was speaking only about a future role assigned to him by God.\r\nC. He says the phrase means Abraham was older than Jesus in every sense.\r\nD. He says Jesus was quoting Samuel and denying that he had met Abraham.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Jesus was not speaking metaphorically and identifies him with the Word of the Lord who appeared to Abraham.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD II — [13:11]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 8:56–58 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+8%3A56%E2%80%9358/>\r\n\r\nQ90. What interpretive caution does the speaker give about phrases such as 'Son of God,' 'firstborn,' and 'begotten Son'?\r\nA. Their meaning must be understood from biblical usage and context, not assumed from a modern or literal-sounding phrase.\r\nB. They always mean that God physically gave birth to Jesus.\r\nC. They have one identical meaning in every biblical passage.\r\nD. They should be interpreted only by modern Western usage.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says biblical terms must be understood in context and warns against assuming that sonship means God physically gave birth to Jesus.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD III — [08:43; 09:46; 10:54]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 1:35 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+1%3A35/>\r\n\r\nQ91. What kind of sonship does the speaker say God appointed for Solomon in 1 Chronicles 28:6?\r\nA. A sonship beginning from Solomon's virgin birth.\r\nB. An eternal sonship that made Solomon equal with God.\r\nC. A royal sonship associated with being chosen to build God's house and rule as David's heir.\r\nD. A sonship meaning that Solomon was the only Israelite called God's child.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker distinguishes Solomon's general identity as a Jew from the royal sonship associated with his appointment and throne.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD III — [11:54]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Chronicles 28:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Chronicles+28%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ92. According to the speaker, why is Jesus called the Son of God in relation to his virgin birth?\r\nA. No human man was involved in his conception, so the speaker relates this sonship to his birth from a virgin.\r\nB. He says Mary was not involved in Jesus' birth.\r\nC. He says the title refers only to Jesus' resurrection and not his birth.\r\nD. He says the title means Jesus was adopted by Joseph after his birth.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites the angel's explanation that the Holy Spirit would overshadow Mary and says no human male was responsible for Jesus' procreation.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD III — [14:37]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+1/>; Luke 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+3/>\r\n\r\nQ93. What example from Luke's genealogy does the speaker use when explaining the phrase 'son of God' in relation to origin?\r\nA. Solomon is called the son of God because he was born of a virgin.\r\nB. David is called the son of God because he built the temple.\r\nC. Samuel is called the son of God because he was raised in the temple.\r\nD. Adam is called the son of God even though he had no human father.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker points to Luke's genealogy ending with Adam, who is identified as the son of God, and relates that to having no human father.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD III — [14:37]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 3:38 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+3%3A38/>\r\n\r\nQ94. What two conditions does the speaker associate with royal sonship in the line of David?\r\nA. Being a prophet and building the temple in Jerusalem.\r\nB. Belonging to David's lineage and ascending to the throne.\r\nC. Being born in Bethlehem and serving as a priest.\r\nD. Being the firstborn son and defeating the Philistines.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says royal sonship requires being an heir from David's line and ascending the throne.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD III — [16:34]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 1:32–33 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+1%3A32%E2%80%9333/>\r\n\r\nQ95. How does the speaker explain the declaration that Jesus was 'Son of God' by the resurrection?\r\nA. In the royal-sonship sense, Jesus' resurrection and enthronement fulfill the promise of David's heir reigning without end.\r\nB. It means Jesus first became God's Son in the moment of his virgin conception.\r\nC. It means Jesus stopped being the Son after his resurrection.\r\nD. It means the resurrection changed Jesus from human to angel.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker connects Romans 1:4 and Hebrews 1 with Jesus' resurrection and the fulfillment of the Davidic throne promise.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD III — [18:35]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 1:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+1%3A4/>; Hebrews 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1/>\r\n\r\nQ96. What does the speaker mean by calling Jesus the Eternal Son?\r\nA. Jesus became the Son only after the Council of Nicaea.\r\nB. Jesus was created as the first angel before becoming human.\r\nC. Jesus became the Son only when he ascended to David's throne.\r\nD. Jesus did not begin to be the Son at the incarnation; he has eternally existed as Son and is described as eternally generated from the Father.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Jesus has always been the Son, eternally existing and eternally generated from the Father, rather than becoming Son at the incarnation.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD III — [19:21]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 1:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ97. Why does the speaker argue that evidence for Christ's deity should also be shown from the Old Testament?\r\nA. Because the speaker says the New Testament contains no references to Jesus' deity.\r\nB. Because the Old Testament was written after the Gospels.\r\nC. Because only the Old Testament can describe the Son as divine.\r\nD. To answer claims that the doctrine arose only at Nicaea or that later New Testament writings about Jesus were altered.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker raises claims about Nicaea and alleged alteration of John and says the Old Testament appearances of the Word provide evidence.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD III — [20:28]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Proverbs 30:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Proverbs+30%3A4/>\r\n\r\nQ98. What question does Proverbs 30:4 pose that the speaker highlights in support of the Son's existence?\r\nA. After describing God's acts in creation, it asks, 'What is his name, and what is his Son's name, if you know?'\r\nB. It says the Son's name is hidden and cannot be asked.\r\nC. It identifies Solomon as the one who gathered the wind and established the earth.\r\nD. It says the Son was created after the earth was established.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker highlights Proverbs 30:4's questions about who controls creation and what is his Son's name.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD III — [21:27]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Proverbs 30:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Proverbs+30%3A4/>\r\n\r\nQ99. What happens after the Son of Man comes before the Ancient of Days in Daniel 7:9–14, according to the speaker?\r\nA. The Son of Man is the only figure in the vision and receives no kingdom.\r\nB. The Ancient of Days gives the Son of Man a temporary earthly throne that is later destroyed.\r\nC. There are thrones in the vision; the Son of Man comes to the Ancient of Days and receives everlasting dominion and a kingdom served by all peoples.\r\nD. The vision describes two angels sharing a throne and ruling only Israel.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker highlights the plural thrones, the Ancient of Days, and the Son of Man receiving dominion and an everlasting kingdom.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD III — [22:15]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Daniel 7:9–14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Daniel+7%3A9%E2%80%9314/>\r\n\r\nQ100. Why does the speaker say Jesus' answer before the high priest was understood as a claim to deity?\r\nA. Jesus denied being the Messiah and asked the high priest to release him.\r\nB. Jesus said they would see the Son of Man coming in the clouds, invoking Daniel 7; the high priest reacted by tearing his clothes.\r\nC. Jesus cited Proverbs 30:4 to claim he was Solomon's descendant only.\r\nD. Jesus called himself a prophet, which the high priest considered a political crime.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Jesus' words in Mark 14:62 invoked Daniel 7 and were understood by the high priest as a divine claim.\r\nREF: JESUS- SON OF GOD III — [23:41; 25:26]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Daniel 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Daniel+7/>; Mark 14:62 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+14%3A62/>\n\nDECK: Discipleship Experience (Section 5) Christian Conduct\r\n\r\nQ1. Why did Paul pray that the believers in Ephesus would receive the spirit of wisdom and revelation in the knowledge of Him?\r\nA. Because they needed to know what was theirs in Christ in order to take full advantage of those provisions.\r\nB. Because they had faith in Christ but had not yet received the spiritual blessings available to those who believe.\r\nC. Because their love unto all the saints showed that they were growing, but they still needed to become faithful in Christ Jesus.\r\nD. Because revelation knowledge was what would give them the inheritance that had not yet been provided in Christ.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says Paul prayed for their understanding because knowing what was theirs in Christ enabled them to benefit from those provisions.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [02:25]–[03:17] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 1:17–18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A17%E2%80%9318/>\r\n\r\nQ2. What does Jeremiah 31 specifically establish about the new covenant?\r\nA. It would renew the covenant God made with their fathers while giving them greater ability to obey it.\r\nB. It would add new instructions to the covenant that was made when God brought their fathers out of Egypt.\r\nC. It is not according to the covenant God made with their fathers.\r\nD. It would preserve the old covenant while placing its requirements in their hearts.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson emphasizes that Jeremiah describes a new covenant, not one identical to the covenant made with the fathers.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [11:09]–[12:53] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Jeremiah 31 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+31/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+8/>\r\n\r\nQ3. According to Romans 6:6 as taught, what happened to the old man?\r\nA. He was gradually weakened as the believer learned more about Christ.\r\nB. He was crucified with Christ.\r\nC. He was removed when the believer began to mortify the deeds of the body.\r\nD. He remains present but no longer has any spiritual relevance.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson teaches that the old man was crucified with Christ.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [25:54]–[28:20] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 6:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ4. A believer says, \"This temptation strengthened me, so God sent it for my growth.\" What's your response based on the lesson?\r\nA. Temptation can produce growth, but only when God directly allows it for that purpose.\r\nB. God can use what the enemy meant for evil without being the one who tempted the believer.\r\nC. If temptation produces growth, the source is no longer important.\r\nD. God may send temptation when He knows the believer is ready to turn it into spiritual growth.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson distinguishes God using a temptation for good from God being the one who tempts.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS III — [06:06]–[07:47] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: James 1:2–3, 12–13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A2%E2%80%933%2C+12%E2%80%9313/>\r\n\r\nQ5. A believer keeps falling in the same environment but says, \"Next time I will try harder.\" What needs attention?\r\nA. His determination, because repeated failure shows that his desire has not become strong enough.\r\nB. His structure, because good intention in the same system may keep producing the same outcome.\r\nC. His spiritual experience, because practical structures cannot replace the work of the Spirit.\r\nD. His accountability, because another person can change him.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson recommends changing the structure or environment rather than relying only on stronger determination.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [01:08]–[04:06] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 13:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+13%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ6. Which accountability response conflicts most with Galatians 6:1 as used in the Lesson?\r\nA. Noticing a pattern the person has become unable to see.\r\nB. Intervening when the person insists that nothing is wrong.\r\nC. Helping the person return to the right path with gentleness.\r\nD. Using the person's shame to make sure he does not repeat the behaviour.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson calls for gentle restoration rather than shaming someone as a corrective response.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [07:57]–[10:21] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Galatians 6:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+6%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ7. How did the Lesson explain the relationship between \"you have put off the old man\" and the instruction to \"put off\" the old man?\r\nA. The two passages refer to two different old men operating at different stages of Christianity.\r\nB. The old man has been put off as a spiritual provision, and the believer must partner by faith to see it in his life.\r\nC. The old man is only considered put off after the believer has successfully stopped the deeds associated with him.\r\nD. The instruction to put off the old man shows that the statement \"you have put off\" is describing a future reality.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson distinguishes the provision in Christ from the believer’s practical response of faith.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [06:30]–[10:02] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Colossians 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+3/>; Ephesians 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+4/>; Jeremiah 31 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+31/>\r\n\r\nQ8. What does Romans 7:22–23 demonstrate about the problem under the law?\r\nA. The person did not delight in the law of God and therefore resisted it.\r\nB. The law was not spiritual enough to overcome the desires of the flesh.\r\nC. Another law in his members warred against the law of his mind.\r\nD. The person understood the law but deliberately chose not to obey it.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson explains that an inner mechanism opposed the desire to follow God’s law.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [16:45]–[17:59] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 7:22–23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+7%3A22%E2%80%9323/>\r\n\r\nQ9. According to the Lesson, what does Romans 8:14 mean in its immediate context?\r\nA. Those who are led by the Spirit will become sons of God.\r\nB. Those who are sons of God are led by the Spirit of God.\r\nC. Those who want to become sons must first learn to follow the Spirit.\r\nD. Those who are spiritually mature are led by the Spirit, unlike other Christians.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson reads Romans 8:14 as describing the Spirit’s leading as characteristic of God’s sons.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [35:28]–[37:51] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 8:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ10. What does the statement \"I'm kept, so I keep\" communicate?\r\nA. The believer keeps himself through discipline while the Spirit watches over his progress.\r\nB. The Spirit keeps the believer, and the believer responds by yielding to the living of the Spirit.\r\nC. The believer does not need to keep his body because the Spirit has already done everything.\r\nD. The believer must keep his body before the Spirit can work in him.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson describes keeping the body as a partnership: the believer yields to the Spirit and acts accordingly.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [40:09]–[41:11] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 8:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A11/>; Colossians 3:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+3%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ11. A mature believer says, \"I know my limits, so I can stay around this temptation.\" What does the Lesson challenge?\r\nA. Self-assessment should not replace God's wisdom; when you can run, you should run.\r\nB. Spiritual maturity should eventually make deliberate boundaries unnecessary.\r\nC. The believer should remain in the situation long enough to prove that his discipline is genuine.\r\nD. Exposure becomes dangerous only when temptation has already become strong.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson warns against overconfidence and advises avoiding unnecessary exposure when possible.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [02:01]–[03:09] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Timothy 2:22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+2%3A22/>\r\n\r\nQ12. Why does the lesson compare Esau's hunger with Jesus' forty-day fast?\r\nA. To show that Esau lacked the biblical knowledge needed to recognise the seriousness of his decision.\r\nB. To show that hunger weakens a person's ability to distinguish right from wrong.\r\nC. To show that fasting gives a believer greater resistance to temptation than ordinary discipline can provide.\r\nD. To show that the condition a person is in can affect his vulnerability so you must train for when defenses are down.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The comparison highlights that vulnerability can depend on a person’s condition, so preparation matters before defenses are weakened.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [03:47]–[06:12] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 25:29–34 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+25%3A29%E2%80%9334/>; Matthew 4:1–4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+4%3A1%E2%80%934/>\r\n\r\nQ13. A believer breaks one destructive habit but soon begins depending heavily on another source of comfort. What does the Lesson suggest?\r\nA. The believer needs stronger discipline because changing the source of pleasure is still a failure of restraint.\r\nB. The new source has simply replaced the old temptation, showing that the first habit was never truly overcome.\r\nC. The believer should remove the new source if it is becoming excessive, regardless of whether it is legitimate.\r\nD. He may have changed the behaviour without addressing where he is looking for satisfaction.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson directs the believer to seek lasting satisfaction in God rather than relying on other sources of comfort.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [02:02]–[03:47] and [06:09]–[06:59] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Jeremiah 2:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+2%3A13/>; John 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+4/>\r\n\r\nQ14. Which best illustrates \"wanting good things badly\"?\r\nA. A legitimate thing becomes excessive because the desire for it moves beyond its proper place.\r\nB. A legitimate thing becomes dangerous simply because the believer enjoys it regularly.\r\nC. A believer deliberately avoids a legitimate thing because he fears that enjoyment could become sinful.\r\nD. A believer exercises restraint with a legitimate thing because he recognises that all pleasure must eventually be denied.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson explains that a legitimate good, such as food, can become sinful when pursued excessively.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [06:12]–[07:02] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Deuteronomy 8:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+8%3A3/>; Matthew 4:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+4%3A4/>\r\n\r\nQ15. What should be the believer's relationship with his mind and body?\r\nA. His mind and body determine his spiritual identity but should gradually be brought under control.\r\nB. His mind and body are irrelevant because only his spirit matters.\r\nC. He is not his mind or his body therefore they are under control and subjected to the will of God.\r\nD. His body is naturally controlled by the Spirit, while his mind must be controlled through human discipline.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson distinguishes the believer from the mind and body, and says they are to be brought under God’s will.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [48:47]–[50:00] and [57:13]–[58:47] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+12/>\r\n\r\nQ16. According to the Lesson, what does a believer demonstrate when he walks in victory over sin?\r\nA. That his personal discipline has become stronger than his previous habits.\r\nB. That he has attained a higher level of Christianity.\r\nC. That the resurrection of Jesus Christ has practical relevance in his life.\r\nD. That his works have become sufficient for his acceptance before God.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson connects victory over sin with the practical relevance of Christ’s resurrection.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [34:01]–[35:28] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 6:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6%3A4/>; Romans 8:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ17. Why can the believer \"mortify\" his members according to the Lesson?\r\nA. Because mortification is what makes him dead to sin.\r\nB. Because the practical action of mortifying follows the knowledge.\r\nC. Because mortification causes the old man to become crucified with Christ.\r\nD. Because the believer's ability to mortify depends on how strongly he feels the desire to resist sin.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says the believer’s practical action follows the spiritual reality of being dead to sin.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [41:11]–[43:21] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 8:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A11/>; Colossians 3:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+3%3A1/>; Romans 8:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ18. According to 2 Peter 1, why should the believer \"give all diligence\" if God has already given all things that pertain to life and godliness?\r\nA. Because God's provision becomes effective only after the believer adds his own works to it.\r\nB. Because the fact that God has already provided it is the very reason the believer can give all diligence.\r\nC. Because the believer must complete what God began by supplying what was missing from the provision.\r\nD. Because escaping the corruption in the world is dependent on human effort.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents God’s provision as the reason believers can diligently live it out, not something their effort must complete.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [45:37]–[47:48] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 2 Peter 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Peter+1/>\r\n\r\nQ19. Why does the Lesson connect the revelation of grace with Romans 12?\r\nA. Grace is meant to move the believer from what he has received into a practical response of presenting himself to God.\r\nB. Grace first establishes doctrine, while presenting yourself to God introduces the separate discipline of Christian living.\r\nC. Romans 12 shows that practical obedience becomes necessary when grace alone has not produced enough spiritual maturity.\r\nD. The revelation of grace reduces the need for discipline because a believer who understands grace should naturally become transformed.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson links the teaching of grace to the practical response of presenting one’s body to God.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [00:16]–[01:05] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Titus 2:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Titus+2%3A11/> | Added from online research: Romans 12:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+12%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ20. Why can a recurring temptation become a \"ticket to the next level\"?\r\nA. Successfully resisting repeated temptation demonstrates readiness for greater ministry responsibility.\r\nB. Repeated temptation exposes weaknesses that, once removed, automatically produce spiritual promotion.\r\nC. The presence of recurring temptation shows that God is preparing the believer for greater spiritual authority.\r\nD. Overcoming it through the Word can strengthen the believer's capacity.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson teaches that overcoming temptation through the Word can strengthen the believer and contribute to spiritual growth.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS III — [04:31]–[06:54] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: James 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A3/>; James 1:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ21. A believer notices that certain movies repeatedly stir desires she later struggles to control. She stops watching them. What is this?\r\nA. Avoiding temptation because mature believers should eventually be able to handle any environment.\r\nB. Legalism, unless the content itself is clearly sinful.\r\nC. A temporary measure that may protect her but does not actually train spiritual discipline.\r\nD. Controlling exposure and building a structure that supports the outcome she wants.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson advises limiting exposure and using structures that support the desired outcome.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [01:08]–[04:06] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Timothy 2:22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+2%3A22/>\r\n\r\nQ22. Someone finds it difficult to give money even when he knows he should. From the Lesson, why can practising generosity become part of his training?\r\nA. The discomfort of letting something go can become an opportunity to practise putting the flesh under.\r\nB. His unwillingness to give reveals that the root of his other struggles is ultimately a lack of faith.\r\nC. Giving is more powerful than all other disciplines because financial discipline controls other areas of the flesh.\r\nD. His money must be brought under control first because attachment to possessions is the major cause of other forms of fleshly behaviour.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents giving, even when it feels difficult, as a way to practise putting the flesh under.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [10:47]–[11:30] (approximate range)\r\n\r\nQ23. Which response best reflects \"zoom out and zoom in\"?\r\nA. Focus on the wider discipline first, then return to the specific struggle once the broader weakness has been corrected.\r\nB. Focus on the specific struggle because broader discipline can distract from the actual issue being addressed.\r\nC. Replace the specific exercise with general spiritual disciplines because the specific behaviour is only a symptom.\r\nD. Address the specific struggle while examining where the same lack of discipline may be appearing elsewhere.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson pairs targeted work on a specific struggle with attention to discipline in other areas.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [08:54]–[12:26] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Galatians 5:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+5%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ24. A believer prays in tongues, casts out demons, and attends church faithfully, yet repeatedly loses his temper. What does the Lesson tell us about this?\r\nA. Those activities have not produced genuine spiritual maturity because anger is still there\r\nB. He may be exercising spiritually without deliberately training the particular area where he is weak.\r\nC. He needs to replace those activities with anger-focused exercises because general disciplines cannot address specific struggles.\r\nD. His anger shows that his spiritual activities have become disconnected from the practical demands of Christian living.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says general spiritual activity may not address a specific area that needs targeted training.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [01:05]–[02:54] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Titus 2:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Titus+2%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ25. A believer is targeting a sexual habit and begins practising restraint with food, sleep and social media. Why can this help based on the Lesson?\r\nA. These areas create the sexual temptation, so removing them removes the source of the struggle.\r\nB. These pleasures are forms of fleshly gratification that must be brought under control before sexual discipline can develop.\r\nC. Restraining the flesh in other areas can train the general capacity needed in the targeted area.\r\nD. The believer is indirectly addressing the sexual habit because all forms of lack of discipline have the same immediate cause.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents self-restraint in other areas as general training that can strengthen discipline in a targeted struggle.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [09:42]–[10:47] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 9:25–27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+9%3A25%E2%80%9327/>\r\n\r\nQ26. A believer understands grace, defends it well, and listens to Lesson on it regularly, but his response to lusts doesn't change. What is he missing?\r\nA. A stronger emphasis on spiritual gifts, since grace should first produce spiritual power.\r\nB. A distinction between grace and holiness, since change comes later with growth.\r\nC. More revelation, because practical obedience cannot be expected until the doctrine is fully understood.\r\nD. The practical response grace is meant to produce which is denying ungodliness.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says the revelation of grace is meant to lead to a practical response, including denying ungodliness and worldly lusts.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [00:16]–[01:05] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Titus 2:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Titus+2%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ27. According to the Lesson, what does the grace message include beyond the fact that sins are forgiven and not imputed?\r\nA. That believers no longer need to be concerned about their conduct because grace covers every failure.\r\nB. That the old man has been dealt with and believers are learning to walk consistently with the provision of Christ.\r\nC. That believers should focus primarily on avoiding condemnation when they fall.\r\nD. That practical holiness should be separated from the message of grace so that grace remains the focus.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson includes both the old man being dealt with and learning to live consistently with Christ’s provision in its account of grace.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [53:22]–[55:07] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5/>; Romans 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6/>\r\n\r\nQ28. Two believers pray, fast and study the Word. One struggles with anger; the other with pornography. What does the gym analogy require them to recognise?\r\nA. The same spiritual exercises should eventually produce the same results, provided they are practised consistently.\r\nB. Different struggles mainly require different levels of revelation.\r\nC. Exercising spiritually does not mean the particular area that needs strengthening is being exercised.\r\nD. Their common disciplines are insufficient because targeted struggles should be addressed separately from normal Christian disciplines.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says general spiritual activity does not necessarily train the particular area where someone is struggling.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [01:05]–[02:54] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Titus 2:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Titus+2%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ29. From the Lesson, why practise discipline in areas where you are not currently struggling?\r\nA. Because learning to say \"no\" in ordinary areas can strengthen the capacity to say \"no\" when a greater temptation comes.\r\nB. Because every area without strict control eventually becomes a spiritual weakness.\r\nC. Because discipline in smaller matters gives a believer authority over stronger temptations.\r\nD. Because the more disciplined a believer becomes generally, the less likely he is to experience serious temptation.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents restraint in ordinary areas as training for situations when a person is more vulnerable.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [06:12]–[07:58] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Galatians 5:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+5%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ30. What best describes the accountability relationship taught in the Lesson?\r\nA. Someone close enough to confront the believer, even if the confrontation creates shame that motivates change.\r\nB. Someone with the same struggle who can therefore understand the believer's experience without needing to challenge him strongly.\r\nC. Someone able to recognise when the believer is in trouble and respond firmly, respectfully and gently.\r\nD. Someone who checks the believer's behaviour closely enough to prevent him from making another mistake.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson recommends someone who can recognize trouble and provide respectful, gentle accountability.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [08:46]–[10:21] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Galatians 6:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+6%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ31. In Romans 1:16, how does the speaker describe what the gospel is?\r\nA. A set of moral instructions that guide believers toward salvation\r\nB. A message of forgiveness that leaves the believer's experience unchanged\r\nC. The power of God unto salvation, demonstrated in men who believe\r\nD. A covenant of rules that restrains people who already love God\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reads Romans 1:16 and says the gospel is the power of God unto salvation, the power of God demonstrated in men.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [00:17]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 1:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+1%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ32. What detail does the speaker use to show that the Ephesians Paul prayed for were already born again?\r\nA. Paul says they had completed a season of fasting before he wrote to them\r\nB. Paul says they had already been ordained to lead the church at Ephesus\r\nC. Paul addresses them as saints, faithful in Christ Jesus, and mentions their faith and love\r\nD. Paul says they had already given up their former habits before he prayed\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker points to how the letter opens (to the saints at Ephesus and the faithful in Christ Jesus) and to Paul's thanks after hearing of their faith and love for all the saints.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [01:06; 01:45]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 1:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ33. According to the speaker, what is it that influences a believer's experience?\r\nA. Spiritual facts about who he is in Christ\r\nB. The intensity of his emotions during worship\r\nC. The number of rules he is able to keep\r\nD. The opinions of mature believers around him\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says spiritual facts about who you are in Christ are what influence your experience, which is why knowledge is the first important task.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [03:17]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 1:17–18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A17%E2%80%9318/>\r\n\r\nQ34. A believer hears 'you are dead to sin' but points to his failures last night. How does the speaker say he should respond?\r\nA. Take it into his spirit as revelation knowledge and adjust his experience to the Word\r\nB. Treat the statement as a goal he will reach once his experience has improved\r\nC. Accept it as head knowledge while trusting his experience as the better guide\r\nD. Qualify the statement until it fits what he actually sees in his own life\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker ranks the Word above experience, says to get it into the spirit (not just head knowledge), and says to adjust your experience to the Word.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [04:23; 05:33]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 6:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ35. What reasoning does the speaker give for why a Christian cannot have a demon?\r\nA. Demons can only operate where believers have stopped praying\r\nB. Demons lose their hold on a believer once he has fasted\r\nC. A person indwelt by the Holy Ghost cannot be cohabited by a demon\r\nD. Demons cannot enter anyone who has been baptized in water\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Recalling Pastor Chris's sermon, the speaker asks how a demon can cohabit a person who has the Holy Ghost indwelling the body.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [04:23; 05:33]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Corinthians 6:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+6%3A19/>; Romans 8:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ36. How does the speaker define 'that old man'?\r\nA. An outside spirit that attacks believers who have neglected their prayer life\r\nB. A list of sinful deeds that a believer must give up one by one in turn\r\nC. The mechanism in fallen man that draws him irresistibly to consistent immorality\r\nD. A stage of immaturity that every Christian eventually outgrows with time\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker describes the old man as the mechanism in the man who is not in Christ that draws him irresistibly to consistent immorality.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [06:30]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Colossians 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+3/>; Ephesians 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+4/>\r\n\r\nQ37. From Colossians 3:9, what question does the speaker raise about the statement 'ye have put off the old man'?\r\nA. If the mechanism behind lying has been put off, why must believers still be told not to lie?\r\nB. If the old man was put off, why does Paul mention only lying and not other sins?\r\nC. If believers must still avoid lying, why did Paul address them as saints?\r\nD. If the old man was put off, why does Paul say this only to the Colossians?\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker asks why Paul tells people not to lie if the mechanism responsible for lying has been put off, since it should be automatic.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [07:58]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Colossians 3:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+3%3A9/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+4/>\r\n\r\nQ38. According to E.W. Kenyon, as cited by the speaker, what two kinds of realities exist in God's provision in Christ?\r\nA. Natural and supernatural realities\r\nB. Positional and conditional realities\r\nC. Legal and vital realities\r\nD. Past and prophetic realities\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites E.W. Kenyon: the provision of God in Christ has legal and vital realities, spiritual facts one is meant to make his experience.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [07:58]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Colossians 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+3/>; Ephesians 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+4/>\r\n\r\nQ39. What two points does the speaker say he will cover in the message?\r\nA. Spiritual facts about victory over sin, then how to live consistently with them\r\nB. The history of the old covenant, then the order of the Ten Commandments given\r\nC. How to receive the Holy Spirit, then how to use the various spiritual gifts\r\nD. The meaning of grace, then the ways a believer can earn God's continued favor\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker states two points: spiritual facts about victory over sin, and how to live in a manner consistent with that provision.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [08:55]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Colossians 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+3/>\r\n\r\nQ40. What point does the speaker make with the Lagos illustration about the new law on bikes?\r\nA. A new law only matters where the authorities are present to enforce it\r\nB. Old and new laws can be kept together if the new one is stricter\r\nC. People may choose whichever law suits their situation best\r\nD. If a new law is meant to replace the old, you cannot keep both\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Using the Lagos bike law, the speaker argues that a new covenant meant to replace the old cannot be kept alongside it.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [12:53]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Jeremiah 31 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+31/>; Hebrews 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+8/>\r\n\r\nQ41. In explaining Hebrews 8, where does the speaker place the fault that made a new covenant necessary?\r\nA. With the priests, who failed to teach the law correctly\r\nB. With the law, because its commands were unclear to the people\r\nC. With the nation's leaders, who changed its terms over time\r\nD. With the people, who did not continue in the covenant\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says God found fault with the people (the law did not make anyone righteous), because they continued not in His covenant.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [14:14; 15:27]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+8/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Jeremiah 31 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+31/>; Romans 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+7/>\r\n\r\nQ42. What does the speaker conclude from Jeremiah's statement that Israel 'continued not' in the covenant?\r\nA. It could not help them continue in it, and righteousness is not attained by the law\r\nB. The covenant was too long for the people to remember and obey faithfully over the years\r\nC. Israel could keep the covenant only with the continued help of the prophets and priests\r\nD. The covenant was withdrawn because it was too lenient with sin and rebellion\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the old covenant could not help them continue in it and that you can never attain a righteous status by doing the law.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [15:27]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Jeremiah 31 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+31/>; Hebrews 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+8/>; Romans 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+7/>\r\n\r\nQ43. Why does the speaker quote 'I delight in the law of God' when explaining why Israel broke the old covenant?\r\nA. To show that his delight in the law was enough to make him keep it\r\nB. To show that delight in the law became possible only after the new covenant\r\nC. To show that lack of interest in keeping the law was not the problem\r\nD. To show that his interest in the law was the cause of his captivity\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses Romans 7:22 to answer those who say people broke the law because they did not want to keep it: interest was not the problem.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [16:45]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 7:22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+7%3A22/>\r\n\r\nQ44. What point does the speaker make with the illustration of a dog that discovers barking is a sin?\r\nA. Laws can restrain a disposition but cannot change the nature behind it\r\nB. Animals and people are judged by different standards of sin\r\nC. A law becomes effective once its penalty is clearly announced\r\nD. Sin depends on whether a creature knows that the law exists\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the best laws can do is restrain people who already have a disposition for evil, and they cannot change them.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [17:59]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 7:22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+7%3A22/>\r\n\r\nQ45. From Romans 7:14, what does the speaker conclude about the law and the real problem?\r\nA. The law is good but was only meant for an earlier generation\r\nB. The law is spiritual and good; the problem is an inner mechanism in man\r\nC. The law is flawed because it lacked the power to be kept\r\nD. The law is spiritual but its rules were too many to follow\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes Paul calls the law spiritual and good, while the man is carnal and sold under sin, so the real problem is an inner mechanism.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [19:19]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 7:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+7%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ46. Which wilderness episode does the speaker use to show Israel already had a disposition toward what the commandments forbade?\r\nA. Israelites turning away from Moses at the Red Sea\r\nB. Hungry Israelites picking up stones to kill Moses\r\nC. Israelites forgetting the commandments soon after leaving Egypt\r\nD. Israelites refusing to follow Moses into the wilderness at all\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that, though 'thou shalt not kill' was given, hungry Israelites in the wilderness picked up stones to kill Moses.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [20:36]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Exodus 17:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+17%3A4/>\r\n\r\nQ47. How does the speaker describe the new covenant in Jeremiah 31?\r\nA. Not regulations but a regulator: an inner mechanism, a new heart\r\nB. A tougher set of regulations that reaches even the desires\r\nC. The old law restated with stronger penalties for disobedience\r\nD. A promise of forgiveness that leaves the heart unchanged\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the new covenant is not new rules but a regulator, an inner mechanism: the work of the Spirit and a new heart.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [22:35; 23:21]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Jeremiah 31:33 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+31%3A33/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Ezekiel 36 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ezekiel+36/>; Romans 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8/>\r\n\r\nQ48. What does the speaker take from Ezekiel 36:26 about how God brings about obedience?\r\nA. He strengthens the stony heart with clearer written instructions from His law\r\nB. He replaces the stony heart and puts His Spirit within to cause obedience\r\nC. He teaches the people to keep His judgments through their own steady effort\r\nD. He sends the prophets to remind the people of His statutes each year\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reads Ezekiel 36:26: the stony heart is taken out and God puts His Spirit within to cause them to walk in His statutes.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [22:35]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Ezekiel 36:26 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ezekiel+36%3A26/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Jeremiah 31:33 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+31%3A33/>\r\n\r\nQ49. What does the speaker say Romans 8:2 announces?\r\nA. The law of Moses has been made easier for the believer to keep with practice\r\nB. The law of sin and death is now restrained by a stricter set of commands\r\nC. The believer is freed from sin's penalty but not yet from its power in life\r\nD. A new law, the law of the Spirit of life, frees the believer from sin and death\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says there is a new law at work within, the law of the Spirit of life in Christ Jesus, that frees from the law of sin and death.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [24:43]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 8:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ50. In Romans 8:3, how does the speaker say the new law at work in the believer came about?\r\nA. God strengthened the flesh so that it could keep the law\r\nB. God condemned sin in the flesh through Christ on the cross\r\nC. Christ taught a higher standard that the flesh now follows\r\nD. God replaced the law with a gentler set of expectations\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that God in Christ condemned sin in the flesh: as the nails pierced Christ, the old man was nailed to the cross.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [25:54]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 8:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A3/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6/>\r\n\r\nQ51. What does the speaker say about what Romans 6 explains?\r\nA. It is a doctrinal truth that is complete once the believer understands it\r\nB. It describes a goal that believers reach only at the end of their lives\r\nC. It is mainly about the emotions that believers feel at their baptism\r\nD. It must find practical reality in life and never remain only theory\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says it is a spiritual reality he expects you to take advantage of, and that it finds practical reality in your life and must never be left as theory.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [28:20; 29:30]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+2/>\r\n\r\nQ52. According to the speaker, which three areas does the fact that believers are seated with Christ have relevance for?\r\nA. Justification, spiritual authority, and sanctification\r\nB. Justification, prophecy, and the gift of healing\r\nC. Sanctification, ordination, and the grace of giving\r\nD. Spiritual authority, worship, and material prosperity\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says being seated speaks of justification (God at rest with you), spiritual authority over principalities and powers, and sanctification.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [31:41; 32:39]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 6:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+6%3A12/>; Romans 6:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6%3A4/> | Added from online research: Ephesians 2:4–6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+2%3A4%E2%80%936/>\r\n\r\nQ53. How does the speaker say Romans 8:14 is commonly misused?\r\nA. It is used to claim that spiritual leading removes the need for Scripture\r\nB. It is applied only to decisions about marriage and relocation\r\nC. It is applied only to decisions such as choosing between two jobs\r\nD. It is used to excuse believers who ignore wise counsel\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the verse is popular but abused, used only for job decisions, whereas it has a more immediate context.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [35:28]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 8:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ54. What does the speaker emphasize about the wording of Romans 8:12?\r\nA. Believers owe nothing to anyone, since the flesh has already been removed\r\nB. Believers are debtors to the law until they have grown to maturity\r\nC. Believers are debtors, but not to the flesh, since a new mechanism directs them\r\nD. Believers' debt to the flesh grows smaller as they continue to grow\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes Paul did not say believers are not debtors; they are debtors, but not to the flesh, because of a new mechanism of the Spirit.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [37:07]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 8:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A12/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 8:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A14/>; Romans 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6/>\r\n\r\nQ55. When Paul saw Christians not bearing fruit consistent with Christ's provision, how does the speaker say he responded?\r\nA. With knowledge, not just judgment, reminding them the body is the Holy Ghost's temple\r\nB. With discipline, removing them from the fellowship until they changed their ways\r\nC. With new commandments that raised the standard of conduct for the church\r\nD. With encouragement to wait until their experience had caught up with the truth\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Paul believed knowledge was what was needed and responded with knowledge (the body as the temple of the Holy Ghost), not just judgment.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [39:05]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 6:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+6%3A19/>\r\n\r\nQ56. In the speaker's illustration, what corresponds to the mortician's regular work on a dead body?\r\nA. Confessing past sins daily to the Lord and receiving His renewed forgiveness\r\nB. Mortifying the sinful promptings of the flesh by the Spirit, as an ongoing task\r\nC. Burying his past so that other believers never see where he came from\r\nD. Keeping a careful record of his progress so that he can measure growth\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the Christian is given the job of a mortician, and ties it to Romans 8:13: by the Spirit you mortify the sinful promptings of the flesh.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [42:19; 43:21]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 8:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A13/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Colossians 3:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+3%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ57. What does the speaker's story of a chicken that keeps running after its head is cut off illustrate?\r\nA. Habits die slowly, so the believer should expect to wait before he is free\r\nB. Activity in the flesh does not change being dead to sin, so reckon yourself dead\r\nC. A believer who still struggles has not truly been put to death with Christ\r\nD. The flesh should be fed a little less each day until it finally stops moving\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says many Christians are like the chicken, but you are dead to sin, and whatever activity you see in your flesh, you reckon yourself dead.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [43:21; 44:23]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 8:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A13/>; 2 Peter 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Peter+1/>\r\n\r\nQ58. How does the speaker explain Paul's words 'work out your salvation with fear and trembling'?\r\nA. As an effort to complete the salvation that Christ left unfinished\r\nB. As a way of earning salvation through a growing fear of God\r\nC. As a reminder that salvation can be lost without continued effort\r\nD. As an outworking of the provision of salvation, because God works in the believer\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says to let there be an outworking of the provision of salvation because it is God who works in you: take advantage of that and walk it out.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [47:48]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Philippians 2:12–13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+2%3A12%E2%80%9313/>\r\n\r\nQ59. What does the speaker say the Greek word behind 'set your affections' in Colossians means?\r\nA. Discipline yourself\r\nB. Deny yourself\r\nC. Interest yourself\r\nD. Humble yourself\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the Greek word behind 'set your affections' means 'interest yourself': do not just accept the facts, take an interest in them.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [47:48; 48:47]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+12/>\r\n\r\nQ60. What paradox does the speaker point out in the phrase 'living sacrifice' in Romans 12:1?\r\nA. A sacrifice is costly, yet the believer is called to give joyfully\r\nB. A sacrifice is temporary, yet the believer is called to give permanently\r\nC. A sacrifice is normally dead, yet the believer is alive to Christ and dead to sin\r\nD. A sacrifice is offered by priests, yet the believer offers himself\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker calls 'living sacrifice' an oxymoron: the sacrifice is always dead, and the believer is life to Christ and death to sin.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [48:47; 50:00]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 12:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+12%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ61. How does the speaker explain 'prove' in Romans 12:2?\r\nA. To approve: to insist on God's will in your life and bear fruit\r\nB. To test whether God's will is really as good as it is said to be\r\nC. To wait until God's will is confirmed by outward circumstances\r\nD. To demonstrate one's worth through faithful years of service\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says 'prove' means approve: insist on God's will in your life, which he equates with bearing fruit.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [50:00; 55:59]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 12:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+12%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ62. What example does the speaker give to show that setting your affection matters for victory over pornography?\r\nA. Reading explicit books occasionally and then repenting quickly afterwards each time\r\nB. Visiting places of temptation only on weekends and avoiding them the rest of the week\r\nC. Watching X-rated movies five days a week, then wondering why there is no victory\r\nD. Listening to worldly music while praying in tongues each morning before work\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says you cannot spend five of seven days watching X-rated movies and then wonder why you lack victory over pornography; set your affection.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [52:10]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 5:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+5%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ63. What tendency does the speaker admit about grace preachers, including himself?\r\nA. They emphasize conduct so heavily that the message of grace is hidden\r\nB. They teach that works are necessary in order to keep one's salvation\r\nC. They avoid the subject of forgiveness altogether in their messages\r\nD. They avoid talking about conduct, fearing it is preaching law\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says grace preachers, himself included, tend not to talk about these things because they think they would be talking law, and that is not true.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [52:10; 53:22]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 5:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+5%3A8/>; Romans 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5/>; Romans 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6/>\r\n\r\nQ64. According to the speaker, what question was Paul answering with 'God forbid' in Romans 6:1?\r\nA. Whether believers should continue in sin so that grace may abound\r\nB. Whether the law should be kept alongside the new covenant\r\nC. Whether baptism is necessary for believers to be saved\r\nD. Whether believers should remain debtors to the flesh\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Paul was addressing the idea of continuing in sin since grace abounds even where there is sin, and answered 'God forbid.'\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [53:22]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 6:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6%3A1/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5/>\r\n\r\nQ65. How does the speaker say Paul would react to someone asking 'Will I go to heaven?' from the bed of fornication?\r\nA. He would reassure the person at length that grace covers the failure\r\nB. He would not debate it but ask why the person is on that bed\r\nC. He would ask the person to confess and be restored by a church leader\r\nD. He would explain that heaven depends on the person's future obedience\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker calls it a stupid question and says Paul would not stand and discuss it but would ask why the person is on the bed of fornication.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [54:17]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5/>; Romans 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6/>\r\n\r\nQ66. What does the speaker say about works and acceptance before God?\r\nA. Works do not make one accepted, so visible fruit is entirely optional in life\r\nB. Works do not make one accepted, but the Spirit's power means fruit should show\r\nC. Works make one accepted once the Spirit has confirmed that they are real\r\nD. Works keep one accepted, with the Spirit's help being only a secondary matter\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says it is not works that make you accepted, but you have received the Holy Spirit who empowers you, so we should see fruit.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [55:07]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 8:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ67. What assurance does the speaker say Paul gave the church at Galatia?\r\nA. Walk in the Spirit and you will not fulfill the lusts of the flesh\r\nB. Keep the law and you will not fall into the flesh\r\nC. Pray in tongues daily and the flesh will lose its strength\r\nD. Mortify the body and the Spirit will then lead you\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Paul gave the Galatians a remarkable assurance: walk in the Spirit and you will not fulfill the (lusts) of the flesh.\r\nREF: THAT OLD MAN — [57:13]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Galatians 5:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+5%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ68. What does Titus 2:11-12 say the grace of God teaches?\r\nA. To rest from works and trust that forgiveness already covers every failure\r\nB. To keep the commands of the law more carefully with each passing day\r\nC. To deny ungodliness and worldly lusts and live soberly, righteously, godly\r\nD. To seek spiritual gifts so that the flesh is overcome by power\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reads Titus 2:11-12: grace teaches us to deny ungodliness and worldly lusts and to live soberly, righteously, and godly.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [00:16]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Titus 2:11–12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Titus+2%3A11%E2%80%9312/>\r\n\r\nQ69. What does the speaker's illustration of a marathon trainer who does bench presses every day show?\r\nA. Strength training is harmful for those whose goal is endurance running\r\nB. Training only works if it is done at the same time every day\r\nC. Cardio is more important than every other form of exercise\r\nD. A person can be exercising yet not doing the exercise he needs\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says such a person is exercising, but not the type of exercise needed, and compares this to people fluent in tongues who still have anger problems.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [02:00]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 13:1-2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+13%3A1-2/>\r\n\r\nQ70. How does the speaker use the Bible's statement that godliness is profitable unto all things and bodily exercise profits little?\r\nA. To argue that physical fitness has no place in the life of a believer\r\nB. To help believers see that spiritual exercises work and that one can improve at them\r\nC. To show that godliness is a gift that cannot be improved through training\r\nD. To teach that spiritual exercise matters only for those who are ministers\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses the comparison to help you understand how spiritual exercises work, that you can get better at it.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [02:00]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Timothy 4:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+4%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ71. What troubled the man who served the Lord for almost 60 years as he lay dying?\r\nA. He feared that he was not saved after so many years of failure\r\nB. He regretted that he had not entered full-time ministry earlier\r\nC. He was forgiven but had never overcome his pornography struggle\r\nD. He was uncertain whether God had forgiven him\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: In the story, the man said he knew he was saved and forgiven, but never got to overcome the pornography problem.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [02:54]\r\n\r\nQ72. What does the speaker say a surface reading of 'Esau sold his birthright' misses?\r\nA. That Esau had no real desire at all for the birthright he gave up\r\nB. That feeling he was starving predisposed him to what he would not normally do\r\nC. That Esau acted without knowing what the birthright was worth to him\r\nD. That Esau was tested far more severely than Jesus was in His fast\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says reading it superficially misses the context: feeling he was dying of starvation predisposed Esau to things he would not normally do.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [03:47]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 25:29–34 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+25%3A29%E2%80%9334/>\r\n\r\nQ73. What does the illustration of a lonely person receiving an 'I love you' call after a broken heart show?\r\nA. Lonely people are always deceived because they cannot discern when lies are told\r\nB. Heartbreak is the main cause of habits that later become full addictions\r\nC. People are most tempted by those whom they once loved and then lost\r\nD. Some situations lower your defenses so you consider what you normally would not\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says some situations make you consider things you would not normally consider, so you must train for situations where your defenses are down.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [04:39; 05:24]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 10:12-13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+10%3A12-13/>\r\n\r\nQ74. After forty days of fasting, what conviction did Jesus hold that the speaker contrasts with Esau?\r\nA. Hunger is the proof that the flesh must be fed first of all\r\nB. Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word of God\r\nC. The Father provides for those who wait without any complaint\r\nD. Pleasure is acceptable as long as it is never taken to excess\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the difference between Esau and Jesus is that even after 40 days of fasting Jesus' conviction was that man shall not live by bread alone.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [05:24; 06:12]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Deuteronomy 8:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+8%3A3/>; Matthew 4:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+4%3A4/>\r\n\r\nQ75. Why does the speaker mention the Bible's instruction to abstain from drunkenness?\r\nA. An example of abstaining from anything that puts your defenses down\r\nB. Proof that alcohol is the root cause behind all of the other habits\r\nC. The first item in a list of habits to be overcome in strict order\r\nD. A rule that applies only to those who have a history of addiction\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker links it to training for situations where defenses are down: anything that puts you in such a situation, abstain from it.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [05:24]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 5:18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+5%3A18/>\r\n\r\nQ76. According to the speaker, what does fasting do besides putting food in its place?\r\nA. It guarantees victory over temptation without any further effort\r\nB. It earns extra favor with God to be used in times of weakness\r\nC. It replaces the need for the believer's other spiritual devotions\r\nD. It sends signals of discipline into every other area of life\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says with fasting you put food in its place and send signals of discipline to every other aspect of your life.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [07:02]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Deuteronomy 8:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+8%3A3/>; Matthew 4:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+4%3A4/>\r\n\r\nQ77. What does the speaker draw from Paul's use of 'the fruit of the Spirit is' (singular) in Galatians 5?\r\nA. The fruit of the Spirit grows only one quality at a time in a believer\r\nB. The same Spirit has different expressions, just as the same flesh does\r\nC. The Spirit's fruit is smaller in number than the works of the flesh\r\nD. Believers must choose a single fruit to focus on at any one time\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes it says 'is' and not 'are,' meaning the same Spirit has different tributaries or expressions, as the same flesh does.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [07:58]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Galatians 5:22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+5%3A22/>\r\n\r\nQ78. What does the speaker's picture of water from the tap, the WC, and the shower all fed from one tank illustrate?\r\nA. Each habit has its own separate cause and should be treated one by one\r\nB. Habits are cleansed one at a time as the water in the house is changed\r\nC. A leak in one area signals that the main supply has been broken\r\nD. Fleshly struggles share one source, so attack them wholesomely at the source\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the fruits of the flesh come from the same source, so turning off one outlet at a time fails; attack it wholesomely at the main source.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [07:58; 08:54]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Galatians 5:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+5%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ79. Which of these does the speaker name as an area to reduce even though it is not necessarily sinful?\r\nA. Time spent in worship and Bible study\r\nB. Uncontrolled social media and TV use\r\nC. Attendance at fellowship gatherings\r\nD. Offerings given to those in need\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says to look for areas where defenses are low, even non-sinful ones, and reduce phone and social media use, uncontrolled TV, and eating.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [09:42]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Galatians 5:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+5%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ80. What does the speaker say happens when you know you ought to pray and do not?\r\nA. You empower the flesh, and Satan can use that strength in your most embarrassing area\r\nB. You lose your standing with God until you have repented of the neglect\r\nC. You weaken the Spirit's ability to speak to you in your future prayer\r\nD. You miss a reward that cannot be recovered at some later time\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says every time you know you ought to pray and do not, you empower the flesh, strength that is useful to Satan in the most embarrassing area.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [10:47]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 26:41 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+26%3A41/>\r\n\r\nQ81. Which example does the speaker give of putting the flesh under that makes it easier to say 'No' to pornography?\r\nA. Controlling yourself when tempted to reply, in person or on social media\r\nB. Telling a friend about the struggle before temptation actually arrives\r\nC. Waking early each day to pray before checking your phone\r\nD. Memorizing verses on purity before you face the temptation itself\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that when you feel tempted to reply, physically or on social media, and you control yourself, you put the flesh under and it is easier to target the specific habit.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS I — [11:30]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Galatians 5:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+5%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ82. Why does the speaker say the statistics on pornography matter?\r\nA. They show that the problem is found mostly outside the church today\r\nB. They prove that technology is the root cause of the whole problem\r\nC. They show that only young people are affected by this struggle\r\nD. Someone you know may be struggling in secret while attending church faithfully\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: After the statistics, the speaker says someone you know may be struggling, coming to church actively, and you do not know it.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [00:16]\r\n\r\nQ83. What share of young people aged 13 to 24 does the speaker say actively seek pornography?\r\nA. 35%\r\nB. 34%\r\nC. 64%\r\nD. 20%\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says 64% of young people between 13 and 24 actively seek porn (35% of downloads are porn related; 34% of users were exposed to unwanted porn).\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [00:16]\r\n\r\nQ84. Why does the speaker say the 2006 report likely understates the situation today?\r\nA. The report counted only downloads and ignored all other forms of activity\r\nB. The report was based only on young people between 13 and 24\r\nC. The report left out exposure through ads and pop-ups entirely\r\nD. Internet access was limited then, especially in Africa, so it is likely twice as bad\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes the report dates from 2006, when many countries, especially in Africa, lacked Internet access, so it is likely at least twice as bad.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [00:16]\r\n\r\nQ85. What two evils does Jeremiah 2:13 say God's people committed, as the speaker reads it?\r\nA. Forsaking the covenant and worshiping the gods of the nations around them\r\nB. Forgetting the law and trusting in their own strength alone\r\nC. Forsaking the fountain of living water and making broken cisterns\r\nD. Rejecting the prophets and building altars on the high places\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reads Jeremiah 2:13: they forsook God, the fountain of living water, and made broken cisterns that cannot hold water.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [01:18; 02:02]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Jeremiah 2:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+2%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ86. How does the speaker describe the root of every addiction?\r\nA. A weak will that has never been trained through fasting and prayer\r\nB. A lack of knowledge about the consequences that sin brings\r\nC. An inherited weakness that no discipline is able to reach\r\nD. Seeking from a temporal source the joy only God can give\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says anyone with an addiction is looking from a temporal source for joy that can only come from a permanent source, namely God.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [02:02]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Jeremiah 2:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+2%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ87. What does the speaker say everyone who yields to temptation expects, and what actually happens?\r\nA. They expect it to bring joy, but it brings only guilt\r\nB. They expect to gain control, but they lose their freedom\r\nC. They expect forgiveness, but they lose their fellowship\r\nD. They expect it to go away, but instead it grows\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says everyone who yields thinks that in yielding it will go, but instead of going, it grows.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [02:02]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Jeremiah 2:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+2%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ88. Whom does the speaker credit with the saying that sin is not just wanting bad things but wanting good things badly?\r\nA. E.W. Kenyon\r\nB. Tim Keller\r\nC. Pastor Chris\r\nD. The apostle Paul\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker attributes the saying to Tim Keller.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [02:51]\r\n\r\nQ89. Which pair of examples does the speaker give of good things taken to the extreme becoming sin?\r\nA. Money becoming greed and work becoming idolatry\r\nB. Sleep becoming laziness and rest becoming neglect\r\nC. Food becoming gluttony and wine becoming drunkenness\r\nD. Friendship becoming lust and love becoming obsession\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says food taken to the extreme becomes gluttony and wine becomes drunkenness: wanting good things badly.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [02:51]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Proverbs 23:20–21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Proverbs+23%3A20%E2%80%9321/>\r\n\r\nQ90. According to the speaker, what must happen for lust to perform?\r\nA. It must be provoked by outside spiritual attack\r\nB. It must be fed, which happens when guards are let down\r\nC. It must be opposed by a strong desire for God\r\nD. It must be hidden from others for a long time\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says lust cannot perform if you do not feed it, and people feed the flesh when they let their guards down.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [03:47]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: James 1:14–15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A14%E2%80%9315/>\r\n\r\nQ91. How does the speaker say many people 'crawled into habits'?\r\nA. A sudden fall that came after a long period of very strict living\r\nB. Friends who pressured them into trying what they had been resisting\r\nC. A lax way of life, such as no longer closing their eyes at kissing scenes\r\nD. Ignorance of what the Bible actually says about purity and holiness\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says many came into habits by a lax way of life: in innocence they closed their eyes at kissing scenes, then began to watch and feed the flesh.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [03:47]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: James 1:14-15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A14-15/>\r\n\r\nQ92. What does the speaker's agriculture illustration show about nature?\r\nA. Growth in nature requires patience far more than it requires effort\r\nB. Sacrifice is part of nature: a seed must die for a plant to emerge\r\nC. Plants flourish when they are simply left alone in good soil\r\nD. Seeds grow only when they are planted in the right season\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says life in agriculture is the evidence of death: a seed had to die for a new plant to emerge.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [04:33]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 12:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+12%3A24/>\r\n\r\nQ93. What irony does the speaker note about overcoming tiredness?\r\nA. To gain energy, you must rest more than you actually work\r\nB. To avoid fatigue, you must eat less food on every single day\r\nC. To feel rested, you must go to sleep earlier on every night\r\nD. To stop waking up tired, you must go to the gym and do some work\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker calls it ironic that the way to overcome waking up tired is to go to the gym and do some work.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [05:21]\r\n\r\nQ94. What does the speaker say psychology shows about someone who lacked a primary figure such as a father or mother?\r\nA. They are more likely to resist any form of accountability\r\nB. They are more likely to look for love and acceptance in the wrong places\r\nC. They are more likely to struggle with anger than with lust\r\nD. They are more likely to hide their struggles from others\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says someone who lacked a primary figure is psychologically more likely to look for love in the wrong places.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [05:21]\r\n\r\nQ95. What does the speaker say the woman in John 4, with her failed marriages, needed?\r\nA. A husband who would remain faithful to her\r\nB. Not a new husband but Christ\r\nC. A community that would accept her past\r\nD. A deeper understanding of the law\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that maybe what she needed was not a new husband but Christ, who offers water that satisfies permanently.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [06:09; 06:59]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+4/>\r\n\r\nQ96. What practical steps does the speaker give for practicing satisfaction in God?\r\nA. Take your walk with God seriously, enjoy worship, and study your Bible\r\nB. Avoid every pleasure until the desire for it has died\r\nC. Memorize passages on thirst so that cravings are replaced\r\nD. Wait for a feeling of fullness before taking other steps\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says to take your walk with God seriously and enjoy worship and Bible study, because if your cup is full there is no space for strange liquids.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [06:59]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+4/>\r\n\r\nQ97. What does the speaker call the bedrock of sin?\r\nA. Ignorance and neglect of Scripture\r\nB. Pride and self-reliance\r\nC. Secrecy and unnecessary privacy\r\nD. Loneliness and rejection\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says secrecy and unnecessary privacy is the bedrock of sin.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [07:57]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+4/>\r\n\r\nQ98. What does the speaker point out about Adam after he sinned?\r\nA. He hid because God had already announced the consequences to him\r\nB. Nobody told him to hide; shame comes with sin as part of the package\r\nC. He hid because Eve had told him what she had done first\r\nD. He hid only after God had confronted him about his act\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says nobody had to tell Adam to hide himself; shame and sin go hand in hand, and shame can be used for good through accountability.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [06:59; 07:57]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 3:8–10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+3%3A8%E2%80%9310/>\r\n\r\nQ99. Which saying does the speaker use to encourage telling someone about a struggle?\r\nA. A problem shared is a problem half solved\r\nB. A burden shared is a burden that is doubled in weight\r\nC. Confession is always good for the soul of a person\r\nD. Iron sharpens iron when two men are together\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the moment you tell someone, a problem shared is a problem half solved, and it should be someone who can respectfully and gently hold you accountable.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [08:46]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: James 5:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+5%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ100. What does the speaker say about the fact that there are hypocrites and bad friends?\r\nA. It means a believer should rely on private discipline alone\r\nB. It shows that accountability partners should come from outside the church\r\nC. It is still God's will to find someone to whom you can be accountable\r\nD. It proves accountability works only with a pastor\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker acknowledges hypocrites and bad friends but says it is still God's will to find someone you can walk up to.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [08:46]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: James 5:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+5%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ101. What does the image of a man in a pit trying to lift his own hands illustrate?\r\nA. Sometimes a person cannot free himself, and someone outside must lift him out\r\nB. A person should wait in the pit until God Himself lifts him out\r\nC. A person who has fallen can be restored by his own discipline\r\nD. A person in the pit should avoid asking for help from others\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses the pit to explain Galatians 6:1: some situations you cannot bring yourself out of, and someone outside the pit must lift you.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [09:38]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Galatians 6:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+6%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ102. Which two structures does the speaker summarize as helpful for overcoming habits?\r\nA. God as the ultimate source of joy and satisfaction, plus accountability\r\nB. Strict rules about entertainment, plus a fixed schedule of fasting\r\nC. Daily confession of sins, plus public repentance before the church\r\nD. Avoiding every pleasure, plus memorizing large parts of Scripture\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: In closing, the speaker says to reassess priorities so that God is the ultimate source of joy, acceptance and satisfaction, and to create a structure of accountability.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS II — [10:21]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Galatians 6:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+6%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ103. What perspective on temptation does the speaker introduce at the start of the message?\r\nA. Temptations come only to those who neglect prayer\r\nB. Temptations are signs that a believer is outside God's will\r\nC. Temptations are removed once a believer is mature\r\nD. You cannot reach the next level of God's plan without temptations\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker opens by asking what if temptations are necessary, since you cannot get to the next level of God's plan without them.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS III — [00:17]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: James 1:2–4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A2%E2%80%934/>\r\n\r\nQ104. What does the speaker say about the words temptation, tests, and trials?\r\nA. They are more alike than people realize\r\nB. They are different names for the same attack from Satan\r\nC. They differ completely in meaning and source\r\nD. They apply to different stages of the Christian life\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the terms are not strictly different in meaning; they are more alike than you realize.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS III — [01:08]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: James 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1/>\r\n\r\nQ105. How does the speaker use the picture of a school to explain trials?\r\nA. Students learn best when the teacher gives only a few tests along the way\r\nB. Promotion in school comes by passing tests, so some classes of the Spirit need tests\r\nC. Tests in school mainly show who deserves praise in front of others\r\nD. Passing tests in school matters less than attending class regularly\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says you do not get promoted without passing tests, and in the school of the Spirit some classes cannot be entered without them.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS III — [01:08; 02:49]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: James 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1/>\r\n\r\nQ106. According to James 1:2-3 as read by the speaker, what does the testing of your faith produce?\r\nA. Steadfastness\r\nB. Humility\r\nC. Boldness\r\nD. Sensitivity\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reads that the testing of your faith produces steadfastness.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS III — [02:05]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: James 1:2–3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A2%E2%80%933/>\r\n\r\nQ107. What explanation does the speaker say people offer for the long dominance of Messi and Ronaldo?\r\nA. Each trained harder than every other player of their era did\r\nB. Each avoided injuries through strict personal discipline off the field\r\nC. Born in the same generation, each drove the other to perform better\r\nD. Each played for a club that supported a long career\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says people opine that, being of the same generation, they drove each other to be better, which shows how tests and trials bring out the best in us.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS III — [02:49]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: James 1:2-4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A2-4/>\r\n\r\nQ108. Why does Paul warn against ordaining a novice, according to the speaker?\r\nA. Because a novice has not yet learned the doctrine of the church\r\nB. Because a novice cannot be trusted with the finances of the church\r\nC. Because a novice will be quickly discouraged by criticism\r\nD. Lest he be lifted up with pride and fall into the condemnation of Satan\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites Paul: do not ordain a novice lest he be lifted up with pride and fall, showing some qualifications are for those who have been tested.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS III — [02:49; 03:39]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Timothy 3:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+3%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ109. Why does the speaker say having been tested helps in ministry?\r\nA. Those going through the same thing listen when you can say, 'I've been there'\r\nB. Experience is the best teacher, so ministers must have lived through everything\r\nC. Tested ministers receive greater authority from the church\r\nD. Tested ministers have less need to rely on the Word\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says experience is not the best teacher, but when someone at his lowest hears 'I've been there,' it aids ministry more than expected.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS III — [03:39; 04:31]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Corinthians 1:3-4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+1%3A3-4/>\r\n\r\nQ110. What does the speaker say Paul's statement 'of whom I am chief' shows about overcoming?\r\nA. His past failures disqualified him from leadership until much later\r\nB. His sin was greater than that of others, so his grace was greater\r\nC. His example proves that struggles end at the moment of conversion\r\nD. His life became a testimony: if he made it out, others can too\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Paul's life became a testimony: if I made it out, you are going to make it out too.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS III — [05:09]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Timothy 1:15–16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+1%3A15%E2%80%9316/>\r\n\r\nQ111. What does the speaker point out about the words for 'trials' in James 1:2 and 'temptations' in James 1:12?\r\nA. They are different Greek words that have opposite meanings in context\r\nB. They are the same word only in Paul's letters and nowhere else\r\nC. They are the same Greek word, though slight differences may exist\r\nD. They are different words that translators have merged together\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says verse 2 speaks of trials and verse 12 of temptations, yet it is the same Greek word, though there may be slight differences.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS III — [05:09; 06:06]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: James 1:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A2/>; James 1:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A12/> | Also cited in lesson segment: James 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A3/>\r\n\r\nQ112. What does the speaker say about the instruction to 'count it all joy'?\r\nA. The experience is joyful because temptation signals God's approval\r\nB. The experience becomes joyful once the temptation is overcome\r\nC. The instruction applies to trials but not to temptation\r\nD. The experience is not joyful, but believers are told to count it joy\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says it is not a joyful experience, but it says to count it joy, because God turns what the enemy meant for evil into good.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS III — [06:54]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: James 1:2–4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A2%E2%80%934/>; James 1:13–15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A13%E2%80%9315/>\r\n\r\nQ113. What biblical event does the speaker cite to show the Lord sometimes allows temptation for a believer's strength?\r\nA. Jesus being led by the Spirit into the wilderness to be tempted\r\nB. Job losing his possessions while the devil looked on from heaven\r\nC. Elijah being fed by ravens at the brook during the long drought\r\nD. Joseph being sold to a passing caravan by his own brothers\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Jesus was led by the Spirit into the wilderness to be tempted, and sometimes the Lord allows it for your strength.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS III — [06:54; 07:47]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Matthew 4:1–11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+4%3A1%E2%80%9311/>\r\n\r\nQ114. How does the speaker say Jesus returned after passing all the tests?\r\nA. In the power of the Spirit\r\nB. With angels attending Him\r\nC. With a larger following of disciples\r\nD. With a rested and strengthened body\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that having passed all the tests, Jesus returned in the power of the Spirit.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS III — [07:47]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 4:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+4%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ115. According to James 1:12, what does the one who stands the test receive?\r\nA. The crown of righteousness for faithful service\r\nB. The inheritance reserved for the saints\r\nC. The crown of life promised to those who love Him\r\nD. The authority to strengthen other believers\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reads James 1:12: when he has stood the test, he shall receive the crown of life promised to those who love God.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS III — [07:47]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: James 1:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ116. What does the speaker take from Jesus' prayer for Peter and the charge to strengthen his brothers?\r\nA. Believers who are sifted are certain to be restored without any effort\r\nB. Passing through tests equips you to help others: a message in the mess\r\nC. Prayer from a leader guarantees that a believer will never be tested\r\nD. Only those who have failed a test are qualified to strengthen others\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says when you pass through tests it equips you to help others in the same: a message in your mess, and the test soon a testimony.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS III — [07:47; 08:47]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: James 1:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ117. What point does the speaker make with the story of the married man surprised by his wife's pregnancy?\r\nA. Many people ignore warning signs until a crisis forces change\r\nB. Many people fail to plan for the consequences of their commitments\r\nC. Many people try to stop the fruit while still planting the seeds\r\nD. Many people are surprised by God's timing in their lives\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says he is really talking about temptation: we focus on stopping the fruit when we are still planting seeds.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [00:16]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: James 1:14-15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A14-15/>\r\n\r\nQ118. Which old saying does the speaker use to introduce controlling exposure?\r\nA. A stitch in time saves nine when the tear is small\r\nB. Prevention is always better than cure, as they say\r\nC. Where there is smoke there is usually fire close by\r\nD. If you don't want to eat the food, don't smell it\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites the old saying: if you don't want to eat the food, don't smell it.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [01:08]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 13:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+13%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ119. According to the speaker, what is one of the surest ways to overcome temptation?\r\nA. Increasing your confidence in your own strength\r\nB. Fighting the sin only when it arrives\r\nC. Waiting for the desire to fade on its own\r\nD. Controlling your exposure\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says one of the surest ways to overcome temptation is to control your exposure, like not dancing in the fire expecting not to be burnt.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [01:08]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Timothy 2:22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+2%3A22/>\r\n\r\nQ120. What godly advice about lust does the speaker say still remains?\r\nA. Resist the devil and he will flee\r\nB. Stand firm and keep watch\r\nC. Flee youthful lusts\r\nD. Put on the whole armor\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites 2 Timothy 2:22, flee youthful lust, and says to avoid unnecessary exposure when you can run away.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [02:01]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 2 Timothy 2:22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+2%3A22/>\r\n\r\nQ121. Which biblical figure does the speaker cite as modeling the wisdom of leaving instead of staying to be strong?\r\nA. Daniel, who refused the king's food\r\nB. Joseph, who left his jacket and ran\r\nC. David, who fled from Saul\r\nD. Samson, who tested his strength\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the wisdom of God is superior to your self-assessment: Joseph left his jacket and ran, and did not stay there being strong.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [02:01; 03:09]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 39:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+39%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ122. What does the speaker say we are all tempted toward, especially those from a decent background?\r\nA. Underestimating God's patience\r\nB. Overestimating the strength of the temptation\r\nC. Underestimating the cost of discipline\r\nD. Overestimating our strength\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says we all have the temptation to be overconfident and may overestimate our strength, but God's wisdom is superior to our self-assessment.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [02:01]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 10:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+10%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ123. What does the speaker say to ask God to restore when a believer has grown comfortable and says 'I can handle it'?\r\nA. Zeal for ministry and the joy of his early salvation\r\nB. Sensitivity to sin, by returning to early innocence\r\nC. The desire for long fasting and extended times of prayer\r\nD. Confidence in his own discipline and past victories\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says to go back to early innocence, ask God to restore your sensitivity to sin, and put structures in place again.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [03:09]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Timothy 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+2/>\r\n\r\nQ124. Which statement does the speaker recall from the first class?\r\nA. You rise to the level of your discipline and fall only through plain neglect\r\nB. You don't rise to the level of your discipline; you fall to the level of your structure\r\nC. You become what you repeatedly choose, whatever kind of structure you have\r\nD. You fall to the level of your knowledge and not to the level of your effort\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says you do not rise to the level of your discipline but fall to the level of your structure, as taught in the first class.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [03:09; 04:06]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Timothy 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+2/>; James 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1/>\r\n\r\nQ125. According to James 1 as read by the speaker, where does temptation arise?\r\nA. From his own lust, which draws him away and entices him\r\nB. From God, who tests believers in order to reveal their weakness\r\nC. From the devil, who acts independently of a person's desires\r\nD. From circumstances that no person is able to avoid at all\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reads that God neither tempts anyone nor can be tempted with evil, but every man is tempted when drawn away by his own lust and enticed.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [04:06]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: James 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1/>\r\n\r\nQ126. Why did the speaker begin with a story about an unexpected pregnancy?\r\nA. To show that marriage creates temptations that single people do not face\r\nB. To teach that sexual sin has consequences that cannot be hidden\r\nC. To illustrate that God sometimes surprises believers with blessings\r\nD. James uses the language of conception: when lust is conceived, it brings forth sin\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the Greek word for conceived means conception or impregnation, which is why he opened with the pregnancy story.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [04:06]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: James 1:14–15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A14%E2%80%9315/>\r\n\r\nQ127. What does the speaker say many people are doing wrong in dealing with temptation?\r\nA. Fighting conception instead of fighting the devil\r\nB. Fighting sin instead of fighting conception\r\nC. Fighting temptation instead of confessing it\r\nD. Fighting alone instead of praying\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says many people are fighting the sin instead of fighting the conception, and should nip things in the bud before they germinate.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [04:55]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: James 1:14–15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A14%E2%80%9315/>; 2 Peter 2:7–8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Peter+2%3A7%E2%80%938/>\r\n\r\nQ128. What does the illustration of a seed on a table teach?\r\nA. A seed grows on its own given enough time, so temptation can't be avoided\r\nB. A seed needs only light, so exposure to the truth defeats temptation\r\nC. A seed won't grow without the right environment, so avoid such environments\r\nD. A seed grows if it is watered, so temptation must be watered less often\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says a seed on a table will not grow; conception took place because you created the environment, so do not allow yourself close to such an environment.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [04:55]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: James 1:14–15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A14%E2%80%9315/>\r\n\r\nQ129. What does the speaker take from Lot being vexed by the filthy conversation of Sodom?\r\nA. Righteous people are always affected by living in wicked cities\r\nB. Don't subject yourself to contradictory environments while trying to be strong\r\nC. Believers are expected to live where their strength will be tested\r\nD. Lot's vexation shows that he had been disobedient to God earlier\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Lot's spirit was vexed every day by where he lived, and you should not subject yourself to such contradiction and try to be strong when the Bible says run.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [05:47]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Peter 2:7–8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Peter+2%3A7%E2%80%938/>\r\n\r\nQ130. According to the speaker, how does deliverance from temptation happen in Lot's case?\r\nA. By God removing the desire from the believer's heart\r\nB. By the believer gaining strength to endure the environment\r\nC. By the believer remaining until the city is judged\r\nD. By burning the source of the temptation\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker asks how God delivered Lot, and answers that deliverance happens when you burn the source, having enough discipline to do without it.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [05:47; 06:49]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Peter 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Peter+2/>\r\n\r\nQ131. What does the speaker suggest about a smartphone that others handle well?\r\nA. One should limit it to specific hours of the day\r\nB. One should hand it to an accountability partner to monitor\r\nC. One should keep it but add filters to limit exposure\r\nD. One may still need to fast from it for a while\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says every other person might handle a smartphone well, but maybe I need a fast from this and to do without it for a while.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [06:49]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 6:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+6%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ132. How does the speaker use 'all things are lawful, but not all things are expedient'?\r\nA. To support building a structure of discipline, even by giving up lawful things\r\nB. To show that lawful things are never harmful to a mature believer\r\nC. To argue that believers must avoid everything not expressly allowed\r\nD. To teach that clergy are held to a stricter standard than laity\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites it with the call to create a structure of discipline, something both clergy and laity must learn to do.\r\nREF: HOW TO OVERCOME HABITS IV — [06:49]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 6:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+6%3A12/>; 1 Corinthians 10:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+10%3A23/>\n\nDECK: Discipleship Experience (Section 6) Prayer\r\n\r\nQ1. Which statement best captures the lesson's treatment of prayer's cumulative effect?\r\nA. Prayer produces gradual spiritual strengthening through consistent investment.\r\nB. Prayer becomes effective mainly through longer individual sessions.\r\nC. Prayer becomes most important when serious spiritual problems arise.\r\nD. Prayer accumulates mainly through increasing the number of requests made.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson describes prayer as a repeated spiritual investment that builds over time.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER X — [04:25]–[07:21] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: James 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+5/>\r\n\r\nQ2. Why does the lesson warn against making \"God, help me\" the entirety of one's prayer life?\r\nA. Prayer should go beyond emergency requests while still making room for short prayers.\r\nB. Short prayers lack effectiveness without extended declarations.\r\nC. God requires every prayer to contain a detailed account of the situation.\r\nD. Short prayers are appropriate mainly when engaging in spiritual warfare.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson accepts the value of short prayers but cautions against relying on them as one’s entire prayer life.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VIII — [05:24]–[06:19] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+19/>\r\n\r\nQ3. According to the teaching, \"not praying\" is dangerous primarily because it:\r\nA. prevents God from knowing what the believer needs.\r\nB. gradually weakens the believer's capacity to withstand pressure.\r\nC. limits the believer's ability to receive immediate answers.\r\nD. causes the believer to lose salvation through inconsistent devotion.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson warns that neglecting prayer gradually leaves the believer more vulnerable and less able to withstand pressure.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [00:16]–[05:19] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 26:41 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+26%3A41/>\r\n\r\nQ4. What principle is captured by \"If you cannot fly, run; if you cannot run, walk; if you cannot walk, crawl\"?\r\nA. Believers should change their spiritual routine whenever life becomes difficult.\r\nB. Spiritual consistency is less important than doing something occasionally.\r\nC. The way you engage may change, but you should keep moving toward God.\r\nD. Difficult seasons mean believers should expect less from themselves.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson encourages continuing in prayer, even when you need to adapt the amount or manner of engagement.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VIII — [07:05]–[07:47] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Thessalonians 5:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ5. A believer prays for an hour, feels nothing, and concludes the prayer was ineffective. What has the lesson identified as the primary error?\r\nA. Measuring spiritual progress by how strongly you feel.\r\nB. Spending too much time in prayer.\r\nC. Assuming prayer must always be emotional.\r\nD. Thinking prayer only works when it produces an immediate result.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson teaches that prayer’s value should not be judged by whether the believer feels a strong response.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IV — [00:16]–[03:47] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 5:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A7/>; 1 Peter 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+1%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ6. What does \"we walk by faith and not by sight\" require in the context of the lesson?\r\nA. Trusting spiritual feelings more than what Scripture says.\r\nB. Refusing to look at any evidence of spiritual growth.\r\nC. Choosing faith-filled experiences over regular prayer.\r\nD. Trusting spiritual realities even when you cannot see or feel them.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says to trust what God’s Word says about spiritual realities, even when they aren’t visible or felt.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IV — [01:06]–[03:47] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 5:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A7/>; 1 Peter 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+1%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ7. What is the lesson's function of Hebrews 11:6 in relation to prayer?\r\nA. It teaches that prayer should always produce a noticeable response.\r\nB. It moves the believer's confidence away from feelings and toward God.\r\nC. It teaches that praying longer will make you feel closer to God.\r\nD. It shows that long prayer is the main requirement for answered prayer.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Hebrews 11:6 directs confidence toward God and His promise, not the felt experience of prayer.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IV — [05:33]–[06:39] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 11:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+11%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ8. Which assumption does the statement \"Prayer will never be a waste of time\" directly confront?\r\nA. Prayer must always be long to be valuable.\r\nB. Prayer is only useful when combined with fasting.\r\nC. If nothing happens immediately, the prayer achieved nothing.\r\nD. Prayer does not work when you do not feel strongly about it.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson rejects the idea that prayer was wasted just because its effect is not immediately apparent.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IV — [04:34]–[05:33] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 11:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+11%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ9. Why does Apostle say \"pray like you're close to God\"?\r\nA. How close you see yourself to God should shape how you approach Him.\r\nB. Feeling far from God always means you have not prayed enough.\r\nC. People become close to God only after spending many hours in prayer.\r\nD. God answers people differently based on how close they feel to Him.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says to approach God with the confidence that He loves and cares for you.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IV — [09:22]–[11:24] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 4:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+4%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ10. The lesson uses Jesus' statement \"Father, I thank you that you always hear me\" primarily to cultivate:\r\nA. confidence that every prayer will be answered immediately.\r\nB. confidence that every request made in faith will be granted.\r\nC. confidence in the believer's ability to pray correctly.\r\nD. confidence that God hears even when the prayer experience does not feel special.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson encourages confidence that God hears, regardless of how a prayer session feels.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IV — [06:39]–[10:18] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 11:41–42 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+11%3A41%E2%80%9342/>\r\n\r\nQ11. Why does Philippians 2:13 become particularly encouraging to the believer who knows they should be doing better?\r\nA. Wanting to improve guarantees that behaviour will change immediately.\r\nB. Wanting to improve means the believer has already overcome the problem.\r\nC. The desire to improve is itself a sign that God is working in them.\r\nD. God takes full responsibility for keeping the believer's prayer life strong.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents the desire to improve as evidence that God is working in the believer.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER V — [03:07]–[03:55] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Philippians 2:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+2%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ12. Which response best represents the full posture the lesson teaches toward struggling with prayer?\r\nA. Recognise the struggle → accept it → wait until you feel stronger → start again.\r\nB. Recognise the struggle → work harder → remove the weakness → maintain the standard.\r\nC. Recognise the struggle → ask for forgiveness → lower the standard → rebuild slowly.\r\nD. Recognise the struggle → reject condemnation → receive God's help → keep going and grow.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson encourages seeking God’s help rather than giving in to despair, then continuing to practise prayer.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER V — [03:07]–[04:50] and [06:43]–[09:36] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Philippians 2:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+2%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ13. According to the lesson, what is the primary weakness of a prayer life centered almost entirely on requests?\r\nA. Requests are less likely to be answered when they are repeated.\r\nB. Request-based prayer usually makes prayer sessions shorter.\r\nC. The believer may miss prayer as a way of growing close to God.\r\nD. God expects believers to spend more time worshipping than asking.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says prayer is also for building intimacy with God, not only making requests.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VII — [06:28]–[07:53] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Psalm 27:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+27%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ14. A believer prays the same request repeatedly because they believe \"more words will eventually make God respond.\" Which principle from the lesson corrects this?\r\nA. Persistence is not needed when someone has strong faith.\r\nB. Prayer length should never be confused with a formula for making God respond.\r\nC. God only responds when prayer is focused on intimacy.\r\nD. Prayer length has no value at all.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson rejects treating more words or a particular prayer length as a mechanical way to make God respond.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VIII — [02:48]–[05:24] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+19/>\r\n\r\nQ15. According to the lesson, what is the believer's confidence concerning prayer?\r\nA. God hears because the believer has enough spiritual authority.\r\nB. God answers more after a believer has prayed for a long time.\r\nC. God responds more quickly when prayer is persistent enough.\r\nD. God hears those who ask in His name\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson grounds the believer’s confidence in God hearing those who ask in Jesus’ name.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER I — [09:53]–[10:36] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 John 5:14–15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+5%3A14%E2%80%9315/>\r\n\r\nQ16. What does Jesus' rebuke, \"Couldn't you watch with me one hour?\", establish within the lesson?\r\nA. There are times when not praying enough can leave a believer unprepared for what comes next.\r\nB. Every believer must pray for at least one hour every day.\r\nC. Prayer is ineffective if it lasts less than an hour.\r\nD. Long prayer is the main measure of spiritual readiness.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson uses Jesus’ rebuke to show that insufficient prayer can leave a person unprepared in particular moments.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VIII — [06:19]–[07:05] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 26:40–41 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+26%3A40%E2%80%9341/>\r\n\r\nQ17. What distinguishes the \"firefighting approach\" from the culture of prayer taught in the lesson?\r\nA. Firefighting responds when there is a crisis; prayer culture builds strength before the crisis.\r\nB. Firefighting focuses on warfare while prayer culture focuses on thanksgiving.\r\nC. Firefighting uses short prayers while prayer culture uses long prayers.\r\nD. Firefighting is emotional while prayer culture is disciplined.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson contrasts waiting for a crisis with prayer that helps maintain spiritual strength beforehand.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [00:16]–[02:09] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 18:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+18%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ18. A Christian still prays occasionally but increasingly finds that prayer no longer shapes their spiritual experience. Which principle most directly addresses this?\r\nA. Pray for longer until the old experience comes back.\r\nB. Return to consistent engagement with God instead of praying only occasionally.\r\nC. Focus mainly on spiritual warfare until the problem improves.\r\nD. Replace personal prayer with group prayer.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says prayer has a cumulative effect and emphasizes consistent daily engagement.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [00:16]–[02:09] and [05:19]–[06:21] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Thessalonians 5:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ19. Why does the lesson describe prayer as a \"strategy for preserving faith\"?\r\nA. Prayer helps keep strong the believer's confidence in what God can do.\r\nB. Prayer guarantees that believers will never doubt.\r\nC. Prayer gives believers power to remove every difficult circumstance.\r\nD. Prayer continually reminds believers of every doctrine they have learned.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson directly says prayer strengthens faith and confidence in God’s ability.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [06:21]–[09:08] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Peter 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+5/>\r\n\r\nQ20. According to the lesson's use of 1 Peter 5:8–9, what constitutes resistance to the devil?\r\nA. Praying more spiritual warfare prayers.\r\nB. Finding every possible strategy the enemy might use.\r\nC. Staying firm and strong in faith.\r\nD. Continuing to pray until all opposition stops.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson identifies steadfastness in faith as the resistance described in 1 Peter 5:8–9.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [09:08]–[10:02] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Peter 5:8–9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+5%3A8%E2%80%939/>\r\n\r\nQ21. What is the point of submitting to \"the boredom of consistency\"?\r\nA. Repetition automatically produces spiritual power.\r\nB. Consistency keeps spiritual strength alive even when things no longer feel new.\r\nC. Spiritual maturity means moving from basic practices to more advanced ones.\r\nD. Emotional prayer is unreliable, so it should be avoided.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents consistent prayer as a way to keep spiritual strength and faith from waning.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [05:19]–[07:15] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Thessalonians 5:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ22. What does the lesson identify as a major reason people struggle with long prayer?\r\nA. They do not know enough prayer terms.\r\nB. They do not know enough about spiritual warfare.\r\nC. They mainly think of prayer as asking God for things.\r\nD. They expect to have an emotional experience every time they pray.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says a request-only view can make extended prayer difficult because it misses prayer’s relational purpose.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VII — [06:28]–[07:53] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Psalm 27:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+27%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ23. Which shift best represents the lesson's intended correction?\r\nA. Moving from short requests to long requests.\r\nB. Moving from spontaneous prayer to scheduled prayer.\r\nC. Moving from emotional prayer to highly structured prayer.\r\nD. Moving from simply getting things from God to growing close to God.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson’s correction is to include intimacy with God, not treat prayer only as a means of getting requests answered.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VII — [06:28]–[07:53] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Psalm 27:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+27%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ24. In the teaching on Luke 11, persistence in prayer is primarily presented as:\r\nA. A way to make God respond when He does not want to.\r\nB. Part of developing the determination needed for a culture of prayer.\r\nC. Proof that someone has prayed long enough for an answer.\r\nD. A replacement for faith when an answer is delayed.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents persistence as part of developing a durable practice of prayer.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VII — [02:44]–[04:42] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Luke 11:18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+11%3A18/>\r\n\r\nQ25. Which principle best integrates the entire teaching on prayer length, consistency, faith, and intimacy?\r\nA. A strong prayer life is mainly measured by how long someone prays.\r\nB. A strong prayer life is mainly shown through powerful spiritual declarations.\r\nC. A strong prayer life mainly prepares believers to avoid future spiritual crises.\r\nD. A mature prayer life combines consistency, spontaneous prayer, persistence, and intimacy with God.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Across the lessons, prayer includes consistent practice, both long and short or spontaneous prayer, persistence, and intimacy with God.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER II — [06:03]–[07:02]; SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [06:21]–[08:11]; SCHOOL OF PRAYER VII — [01:11]–[03:39] and [06:28]–[07:53] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Peter 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+5/>; 1 Thessalonians 5:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A17/>; Luke 11:5–8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+11%3A5%E2%80%938/>\r\n\r\nQ26. According to the lesson, why is being able to \"conveniently go three days without praying\" treated as a serious warning sign?\r\nA. It demonstrates that the believer has lost interest in spiritual things.\r\nB. It proves that the believer has replaced God with other priorities.\r\nC. It indicates a level of spiritual vulnerability that may not yet be externally visible.\r\nD. It means the believer has already become spiritually ineffective.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents going days without prayer as a sign of spiritual vulnerability, even if it is not outwardly apparent.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [01:15]–[04:23] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 26:41 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+26%3A41/>\r\n\r\nQ27. Why does Apostle insist on not breaking the prayer streak?\r\nA. Consistency itself guarantees spiritual protection.\r\nB. Maintaining daily engagement helps prevent gradual spiritual deterioration.\r\nC. Missing prayers gives the enemy immediate legal access.\r\nD. A prayer streak is evidence that a believer has developed spiritual maturity.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson links daily prayer with preventing gradual spiritual decline and maintaining spiritual strength.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [00:16]–[01:15] and [05:19]–[06:21] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Thessalonians 5:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ28. The lesson's \"alarm\" analogy primarily challenges which assumption?\r\nA. Spiritual danger matters only after its consequences become visible.\r\nB. Spiritual problems usually begin with dramatic external attacks.\r\nC. Prayer becomes valuable only when a specific problem can be identified.\r\nD. Early discipline matters less than responding correctly after failure.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The analogy emphasizes noticing spiritual danger before its consequences become obvious.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [00:16]–[02:09] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 26:41 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+26%3A41/>\r\n\r\nQ29. What does \"pray without ceasing\" mean within the framework taught?\r\nA. Maintaining continual fellowship with God alongside scheduled prayer times.\r\nB. Maintaining uninterrupted verbal prayer throughout the entire day.\r\nC. Replacing scheduled prayer with spontaneous communication throughout the day.\r\nD. Increasing prayer frequency until other daily responsibilities become secondary.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson describes continual prayer as ongoing communication with God alongside scheduled prayer.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VII — [01:11]–[02:44] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Thessalonians 5:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ30. What point does the speaker draw from the NBA height illustration when he introduces the subject of prayer?\r\nA. Eloquent word choice is the one thing within a believer's reach that decides the outcome of prayer.\r\nB. Only a small group of gifted believers can pray effectively, so most should expect little.\r\nC. Prayer succeeds mainly through the same years of rigorous effort that professional athletes invest.\r\nD. Prayer starts with who you are before what you do, as height qualifies a player whatever his effort.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says one qualifying factor, height, is a gift beyond a player's control despite all the effort, and that prayer likewise 'begins with being before doing,' not eloquence.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER I — [00:58; 02:42]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 6:7-8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+6%3A7-8/>\r\n\r\nQ31. In the story of a stranger asking for 200, what does the speaker say explains why people respond so differently to the same request?\r\nA. The difference lies in how much money the person who is being asked happens to have available.\r\nB. The difference lies in how urgent the asker says the need is at that particular moment.\r\nC. The difference lies in how persuasively and politely the request is phrased by the person asking.\r\nD. The difference lies in who is asking and their standing, not in how the request is worded.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He states that the difference is not the way the question was asked but who is asking, and applies this to prayer.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER I — [02:42; 03:30]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 7:7-11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+7%3A7-11/>\r\n\r\nQ32. What does the speaker say about praying in King James-style language such as 'Thou, O Lord, art'?\r\nA. It does not make prayer more spiritual, since King James language is not heavenly language.\r\nB. It is acceptable only in public prayer, where formal language shows greater reverence for God.\r\nC. It is the preferred form of prayer because Scripture is best echoed in its traditional English wording.\r\nD. It helps believers sound more spiritual, provided that the heart of the speaker is right.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says some people think they sound more spiritual this way, but Jesus never spoke like that and King James language is not heavenly language.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER I — [03:30; 04:32]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: James 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+5/>\r\n\r\nQ33. What lesson does the speaker draw from the sons of Sceva copying Paul's exorcism?\r\nA. Exorcism works only for apostles, so ordinary believers should leave spiritual warfare alone completely.\r\nB. Mannerism and tone matter greatly, so the sons of Sceva failed only because they imitated Paul imperfectly.\r\nC. Copying words, tone, and mannerisms is not enough, because the result depends first on who you are.\r\nD. Demons respond to the name of Jesus alone, regardless of the person who happens to speak it.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The demons answered, 'Jesus we know, Paul we know, who are you?' The speaker concludes it is first about who you are, not how you said it.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER I — [04:32; 05:40]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 19:13–16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+19%3A13%E2%80%9316/>\r\n\r\nQ34. According to the passage the speaker cites about God's hand not being too short, what stands between people and God?\r\nA. A lack of effort in prayer, which Jesus came to make up on behalf of struggling believers.\r\nB. Sin, not a lack of words or eloquence, is the problem that Jesus addressed by taking our place.\r\nC. God's limited ability to reach people who have strayed a long way from Him over many years.\r\nD. A lack of eloquence, which is why believers need careful teaching on how to word their prayers.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the problem is not lack of words but that sin has separated people from God, and that God sent Jesus to take our place.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER I — [05:40]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Isaiah 59:1–2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+59%3A1%E2%80%932/>\r\n\r\nQ35. What does the speaker say about a saved person who has never read a book on prayer and can only say 'God, help me'?\r\nA. That person must first learn eloquent prayer before heaven will give full attention to the requests.\r\nB. That person receives the same hearing as anyone, saved or unsaved, who prays eloquently.\r\nC. That person has more attention from heaven, because prayer is not a game of eloquence.\r\nD. That person receives a hearing only after praying at length over many hours without stopping.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He links righteousness in Christ to authority in prayer and says such a saved person has more attention from heaven.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER I — [06:39]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Corinthians 5:21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+5%3A21/>\r\n\r\nQ36. What does the speaker's image of a nursing mother hearing her baby's cry illustrate about prayer?\r\nA. Prayer should be urgent and loud so that God is not distracted by other concerns in heaven.\r\nB. A cry is a cry: God responds to His children's cry without requiring eloquence, as 'Abba' suggests.\r\nC. God responds only to prayers that have been rehearsed carefully and spoken with clear words.\r\nD. A believer's tears carry more weight in prayer than the words that the believer speaks.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He connects 'Abba' to a baby's mumbling and says a mother is not looking for eloquence but rushes to the cry.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER I — [06:39; 07:51]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 4:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+4%3A16/> | Added from online research: Romans 8:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ37. How does the speaker define righteousness in connection with coming boldly to the throne of grace?\r\nA. A special spiritual standing reserved for ministers who lead other believers in prayer.\r\nB. The ability to stand before God without feeling inferior because of Christ's saving grace.\r\nC. The confidence that comes from mastering the right words and posture for approaching God.\r\nD. The moral record a believer builds through consistent obedience and many hours of prayer.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: After citing Hebrews 4:16, he explains that righteousness is the ability to stand before God without inferiority, which is what coming boldly means.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER I — [07:51; 08:51]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 4:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+4%3A16/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ38. According to the speaker, how does the consciousness of adoption and sonship change a believer's approach to prayer?\r\nA. Believers pray only about matters that concern their own personal salvation.\r\nB. Believers approach God as servants who must first earn the right to bring any request.\r\nC. Knowing their new status, believers approach God as children who cry 'Abba, Father.'\r\nD. Believers concentrate on proving their sonship through eloquent and well-structured prayers.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the consciousness of sonship changes the approach to prayer and that one thing to understand is your new status in the Lord.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER I — [10:36]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 8:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A15/>; Galatians 4:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+4%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ39. In the C.S. Lewis thought the speaker shares, why does God leading Abraham without a map matter for believers today?\r\nA. Abraham needed daily connection only until God confirmed the destination of his journey.\r\nB. Abraham needed daily connection to follow God, as believers know His plan but not its details.\r\nC. Abraham's journey shows that God reveals His whole plan in advance to those who obey Him.\r\nD. Abraham's example shows that detailed planning is the best way to fulfil God's purposes.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Abraham had to sustain daily connection and that all believers are in the same position because they do not know the details of God's plan.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER II — [00:16]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 12:1–4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+12%3A1%E2%80%934/>\r\n\r\nQ40. What does the speaker say Abraham did at the next place after God led him, in order to secure instructions for the next move?\r\nA. He built an altar there to secure God's instructions for the next move.\r\nB. He fasted for several days and waited for a map to be revealed to him.\r\nC. He consulted a prophet to confirm the direction that he had received from God.\r\nD. He sent scouts ahead to survey the next stage of the journey before leaving.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says God leads Abraham and then he has to build an altar to secure instructions for the next move.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER II — [01:15]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 12:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+12%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ41. What does the speaker say about someone who only prays when they feel like praying?\r\nA. That person has a prayer life that God honours more than scheduled prayer.\r\nB. That person has an honest prayer life that is led naturally by the Spirit.\r\nC. That person does not have a prayer life, since following God sometimes feels hard.\r\nD. That person has a healthy prayer life that is simply not yet mature.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He repeats that if you only prayed when you felt like it, you don't have a prayer life, and admits even pastors don't always feel like praying.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER II — [01:15]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 18:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+18%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ42. Why does the speaker point to the disciples at Gethsemane and to Peter's trance in Acts 10?\r\nA. To show that sleepiness in prayer signals a spiritual attack that must be rebuked at once.\r\nB. Even spiritual people slip off in prayer, so believers should press on rather than feel alone.\r\nC. To show that Jesus withdrew His help from disciples who failed to stay awake.\r\nD. To show that only those called to leadership are excused when they fall asleep praying.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says that if you pray and slip off you are not the only one, noting that every revered man of God goes through the same difficulties, so you press on.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER II — [02:10]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10/>\r\n\r\nQ43. According to the speaker, what is a believer's real security?\r\nA. The number of years they have been a faithful member of a local church.\r\nB. The depth of their biblical knowledge and their ability to explain doctrine clearly.\r\nC. The vibrance of their connection in the realm of the spirit, not how much they know.\r\nD. The strength of their spiritual gifts as displayed in public ministry.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says it doesn't matter what you know; your real security is the vibrance of your connection in the realm of the spirit.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER II — [03:06]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10/>\r\n\r\nQ44. What pattern does the speaker draw from the golden calf story, citing C.S. Lewis?\r\nA. Being tired of waiting on God can push people toward idolatry, including skipping prayer.\r\nB. Idolatry arises mainly from open rebellion against clear commands rather than from impatience.\r\nC. Idolatry develops when leaders give people too many rules about how they must wait.\r\nD. Idolatry begins when people grow proud of their spiritual achievements and stop seeking God.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Israel made the calf because they were tired of waiting for Moses, and that what you would rather do when you ought to pray may be an idol.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER II — [05:03]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Exodus 32:1–6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+32%3A1%E2%80%936/>\r\n\r\nQ45. What point does the speaker make by comparing spiritual exercise to eating?\r\nA. Fasting is more beneficial than eating, so prayer should be joined with regular fasting.\r\nB. Prayer should be taken in measured portions, since overdoing it will weaken the believer.\r\nC. Eating a lot today doesn't remove tomorrow's need, so daily prayer beats one big session.\r\nD. Prayer, like a meal, should be kept to fixed times so that the habit does not become routine.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the laws of spiritual exercise follow the laws of eating, so the streak matters most, and that 20 daily minutes beat not maintaining a streak.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER II — [06:03; 07:02]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Timothy 4:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+4%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ46. Why does the speaker say distractions like social media are so powerful, and what remedy does he offer?\r\nA. Because they are enjoyable, so replace them with entertainment that has spiritual content.\r\nB. They are easy, so create a system (alarm, church, accountability) that makes devotion easy.\r\nC. Because they are habit-forming, so rely on willpower and accept that devotion is meant to be hard.\r\nD. Because they are everywhere, so remove them entirely before attempting any prayer time.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says distractions are powerful because they are easy and urges a system that makes devotion easy: church, an alarm, friends, and accountability partners.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER II — [07:02; 08:00]\r\n\r\nQ47. Why does the speaker say well-known preachers keep a vibrant prayer life even though they sometimes do not feel like praying?\r\nA. They have received a special grace that removes all reluctance from their prayer times.\r\nB. They have grown beyond needing to feel anything at all when they pray.\r\nC. They have teams of intercessors who pray on their behalf during dry periods.\r\nD. They built a discipline that carries them when praying is inconvenient.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says even favorite preachers sometimes don't feel like praying, and that they keep a vibrant prayer life because they've built a discipline.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER III — [00:16; 01:08]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 18:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+18%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ48. What does Jesus' temptation after fasting forty days and nights illustrate, according to the speaker?\r\nA. Temptation is avoided by believers who rest fully after every demanding season.\r\nB. Temptation comes only to believers who have become careless about their devotion.\r\nC. Temptation is a sign that a believer's fasting was not carried out properly.\r\nD. Temptation can strike in a robust spiritual season, especially after great effort.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He describes exhaustion after demanding programs as vulnerable and says temptation can come right in your most robust spiritual season, as with Jesus.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER III — [02:07; 03:02]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 4:1–11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+4%3A1%E2%80%9311/>\r\n\r\nQ49. What is the first thing the speaker says to do when you do not feel like praying, and why does it help?\r\nA. Pray shorter prayers, because brief prayers remove the pressure to perform.\r\nB. Get a prayer partner, because someone else's faith can provoke you.\r\nC. Read Scripture aloud, because the Word creates the desire to pray.\r\nD. Fast for a day, because fasting forces the flesh to submit.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says first and foremost to get a prayer partner and cites Hebrews 10:24-25, explaining someone else's faith can provoke you.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER III — [03:02; 04:01]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 10:24–25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+10%3A24%E2%80%9325/>\r\n\r\nQ50. What does the air-conditioner compressor illustration describe about praying with the speaker's prayer partner?\r\nA. The first hours feel laid back, then around three hours the prayer shifts momentum.\r\nB. The prayer starts with intensity but gradually cools as the hours pass.\r\nC. The prayer begins only after both partners have finished tidying up their requests.\r\nD. The prayer stays at a steady pace throughout and ends with a loud declaration.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He compares the delayed kick-in of an AC compressor to a partner whose prayer shifts momentum after about three hours.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER III — [04:01; 04:54]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 10:24, 25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+10%3A24%2C+25/>\r\n\r\nQ51. What does the speaker advise people who find studying easier than praying, or praying easier than studying?\r\nA. Alternate each week so that neither area becomes more dominant than the other.\r\nB. Concentrate on the area you enjoy and leave the other for a later season.\r\nC. Treat the difficulty as a sign that the weaker area is not your calling.\r\nD. Deliberately work on whichever area you find weak, whether praying or studying.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He notes some struggle to study but not to pray and vice versa, and says to work on the areas where you're weak.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER III — [05:49]\r\n\r\nQ52. How does the speaker use prayer clips when he is not as zealous as he would like to be?\r\nA. He saves them for retreats and church programs, where group prayer is expected.\r\nB. He prays along with others who are praying on the clips, since prayer is contagious.\r\nC. He studies the preachers' wording so that he can copy their style in his own prayer.\r\nD. He plays them only after he has finished his own scheduled prayer time.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says prayer is contagious and that he just prays along when hearing others pray, even if he is not as zealous at that moment.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER III — [06:43]\r\n\r\nQ53. Why does the speaker suggest praying 'fifteen minutes four times' instead of an hour?\r\nA. Jesus prayed for exactly fifteen minutes at a time, so the pattern is commanded.\r\nB. Shorter segments let believers pray without needing any set schedule.\r\nC. Fifteen-minute segments are more effective than longer prayer because attention fades after a quarter hour.\r\nD. Praying a full hour feels exhausting, but shorter segments feel doable, as with Jesus' watches.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He calls it a mind hack: one hour is emotionally and psychologically exhausting, whereas 15 minutes four times feels doable.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER III — [07:47; 08:47]\r\n\r\nQ54. How does the speaker's stopwatch method work?\r\nA. He sets a timer to cap his prayer so that it never exceeds a fixed number of minutes.\r\nB. He sets a short timer and restarts it as many times as needed, counting blocks.\r\nC. He uses the timer only on days when he feels no resistance to praying.\r\nD. He sets one long timer and refuses to stop until it sounds, however tired he feels.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He sets short intervals and repeats them, so that instead of saying he prayed six hours he says he did one hour six times.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER III — [08:47; 09:46]\r\n\r\nQ55. What technological advantage does the speaker describe when listening to recordings of people who are full of the Spirit?\r\nA. Technology replaces the need for a prayer partner or for gathering with other believers.\r\nB. Technology lets us relive such Spirit-filled moments in a present continuous manner.\r\nC. Technology guarantees that listeners will receive the same anointing as the speaker.\r\nD. Technology allows believers to study the exact wording of anointed prayers for reuse.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says listening to people full of the Spirit is contagious and that technology trapped those experiences so you can relive them in a present continuous manner.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER III — [13:09]\r\n\r\nQ56. When a believer feels too weak to pray at all, what does the speaker recommend?\r\nA. Stop teaching others for a season and withdraw from church programs.\r\nB. Fast and pray at greater length until determination breaks through the weakness.\r\nC. Care for physical and emotional health, cast your cares on God, and keep content playing.\r\nD. Wait until strength returns before praying, so that the prayer is not offered in the flesh.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He advises attending to physical and emotional health, writing down and casting cares on God, and keeping content playing even when you are too weak to pray.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER III — [14:13; 15:19]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Peter 5:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+5%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ57. What does the speaker say about a performance-based devotion?\r\nA. It builds discipline that eventually produces closeness once the performance is sustained.\r\nB. It never yields a consistent walk, since you can pray hours and still feel far from God.\r\nC. It is necessary at first but should be dropped once a believer matures.\r\nD. It works well for prayer but fails when it is applied to fasting.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says you can pray six hours or fast three days and not feel close to God, and that performance-based devotion never yields a consistent walk.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IV — [00:16]\r\n\r\nQ58. How does the speaker apply 'we walk by faith and not by sight' to someone who prays in the morning and feels nothing?\r\nA. They may have covered real spiritual distance even though they do not feel it.\r\nB. They should trust their feelings over Scripture, since faith follows emotional experience.\r\nC. They should assume the prayer failed and look for sin that is blocking it.\r\nD. They should repeat the prayer until the feeling confirms that it has been heard.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says you may pray and have covered serious spiritual distance without feeling it, because we walk by faith and not by sight.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IV — [01:06]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 5:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ59. What mistake does the speaker say people make when they judge their praying by feeling?\r\nA. They keep praying too long because strong feelings make them lose track of time.\r\nB. They rely on the opinions of others about their prayers instead of their own feelings.\r\nC. They feel full and stop when they should pray more, and feel nothing after real work.\r\nD. They treat dramatic experiences as proof that God has answered their request.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says if you judge by feeling you will be wrong: people feel full and stop when they should pray more, and don't feel it after real work.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IV — [02:08]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 5:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ60. What does the speaker draw from Jesus' words to Thomas about believing without seeing?\r\nA. Doubt is a serious sin that Jesus rebuked by refusing to show Thomas His wounds.\r\nB. Seeing is necessary for faith to grow, which is why Jesus appeared to Thomas.\r\nC. Supernatural encounters are the main proof that a believer's faith is genuine.\r\nD. The blessing comes from believing in your relationship with God though unseen.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the blessing is in believing even when you have not seen, noting we love a God we have never seen.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IV — [02:51; 03:47]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Peter 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+1%3A8/> | Added from online research: John 20:29 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+20%3A29/>\r\n\r\nQ61. What does the speaker conclude about prayers that seem to have 'hung on the ceiling'?\r\nA. Such prayers likely point to unconfessed sin that should be dealt with before praying again.\r\nB. Such prayers mean the believer should change the approach rather than keep going.\r\nC. Prayer is never a waste of time, because God has not called anyone to seek Him in vain.\r\nD. Such prayers may be wasted, though persistence will eventually carry them through.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He insists prayer will never be a waste of time, including prayers that seem not to pass the ceiling, and cites that God has not called Jacob's seed to seek in vain.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IV — [04:34]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Isaiah 45:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+45%3A19/>\r\n\r\nQ62. How does the speaker apply Hebrews 11:6 ('he that comes to Him must believe that He is') to prayer?\r\nA. Believing that He is must be faith, not a feeling, so honest prayer is never wasted.\r\nB. Believers should look for signs, since seeking and finding are guaranteed in visible form.\r\nC. Believers must feel God's presence first, and faith then confirms what they already sense.\r\nD. Believers must pray with great eloquence so that God will reward their diligence.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says believing that God is 'is not a feeling,' and that if you pray honestly and feel nothing, you are wrong to doubt.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IV — [05:33]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 11:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+11%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ63. What does the speaker propose in place of judging prayer by feelings?\r\nA. Judging by community: letting trusted friends rate the quality of your prayer.\r\nB. Judging by results: measuring prayer by how quickly answers appear.\r\nC. Judging by structure: a set time, such as thirty minutes, whatever you feel.\r\nD. Judging by effort: counting how many requests you managed to present.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He asks what if you stopped judging by feelings and judged by structure, praying 30 minutes, two hours, or three hours regardless of feeling.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IV — [07:40]\r\n\r\nQ64. Which three simple 'hacks' does the speaker give for approaching prayer with confidence?\r\nA. Thank Him that He hears, pray like you are close to God, and use the force of confession.\r\nB. Thank Him that He hears you, fast regularly, and keep a record of answered prayers.\r\nC. Use the force of confession, pray in tongues daily, and keep an unbroken prayer streak.\r\nD. Pray like you are close to God, use a prayer partner, and play worship music.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He lists three hacks: thank Him that He hears you, pray like you are close to God, and the force of confession.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IV — [08:34; 09:22; 10:18]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 John 5:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+5%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ65. What does the speaker mean when he says Jesus' prayer was 'like a letter'?\r\nA. It addressed the Father directly and expected a reply, showing closeness.\r\nB. It was composed in advance and delivered in a formal and reserved tone.\r\nC. It listed requests in sequence like a petition sent to a distant ruler.\r\nD. It followed a fixed written form that the disciples were to repeat word for word.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Jesus' prayer was like a letter, addressing the Father and expecting a reply, and urges praying like you are close to God.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IV — [10:18]\r\n\r\nQ66. Why does the speaker say confessing who you are in Christ is not bragging?\r\nA. It is not bragging because the believer is reminding God of His promises.\r\nB. It is not bragging as long as the believer gives God the credit for every claim made.\r\nC. It is bragging in some cultures but is permitted in private prayer where no one hears.\r\nD. In the realm of the Spirit it is important, and not saying it is not modesty.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says that though it may seem like bragging in our culture, in the realm of the Spirit these things are not bragging but important, and it is not modesty to withhold them.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IV — [11:24]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Peter 2:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+2%3A9/>\r\n\r\nQ67. What reasoning does the speaker give for arguing that Peter's act may have been worse than Judas's?\r\nA. Peter's failure was public, while Judas's betrayal was done in secret.\r\nB. Peter's denial harmed the other disciples more than Judas's betrayal did.\r\nC. Judas at least identified with Jesus, whereas Peter denied knowing Him.\r\nD. Peter's failure came after he had received the Spirit, while Judas had not yet received Him.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He argues Judas claimed to know Jesus while Peter said he did not, and since salvation is predicated on identifying with Jesus, denial is more egregious.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER V — [01:04; 02:03]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Philippians 2:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+2%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ68. According to the speaker, why did Peter go on to bless the church while Judas ended his life?\r\nA. Judas sank into despair instead of receiving God's forgiveness, and Peter did not.\r\nB. Peter had been closer to Jesus and therefore had greater grace to recover.\r\nC. Judas never tried to repent, while Peter confessed his failure immediately.\r\nD. Peter's sin was smaller, so it did not require the same forgiveness.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the real reason is that Judas sank in despair rather than receiving forgiveness, and he had earlier noted that Judas did try to repent.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER V — [02:03]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Philippians 2:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+2%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ69. What 'false idea' does the speaker say a brand of Christianity has given about devotion?\r\nA. That believers must confess every failure before God will help them pray.\r\nB. That the responsibility rests on believers to impress God, as if He were a boss.\r\nC. That devotion is unnecessary once a believer has been saved by grace.\r\nD. That God is distant and unconcerned about the devotional struggles of believers.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says a brand of Christianity has given the false idea that the onus is on us to impress God, whereas God is there as a trainer, not a boss.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER V — [03:07]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Philippians 2:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+2%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ70. How does the speaker say Philippians 2:13 reframes a believer's desire to do better?\r\nA. The desire is a warning that the believer has neglected his duties for too long.\r\nB. The desire itself comes from God working in them, so God is not as far away as they think.\r\nC. The desire is only the believer's own resolve, which God then rewards with strength.\r\nD. The desire proves that the believer has already succeeded in overcoming the problem.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says it is God who works in you to will, so the mere desire to do better comes from God, who is not as far away as you think.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER V — [03:07]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Philippians 2:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+2%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ71. How does the speaker describe God's role toward a believer who is learning devotion?\r\nA. Like a judge who evaluates each failure and sets the believer's penalty.\r\nB. Like a chief executive who sets targets and expects reports on progress.\r\nC. Like a dad or trainer who helps and gives tasks to improve, not a boss to impress.\r\nD. Like a distant examiner who checks whether the believer has met the standard.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says God is not a boss to impress but like a dad recognizing his son needs to learn, who will help him get better.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER V — [03:55]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 7:9-11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+7%3A9-11/>\r\n\r\nQ72. What does the speaker highlight about Jesus' response when the disciples slept at Gethsemane?\r\nA. His correction: He told them their failure would cost them their place as apostles.\r\nB. His disappointment: He withdrew from them and prayed alone from then onward.\r\nC. His patience: He woke them repeatedly and acknowledged that the flesh is weak.\r\nD. His indifference: He prayed on and let them sleep until the morning came.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the disciples slept three times, Jesus woke them three times, and he calls it patience at the most vulnerable time of Jesus' life.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER V — [04:50; 05:53]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 26:40–41 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+26%3A40%E2%80%9341/>\r\n\r\nQ73. How does the speaker qualify his encouragement that God understands devotional weakness?\r\nA. It is no license to go without prayer, and one must pray and never despair.\r\nB. It means weakness has no consequences as long as the believer eventually feels sorry.\r\nC. It applies only to those who have never had a consistent prayer life before.\r\nD. It means believers should lower the standard until they can meet it easily.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says it is not an encouragement to continue in spiritual oblivion and that, while believers must pray, they should never allow despair.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER V — [06:43]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 4:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+4%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ74. What does the speaker suggest when a believer feels sleepy while trying to pray?\r\nA. It may not be the devil, since great spiritual people like Peter also got sleepy.\r\nB. It shows the prayer is in the flesh, so the believer should change the topic.\r\nC. It shows the believer should stop and resume the next day with fresh energy.\r\nD. It is probably a spiritual attack, so the believer should rebuke it and then continue.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says maybe it's not the devil, since Peter, James and John all slept and Peter fell into a trance in Acts 10.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER V — [07:39; 08:42]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10/>\r\n\r\nQ75. What contrast does the speaker draw between two ways of handling devotional failure?\r\nA. Those who confess publicly are restored; those who keep failures private slide deeper.\r\nB. Those who set strict penalties improve; those who accept grace become complacent.\r\nC. Those who plan carefully succeed; those who rely on emotion tend to give up.\r\nD. Those who whine get worse; those who keep trying, trusting God works in them, grow.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says those who whine get worse, while those who say it is God who works in them and keep trying build a habit.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER V — [09:36]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Philippians 2:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+2%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ76. What does the speaker conclude from Peter's example about failure and God's plan?\r\nA. Failure disqualifies leaders from ministry unless they serve a long probation.\r\nB. It does not matter where you failed; God's plan did not change for Peter.\r\nC. Failure is forgiven, but the believer's role is permanently reduced.\r\nD. Failure delays God's plan, so believers must wait until they have fully recovered.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says it doesn't matter where you failed, God's plan has not changed, and Peter is still the rock on which Christ builds the church.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER V — [10:20]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 21:15-17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+21%3A15-17/>\r\n\r\nQ77. What does the speaker's illustration about treatable conditions caught by early diagnosis teach about prayer?\r\nA. Prayer prevents illness, so believers who pray need not worry about early diagnosis.\r\nB. Prayer works best when it is combined with regular medical checkups and fasting.\r\nC. Prayer is mainly a remedy to be applied once serious problems have been diagnosed.\r\nD. Prayer has a cumulative effect, and not praying has a gradual deteriorative effect.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says prayer has a cumulative effect and, conversely, not praying daily has a gradual but sure deteriorative effect.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [00:16]\r\n\r\nQ78. What does the speaker's alarm image ('before the house is burnt to ashes') argue for?\r\nA. Catching trouble when the fire begins instead of when things are really bad.\r\nB. Praying intensely at the first sign of a visible attack from the enemy.\r\nC. Relying on friends or leaders to notice and report spiritual problems.\r\nD. Waiting for a clear spiritual warning before changing one's prayer habits.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He contrasts a firefighting approach, noticing only when things are really bad, with an alarm that goes off as the fire begins and can be extinguished.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [01:15]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 26:41 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+26%3A41/>\r\n\r\nQ79. What does the speaker's illustration of food put on the fire but not warmed teach?\r\nA. Prayer must be offered at a certain temperature of emotion before it is accepted.\r\nB. Praying without warming your heart won't preserve you, so warm your soul daily.\r\nC. A single fervent season of prayer is enough to preserve faith for a long period.\r\nD. Spiritual food must be prepared by others before it can be received by the believer.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says if you pray but it has been long since you warmed your heart, it is like food not warmed on the fire: it will still go bad.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [02:09]\r\n\r\nQ80. According to the speaker, what does Jesus tell us will happen if we do not pray?\r\nA. We will faint, unable to handle some situations, and fall into temptation.\r\nB. We will lose our salvation and have to be restored through repentance later.\r\nC. We will forfeit our authority and be unable to rebuke the enemy effectively.\r\nD. We will stop hearing from God and lose sight of His plan for our lives.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He cites 'men ought always to pray, not to faint' and 'pray that you do not fall into temptation' as what Jesus says will happen otherwise.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [04:23]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 18:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+18%3A1/>; Matthew 26:41 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+26%3A41/>\r\n\r\nQ81. Which streaks does the speaker say a believer must never break?\r\nA. Both the prayer streak and the church attendance streak, even when traveling.\r\nB. Both the prayer streak and the Bible study streak, even when devotion seems elementary.\r\nC. Both the fasting streak and the giving streak, even when other duties press in.\r\nD. Both the study streak and the testimony streak, even when it feels repetitive.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says repeatedly never to break your prayer streak and also not to break your study streak.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [05:19]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Thessalonians 5:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A17/>; Joshua 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Joshua+1%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ82. What attitude toward personal and family devotion does the speaker criticize?\r\nA. Thinking that they have outgrown devotion as too elementary.\r\nB. Spending so much time in devotion that other duties are neglected.\r\nC. Using devotional books in place of reading the Bible directly.\r\nD. Doing devotions mainly for appearance, to impress other believers.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says many of us outgrew devotion, calling it too elementary, and urges subjecting yourself to the boredom of consistency so the fire will not go out.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [05:19]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Thessalonians 5:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ83. What does the speaker say prayer does for faith?\r\nA. It supplies new doctrines that Bible study alone could not teach.\r\nB. It strengthens conviction about what God can do, even about healing.\r\nC. It replaces the need to read Scripture, since conviction comes from experience alone.\r\nD. It tests faith so that weak believers leave and only the strong remain.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says prayer strengthens your faith, and that if you are no longer sure God heals, you're not praying.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [06:21; 08:11]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Peter 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+5/>\r\n\r\nQ84. What does the speaker say Jude's instruction about 'praying in the Holy Ghost' shows prayer does?\r\nA. It proves the Spirit is present, whether or not the believer builds up faith.\r\nB. It marks true believers off from the mockers described in the letter.\r\nC. It keeps you in the love of God and renews your spiritual energy and fortitude.\r\nD. It guarantees protection from false teachers who creep into the church.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says praying in the Holy Ghost keeps you in the love of God, and that this renews spiritual energy and gives fortitude.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [07:15]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Jude 20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jude+20/>\r\n\r\nQ85. What does the speaker say about resisting the devil in 1 Peter 5:8-9?\r\nA. Resistance means avoiding suffering, since suffering signals a failure to resist.\r\nB. Resistance depends on knowing the enemy's strategies and countering each one.\r\nC. Being steadfast in the faith resists him; some warfare comes from giving a foothold.\r\nD. Resistance requires frequent warfare prayers, which keep believers watchful.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says one resists by being steadfast in the faith, and that some warfare is unnecessary because it comes from having given the devil a foothold.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [09:08]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Peter 5:8–9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+5%3A8%E2%80%939/>\r\n\r\nQ86. Why does the speaker say he prays even when he does not feel like praying?\r\nA. Because he does not want to give the enemy, who seeks advantage, an opening.\r\nB. Because he believes feelings eventually follow if a believer prays long enough.\r\nC. Because he fears losing his salvation if he misses a day of prayer.\r\nD. Because he has promised God a minimum amount of prayer each day.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the enemy is looking for advantage, and he prays even when he does not feel like it so as not to give the devil advantage.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VI — [10:02]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 4:27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+4%3A27/>\r\n\r\nQ87. Which examples does the speaker give to show that Jesus prayed short prayers?\r\nA. 'Take up your bed' (Mark 2:11) and 'Follow Me' (Matthew 4:19).\r\nB. 'Peace be still' (Mark 4:39) and 'Lazarus, come forth' (John 11:43).\r\nC. 'Not my will, but Yours' and 'Let this cup pass from Me' at Gethsemane.\r\nD. 'Let there be light' (Genesis 1:3) and 'It is finished' (John 19:30).\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He names 'Peace be still' (Mark 4:39) and 'Lazarus come forth' (John 11:43) as simple, straightforward prayers.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VII — [00:16]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Mark 4:39 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+4%3A39/>; John 11:43 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+11%3A43/>\r\n\r\nQ88. What balance does the speaker say a believer's Christian work needs?\r\nA. A combination of long and short prayers, plus unceasing communion with God.\r\nB. A combination of private prayers and corporate prayers offered each week.\r\nC. A combination of daily fasting and weekly extended times of silence.\r\nD. A combination of fixed liturgical prayers and spontaneous petitions.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the nutshell answer is that for a believer's Christian work to be balanced there must be a combination of long and short prayers.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VII — [01:11]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 11:41–42 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+11%3A41%E2%80%9342/>; 1 Thessalonians 5:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ89. How does the speaker explain 'pray without ceasing', given that it cannot be taken literally?\r\nA. Pray aloud continually and delegate daily tasks to others wherever possible.\r\nB. Pray in tongues silently so that the mind is free for other tasks.\r\nC. Replace scheduled prayer with short spontaneous prayers throughout the day.\r\nD. A culture of prayer with constant communion alongside scheduled prayer times.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He notes a literal reading would mean never sleeping or eating, so it means incorporating a culture of prayer and constant communication alongside scheduled prayer.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VII — [01:11; 01:57]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Thessalonians 5:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ90. What does the speaker say about Adam's work in Eden?\r\nA. It was carried out in silence, which is the model for prayer during work.\r\nB. It was labour without spiritual meaning, which shows that work is spiritually neutral.\r\nC. It was separate from fellowship, which began only after the fall of humanity.\r\nD. It was in the secret place, so work must not excuse us from fellowship with God.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Adam's work was in Eden, in the secret place, so we should ensure work is not an excuse from fellowship with the Lord.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VII — [02:44]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 11:18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+11%3A18/>\r\n\r\nQ91. What does the parable of the sleeping friend at the door illustrate in the speaker's teaching on persistence?\r\nA. Importunity: persistence in asking brings a response, alongside consistency.\r\nB. God responds quickly to friends but slowly to strangers.\r\nC. God is reluctant like the sleeper and must be pressured into responding.\r\nD. God answers only after the believer has found exactly the right words.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the parable gives a picture of importunity: with consistency the sleeper eventually responds, so keep on asking.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VII — [03:39]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 11:5–8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+11%3A5%E2%80%938/>\r\n\r\nQ92. Before saying 'Lazarus, come forth,' what does the speaker say Jesus had done?\r\nA. Prayed once, briefly, and then spoke with confident authority.\r\nB. Prayed for four days about the situation before speaking.\r\nC. Waited for the Father to give an audible instruction.\r\nD. Fasted for forty days to prepare for raising the dead.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says short prayers were directed at situations but that before saying 'Lazarus, come forth' you spend four days praying about it.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VII — [03:39]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 11:18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+11%3A18/>\r\n\r\nQ93. What does the speaker mean by 'we pray long so that we can pray short'?\r\nA. Long prayers can later be summarised in short ones that have the same effect.\r\nB. Short, mighty declarations are sponsored by a robust, private prayer life.\r\nC. Believers should pray long first and are then excused from praying again that day.\r\nD. Short prayers are only a stepping stone that long prayer is meant to replace.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says short prayers of faith, mighty declarations, are sponsored by a robust, private, secret prayer life.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VII — [04:42]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 6:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+6%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ94. What does the speaker say is the purpose of long praying?\r\nA. Not to overcome God's reluctance, but to generate divine energy to get things done.\r\nB. To exhaust the believer's own desires so that God's will can be accepted.\r\nC. To show God that the believer is serious enough to deserve an answer.\r\nD. To inform God of the details of a situation so that He can respond accurately.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says long praying is not so God can give up His reluctance but a mechanism for generating the divine energy needed to get things done.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VII — [05:35]\r\n\r\nQ95. What does the speaker identify as a reason many people struggle with long prayer?\r\nA. They have not learned to pray in tongues for extended periods.\r\nB. They believe long prayers are for ministers and not for ordinary believers.\r\nC. They have not been taught the biblical vocabulary needed to sustain long prayer.\r\nD. All they know is making requests, treating God like an ATM that answers quickly.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says all many know is making requests, so they ask why not say it in five sentences, expecting an 'ATM machine of a God' to answer; intimacy comes first.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VII — [06:28; 07:12]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Psalm 27:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+27%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ96. What point does the speaker make about prayer in heaven?\r\nA. There will still be prayer, but not requests such as healing or breakthrough.\r\nB. There will be no prayer, since everything the believer needs will be present.\r\nC. There will be prayer, and it will be mainly intercession for those still on earth.\r\nD. There will be prayer, but only from those who prayed at length while on earth.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says in heaven there will still be prayers, but not prayers of requests, since no healing or breakthrough will be needed.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VII — [07:12]\r\n\r\nQ97. What stood out to the speaker in the showdown between Elijah and the prophets of Baal?\r\nA. How loudly Elijah prayed compared with the quiet of the prophets of Baal.\r\nB. How promptly God responded compared with the long, fruitless wait of Baal's prophets.\r\nC. How many sacrifices Elijah offered compared with the single bull of the prophets.\r\nD. How long Elijah fasted before the fire fell compared with the prophets of Baal.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says what stood out was how prompt God's response was compared with how long Baal's prophets waited in vain, and that promptness separated the true God.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VIII — [00:16; 01:55]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Kings 18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Kings+18/>\r\n\r\nQ98. What do the actions of Baal's prophets (shouting, leaping, cutting themselves) show about prayer?\r\nA. Persistence, even at great cost, is what eventually moves God to respond.\r\nB. Physical sacrifice makes prayer effective when it is joined with fervent words.\r\nC. Efficacy is not in how loud or dramatic prayer is, though emotion has its place.\r\nD. Emotional expression in prayer should be avoided because it resembles idolatry.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the efficacy of prayer is not in how loud or dramatic it is, while noting he is a strong advocate for expressing emotions in devotion.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VIII — [01:08]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Kings 18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Kings+18/>\r\n\r\nQ99. How does the speaker describe Elijah's prayer in 1 Kings 18:37?\r\nA. A silent prayer offered after Elijah had fasted for many days.\r\nB. A simple prayer, 'Hear me,' after which the fire of the Lord fell.\r\nC. A loud prayer accompanied by dramatic movement around the altar.\r\nD. A long prayer of repetition, after which the fire came at dusk.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Elijah prayed a simple prayer, 'Hear me,' and the fire of the Lord fell and consumed the sacrifice.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VIII — [01:55]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Kings 18:37 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Kings+18%3A37/>\r\n\r\nQ100. In Matthew 6:7, what distinction is Jesus drawing, according to the speaker?\r\nA. Between fixed prayers recited from memory and prayers freshly composed.\r\nB. Between private prayers and public prayers offered in the synagogue.\r\nC. Between His children's prayers and the much speaking of false-god worshipers.\r\nD. Between prayers of request and prayers of thanksgiving and praise.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Jesus distinguishes the prayer of His children from that of false gods' worshipers, who think they shall be heard for their much speaking.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VIII — [01:55; 02:48]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 6:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+6%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ101. What does the speaker say about the length of prayer and God's answers?\r\nA. God answers promptly only those who have first prayed long enough to warm up.\r\nB. God answers promptly only when prayer is also loud and dramatic.\r\nC. God answers in proportion to the hours given, as Jesus' long prayers show.\r\nD. Length is not what moves God; He answers promptly, and prayer is no formula.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He rejects a formula where words said at a certain length and speed compel God, and says it is not the length that incentivizes God to answer.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VIII — [03:33; 04:25]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+19/>\r\n\r\nQ102. What does the Acts 19 example of Diana's worshipers calling out for two hours show?\r\nA. People can pray at length to false gods, so length alone is not what moves God.\r\nB. Two hours is the minimum length for a prayer to carry spiritual authority.\r\nC. Repetition is more effective when it is directed toward a true object of worship.\r\nD. Public prayer in a crowd is more effective than prayer offered privately.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He cites Diana's worshipers chanting for two straight hours to show it is not really about the length.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VIII — [04:25]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+19/>\r\n\r\nQ103. What balance does the speaker urge regarding 'God help me' prayers and long prayer?\r\nA. Teach liturgy and form first, so that time with God follows naturally.\r\nB. Teach long prayer as the standard and treat short prayers as a sign of weakness.\r\nC. Say 'God help me' alone isn't enough, but stress time with God over mechanics.\r\nD. Teach short prayers as the ideal and avoid emphasizing time spent with God.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says if 'God help me' is all there is, you're in trouble, but that the emphasis must be on spending time with God, not the mechanics of long praying.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VIII — [05:24]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+19/>\r\n\r\nQ104. What warning does the speaker draw from Jesus saying, 'Now is the hour in the time of darkness'?\r\nA. Spiritual darkness arrives only for those who have abandoned prayer altogether.\r\nB. Prayer offered at night carries more power than prayer offered by day.\r\nC. Jesus was telling the disciples to abandon prayer and go back to sleep.\r\nD. It is possible to be too late to pray if you do not pray when you ought to.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says if you don't pray when you ought to, it will be too late, noting Jesus' words to the sleeping disciples.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VIII — [06:19]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 22:53 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+22%3A53/>\r\n\r\nQ105. What advice does the speaker give to a discouraged person who has strayed from God and thinks they have little time?\r\nA. Spend the time confessing every failure before making any request.\r\nB. Combine the minutes with fasting so that the prayer carries more authority.\r\nC. Wait until you can set aside a full hour so that the prayer is not rushed.\r\nD. Put your heart into even ten or fifteen minutes and invest your emotions.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says even with 15 or 10 minutes, put your heart in it and invest your emotions and watch things turn around.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER VIII — [07:05]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Joel 2:12-13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Joel+2%3A12-13/>\r\n\r\nQ106. How does the speaker define worship in relation to prayer?\r\nA. Worship prepares the heart for prayer but remains a separate activity from it.\r\nB. Worship is prayer that is performed by a group rather than by an individual.\r\nC. Worship is prayer with a melody, and the melody does not lessen its efficacy.\r\nD. Worship is a lesser form of prayer that is used mainly for giving thanks.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says worship is really just prayer with a melody to it and asks whether a melody would make it less efficacious.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IX — [00:16; 01:21]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 5:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+5%3A19/>\r\n\r\nQ107. How does the speaker use heaven to argue for including worship in a believer's schedule?\r\nA. Worship in heaven demands training, so earthly worship should be perfected.\r\nB. Heaven's worship is led by angels, so believers need only observe it.\r\nC. Worship in heaven will replace prayer, so believers should practise it first.\r\nD. Heaven centers on worship, not requests, so worship belongs in our schedule.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says in heaven there will be no requests for healing, the preoccupation is worship of the Father, so incorporate it into your schedule.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IX — [01:21]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Revelation 4:8–11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+4%3A8%E2%80%9311/>\r\n\r\nQ108. What assumption does the speaker challenge when he says worship's ease does not make it less efficacious?\r\nA. That anything done alone is more sincere than anything done with others.\r\nB. That anything planned in advance is less spiritual than what is spontaneous.\r\nC. That anything harder brings us closer to God.\r\nD. That anything shorter is less pleasing to God.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the fact worship seems easier or more enjoyable doesn't make it less efficacious, since we feel anything harder brings us closer to God, which isn't necessarily so.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IX — [02:17]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Philippians 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+4/>\r\n\r\nQ109. According to the speaker, how do many Christians wrongly treat thanksgiving in Philippians 4:6?\r\nA. As an addendum to the main prayer, when it is itself a type of prayer.\r\nB. As unnecessary after supplication, since requests already express faith.\r\nC. As a private matter that does not belong in corporate prayer.\r\nD. As the main element of prayer, with requests treated as optional.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says many treat prayer and supplication as main and thanksgiving as an addendum, but thanksgiving is literally a type of prayer.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IX — [02:17; 03:04]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Philippians 4:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+4%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ110. What pattern does the speaker point to among ministers who carry a rich atmosphere of God's glory?\r\nA. They are known for fasting more often than other believers.\r\nB. They are known mainly for studying Scripture more than other ministers.\r\nC. They are known for vibrant, deep worship, healing ministers included.\r\nD. They are known for prayer lasting many hours without any singing.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says anyone known for a rich atmosphere of God's glory is known for vibrant, deep worship, and notable healing ministers make worship crucial to their devotion.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IX — [03:04; 04:03]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Philippians 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+4/>\r\n\r\nQ111. Why does the speaker suggest singing seven songs to the Lord daily?\r\nA. It is the minimum that guarantees angelic encounters and answered prayer.\r\nB. It matches the seven days of the week as a pattern for devotion.\r\nC. It is the number prescribed in Scripture for a complete session of worship.\r\nD. A definite yardstick that gives a goal and an average time, not a magic number.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He offers seven songs as a personal yardstick that provides a goal and an average time of about 40 minutes, while saying it is not a magic number.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IX — [04:03; 04:57]\r\n\r\nQ112. What does the speaker tell people to do while they worship?\r\nA. Close their eyes and avoid thinking, so that the Spirit can lead freely.\r\nB. Repeat each song until they feel an emotional response before moving on.\r\nC. Concentrate on the melody and rhythm so that their worship stays varied.\r\nD. Think deeply about God and the lyrics, and invest their emotions.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says to think deeply about God and the lyrics and let there be a deep, rich investment of emotions.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IX — [05:56]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Colossians 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+3%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ113. What does the speaker call a warning sign in a believer's worship life?\r\nA. Going a week or two without a deep, intense moment of worship.\r\nB. Going a week without hearing the testimony of another believer in church.\r\nC. Going a month without learning a new song for their playlist or list.\r\nD. Going three days without any scheduled worship time during the week.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says it is a warning sign if a week or 14 days pass without a deep, intense moment of worship where the soul's texture feels soft.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER IX — [06:52]\r\n\r\nQ114. According to the hypertrophy illustration, what must happen for muscles to grow bigger?\r\nA. The muscle must be rested for long periods after each intense session.\r\nB. The muscle must be worked until it feels sore, regardless of the weight.\r\nC. Stress it enough, near failure, for tissue to break a little and regrow bigger.\r\nD. The muscle must be exercised daily at a light weight with many repetitions.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He explains that muscle must be stressed enough, almost to failure, so that tissues break a little and regrow bigger.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER X — [00:16; 01:00]\r\n\r\nQ115. What two things does the speaker say are important in prayer?\r\nA. Frequency and location, meaning how often one prays and where one prays.\r\nB. Intensity and eloquence, meaning how passionately and how well one prays.\r\nC. Emotion and duration, meaning how much one feels and how long one prays.\r\nD. Volume and consistency, meaning praying enough and doing so regularly.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says that in prayer two things are very important: volume and consistency.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER X — [02:48]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Thessalonians 5:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A17/>; Luke 6:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+6%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ116. What does the gym-outfit story (nice outfits, a few photos, then months of absence) illustrate?\r\nA. Occasional showing up gets no results; consistency matters as much as volume.\r\nB. Public displays of devotion are less valuable than private consistency.\r\nC. Appearance and preparation matter as much as the work done in prayer.\r\nD. Beginners should start small and increase gradually without expecting results.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says people lift a few things, take pictures, then vanish for a year, and you can't gain results that way, so consistency matters as well as volume.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER X — [02:48; 03:34]\r\n\r\nQ117. What do the battery picture and James 5:16b say about prayer?\r\nA. Prayer converts emotional fervor into power that fades by the next day.\r\nB. Prayer makes tremendous power available, building up like spiritual energy.\r\nC. Prayer drains energy that must be restored through rest and fasting.\r\nD. Prayer powers only the moment it is offered and does not carry over.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says prayer works like a battery of spiritual energy and cites James 5:16b as showing prayer has a cumulative effect, a build-up.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER X — [04:25]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: James 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+5/>\r\n\r\nQ118. What caveat does the speaker give when comparing a believer of ten or fifteen years with one of five years?\r\nA. The newer believer matches them as long as he has had more dramatic experiences.\r\nB. The newer believer has an advantage because fresh zeal outweighs older practice.\r\nC. The newer believer cannot catch up because earlier years of prayer cannot be replaced.\r\nD. A newer believer may match them with a better teacher and more spiritual exercise.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the longer-serving believer usually has more investment, except if the newer believer has a better teacher, quicker understanding, and exercises more.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER X — [05:25]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: James 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+5/>\r\n\r\nQ119. What does the 'brick upon brick' building image from Jude illustrate?\r\nA. Spiritual growth depends on others laying the foundation on the believer's behalf.\r\nB. Spiritual growth is completed once the believer reaches a particular level of maturity.\r\nC. Spiritual growth should be inspected regularly, like a building under construction.\r\nD. Spiritual investment adds a new layer or level in your spirit, like a rising building.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the Greek building term means laying brick upon brick, and that spiritual investment can add a new layer or level in your spirit.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER X — [06:28; 07:21]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Jude 20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jude+20/>\r\n\r\nQ120. What is the speaker's 'more controversial' opinion about consistency versus length?\r\nA. Praying once a month at length is better than praying briefly and daily.\r\nB. Twenty daily minutes yield a healthier walk with God than seven hours monthly.\r\nC. Praying seven hours monthly is equivalent to twenty minutes of daily prayer.\r\nD. Praying forty-five minutes daily is the minimum for a healthy walk with God.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says a consistent, even shorter, daily time yields more than praying long once in a while, adding that this is not an excuse to reduce how long you pray.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER X — [07:21; 08:32]\r\n\r\nQ121. How does the speaker say you can know from your spirit that you have prayed enough?\r\nA. When your emotions have settled and you feel at peace.\r\nB. When you have prayed for the amount of time you set at the start.\r\nC. When another believer confirms that the prayer has reached its mark.\r\nD. Through a note of victory: you simply know it is done.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says it is a spiritual thing, a note of victory: you just know it is done, and warns that the devil can torment people into thinking prayer is never enough.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER X — [09:30]\r\n\r\nQ122. What does Jesus praying three times at Gethsemane show, according to the speaker?\r\nA. You can split a prayer into sessions and return later, even if not done.\r\nB. A prayer should always be completed in a single sitting to remain effective.\r\nC. A prayer repeated three times shows that the first two were not heard.\r\nD. A prayer must be repeated exactly three times before it is complete.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Jesus divided His prayer into three, so you can adjourn and come back, and need not pray it through in one stretch.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER X — [10:30]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 26:36–44 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+26%3A36%E2%80%9344/>\r\n\r\nQ123. What does the speaker say happens when the devil learns you can stick to a matter for twenty-one days?\r\nA. He will increase his attacks to test whether your commitment is genuine.\r\nB. He will attack you only through those closest to you rather than directly.\r\nC. He will play with you less and come only when he is sure you are ready.\r\nD. He will leave you alone permanently once you complete a full prayer cycle.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says when the devil learns you know how to stick to a matter for 21 days, he will play with you less and only come if he is sure you are ready.\r\nREF: SCHOOL OF PRAYER X — [11:21]\n\nDECK: Discipleship Experience (Section 7) Bible Experience I\r\n\r\nQ1. According to Biblical Interpretation, the defining quality God prioritizes in those entrusted to pass on doctrine is:\r\nA. Charisma\r\nB. Anointing\r\nC. Faithfulness\r\nD. Eloquence\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson prioritizes faithfulness in preserving doctrine and handing it on.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION III — [11:56]–[14:32] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Timothy 2:2–3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+2%3A2%E2%80%933/>; Deuteronomy 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+4/>; Galatians 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+1%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ2. Final authority of Scripture means that the Word of God has:\r\nA. The last word on all matters of faith, doctrine and practice\r\nB. The final word wherever questions of right and wrong arise\r\nC. Supreme authority over doctrine and the believer's spiritual life\r\nD. Ultimate authority whenever human reasoning reaches its limits\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says Scripture is the ultimate authority in matters of faith, doctrine, and practice.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW I — [00:16]–[01:26] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Timothy 3:16-17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+3%3A16-17/>\r\n\r\nQ3. According to the lesson on hermeneutics, the apostles and prophets were given the responsibility of:\r\nA. Creating new doctrine based on their personal encounters with God\r\nB. Translating Old Testament prophecy into culturally relevant Christian teaching\r\nC. Establishing which Old Testament passages should remain authoritative\r\nD. Receiving revelation and laying the foundation of doctrine\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson assigns the apostles and prophets the role of receiving revealed truth and laying doctrine’s foundation.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [17:59]–[18:51] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+3/>\r\n\r\nQ4. The statement \"Doctrine is not your invention\" primarily means that doctrine:\r\nA. Is established independently of the individual who encounters it.\r\nB. Is inherited from the church and therefore should not be questioned.\r\nC. Is determined by the interpretation of Scripture already accepted by believers.\r\nD. Is established before an individual studies Scripture and therefore requires no interpretation.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents doctrine as established independently of an individual’s personal interpretation.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [00:57]–[02:04] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Jude 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jude+3/>\r\n\r\nQ5. A believer who has become established in fundamental doctrine reaches a stage where Bible study should increasingly help them:\r\nA. Discover increasingly radical interpretations of familiar passages\r\nB. Move from personal study into teaching ministry\r\nC. Replace foundational teaching with deeper and more advanced revelations\r\nD. Appreciate what they have always known rather than constantly searching for what is new\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Bible study can deepen appreciation of established truth rather than focus on finding something new.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION I — [01:12]–[02:59] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Peter 1:12-15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Peter+1%3A12-15/>\r\n\r\nQ6. Which of the following is the Greek word for revelation?\r\nA. Rhema\r\nB. Logos\r\nC. Gnosis\r\nD. Apocalypsis\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson identifies apocalypsis as the Greek word for revelation.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [04:41]\r\n\r\nQ7. In Having a Biblical Worldview, \"All Scripture is given by inspiration of God\" establishes primarily that:\r\nA. The human writers were moved by God in the process of writing Scripture.\r\nB. Scripture communicates God's will through the experiences of its writers.\r\nC. God is the source of all Scripture.\r\nD. The content of Scripture ultimately reflects God's intention through human authorship.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson explicitly says inspiration establishes God as the source of Scripture.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW I — [09:00]–[10:11] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 2 Timothy 3:16–17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+3%3A16%E2%80%9317/>\r\n\r\nQ8. According to the lesson on Having a Biblical Worldview, what should happen when a believer discovers that an ideology they hold is wrong?\r\nA. The Word should straighten the ideology rather than the believer searching for a church that reinforces it.\r\nB. The believer should retain the conviction until they receive a clearer personal revelation.\r\nC. The believer should consider whether the ideology is culturally acceptable before changing it.\r\nD. The believer should compare the ideology with the prevailing interpretation within their church.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says the Word should correct a believer’s ideology, not merely confirm it.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [08:44]–[09:26] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Timothy 3:16-17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+3%3A16-17/>\r\n\r\nQ9. The Greek word musterion in Ephesians 3, is explained in the lesson as referring to:\r\nA. A truth that can only be understood by spiritually mature believers\r\nB. A truth deliberately withheld because God had not yet decided to reveal it\r\nC. A supernatural truth that remains inaccessible through ordinary reading\r\nD. Something concealed that is now revealed\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson defines musterion as something concealed that is now revealed.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [01:06]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Ephesians 3:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+3%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ10. The Latin expression for faith alone is:\r\nA. Sola Scriptura\r\nB. Sola Gratia\r\nC. Soli Deo Gloria\r\nD. Sola Fide\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson gives sola fide as the Latin expression for faith alone.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW I — [03:38]\r\n\r\nQ11. The phrase \"retain it in its originality and hand it over to people who will hand it over to others\" is presented as the practical evidence of:\r\nA. Faithfulness to an orthodox faith that is not subject to personal invention\r\nB. Spiritual maturity demonstrated through the ability to reproduce one's interpretation\r\nC. Apostolic authority demonstrated through successive generations of teachers\r\nD. Accurate Bible study demonstrated through the ability to explain Scripture creatively\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson identifies preserving and passing on doctrine without inventing new interpretations as evidence of faithfulness.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION III — [11:56]–[14:32] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Timothy 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+2/>; Deuteronomy 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+4/>; Galatians 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+1/>\r\n\r\nQ12. The lesson on hermeneutics describes Christ as \"the most important piece in Bible interpretation.\" What does this mean within the specific argument being made?\r\nA. Every biblical passage must contain the literal name \"Jesus\" before it can be interpreted\r\nB. Christ provides the interpretive key for understanding the Old Testament's types, shadows and symbols\r\nC. The New Testament should be studied independently of the Old Testament because Christ replaced its message\r\nD. Every Old Testament passage should be interpreted exclusively as a direct prediction of Jesus' earthly ministry\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents Christ as the key to understanding the Old Testament’s symbols and types.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) I — [13:48]–[16:49] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+4/>\r\n\r\nQ13. In Having a Biblical Worldview, the word \"so\" in John 3:16 is explained as primarily communicating:\r\nA. The intensity with which God loved the world\r\nB. The magnitude of God's love for humanity\r\nC. The depth of God's emotional affection toward the world\r\nD. The manner in which God demonstrated His love\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson explains “so” as describing how God showed His love, rather than its intensity.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION II — [09:24]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ14. God's plan for the church, as presented in the lesson on Bible Interpretation, is that believers:\r\nA. Become sufficiently grounded to recognize sound teaching when they hear it\r\nB. Come into the unanimity of the faith with accuracy and clarity\r\nC. Become increasingly dependent on the teaching ministry of their pastor\r\nD. Develop enough personal revelation to contribute meaningfully when they gather\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson describes the church’s goal as believers sharing the faith with accuracy and clarity.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION I — [00:16]–[01:12] (approximate range)\r\n\r\nQ15. Why does the lesson on Having a Biblical Worldview connect Paul's statement about failing to provide for one's relatives with denying the faith?\r\nA. Because Christian families have a specific obligation to financially support one another\r\nB. Because practical care is one of the strongest ways believers demonstrate their righteousness.\r\nC. Because certain conduct is so inconsistent with professed faith that its absence can contradict the profession itself.\r\nD. Because failure to care for relatives demonstrates that the believer has misunderstood the doctrine of grace.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson treats the absence of practical care in this example as inconsistent with a profession of Christian faith.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [14:41]–[15:58] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Timothy 5:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+5%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ16. What is the strongest indication that biblical interpretation is being done correctly?\r\nA. The believer becomes more confident when defending doctrine\r\nB. The believer develops greater knowledge of historical and theological details\r\nC. The believer becomes increasingly skilled at identifying erroneous interpretations\r\nD. The knowledge drives the believer toward worship and greater response to God\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says correctly interpreted knowledge should lead to worship and a greater response to God.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION III — [16:44]–[20:09] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 2:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+2%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ17. What does \"where the Spirit of the Lord is, there is liberty\" mean in its immediate interpretive context?\r\nA. Freedom to express oneself physically during church services\r\nB. Freedom from the veil so that believers can behold the glory and intent of God's writings\r\nC. Freedom from the need to interpret Scripture according to its original context\r\nD. Freedom from the authority of the Old Testament because the Spirit has replaced Scripture\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: In context, liberty means the veil is removed so the writings’ meaning and God’s glory can be seen.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) I — [17:45]–[18:32] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 3:14–18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+3%3A14%E2%80%9318/>\r\n\r\nQ18. The lesson on hermeneutics uses the description of Christ as \"the express image of his person\" to emphasize that:\r\nA. Christ's teachings are more practical than those of the prophets\r\nB. Christ represents the cultural fulfillment of Old Testament prophecy\r\nC. Christ is the final prophet through whom the Old Testament can be interpreted\r\nD. In Christ, we understand exactly who God is\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says Christ reveals exactly who God is.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) II — [01:13]–[02:57] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A3/>\r\n\r\nQ19. Which pair correctly matches the biblical statement with the figure of speech identified in the hermeneutics lesson?\r\nA. \"I am the door\" - simile\r\nB. \"Behold, I send you forth as sheep\" - metaphor\r\nC. \"I am the vine\" - hyperbole\r\nD. \"I send you forth as sheep in the midst of wolves\" - simile\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson identifies the comparison using “as” as a simile.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [09:27]–[10:36] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 11:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+11%3A16/>; Matthew 10:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+10%3A16/>; John 10:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+10%3A9/>\r\n\r\nQ20. Which combination accurately reflects the languages identified in the lesson?\r\nA. Old Testament - Hebrew and Aramaic; New Testament - Koine Greek\r\nB. Old Testament - Hebrew and Greek; New Testament - Aramaic and Greek\r\nC. Old Testament - Hebrew only; New Testament - Koine Greek and Latin\r\nD. Old Testament - Aramaic and Latin; New Testament - Hebrew and Koine Greek\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson identifies Hebrew and Aramaic for the Old Testament and Koine Greek for the New Testament.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [05:26]–[06:27] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 1:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+1%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ21. Stephen's description of his Jewish audience as \"uncircumcised in the heart\" is used in the lesson on hermeneutics to establish what distinction?\r\nA. Physical circumcision could exist without the spiritual reality it was intended to signify\r\nB. Physical circumcision was never actually commanded by God\r\nC. The Jews had abandoned the practice of circumcision before Stephen addressed them\r\nD. Circumcision of the heart was exclusively a New Testament doctrine with no Old Testament precedent\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson distinguishes physical circumcision from the spiritual reality of circumcision of the heart.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) II — [14:44]–[16:44] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 7:51 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+7%3A51/>; Ephesians 2:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+2%3A11/>; Philippians 3:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+3%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ22. Which interpretation does the lesson on Hermeneutics explicitly reject concerning the statement \"the letter kills\"?\r\nA. That \"the letter\" specifically refers to the law written on stones\r\nB. That the statement refers to the law in contrast with the ministry of the Spirit\r\nC. That \"the letter\" means the entire Bible is incapable of giving life without a personal rhema\r\nD. That Paul was contrasting the ministry associated with the law with the ministry of the New Testament\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson rejects applying “the letter kills” to the entire Bible or claiming Scripture needs a personal rhema to give life.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) I — [06:32]–[07:19] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3/>; 2 Corinthians 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+3/>; John 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+6/>\r\n\r\nQ23. Which statement most accurately distinguishes \"sundry times\" from \"diverse manners\" according to the lesson?\r\nA. Sundry times concerns different prophets, while diverse manners concerns different generations\r\nB. Sundry times concerns prophecy, while diverse manners concerns Scripture\r\nC. Sundry times refers to communication in many different parts, while diverse manners refers to different modes of communication\r\nD. Sundry times refers to Old Testament communication, while diverse manners refers exclusively to New Testament communication\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson distinguishes communication given in many parts from communication delivered through different modes.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) II — [02:57]–[03:55]; HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) I — [01:46]–[02:44] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ24. The instruction to read \"per book, per chapter\" is specifically intended to prevent the interpreter from:\r\nA. Relying on individual verses instead of the full counsel of Scripture\r\nB. Removing a text from its context and assigning meaning based on the words alone\r\nC. Reading Scripture according to personal experiences and previously held convictions\r\nD. Reading Scripture according to personal experiences and previously held convictions\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Reading by book and chapter helps prevent taking a text out of its context.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION II — [03:33]–[04:26] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Timothy 2:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+2%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ25. Romans 16:16 is primarily an example of which interpretation barrier according to the lesson on Biblical Interpretation?\r\nA. Translation\r\nB. Language\r\nC. Culture\r\nD. Doctrine\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson uses Romans 16:16 to show how culture affects interpretation of a biblical instruction.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [12:45]–[15:57] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 16:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+16%3A16/> | Also cited in lesson segment: John 15:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+15%3A5/>; John 12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+12/>; John 21:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+21%3A25/>; 1 Corinthians 16:21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+16%3A21/>; 1 Corinthians 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+2/>\r\n\r\nQ26. According to the hermeneutics lesson, why can an honest and careful translation still make Bible interpretation difficult?\r\nA. Translators inevitably change the doctrines of the original writers\r\nB. Biblical languages cannot adequately express spiritual realities in English\r\nC. English translations necessarily remove figures of speech from the text\r\nD. Some meaning and nuance can become less immediately clear through translation\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: A faithful translation can still make the intended meaning difficult to understand, so interpretation may be needed.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [05:26]–[06:27]; INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION II — [05:56]–[08:17] (approximate ranges)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 1:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+1%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ27. Which text does the lesson on Bible Interpretation identify as having had an exceptionally significant impact on world history?\r\nA. John 3:16\r\nB. John 1:1\r\nC. 2 Corinthians 2:11\r\nD. Galatians 3:11\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson identifies Galatians 3:11 as having had exceptional historical impact.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION I — [07:05]–[09:26] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Galatians 3:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+3%3A11/> | Also cited in lesson segment: John 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ28. In Having a Biblical Worldview, \"Your word is a lamp unto my feet and a light unto my path\" was used to mean:\r\nA. Scripture gives believers insight into God's specific plan for their future.\r\nB. Scripture orders the believer's steps and determines how they navigate life.\r\nC. Scripture provides principles by which believers can evaluate their circumstances.\r\nD. Scripture gives believers sufficient wisdom to make decisions without external counsel.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson uses the verse to describe Scripture as a guide for navigating life and ordering one’s steps.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW I — [06:51]–[07:50] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Psalm 119:105 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+119%3A105/>\r\n\r\nQ29. Which combination best reflects the progression identified in Isaiah 53 within the lesson on hermeneutics?\r\nA. Christ's incarnation -> earthly ministry -> ascension -> enthronement\r\nB. Christ's rejection and suffering -> bearing of sin -> death -> resurrection implied\r\nC. Israel's rejection -> restoration -> establishment of the church -> final judgment\r\nD. The law -> the prophets -> the gospel -> justification by works\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson traces Christ’s rejection and suffering, sin-bearing, death, and implied resurrection through Isaiah 53.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) II — [03:05]–[07:13] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Isaiah 53 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+10/>; Acts 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+8/>\r\n\r\nQ30. The biblical worldview becomes evident when the Word of God becomes the believer's:\r\nA. Standard for evaluating spiritual experiences\r\nB. Foundation for establishing doctrinal convictions\r\nC. Guide for making decisions concerning Christian practice\r\nD. Lens through which they see the world\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson describes a biblical worldview as seeing the world through the lens of God’s Word.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW I — [06:51]–[07:50] (approximate range)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Timothy 3:16, 17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+3%3A16%2C+17/>\r\n\r\nQ31. The speaker says that, in Protestant circles, the Latin expression sola scriptura means:\r\nA. Faith alone\r\nB. Grace alone\r\nC. Scripture alone\r\nD. To the glory of God alone\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker identifies sola scriptura as the Latin expression for Scripture alone, distinct from the faith, grace and glory-of-God expressions.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW I — [02:38]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Timothy 3:16-17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+3%3A16-17/>\r\n\r\nQ32. According to the speaker, what happened when only a few people had access to the Scriptures?\r\nA. Church practice stayed more carefully tied to what Scripture taught\r\nB. Church leaders became more cautious about adding new practices to worship\r\nC. Guardrails were easily removed and extremes were entertained in the church\r\nD. Believers became more devoted to studying the Latin text of the Bible\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says limited access made it easy for guardrails to be taken out of the way and for extremes to be entertained, which the Reformers opposed.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW I — [02:38]\r\n\r\nQ33. Which set of expressions does the speaker list as the Reformers' Latin 'alone' statements?\r\nA. Faith alone, grace alone, to the glory of Christ alone, and the church alone\r\nB. Faith alone, works alone, to the glory of God alone, and Scripture alone\r\nC. Faith alone, grace alone, the priesthood alone, and Scripture alone\r\nD. Faith alone, grace alone, to the glory of God alone, and Scripture alone\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Describing the Reformers' conviction, the speaker lists sola fide (faith alone), grace alone, to the glory of God alone, and Scripture alone.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW I — [03:38]\r\n\r\nQ34. Which practice does the speaker give as an example of the church doing things that, in the Reformers' view, were not biblical?\r\nA. Allowing ordinary believers to read the Bible in their own language\r\nB. Charging payments for sin at different prices to lift a person's sins\r\nC. Refusing to recant when pressured by religious authorities\r\nD. Teaching that the just shall live by faith and by grace alone\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker's example is the payments made for sin, with different prices for different sins, after which the sins would supposedly be lifted.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW I — [02:38; 03:38]\r\n\r\nQ35. Why does the speaker say believers should understand how far church history has come?\r\nA. So they will not undo progress won by people who died for believers' liberty\r\nB. So they can trace which denominations keep the oldest church practices\r\nC. So they can finish correcting the errors the Reformers left unaddressed\r\nD. So they can decide which church traditions deserve equal weight with Scripture\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says to understand church history so that we will not set back the progress people made, since they died for the liberty believers have in Christ.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW I — [03:38; 04:35]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+4/>\r\n\r\nQ36. According to the lesson on the final authority of Scripture, what must be judged by the authority of the Scriptures?\r\nA. Only church traditions, since experiences are guided by the Holy Spirit\r\nB. Every experience and every church tradition, however long it has been practiced\r\nC. Only personal experiences, since church traditions are protected by their age\r\nD. Doctrine and practice, but not claims of dreams and visions\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker states that every experience must be judged by the authority of Scripture, and that church traditions must be judged by it no matter how long they have been practiced.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW I — [04:35]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+4/>\r\n\r\nQ37. What point does the speaker make with the Samaritan woman's words, 'Our fathers worshipped on this mountain'?\r\nA. Jesus approved worship that had been handed down over centuries\r\nB. Long-standing devotional practice gives a tradition its authority\r\nC. The Samaritans' descent from Abraham made their worship acceptable\r\nD. A practice can continue for centuries and still be wrong\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says she referred to centuries of devotional practice, yet Jesus told her she did not know what she worshipped, showing that something can be done for centuries and be wrong.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW I — [05:49]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+4/>\r\n\r\nQ38. How does the speaker respond to believers who say 'put scripture aside' when explaining what they want to do?\r\nA. Scripture may yield to personal conviction on matters the Bible does not mention\r\nB. Scripture may be set aside once a believer has prayed about the decision\r\nC. Scripture should be set aside only when a church tradition disagrees with it\r\nD. Scripture is never put aside; the Word of God must influence how a believer reasons\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the Word of God must influence how you reason and that you never put scripture aside again.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW I — [07:50]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Timothy 3:16, 17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+3%3A16%2C+17/>\r\n\r\nQ39. How does the speaker answer the objection that the Bible cannot be God's word because it was written by men?\r\nA. Only the portions written by prophets count as God's word, while the rest is human\r\nB. It can be both, because God is sovereign and can work through the freedom of men\r\nC. God dictated the text word for word, so the writers' lives played no part in it\r\nD. The writers' own views are mixed into the text, so readers must separate them out\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker answers that it can be both, because God is sovereign and can walk with the freedom of men, arranging the writers' experiences so that what they wrote was His will.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW I — [11:02; 11:54]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Peter 1:20-21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Peter+1%3A20-21/>\r\n\r\nQ40. What does the speaker use the story of Joseph to illustrate about God's leading?\r\nA. A dream from God must be explained from Scripture before anyone can act on it\r\nB. God's purposes depend on people making the right political choices at key moments\r\nC. People can act in ways they think are free while their actions advance God's purpose\r\nD. God always gives explicit step-by-step directions before a person acts on a dream\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says God did not have to tell Joseph to go to Egypt; everyone acted in a manner they thought free, yet contributed to the prophetic story and its intended end.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW I — [10:11; 11:02]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 50:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+50%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ41. According to the speaker, how did God change Peter's belief that salvation was for the Jews alone?\r\nA. By a trance of animals, then men from Cornelius's house arriving\r\nB. By explaining from the Scriptures that salvation is for all men\r\nC. By a letter from church leaders in Jerusalem urging him to go\r\nD. By Cornelius's household arguing the point from the Old Testament\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says God did not explain from Scripture but showed Peter a trance of animals, and the people from Cornelius's house were already at his door when he woke.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW I — [12:46]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10/>\r\n\r\nQ42. Once you have established what the Word of God says, the speaker says that is:\r\nA. The end of all arguments\r\nB. A starting point to be weighed against tradition\r\nC. The start of a deeper debate\r\nD. The point at which personal experience should be consulted\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that the moment you establish what the Word of God says, that is the end of all arguments.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW I — [01:26]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Timothy 3:16-17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+3%3A16-17/>\r\n\r\nQ43. What does the speaker say 'profitable for doctrine' means for someone reading the Bible?\r\nA. The believer is given freedom to choose between competing teachings\r\nB. Each reader receives a personal meaning suited to their circumstances\r\nC. Reading the Bible, the believer arrives at a doctrinal position\r\nD. The believer is prepared to defend any church's position\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that 'profitable for doctrine' means that when I read the Bible, I arrive at a doctrinal position.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [00:17]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Timothy 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+3%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ44. What contrast does the speaker draw between Catholics, Reformed, Lutherans and Methodists on one hand and Pentecostals on the other?\r\nA. The first groups disagree among themselves, while Pentecostals agree on every point\r\nB. The first groups hold uniform beliefs, while Pentecostals have no written beliefs\r\nC. The first groups rely on tradition, while Pentecostals rely on Scripture alone\r\nD. The others know what they believe, but for Pentecostals it depends on the church\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Catholics, Reformed, Lutherans and Methodists know what they believe, but among Pentecostals it depends on the church you attend.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [00:50; 00:57]\r\n\r\nQ45. When reading the Bible, what does the speaker say he wants to understand about Paul?\r\nA. How Paul's teaching has been applied within each denomination\r\nB. What Paul actually believed, since some preach things Paul never said\r\nC. What Paul would say about the questions raised by modern culture\r\nD. Which of Paul's letters carry the most authority for the church today\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says he wants to see and understand what Paul believed, noting that some people have accused Paul of saying things he never said.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [04:16]\r\n\r\nQ46. What does the speaker say about the Spirit of God in relation to the written Word?\r\nA. The Spirit of God will never contradict the written Word\r\nB. The Spirit guides each believer's doctrine apart from the written Word\r\nC. The Spirit may extend the written Word with teachings it does not contain\r\nD. The Spirit may correct the written Word where it is unclear\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker insists doctrine is established, not 'as the Spirit leads', and that the Spirit of God will never contradict the written word.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [01:39; 01:55]\r\n\r\nQ47. How does the speaker define doctrine?\r\nA. A teaching adopted whenever it feels spiritually fresh\r\nB. A set of opinions agreed upon by a local congregation\r\nC. A personal conviction formed through private revelation\r\nD. A long-established, inherited way of thinking or acting\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker states that doctrine is a long established, inherited way of thinking or acting.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [04:06]\r\n\r\nQ48. What criticism does the speaker make about the term 'rhema'?\r\nA. The term describes the way Paul's letters were first received\r\nB. The term wrongly replaces the Greek word for the written Scriptures\r\nC. Such phrases have been coined to excuse exegetical understanding of the Bible\r\nD. The term is a legitimate name for the Spirit's present-day guidance\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says we have coined phrases like rhema to excuse exegetical understanding of the Bible, and that people who don't know Greek have confused us.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [04:41]\r\n\r\nQ49. What does the speaker say the Greek word translated 'reproof' in 2 Timothy 3:16 actually means?\r\nA. Instruction, in the sense of teaching righteousness\r\nB. Conviction, so that it could be translated 'proof'\r\nC. Correction, as most readers assume it means\r\nD. Rebuke, in the sense of a sharp spoken warning\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Using the lexicon, the speaker says people assume reproof means correction, but the Greek word actually means conviction and should be translated proof.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [05:30; 06:02]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Timothy 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+3%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ50. When explaining God to atheists, which does the speaker call the ultimate evidence?\r\nA. The Bible\r\nB. Nature\r\nC. Personal experiences\r\nD. Good arguments\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker thanks God for experiences and good arguments and says nature can be used, but the Bible is the ultimate evidence and the reason he believes.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [06:17; 07:16]\r\n\r\nQ51. How does the speaker say the first page of the Bible treats the idea that there is no God?\r\nA. It leaves the question open until later books give their answer\r\nB. It argues from the order of creation to prove that God exists\r\nC. It addresses the idea briefly before moving on to creation\r\nD. It discredits the idea and simply begins with God creating the world\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the first page discounts and discredits any impression that there is no God, and does not even pay attention to it.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [06:44]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+1%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ52. The speaker says the Greek word for 'correction' in 2 Timothy 3:16 means:\r\nA. To remove what is unnecessary\r\nB. To restore what is lost\r\nC. To build up what is weak\r\nD. To straighten what is bent\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the Greek word used for correction means to straighten what is bent, illustrating it with setting a broken bone.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [08:18]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Timothy 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+3%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ53. What does the speaker say is missing if the voice of the Holy Spirit is only ever 'pampering' you?\r\nA. Greater emphasis on the believer's feelings\r\nB. Sharp correction, given in love\r\nC. Deeper reassurance of the believer's identity\r\nD. A clearer sense of the Spirit's gifts\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that if the Spirit's voice is only always pampering you, something is missing; there should be sharp correction in love.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [09:26; 09:46]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 16:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+16%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ54. How does the speaker describe 'instruction in righteousness' for a child of God?\r\nA. It is a suggestion that culture may adjust\r\nB. It is an ideal believers approach over time\r\nC. It is an instruction, not a suggestion\r\nD. It is a guideline that varies between denominations\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that for a child of God it is not a suggestion but an instruction, even where culture holds varying opinions.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [09:46]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Timothy 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+3%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ55. What point does the speaker make with the examples of toothpaste on a beard, clothes worn inside out, and mismatched shoes?\r\nA. Love overlooks small flaws so that people are not embarrassed in public\r\nB. Acceptance is the fullest and safest expression of love in a home\r\nC. Love tells a person the truth gently instead of simply accepting them\r\nD. Correction belongs to pastors, not to family members or friends\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Answering the idea that loving means accepting everyone, the speaker says the onus is on someone who loves you to tell you as gently as possible.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [11:30; 12:40]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 4:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+4%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ56. What does the speaker's illustration about raising children, who cry when stopped from touching dangerous things, show?\r\nA. Love is measured by how quickly a child is comforted afterward\r\nB. Love sometimes refuses what a person wants, even when it makes them cry\r\nC. Love should shield children from every form of discomfort and tears\r\nD. Love leaves harmful choices to be learned from experience first\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says parents who love their children will not give in when they cry, because it is love that saves them from danger.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [12:56; 13:53]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 12:6-11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+12%3A6-11/>\r\n\r\nQ57. Alongside thanking God for the message of grace, what extreme does the speaker warn against?\r\nA. Suggesting it is okay to have no evidence of faith in one's life\r\nB. Teaching that believers must earn righteousness through works\r\nC. Teaching that grace applies only to those with evidence of faith\r\nD. Denying that the message of grace is prevailing in the church\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cautions against suggesting that having no evidence of faith is okay, saying Christianity is either demonstrative or you are an imposter.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [13:53]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: James 2:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+2%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ58. According to the speaker, what must our knowledge of grace never be allowed to do?\r\nA. Stop us from holding people accountable\r\nB. Weaken our confidence in the Greek lexicon\r\nC. Lessen the importance of good works\r\nD. Replace the call to study Scripture\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker closes by saying we must not come to a point where our knowledge of grace stops us from holding people accountable.\r\nREF: HAVING A BIBLICAL WORLDVIEW II — [17:01]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Galatians 6:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+6%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ59. What does the speaker say about how grounded a person's pastor is in teaching and the things of the Spirit?\r\nA. It determines which believers can be trusted to teach in a gathering\r\nB. It matters little once a believer has had a personal revelation from God\r\nC. It does not matter much, since God wants all believers in the unanimity of the faith\r\nD. It matters most, because believers will rarely be sounder than their pastor\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says it doesn't really matter how grounded your pastor is, because God wants all of us to come into the unanimity of the faith.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION I — [00:16; 01:12]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 4:11-13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+4%3A11-13/>\r\n\r\nQ60. Even after a believer is established in fundamental doctrine, what does the speaker say they will still need?\r\nA. A pastor, and the Word of God must still touch and pierce them\r\nB. A teaching ministry that supplies increasingly radical content\r\nC. Only private study, since new teaching adds nothing further\r\nD. Only new revelations to keep Bible study interesting\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that at that point you will still need a pastor and the Word of God must still touch and pierce you.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION I — [02:05]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 4:11–13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+4%3A11%E2%80%9313/>\r\n\r\nQ61. What does the speaker urge believers to immerse themselves in when he says, 'one thing is needful'?\r\nA. Daily fasting and extended prayer\r\nB. Testimonies of experiences with God\r\nC. Gifts such as the word of knowledge\r\nD. The knowledge of the doctrines of Christ\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says one thing is needful: to immerse yourself in the knowledge of the doctrines of Christ.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION I — [04:55]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 10:42 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+10%3A42/>\r\n\r\nQ62. In the speaker's account of Jesus's temptation, what finally made Satan react?\r\nA. Jesus praying through forty days in the wilderness\r\nB. Jesus quoting the Word and saying, 'It is written'\r\nC. Jesus commanding Satan to leave in His own name\r\nD. Jesus fasting from food during the forty days\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Satan was unperturbed by forty days of prayer and fasting, and was bothered only when Jesus quoted the Word: it is written.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION I — [04:55]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 4:1–11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+4%3A1%E2%80%9311/>\r\n\r\nQ63. Why does the speaker say the priest was so powerful in the Middle Ages?\r\nA. Priests alone were permitted to copy Scripture by hand in the monasteries\r\nB. Kings had granted priests authority over every translation of the Latin Bible\r\nC. Priests alone had been trained to preach in the common language of the people\r\nD. The Bible ran the state, was in a rare language, and only priests could read it\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that the Bible ran the state, was written in rare Latin, and only the priests could read it, making the priest very powerful.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION I — [09:26]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16/>; Galatians 3:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+3%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ64. According to the speaker, what did paying money after a confession supposedly do?\r\nA. Secure a place in the priesthood\r\nB. Remove the penalty of public confession\r\nC. Reduce the person's time in purgatory\r\nD. Cancel the need for baptism\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says people would confess, pay some money, and their time in purgatory would be reduced because they paid.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION I — [10:32]\r\n\r\nQ65. What did the speaker say Martin Luther realized about how salvation comes?\r\nA. It comes only by faith, not by sacraments, relics, prayer or fasting\r\nB. It comes by faith together with observance of the sacraments\r\nC. It comes by faith strengthened by prayer and fasting as well\r\nD. It comes by faith confirmed through a priest's confession to God\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Luther realized salvation does not come by sacraments, relics, prayer or fasting; it comes only by faith, sola fide.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION I — [11:42]\r\n\r\nQ66. About how much did the speaker say the indulgence certificate cost?\r\nA. About six months' salary\r\nB. About a tenth of a person's yearly income\r\nC. About a year's salary\r\nD. About one month's salary\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Describing the priest's collection of money, the speaker says it was like six months salary.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION I — [12:45]\r\n\r\nQ67. According to the speaker, what made Luther's teachings spread when earlier attempts at revolution had not?\r\nA. Support from kings who no longer answered to the church\r\nB. The sudden availability of Latin Bibles\r\nC. The advent of the printing press\r\nD. Approval of the 99 theses by church councils\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes others had tried to start revolutions, but in Luther's time came the advent of the printing press, and his teachings went viral.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION I — [12:45]\r\n\r\nQ68. Which text does the speaker cite in support of Luther's claim that God's plan is priesthood for all men?\r\nA. 2 Timothy 2:2\r\nB. 1 Peter 2:9\r\nC. 1 Timothy 3:13\r\nD. Galatians 3:11\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Luther argued the papacy is a human invention because God's plan is priesthood for all men, citing 1 Peter 2:9.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION I — [13:53]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Peter 2:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+2%3A9/>\r\n\r\nQ69. What disadvantage does the speaker say came with religious freedom and everyone having Bibles?\r\nA. Believers lost interest in reading the Bible for themselves at home\r\nB. Fewer people felt any need to attend church gatherings after that\r\nC. Governments began restricting who was allowed to print Bibles in public\r\nD. Everyone could interpret for themselves, so denominations kept multiplying\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says religious freedom had blessings, but with everyone having Bibles everyone can have their own interpretation, so the movement kept splitting.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION I — [14:42]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Peter 2:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+2%3A9/>\r\n\r\nQ70. What does the speaker's story of the man who read 1 Timothy 3:13 illustrate?\r\nA. A verse about deacons was correctly applied to a believer's schooling\r\nB. A verse about deacons was read as God's order to attend a private university\r\nC. A believer who ignored a verse about deacons remained out of school\r\nD. A verse about church leaders was shown to apply to every believer equally\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the man believed God told him to attend a private university from 'a good degree' in 1 Timothy 3:13, showing why interpretation is important.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION I — [15:59]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Timothy 3:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+3%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ71. What point does the speaker make about the charismatic church where only the pastor speaks in tongues?\r\nA. Tongues is a gift that Scripture reserves for the pastor alone\r\nB. Even those who believe in tongues differ, which shows why interpretation matters\r\nC. Tongue-speaking churches rarely hold a clear doctrine of any kind\r\nD. Speaking in tongues should be limited to those in church leadership\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses the church example to show that even among people who believe in tongues there are different understandings, so Bible interpretation is important.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION I — [17:15]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Timothy 3:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+3%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ72. How does the speaker summarize the nature of the Scriptures as the first reason the Bible must be interpreted?\r\nA. The Bible is the word of God given without any human medium\r\nB. The Bible is a historical record that contains God's word in places\r\nC. The Bible is human reflection on God given across history\r\nD. The Bible is the word of God given in human words in history\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker's first reason is the nature of the Scriptures: the Bible is the word of God given in human words in history.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION II — [00:16]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Peter 1:20-21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Peter+1%3A20-21/>\r\n\r\nQ73. What does the speaker mean by the 'natural side' of the Bible?\r\nA. Literature written in human language to people with a historical context\r\nB. The part of Scripture that describes the natural world and creation\r\nC. The part that readers can understand without the Holy Spirit's help\r\nD. The part limited to Israel's laws and customs in the wilderness\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the Bible has a divine side and a natural side; from the natural side it is a literature written in human language to people with a historical context.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION II — [01:20]\r\n\r\nQ74. What is the first test a text must pass, according to the speaker?\r\nA. It must be filtered by its use in the early church\r\nB. It must be filtered by whether it was first spoken to Israel\r\nC. It must be filtered by whether a believer receives it by revelation\r\nD. It must be filtered by its consistency with what Christ has done\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says a text must first be filtered based on its consistency with what Christ has done, meaning it must be relevant in Christ.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION II — [02:36]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 24:27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A27/>\r\n\r\nQ75. How does the speaker explain why randomly opening the Bible might seem to work once or twice?\r\nA. It shows the Bible can be opened for guidance in any order\r\nB. It shows the Spirit overriding a passage's context when needed\r\nC. It may be God's mercy saving you at your level of understanding\r\nD. It shows a legitimate way of receiving a rhema word from God\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that days of ignorance God winked at, so a success could be the mercy of God functioning in a prophetic way; it is not how to study the Bible.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION II — [03:33]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 17:30 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17%3A30/>\r\n\r\nQ76. According to the speaker, what do you become if you remove a text from its context?\r\nA. A scholar\r\nB. A con man\r\nC. A faithful messenger\r\nD. A prophetic teacher\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says if you remove the text from its context you are left with nothing exact, and you become a con man; many have used the word of God that way.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION II — [04:26]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Timothy 2:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+2%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ77. How does the speaker answer the claim that translation means some things were lost?\r\nA. Translation always loses some nuance, so readers must lean on lexicons\r\nB. The original text was lost, so translators worked from church tradition\r\nC. Translators worked from earlier translations, so meaning was gradually lost\r\nD. That is not true; translators knew the original languages and translated directly\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says translators understood the original language and translated directly rather than translating from a translation.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION II — [05:56]\r\n\r\nQ78. Why does the speaker say faithful translation created a need for interpretation?\r\nA. Translators deliberately simplified difficult expressions for the common reader\r\nB. Translators added explanations the original writers had not given in the text\r\nC. Translators replaced the figures of speech with plain statements of doctrine\r\nD. Faithful, literal translation of expressions did not always convey the meaning\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker argues that in the bid to be faithful, translators translated literally, and literal translation of expressions was not always helpful, hence the need for interpretation.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION II — [07:03; 08:17]\r\n\r\nQ79. What does the speaker say Paul meant by 'flesh' in 'mortify the flesh'?\r\nA. The physical body, to be mutilated\r\nB. The material world\r\nC. The unrenewed mind\r\nD. Sexual desire in particular\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Paul is not asking anyone to mutilate himself; by flesh he meant the unrenewed mind.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION II — [08:17]\r\n\r\nQ80. What does the speaker say about the King James Version as 'God's holy language'?\r\nA. It preserves the very language in which God spoke to the prophets\r\nB. It is just how people spoke then; praying that way is nothing special\r\nC. It is the most accurate English rendering of the original Greek text\r\nD. It is the only version in which prayer is effective before God\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the KJV is simply how they talked at the time; there is nothing special about it, and praying that way does not make you more special.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION II — [10:39]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ81. How many Bible texts does the speaker say give the instruction to greet one another with a holy kiss?\r\nA. Four\r\nB. Two\r\nC. Three\r\nD. Five\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the word of God says it four times and lists Romans 16:16, 1 Corinthians 16:20, 2 Corinthians 13:12 and 1 Thessalonians 5:26.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION II — [12:08]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 16:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+16%3A16/>; 1 Corinthians 16:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+16%3A20/>; 2 Corinthians 13:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+13%3A12/>; 1 Thessalonians 5:26 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A26/>\r\n\r\nQ82. What does the speaker say is the real interpretation of the instruction to greet with a holy kiss?\r\nA. To signal forgiveness between believers who had quarreled\r\nB. To be cordial, which was how cordiality was shown in their day\r\nC. To show reverence through a physical gesture of worship\r\nD. To mark church members as belonging to one spiritual family\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the real interpretation is for you to be cordial; this was the way they showed cordiality in their day.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION II — [13:14]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 16:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+16%3A16/>; 1 Corinthians 16:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+16%3A20/>; 2 Corinthians 13:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+13%3A12/>; 1 Thessalonians 5:26 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A26/>\r\n\r\nQ83. What does the speaker say Paul is doing when he says 'Judge in yourselves' in 1 Corinthians 11:13?\r\nA. Leaving the matter to each believer's private conscience before God\r\nB. Rebuking the church for ignoring an Old Testament command\r\nC. Appealing to what was logically and morally fitting in that day\r\nD. Giving a permanent ordinance for every church in every culture\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Paul is not just giving a firm doctrinal practice but saying something logically and morally consistent in that day, appealing to what was cultural.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION II — [17:57]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Corinthians 11:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+11%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ84. What tension does the speaker raise with the question of whether married people should wear rings?\r\nA. The Bible does not require rings, yet couples wear them and churches bless them\r\nB. The Bible mentions rings only in connection with the Old Testament\r\nC. The Bible requires rings, yet many believers do not wear them at all\r\nD. The Bible forbids rings, yet churches bless them at their weddings\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker asks whether the Bible requires married people to wear rings and notes that even in CCI weddings rings are blessed, though it is not in the Bible.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION II — [19:26]\r\n\r\nQ85. How does the speaker apply the instruction for a wife to submit to her husband across cultures?\r\nA. She decides what submission means in her marriage through prayer alone\r\nB. She does what submission means in her culture, if it doesn't contradict the Word\r\nC. She keeps only the principle and sets aside every cultural expression\r\nD. She follows the exact form of submission described in the New Testament\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says whatever submission means in that culture, as long as it is not in contradiction to the word of God, you do that.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION III — [00:17]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 5:22–24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+5%3A22%E2%80%9324/>\r\n\r\nQ86. Which modern practice does the speaker say the veil in 1 Corinthians 11 is akin to?\r\nA. Wearing a head covering in church today\r\nB. Taking a communion vow\r\nC. Wearing rings\r\nD. Dressing modestly for worship\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says he believes the veil is a little akin to what rings are today.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION III — [00:17]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 11:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+11%3A5/>\r\n\r\nQ87. What does the speaker conclude about the veil from the question, 'What if she is single?'\r\nA. The veil applied only to married women in the Corinthian church\r\nB. The veil was a sign of a woman's wealth and social standing\r\nC. The veil was a symbol of submission, a woman under subjection\r\nD. The veil was a sign of spiritual maturity before the assembly\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker asks what if she is single and says it shows the veil was a symbol of submission and a woman under subjection.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION III — [01:32]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Corinthians 11:14–16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+11%3A14%E2%80%9316/>\r\n\r\nQ88. In 1 Corinthians 11:5, the speaker says an uncovered woman's dishonor is between her and whom?\r\nA. Her head, not God\r\nB. Her husband and God equally\r\nC. God, not her husband\r\nD. The church, not her husband\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the dishonor in verse 5 is between her and her head, not her and God, and was something customary at the time.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION III — [04:50]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 11:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+11%3A5/>\r\n\r\nQ89. What do historians say, according to the speaker, about women who did not cover their hair in that day?\r\nA. They were foreigners living in the city\r\nB. They were priestesses of pagan temples\r\nC. They were slaves of wealthy households\r\nD. They were ladies of easy virtue\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says historians tell us it was ladies of easy virtue who did not cover their hair in those days.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION III — [07:20]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 13:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+13%3A1/>; Romans 16:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+16%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ90. Why, according to the speaker, does Paul bring up praying in this passage?\r\nA. Prayer is the only setting in which covering a woman's head matters\r\nB. Women's prayer needed more protection than men's in public worship\r\nC. A woman's covering is required for her prayer to be heard by God\r\nD. Spirituality is no excuse; devotion should show even more submission\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says you don't use spirituality as an excuse, and your devotion should show submission and loyalty in consistency with your culture.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION III — [07:20]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 8:26–31 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+8%3A26%E2%80%9331/>\r\n\r\nQ91. How does the speaker classify the head-covering issue?\r\nA. A core doctrine of Christianity on which churches must agree\r\nB. An Old Testament law that is now fulfilled and set aside in Christ\r\nC. Not a core doctrine of Christianity, so not something to fight over\r\nD. A matter of private conscience that the Spirit settles for each person\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says this is not one of the core doctrines of Christianity, so it is not something to fight over.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION III — [08:31]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Timothy 2:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+2%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ92. What does the speaker say about the absence of a marking scheme for preaching?\r\nA. No human marking scheme exists, but God has a minimum standard\r\nB. Preaching is judged by the preacher's anointing and nothing else\r\nC. The congregation's response is the only real measure of a sermon\r\nD. God leaves the standard of preaching to each church's own culture\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says nobody may have the nerve to grade a sermon and there is no marking scheme that we know of, but God has one.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION III — [09:48]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Timothy 2:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+2%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ93. What does the speaker say the Greek word translated 'workman' in 2 Timothy 2:15 implies?\r\nA. The preacher should be formally recognized by the church\r\nB. Others should be able to see the preacher's labor in the word\r\nC. The preacher should be paid according to the work he does\r\nD. The preacher should be skilled in public speaking and delivery\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the word translated workman means laborer, meaning we should be able to see your labor in the word.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION III — [10:45]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 2 Timothy 2:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+2%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ94. What does the speaker's story about Deuteronomy 4 plus Galatians 1 equaling five illustrate?\r\nA. Bible study is for finding what the writer meant, not your own expression\r\nB. Personal revelation can safely add to the plain meaning of a text\r\nC. Combining separate passages is the best way to uncover new doctrine\r\nD. Numbers in Scripture carry hidden symbolic meanings for readers\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: After the story, the speaker says you don't come to Bible study to give your own expression; you want to discover what Paul meant.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION III — [13:15; 14:32]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Deuteronomy 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+4/>; Galatians 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+1/> | Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Timothy 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+2/>\r\n\r\nQ95. According to the speaker, what should the Reformation have corrected?\r\nA. The authority of church leaders, while keeping tradition\r\nB. Proper interpretation, while retaining authority\r\nC. The role of the priesthood, while keeping doctrine\r\nD. The translation of the Bible, while keeping interpretation\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the Reformation was not meant to throw away the baby with the bath water; what it should have corrected was proper interpretation, retaining authority.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION III — [14:32]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Deuteronomy 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+4/>; Galatians 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+1/>\r\n\r\nQ96. What does the speaker say is wrong with deciding that a few friends can just gather instead of attending church?\r\nA. Small groups lack the numbers needed for effective worship together\r\nB. Church gatherings are required by civil authority in most nations\r\nC. Friends cannot interpret Scripture without first earning a formal degree\r\nD. God's plan has not changed; there must be a pastor called of God for you\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Citing the gifts given to men, the speaker says God's plan is unchanged and there must be a pastor called, anointed and given to you.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION III — [15:42]\r\n\r\nQ97. Besides anointing, what does the speaker say divinely approved authority needs?\r\nA. Approval from the leadership of a larger denomination\r\nB. Many years of experience as a teacher in another church\r\nC. A proven record of fruitful ministry among the people\r\nD. Some training, formal or informal, in Bible interpretation\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says authority must not just be anointing; there must be some form of training, either formal or informal, in Bible interpretation.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION III — [16:44]\r\n\r\nQ98. What does the speaker say about historical insights from Bible scholars who are not born again?\r\nA. They are useful only for checking the accuracy of translations\r\nB. They should be avoided because they come from unbelieving scholars\r\nC. They may show new things, but it is a waste if it does not point to Jesus\r\nD. They are the most reliable because they are free of devotional bias\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says such scholars show things you don't know, but it's all a waste because it's all meant to point to Jesus.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION III — [18:01]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 2:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+2%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ99. What does the speaker say knowledge of Scripture must do, according to 'grace be multiplied through the knowledge'?\r\nA. Multiply grace, so that Jesus is magnified in our eyes by theology\r\nB. Settle every doctrinal question the believer might raise\r\nC. Increase the believer's standing among those who know less\r\nD. Equip the believer to correct others where they are wrong\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says knowledge must multiply grace, so that Jesus is glorified and magnified in our eyes by theology, rather than puffing us up.\r\nREF: INTRODUCTION TO BIBLICAL INTERPRETATION III — [16:44]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Exodus 2:1–10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+2%3A1%E2%80%9310/>\r\n\r\nQ100. How does the speaker define hermeneutics?\r\nA. The history of how the Bible was translated\r\nB. The study of the original biblical languages\r\nC. The act or art of Bible interpretation\r\nD. The method of preaching from a single verse\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says hermeneutics is simply the act or the art of Bible interpretation.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [00:17]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 3:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+3%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ101. How does the speaker explain the word 'dispensation' in Ephesians 3:2?\r\nA. Think of a treasury, something by which wealth is kept\r\nB. Think of a dispenser, something by which something is released\r\nC. Think of a covenant, something by which a promise is sealed\r\nD. Think of a steward, something by which a house is managed\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains dispensation by saying to think of a dispenser, something by which something is released, so the grace of God is communicated through Paul.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [01:06]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Ephesians 3:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+3%3A2/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 3:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+3%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ102. What picture does the speaker use for the Old Testament's communication of God's mystery?\r\nA. A man who writes a letter and seals it\r\nB. A man who speaks from behind a closed door\r\nC. A man who says something with his mouth covered\r\nD. A man who whispers a message to a friend\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the Old Testament is akin to a man who says something but with his mouth covered, which is why some readers do not understand it.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [01:56]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 3:4-6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+3%3A4-6/>\r\n\r\nQ103. Why does the speaker say the New Testament seems clearer than the Old Testament?\r\nA. God changed His character between the Old and New Testaments\r\nB. The Old Testament was written for a much smaller audience of readers\r\nC. God did not change; He stopped speaking in mysteries and spoke plainly\r\nD. The New Testament writers used a simpler and more modern vocabulary\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says it's not as if God changed; now God no longer spoke with mysteries and just spoke in plain language.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [02:51]\r\n\r\nQ104. What does the speaker conclude from 2 Corinthians 1:13 when a reader does not understand Paul?\r\nA. Paul wrote deliberately deep so that his apostleship would be proven\r\nB. Paul wrote nothing that cannot be understood, so the reader has basics to learn\r\nC. A rhema word from the Spirit is needed before the text is understood\r\nD. Only trained scholars can ever reach Paul's meaning in his letters\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Reading 2 Corinthians 1:13, the speaker says Paul wrote nothing you cannot read and understand, so if you don't understand, there are basic things you need to learn.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [04:32]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 2 Corinthians 1:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+1%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ105. In what order does the speaker present the first three common barriers to Bible interpretation?\r\nA. Language, culture, figures of speech\r\nB. Language, figures of speech, culture\r\nC. Culture, language, figures of speech\r\nD. Figures of speech, language, culture\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says number one is language, the second thing is that the Bible is replete with figures of speech, and the third barrier is culture.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [05:26; 08:32; 12:45]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 1:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+1%3A13/>; John 15:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+15%3A5/>; John 12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+12/>; John 21:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+21%3A25/>; Romans 16:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+16%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ106. What does the speaker say can easily and cheaply correct language barriers?\r\nA. Reading only the King James Version\r\nB. Learning Hebrew and Greek before reading\r\nC. Waiting for a rhema word on difficult passages\r\nD. Using simpler translations, and comparing them\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says language barriers can easily and cheaply be corrected by using simpler translations, comparing good ones such as ESV, NIV and NLT.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [07:22]\r\n\r\nQ107. What does the speaker say 'evil communication' meant in 'evil communication corrupts good manners'?\r\nA. Association\r\nB. Conversation\r\nC. Gossip\r\nD. Instruction\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the text simply meant association, because that was how communication was understood in that day.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [08:32]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 15:33 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15%3A33/>\r\n\r\nQ108. What does the speaker say Jesus means by 'I am the door' in John 10:9?\r\nA. Jesus is the guardian who watches over the sheepfold\r\nB. Salvation is restricted to those who enter through Israel\r\nC. Believers must enter the church community through baptism\r\nD. He is the way of access to eternal life and salvation\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker calls it a metaphor and says there is no revelation about a wooden door; it means that by Him you have access to eternal life and salvation.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [10:36]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 10:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+10%3A9/>\r\n\r\nQ109. How does the speaker explain Jesus's saying about hating father and mother as hyperbole?\r\nA. Jesus meant that relatives who oppose the gospel must be avoided\r\nB. Jesus taught that family ties are to be set aside for ministry work\r\nC. Jesus was contrasting Old Testament commands with New Testament love\r\nD. You must love the Lord so much that other love looks like hatred by comparison\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker defines hyperbole as extravagant words to drive home a point and says love for others should seem like hatred compared with love for the Lord.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [11:33]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 14:26 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+14%3A26/>\r\n\r\nQ110. What point does the speaker make with the example of a husband traveling without his wedding ring?\r\nA. A ring can express biblical love and accountability, though it is not commanded\r\nB. Wearing a ring is a biblical command for all married couples today\r\nC. Rings should be avoided by believers because the Bible is silent on them\r\nD. A husband who does not wear a ring should be disciplined by the church\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the Bible did not tell husbands and wives to wear rings, but love and accountability can be expressed using a ring in our culture.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [14:52; 15:57]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 16:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+16%3A16/>; 1 Corinthians 16:21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+16%3A21/>; 1 Corinthians 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+2/>\r\n\r\nQ111. What does the speaker's Christmas presents analogy picture?\r\nA. Old Testament blessings were given only to the children of Israel\r\nB. God rewards believers who wait patiently for the gifts He promised\r\nC. Old Testament revelation was concealed for believers' glory, waiting to be opened\r\nD. God withheld revelation from those who were not yet prepared to receive it\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker compares presents under the tree to the Old Testament, saying revelation was not hidden from us, it was hidden for us.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [17:03; 17:59]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Corinthians 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+2/>\r\n\r\nQ112. Why does the speaker say we could not simply learn all that Paul knew by reading the Old Testament?\r\nA. The Spirit gave Paul the revelation, which he then wrote plainly\r\nB. Paul's knowledge came mainly from his early training as a Pharisee\r\nC. Paul added teachings that the Old Testament did not contain at all\r\nD. Paul drew on other ancient writings in addition to the Old Testament\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Paul's preaching was a commentary on the Old Testament, but that is where the Spirit of God comes in and the revelation was given to him.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [19:44]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+3/>\r\n\r\nQ113. How does the speaker use Peter's explanation of the 120 speaking in tongues to describe New Testament teaching?\r\nA. It brings to light what was foreordained, as Peter did with Joel's prophecy\r\nB. It introduces new doctrines that the Old Testament did not anticipate\r\nC. It replaces Old Testament prophecy with direct revelation to the apostles\r\nD. It teaches that speaking in tongues is the primary sign of the Spirit\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Peter pointed the crowd to Joel's prophecy, which is how New Testament teaching brings to light things foreordained in the Old Testament.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) I — [19:44; 20:41]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 2:16–21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+2%3A16%E2%80%9321/>\r\n\r\nQ114. What does the speaker conclude from 'God, who at sundry times and in divers manners spoke... by the prophets' about Old Testament communication?\r\nA. It was incomplete in doctrine\r\nB. It was given only in visions\r\nC. It was veiled\r\nD. It was addressed only to the prophets\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the phrase means the communication of God in the Old Testament was veiled, while in Christ we understand exactly who God is.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) II — [01:13]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+3/>; Hebrews 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1/>\r\n\r\nQ115. Which Greek word does the speaker give for 'sundry times', meaning 'in many different parts'?\r\nA. Apocalypsis\r\nB. Musterion\r\nC. Polumeros\r\nD. Elencho\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker gives the Greek word as polumeros, spelled P-O-L-U-M-E-R-O-S, meaning in many different parts.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) II — [02:57]\r\n\r\nQ116. Which Old Testament text does the speaker identify as the one quoted in Matthew 1:23 about the virgin conceiving?\r\nA. Jeremiah 31:15\r\nB. Isaiah 7:14\r\nC. Micah 5:2\r\nD. Hosea 11:1\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Matthew 1:23 quotes Isaiah 7:14, and that the angel's statement fulfilled the prophecy in Isaiah.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) II — [05:42; 06:36]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Matthew 1:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+1%3A23/>; Isaiah 7:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+7%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ117. How does the speaker say an interlinear Bible helps a reader of Matthew?\r\nA. It lists the original Greek words beneath each English word\r\nB. A superscript letter points to the Old Testament text being quoted\r\nC. It gives a short commentary beneath each passage in the text\r\nD. It marks the verses that Jesus spoke directly to the crowds\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker describes a superscript letter at Matthew 1:23 whose text reference shows which text is being quoted.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) II — [05:42; 06:36]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Matthew 1:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+1%3A23/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Isaiah 7:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+7%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ118. In Matthew 2, to which prophets does the speaker trace the flight to Egypt and the slaughter of the children?\r\nA. Hosea and Jeremiah\r\nB. Isaiah and Jeremiah\r\nC. Micah and Hosea\r\nD. Hosea and Malachi\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker traces 'out of Egypt I have called my son' to Hosea 11:1 and the weeping of Rachel to Jeremiah 31:15.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) II — [09:08; 10:02]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Matthew 2:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+2%3A13/>; Hosea 11:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hosea+11%3A1/>; Matthew 2:17–18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+2%3A17%E2%80%9318/>; Jeremiah 31:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+31%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ119. According to the speaker, what was the book of Matthew written for?\r\nA. To record the events of Jesus's life for the very first time\r\nB. To correct the Old Testament's errors about the coming Messiah\r\nC. To explain Jewish customs and law to its Gentile readers abroad\r\nD. To show how Jesus's life fulfilled what the prophets had prophesied\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Matthew was not just to tell what happened but to tell how it was a fulfillment of what the prophets had prophesied.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) II — [12:06]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Micah 5:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Micah+5%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ120. What picture does the speaker use for the Old Testament prophecies scattered across different books?\r\nA. Threads woven into one tapestry\r\nB. Chapters of one continuous letter\r\nC. Different pieces of a jigsaw puzzle\r\nD. Stones fitted together in a wall\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the story of Matthew was told in sundry times, in several parts, like different parts of a jigsaw puzzle that form one picture.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) II — [11:15]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hosea 11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hosea+11/>; Matthew 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+2/>; Jeremiah 31:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+31%3A15/>; Micah 5:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Micah+5%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ121. Which pair correctly completes: 'The New Testament is in the Old Testament ___, and the Old Testament is in the New Testament ___'?\r\nA. Concealed; revealed\r\nB. Concealed; concealed\r\nC. Revealed; revealed\r\nD. Revealed; concealed\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker tells the class to write it down: the New Testament is in the Old Testament concealed, and the Old Testament is in the New Testament revealed.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) II — [12:59]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 24:27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A27/>\r\n\r\nQ122. Which reference does the speaker give for the prophecy that the Messiah would ride into Jerusalem on a donkey?\r\nA. Psalm 41:9\r\nB. Zechariah 11:12-13\r\nC. Malachi 3:1\r\nD. Zechariah 9:9\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: In his list of prophecies, the speaker gives Zechariah 9:9 for riding into Jerusalem on a donkey.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) II — [15:07]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Zechariah 9:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Zechariah+9%3A9/>\r\n\r\nQ123. Which reference does the speaker give for the betraying friend who will be paid 30 pieces of silver?\r\nA. Zechariah 9:9\r\nB. Zechariah 11:12-13\r\nC. Psalm 41:9\r\nD. Psalm 22:16\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker lists the betraying friend paying 30 pieces of silver at Zechariah 11:12-13.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) II — [16:30]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Zechariah 11:12–13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Zechariah+11%3A12%E2%80%9313/>\r\n\r\nQ124. Why does the speaker call the prophecy of crucifixion phenomenal?\r\nA. Crucifixion did not yet exist as a form of execution at that time\r\nB. It was repeated in identical words across several prophetic books\r\nC. It named the exact day on which the Messiah would be executed\r\nD. It was fulfilled at the very place where it had been spoken\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says it was phenomenal because at the time it was prophesied, crucifixion did not even exist as a mode of execution.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) II — [16:30]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Malachi 3:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Malachi+3%3A1/>; Isaiah 43 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+43/>; Isaiah 35:5, 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+35%3A5%2C+6/>; Zechariah 9:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Zechariah+9%3A9/>; Psalm 41:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+41%3A9/>; Zechariah 11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Zechariah+11/>; Isaiah 50:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+50%3A6/>; Isaiah 56 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+56/>; Psalm 28:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+28%3A19/>; Isaiah 53:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53%3A7/>; Psalm 22:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+22%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ125. Which prophecy does the speaker connect with the two thieves crucified beside Jesus?\r\nA. He will not defend himself (Isaiah 53:7)\r\nB. None of his bones will be broken (Psalm 34:20)\r\nC. He will be beaten, mocked and spat on (Isaiah 50:6)\r\nD. He will be crucified with sinners (Isaiah 53:12)\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says 'crucified with sinners' is how the two thieves came to be by His side, citing Isaiah 53:12.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) II — [18:09]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Isaiah 53:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ126. Which sequence correctly pairs the references the speaker gives for the resurrection, the ascension and sitting at the right hand of God?\r\nA. Psalm 110:1; Psalm 68:18; Psalm 16:10\r\nB. Psalm 16:10; Psalm 68:18; Psalm 110:1\r\nC. Psalm 16:10; Psalm 110:1; Psalm 68:18\r\nD. Psalm 68:18; Psalm 16:10; Psalm 110:1\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker gives Psalm 16:10 for the resurrection, Psalm 68:18 for the ascension, and Psalm 110:1 for sitting at the right hand of God.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 1) II — [18:09]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Psalm 16:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+16%3A10/>; Psalm 68:18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+68%3A18/>; Psalm 110:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+110%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ127. What does the speaker say about the Old Testament's communication not being 'exact' in Christ's comparison?\r\nA. It means it was meant only for a past generation\r\nB. It means it was spoken only in symbols with no literal meaning\r\nC. It does not mean it was inaccurate; it means it was veiled\r\nD. It means it was inaccurate in places\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that if Christ is the exact image, Old Testament communication was not exact, which doesn't mean inaccurate, just veiled.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) I — [00:57]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1/>\r\n\r\nQ128. What does the speaker say 'diverse manners' implies about how God spoke in the Old Testament?\r\nA. He used different modes, beyond just verbal communication\r\nB. He addressed every generation in exactly the same words\r\nC. He used only dreams and visions to reach the prophets\r\nD. He spoke in different languages to different prophets in turn\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says diverse ways means different modes of communication, maybe beyond verbal communication, such as demonstrating, writing or sending someone.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) I — [01:46]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 1:1-2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A1-2/>\r\n\r\nQ129. According to the speaker, what did the brazen serpent lifted up in the wilderness symbolize?\r\nA. Christ and His crucifixion\r\nB. The healing power of Israel's sacrifices\r\nC. Moses's authority over the people\r\nD. The devil's defeat by the law\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Using John 3:14, the speaker says the brazen serpent was a symbol of Christ and his crucifixion.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) I — [02:44]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 3:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ130. In the speaker's reading, what did the Israelites' act of looking at the serpent represent?\r\nA. Repenting of their grumbling and turning from sin\r\nB. Trusting in the sacrifice, picturing faith in the cross\r\nC. Obeying a ceremonial command given through Moses\r\nD. Remembering God's judgment on Egypt's firstborn\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says all the people had to do to be healed was look, which pointed to trusting in the serpent and its sacrifice, a picture of the crucifixion.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) I — [03:49]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3/>\r\n\r\nQ131. What does the speaker say 'types and shadows' are?\r\nA. Historical events that were later shown to be disproved\r\nB. Laws that remain unchanged and binding in the New Testament\r\nC. Symbolisms in the Old Testament that the New Testament unpacks\r\nD. Poetic passages that carry no doctrinal meaning at all\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the New Testament unpacks what theologians call types and shadows, symbolisms in the Old Testament.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) I — [05:36]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3/>; 2 Corinthians 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+3/> | Added from online research: Hebrews 10:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+10%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ132. What did the fading of the glory on Moses's face symbolize, according to the speaker?\r\nA. That Moses had failed to remain faithful to God's commands\r\nB. That God's presence would eventually leave the tabernacle\r\nC. That Israel's obedience to the law would decline over the years\r\nD. That the Old Testament's glory would be done away for something better\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the glory on Moses's face diminished, symbolizing that the Old Testament's glory would be done away and something better was coming.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) I — [08:04]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+6/>\r\n\r\nQ133. What does the speaker infer from Paul's words, 'we use plainness of speech, not as Moses'?\r\nA. Moses was unable to speak clearly in front of the people\r\nB. Moses's speech was not plain; he spoke in types and shadows\r\nC. Moses spoke only in legal commands rather than in parables\r\nD. Paul's speech carried less authority than that of Moses\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Reading 2 Corinthians 3:12, the speaker says it means Moses's speech was not plain and that he spoke in types and shadows.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) I — [08:59]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 2 Corinthians 3:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+3%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ134. Why does the speaker say many people still practice Judaism?\r\nA. They lack the keys to interpretation and miss that lambs and feasts were types\r\nB. They believe the Old Testament has been preserved without change\r\nC. They reject the New Testament's account of the death of Christ\r\nD. They follow the teaching of Moses more faithfully than Christians do\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says they lack the keys to Bible interpretation and did not see that there was an end to lamb-slaughtering and feasts, which were types and shadows.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) I — [11:58]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Colossians 2:16–17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+2%3A16%E2%80%9317/>; Hebrews 9:11–14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+9%3A11%E2%80%9314/>\r\n\r\nQ135. In the speaker's reading of 2 Corinthians 3:14, what did the veil on Moses's face symbolize?\r\nA. The veil on the minds of the Jews\r\nB. The veil over the holy of holies\r\nC. The glory that Moses wanted to hide\r\nD. The separation of Jews from Gentiles\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the veil on Moses's face symbolized the veils on the minds of the Jews, whose minds were blinded.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) I — [12:59]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 2 Corinthians 3:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+3%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ136. According to the speaker, what did all the lambs slaughtered in the Old Testament show in symbolism?\r\nA. That each tribe needed its own sacrifice for its own sins\r\nB. That God accepted only the blood of unblemished animals\r\nC. That sin could be removed by offering sacrifices again and again\r\nD. That the Messiah would come once and for all to save all men\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the lambs showed in symbolism that the Messiah would come once and for all and provide salvation for all men.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) I — [14:32]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 9:11–14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+9%3A11%E2%80%9314/>\r\n\r\nQ137. What does the speaker say happens when we turn to the Lord in our interpretation of the Scriptures?\r\nA. The glory of Moses returns\r\nB. The veil is taken away\r\nC. The types and shadows are made obsolete\r\nD. The Old Testament is set aside\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Reading 2 Corinthians 3:16, the speaker says when we turn to the Lord in our interpretation, the veil shall be taken away.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) I — [14:32]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 2 Corinthians 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+3%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ138. What does the speaker say about the name Joshua?\r\nA. It is a Greek form of the name Moses used in the New Testament\r\nB. It is a title for Moses's successor with no link to Christ at all\r\nC. It means 'rest' and refers to the Sabbath that God instituted\r\nD. It is the Hebrew form of 'Jesus,' and the promised land pictures salvation\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Joshua is the Hebrew transliteration of the name we call Jesus, and taking the people to the promised land pictures salvation.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) I — [15:39]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Joshua 1:1–2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Joshua+1%3A1%E2%80%932/>; Hebrews 4:8–10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+4%3A8%E2%80%9310/>\r\n\r\nQ139. According to the speaker, what does God's seventh-day rest picture?\r\nA. Israel's duty to cease from all work one day each week\r\nB. The end of the Old Testament feasts and festival days\r\nC. Man opening his eyes into the rest of God, a picture of salvation\r\nD. God's weariness after the long labor of creating the world\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says God cannot be tired, so the rest points to man, created on the sixth day, opening his eyes on the seventh day into the rest of God.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) I — [15:39]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 2:2–3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+2%3A2%E2%80%933/>; Hebrews 4:9–10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+4%3A9%E2%80%9310/>\r\n\r\nQ140. Why does the speaker say the light created on the first day was not literal light?\r\nA. Genesis is referring to the shining light of Moses's face\r\nB. Light cannot be created apart from the sun and the stars\r\nC. The text uses light only as a poetic image of God's beauty\r\nD. The sun, moon and stars came days later, so it was spiritual light\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the sun and moon came days later, so the first-day light was spiritual light, as 2 Corinthians 4 explains.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) I — [16:49]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 2 Corinthians 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+4/>\r\n\r\nQ141. What did Jesus say on the Emmaus road that the prophets had spoken?\r\nA. That Christ would come as a conquering warrior king\r\nB. That Christ would restore the kingdom to Israel at once\r\nC. That Christ ought to suffer and then enter into His glory\r\nD. That Christ would be recognized by the chief priests\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: In Luke 24:25, Jesus calls them fools to not believe what the prophets spoke: that Christ ought to have suffered and entered into His glory.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) II — [01:15]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Luke 24:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A25/> | Also cited in lesson segment: John 5:39 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+5%3A39/>; Isaiah 53 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53/>\r\n\r\nQ142. What does the speaker say 'Who has believed our report?' in Isaiah 53:1 tells us?\r\nA. That very few people would ever hear the gospel preached\r\nB. That Israel's leaders would reject the report out of pride\r\nC. That the gospel is such good news some would struggle to believe it\r\nD. That only the prophets would believe the message given\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Isaiah is telling us centuries ahead that the gospel is going to be so good in news, some people will struggle to believe it.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) II — [02:17]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Isaiah 53:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53%3A1/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+10/>\r\n\r\nQ143. What does the speaker link to Job's friends when discussing 'stricken and smitten of God'?\r\nA. The teaching that suffering earns a spiritual reward from God\r\nB. The simplistic idea that trials are punishment for what you did\r\nC. The belief that God abandons the righteous whenever they suffer\r\nD. The idea that only prophets are permitted to suffer for Him\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says people thought Jesus's cross was God's judgment, the same simplistic thinking Job's friends used.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) II — [03:53]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Isaiah 53:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53%3A4/>\r\n\r\nQ144. What does the speaker say the Ethiopian eunuch episode in Acts 8 shows about Isaiah 53?\r\nA. It refers to Jesus, so it is not exegetical gymnastics\r\nB. It cannot be understood without a guide to explain it\r\nC. It refers to Isaiah himself and his own sufferings\r\nD. It refers to the nation of Israel in its exile\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Philip began at the same scripture and preached Jesus, so we are not just doing exegetical gymnastics; it is really about Jesus.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) II — [05:33]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+8/>; Isaiah 53 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53/>\r\n\r\nQ145. What does the speaker say 'he shall see his seed' after making His soul an offering for sin intimates?\r\nA. That His followers would multiply after His death\r\nB. That death is not the end, since a resurrection is intimated\r\nC. That Israel would be fully restored under His reign\r\nD. That He would have physical descendants of His own\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says after He has died He shall see His seed, meaning death will not be the end and a resurrection is intimated.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) II — [06:34]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Isaiah 53:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53%3A10/>\r\n\r\nQ146. What does the speaker draw from 'by His knowledge shall My righteous Servant justify many'?\r\nA. Justification comes by knowing the Messiah, not by works of righteousness\r\nB. Justification is earned through careful obedience to the law\r\nC. Justification is given only to servants who are already righteous\r\nD. Justification is reserved for those who suffer with Christ\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says it preaches how justification will be received in the New Testament: by the knowledge of God, not by works of righteousness.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) II — [07:13]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Isaiah 53:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ147. What does the speaker say 'the God of the whole earth shall He be called' in Isaiah 54 predicted?\r\nA. God's covenant would be restricted to the faithful remnant\r\nB. Israel would come to rule over the whole earth in time\r\nC. Only Israel would continue to know God's name as before\r\nD. The wall between Jew and Gentile would fall so all could be saved\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says once only the Jews knew Him but now He is God of the whole earth, predicting the middle wall of partition would be broken so everyone can be saved.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) II — [08:03; 08:59]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Isaiah 54 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+54/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Isaiah 53 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53/>\r\n\r\nQ148. How does the speaker contrast Isaiah 53 and Isaiah 54?\r\nA. Isaiah 53 speaks of Christ's birth and Isaiah 54 of His resurrection\r\nB. Isaiah 53 shows Christ's sufferings; Isaiah 54 the glory that follows\r\nC. Isaiah 53 speaks of Israel's restoration and Isaiah 54 of Christ's death\r\nD. Isaiah 53 describes the law and Isaiah 54 describes the age of grace\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Isaiah 53 highlights the sufferings of Christ, and Isaiah 54 the glory that should follow.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) II — [08:59]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Isaiah 53 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53/>; Isaiah 54 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+54/>\r\n\r\nQ149. How does the speaker define typology?\r\nA. A study of Hebrew and Greek word types in the original text\r\nB. A method of dating Old Testament events from the New Testament\r\nC. A branch of theology studying Old Testament types that prefigured Christ\r\nD. A system for grouping the books of the Bible by their authors\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says there is a branch of theology called typology, the study of types in the Old Testament that prefigured Christ.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) II — [10:54]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Colossians 2:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+2%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ150. What does the speaker say the writer of Hebrews clarifies about the blood of bulls and goats?\r\nA. It cannot take away sins; Jesus did it once and for all\r\nB. It covered Israel's ceremonial sins but never moral guilt\r\nC. It was accepted by God only for sins done unintentionally\r\nD. It could remove sins only temporarily until the next feast\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Moses preached forgiveness by slaughtering lambs, but Hebrews clarifies the blood of bulls and goats cannot take away sins; Jesus did it once and for all.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) II — [10:54]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 9:11–14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+9%3A11%E2%80%9314/>; Hebrews 10:4–14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+10%3A4%E2%80%9314/>\r\n\r\nQ151. How does the speaker distinguish Old Testament circumcision from medical circumcision?\r\nA. It was performed to mark a boy's passage to physical maturity\r\nB. It involved cutting away more than the medical procedure does\r\nC. It was a medical procedure meant to prevent disease in infants\r\nD. It was symbolic, just a slit with some blood, rather than medical\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says medical circumcision is mostly for aesthetics, but the Old Testament one was more symbolic, just a slit with some blood.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) II — [11:44]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Colossians 2:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+2%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ152. According to the speaker, what does 'the circumcision made without hands' in Colossians 2:11 refer to?\r\nA. The Old Testament rite performed by the priests of Israel\r\nB. The rite of baptism as practiced in the New Testament\r\nC. The cutting of the heart through personal repentance alone\r\nD. The putting off of the body of sins by Christ's circumcision\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the real circumcision is made without hands, in the putting off of the body of sins of the flesh by the circumcision of Christ.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) II — [11:44; 12:41]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Colossians 2:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+2%3A11/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Ezekiel 36:26 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ezekiel+36%3A26/>\r\n\r\nQ153. What does the speaker say Ezekiel 36:26 describes?\r\nA. The true circumcision: stony heart removed, heart of flesh given\r\nB. The judgment awaiting Israel's disobedient priests and kings\r\nC. The ordinance of circumcision first given to Abraham's household\r\nD. The rebuilding of the temple after the long exile in Babylon\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites Ezekiel 36:26 for the real circumcision, where God takes away the stony heart and puts His Spirit in us, so sinful propensity is removed by His Spirit.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) II — [12:41; 13:38]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Ezekiel 36:26 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ezekiel+36%3A26/>; Colossians 2:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+2%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ154. Whom does the speaker say Paul warns against in Philippians 3:2 with 'beware of the concision'?\r\nA. Teachers who deny the bodily resurrection of Christ\r\nB. Leaders who demand payment in exchange for their teaching\r\nC. Believers who return to observing Jewish festivals and sabbaths\r\nD. Those who still want to cut the flesh after the real circumcision\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reads 'beware of the concision' as beware of people who still want to slit your flesh when the real circumcision is available by faith in Christ.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) II — [14:44]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Philippians 3:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+3%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ155. What does the speaker say proves that Old Testament sacrifices were a type and shadow (Hebrews 10:1)?\r\nA. They were eventually replaced by regular temple services and feasts\r\nB. They had to be repeated year by year; one perfecting sacrifice would suffice\r\nC. They were accepted by God only for sins committed in ignorance\r\nD. They were offered only by the priests of Israel at the temple\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the proof that they were a type and shadow was that they had to do it every year; if it was efficacious enough, one would be enough.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) II — [16:44]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 10:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+10%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ156. What criterion does the speaker give for identifying a type in the Old Testament?\r\nA. It must resemble the life and ministry of Christ closely\r\nB. It must have been confirmed by the early church fathers\r\nC. It must be clearly identified as a type in the New Testament\r\nD. It must have been explained as a type by the later prophets\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: In the assignment, the speaker says you are not making it up; it must have been clearly identified as a type in the New Testament.\r\nREF: HERMENEUTICS (PART 2) II — [18:31]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 1:29 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A29/>\n\nDECK: Discipleship Experience (Section 8) Bible Experience II\r\n\r\nQ1. What is Matthew primarily demonstrating through his repeated references to Old Testament prophecy?\r\nA. That Jesus' ministry followed the traditions of Israel\r\nB. That Jesus is the Messiah the Old Testament had prophesied about\r\nC. That Jesus was a teacher who restored Old Testament practices\r\nD. That the events of Jesus' life resembled those of earlier prophets\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Matthew repeatedly presents Jesus as the Messiah prophesied in the Old Testament.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS — 00:15–01:17\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Matthew 1:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+1%3A20/>; Isaiah 7:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+7%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ2. How does the lesson reconcile God's sovereignty with human free will?\r\nA. Human freedom exists only when God is not involved\r\nB. God can accomplish His purposes without overriding human choices\r\nC. Human choices ultimately determine whether God's plans succeed\r\nD. God's sovereignty means human choices have no real significance\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: God can fulfill his purposes while human choices remain consequential and are not overridden.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — 08:49–09:44\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 50:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+50%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ3. What is the danger of reading Scripture without a Christ-centered lens?\r\nA. We can miss how different parts of Scripture point to Christ\r\nB. We may focus too much on the historical setting of the Bible\r\nC. We may give Old Testament passages more authority than New Testament passages\r\nD. We may interpret every passage literally instead of symbolically\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: A Christ-centered lens helps readers see how different parts of Scripture point to Christ.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS — 05:29–10:48\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 24:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A25/>; Hebrews 10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+10/>\r\n\r\nQ4. Which resource is particularly useful when the difficulty lies in the meaning or usage of biblical words?\r\nA. A devotional book\r\nB. A biography of a biblical character\r\nC. A lexicon or Bible dictionary\r\nD. A collection of modern Christian testimonies\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: A lexicon or Bible dictionary can help clarify biblical word meanings and changes in usage.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — 03:56–04:56\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 3:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+3%3A10/>; Matthew 10:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+10%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ5. Why does the lesson describe the Bible as a \"library\"?\r\nA. It contains books written for different audiences only\r\nB. It is composed of multiple books rather than being a single literary work\r\nC. It was originally stored in a library\r\nD. Its books were compiled by one group of editors\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The Bible is described as a library because it consists of multiple books.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — 09:44–10:47\r\n\r\nQ6. What does the repetition of sacrifices communicate?\r\nA. That the sacrifices had to be performed correctly each time\r\nB. That Israel had not yet fully understood the purpose of the law\r\nC. That the sacrifices themselves were not the ultimate and complete sacrifice\r\nD. That forgiveness depended on continual participation in the sacrificial system\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The repeated sacrifices pointed to Christ as the ultimate sacrifice rather than being complete in themselves.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS — 09:03–09:59\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+10/>\r\n\r\nQ7. What assumption about divine guidance does the lesson challenge?\r\nA. That God can use circumstances to lead people\r\nB. That God's guidance must always come through recognizable means.\r\nC. That Scripture is important for understanding God's will\r\nD. That God can work through human decisions\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson challenges limiting divine guidance to audible or otherwise immediately recognizable means and highlights providence.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — 01:48–04:59\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Proverbs 16:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Proverbs+16%3A9/>\r\n\r\nQ8. What does biblical inerrancy mean within the lesson's framework?\r\nA. Every biblical statement must satisfy modern scientific expectations\r\nB. Scripture contains no errors of truth in what it teaches\r\nC. Every biblical description must use technically precise modern language\r\nD. Every passage must be interpreted literally\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Inerrancy means Scripture has no errors of truth in what it teaches.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — 17:23–18:37\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 17:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+17%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ9. What connection does the lesson make between Joshua and Jesus?\r\nA. Both were prophets who performed similar miracles\r\nB. Joshua was presented as the first human king pointing toward Jesus\r\nC. Their names are essentially the same, providing a picture pointing toward Jesus\r\nD. Jesus repeated Joshua's military victories in His earthly ministry\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson connects Joshua and Jesus through their names and presents Joshua as a type pointing to Christ.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS — 10:48–11:51\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1/>; Matthew 24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+24/>\r\n\r\nQ10. Why can the Old Testament tell Christ's story without mentioning His name throughout?\r\nA. Because the Old Testament was written mainly as historical literature\r\nB. Because Christ is revealed through different events and prophecies that come together\r\nC. Because the identity of the Messiah was intentionally hidden until the New Testament\r\nD. Because the Old Testament focuses more on Israel than on individual people\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says Christ’s story appears in different parts of the Old Testament and becomes clearer when those pieces are brought together.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS — 04:35–06:10\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Matthew 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+2/>; Isaiah 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+7/>; Micah 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Micah+5/>; Hosea 11:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hosea+11%3A1/>; Jeremiah 31:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+31%3A15/>; Luke 24:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A25/>\r\n\r\nQ11. What is the key distinction in the statement that God did not make Pharaoh wicked?\r\nA. God can use the consequences of human wickedness without being its author\r\nB. God cannot influence events involving wicked people\r\nC. Pharaoh's wickedness was necessary for God's plan\r\nD. Human wickedness is outside God's sovereignty\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: God can work through Pharaoh’s wicked actions without being the one who made him wicked.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — 08:49–09:44\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Exodus 1:22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+1%3A22/>\r\n\r\nQ12. What does it mean to say that the Bible is \"written for you, but not written to you\"?\r\nA. Its teachings are relevant only to its original audience\r\nB. Modern Christians should avoid applying historical passages\r\nC. Its message reaches us, while its original context belongs to another audience\r\nD. Every passage requires a completely different interpretation today\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The Bible can speak to modern readers while retaining its original audience and context.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — 03:02–03:56\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 1:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+1%3A13/>; Ephesians 3:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+3%3A10/>\r\n\r\nQ13. What meaning of gune is highlighted in the lesson?\r\nA. It refers exclusively to an unmarried woman\r\nB. It can refer either to a wife or to a woman generally\r\nC. It refers exclusively to a female church leader\r\nD. It always refers to women collectively rather than individually\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says the Greek term can mean a married wife or women generally, depending on context.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — 24:20–25:16\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+3/>\r\n\r\nQ14. What distinguishes mature appreciation of God from a merely pragmatic relationship with Him?\r\nA. Wanting God's blessings while remaining committed to doctrine\r\nB. Seeking God's guidance before making decisions\r\nC. Appreciating who God is, not merely what He can provide\r\nD. Studying Scripture primarily to solve personal problems\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson distinguishes appreciating God for who He is from focusing only on what He can do or give.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — 01:05–01:48\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Psalm 73:25-26 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+73%3A25-26/>\r\n\r\nQ15. When a biblical practice does not translate directly into a modern culture, what should the believer first seek?\r\nA. The principle the practice was communicating\r\nB. A modern equivalent with exactly the same form\r\nC. A reason to remove the passage from application\r\nD. The interpretation most accepted within the believer's culture\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: When a specific practice is cultural, the lesson says to identify the principle it communicates.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — 08:34–09:06\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 9:19-23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+9%3A19-23/>\r\n\r\nQ16. Why does the lesson reject the argument that an apparent scientific inconsistency automatically disproves Scripture?\r\nA. Scientific knowledge is never reliable\r\nB. Biblical writers were scientifically trained\r\nC. Scripture should not be judged as though its purpose were to function as a science textbook\r\nD. Scientific questions cannot be discussed from Scripture\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says Scripture should not be judged by standards outside its purpose, such as functioning as a science book.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — 17:23–18:37\r\n\r\nQ17. What is the main interpretive danger of treating every biblical statement literally?\r\nA. It can cause figures of speech to be mistaken for factual descriptions\r\nB. It can make historical passages appear less important\r\nC. It can cause readers to reject difficult doctrines\r\nD. It can prevent readers from studying original languages\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Treating figurative language literally can obscure the meaning the speaker intended.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — 05:54–07:52\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Matthew 10:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+10%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ18. Who first encountered the risen Jesus?\r\nA. Peter and John\r\nB. The male disciples\r\nC. The women\r\nD. The religious leaders\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson identifies women as the first witnesses to the risen Jesus.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — 08:36–09:38\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 28:1–10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+28%3A1%E2%80%9310/>\r\n\r\nQ19. What two aspects of Christ's work are emphasized in the lesson?\r\nA. His teaching ministry and His miracles\r\nB. His earthly ministry and His resurrection appearances\r\nC. His public sacrifice and His heavenly intercession\r\nD. His birth and His ascension\r\nANSWER: D\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS — 08:03–09:03\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 2:1–7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+2%3A1%E2%80%937/>; Acts 1:6–11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+1%3A6%E2%80%9311/>\r\n\r\nQ20. How does the lesson describe the Old Testament's presentation of Christ?\r\nA. Christ is revealed gradually, in pieces and through different parts of Scripture\r\nB. Christ is revealed almost entirely through direct Messianic statements\r\nC. Christ is revealed mainly through the writings of the prophets\r\nD. Christ is revealed only through the sacrificial system\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson describes Christ as revealed through pieces of the Old Testament that come together into a fuller picture.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS — 04:35–06:10\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Matthew 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+2/>; Isaiah 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+7/>; Micah 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Micah+5/>; Hosea 11:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hosea+11%3A1/>; Jeremiah 31:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+31%3A15/>; Luke 24:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A25/>\r\n\r\nQ21. How does Romans 16 describe Phoebe?\r\nA. As a prophet and teacher\r\nB. As a servant/deacon\r\nC. As an apostle and elder\r\nD. As a missionary who planted churches\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says Romans 16 describes Phoebe as a servant, using the same word translated “deacon.”\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — 12:21–13:20\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+16/>\r\n\r\nQ22. When a biblical passage is difficult, what should guide its interpretation?\r\nA. Clearer and better-established biblical evidence\r\nB. The interpretation most commonly accepted by Christians\r\nC. The meaning that appears most literal at first reading\r\nD. The cultural assumptions of the modern reader\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson recommends using what is known and established to help interpret difficult passages.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — 10:38–11:25\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Galatians 3:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+3%3A28/>; 1 Corinthians 14:34 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+14%3A34/>; 1 Timothy 2:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+2%3A15/>; 1 Corinthians 11:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+11%3A5/>; Acts 21:8, 9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+21%3A8%2C+9/>\r\n\r\nQ23. The \"jigsaw puzzle\" picture of Scripture mainly emphasizes what?\r\nA. Every biblical passage can be understood independently\r\nB. The Bible contains unrelated pieces that require different methods of interpretation\r\nC. Individual pieces of revelation come together to give a fuller picture of Christ\r\nD. Difficult passages should be ignored until all other passages have been understood\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson uses the jigsaw image for bringing separate scriptural pieces together to reveal Christ.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS — 05:29–06:10\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 24:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A25/>\r\n\r\nQ24. What concept does the lesson identify as a primary way God can direct events?\r\nA. Divine providence\r\nB. Human intuition\r\nC. Audible instruction\r\nD. Spiritual symbolism\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson directly identifies divine providence as a primary way God leads.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — 04:59–05:45\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Proverbs 16:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Proverbs+16%3A9/>\r\n\r\nQ25. According to the lesson, what resource cannot simply be replaced by independent study tools?\r\nA. A newer Bible translation\r\nB. A Bible teacher\r\nC. A Bible dictionary\r\nD. A commentary\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents a Bible teacher as important beyond independent study tools such as lexicons and commentaries.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — 09:53–10:48\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 4:11-13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+4%3A11-13/>\r\n\r\nQ26. Joseph's brothers unintentionally contributed to the very outcome they were trying to prevent. What does this illustrate?\r\nA. Human opposition can unknowingly become part of providential fulfillment\r\nB. God removes human responsibility when He has a plan\r\nC. Joseph's brothers were unknowingly righteous\r\nD. Divine plans require people to understand them beforehand\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson uses Joseph’s brothers to show that opposition can unintentionally further the outcome God intends.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — 06:24–07:06\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 50:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+50%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ27. According to the lesson, Galatians 3:28 primarily reinforces what?\r\nA. Men and women should have identical responsibilities in every setting\r\nB. There is no distinction between men and women in their standing in Christ\r\nC. Women were given authority over men within the church\r\nD. Cultural distinctions between men and women no longer existed\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents Galatians 3:28 as affirming that men and women are one in Christ.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — 01:01–01:52\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Galatians 3:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+3%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ28. How does the lesson approach 1 Corinthians 14:34, where women are told to keep silent?\r\nA. It interprets the instruction within the chapter's context of spiritual gifts, prophecy, order, and confusion\r\nB. It treats the instruction as a universal command against women speaking in church\r\nC. It concludes that Paul changed his position on women between 1 Corinthians and Romans\r\nD. It interprets \"silence\" as a command for women to avoid all forms of public ministry\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson interprets the instruction in the chapter’s context of spiritual gifts, prophecy, and maintaining order.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — 16:18–18:14\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Corinthians 14:34 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+14%3A34/>\r\n\r\nQ29. What does the lesson's example of Cornelius and Peter primarily emphasize?\r\nA. God can use people as instruments in communicating foundational truth\r\nB. Spiritual experiences are unnecessary for understanding Scripture\r\nC. Every believer must receive revelation directly from God\r\nD. Lens through which they see the world\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson uses Cornelius and Peter to emphasize God’s use of people to teach foundational truths.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — 10:48–11:46\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10/>\r\n\r\nQ30. According to the speaker, what did a historian say about the significance of Jesus's birth for women?\r\nA. It was the first time a religious teacher allowed women to attend school\r\nB. It ended the Roman laws that gave men control over their wives\r\nC. Jesus's birth was one of the best things to happen to women\r\nD. It made Jewish tradition more favorable toward women's education\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker answers 'an emphatic yes' to whether Jesus, in a countercultural way, changed how women were viewed, citing a historian who said one of the best things to happen to women in history was Jesus's birth.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [01:01]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Galatians 3:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+3%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ31. What does the speaker say many readers miss about Galatians 3:28?\r\nA. They read it theologically, missing how radical it was for its culture\r\nB. They treat it as a passage about slavery only, ignoring its reference to Jews and Greeks\r\nC. They assume Paul wrote it to correct Greek household customs specifically\r\nD. They overlook that it was addressed only to church leaders rather than all believers\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says people may have read Galatians 3:28 many times but theologically, without understanding the context and how radical the text was in a culture that devalued women.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [01:01]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Galatians 3:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+3%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ32. The speaker cites Plutarch that the average Greek woman had the social status of a slave. How does he qualify this statement?\r\nA. They were slaves in law, which is why they could not own property or go out freely\r\nB. They were treated as slaves only in poor households, while wealthy women had freedom\r\nC. They were free citizens but were legally barred from ever leaving their home city\r\nD. They were not actually slaves but were treated like them, kept under lock and key\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says it 'doesn't mean they were slaves' but that they were treated like slaves, kept under lock and key and allowed out only with a male slave.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [01:52]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Galatians 3:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+3%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ33. How does the speaker describe education for girls in the Greek world, in an age that prized learning?\r\nA. Only boys went to school for poetry, music and gymnastics; girls were excluded\r\nB. Girls attended the same schools but studied only household management and weaving\r\nC. Girls were taught poetry and music at home by their fathers instead of at school\r\nD. Girls could attend school for a few years, but only until the age of marriage\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Despite the Greeks' emphasis on education, only boys went to school; girls were not allowed to go at all.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [01:52; 02:40]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Galatians 3:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+3%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ34. What does the speaker say Hilarion's message to his wife at the point of birth illustrates?\r\nA. That Greek fathers valued daughters for their economic contribution\r\nB. That female children were seen as a social burden and a liability\r\nC. That Roman law required fathers to accept every child born to them\r\nD. That Hebrew tradition forbade abandoning infants of either sex\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Hilarion told his wife that a male child should live and a female child be cast away; the speaker uses this to show female children were viewed as a burden.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [02:40]\r\n\r\nQ35. What did Augustus Caesar do to Roman law on a man's authority, as the speaker describes it?\r\nA. He limited a father's power to kill his children to those born outside marriage\r\nB. He reinforced the law and let men kill wives, especially on suspicion of adultery\r\nC. He abolished the killing of married daughters but kept it for unmarried ones\r\nD. He transferred authority over wives from their husbands to the Roman courts entirely\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Roman men already had absolute control over their children; Augustus reinforced the law and extended it to killing wives, especially over suspected adultery.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [03:34]\r\n\r\nQ36. What does a rabbinical writing quoted by the speaker say about teaching one's daughter the law?\r\nA. It is as though he gave her a priestly inheritance\r\nB. It is a duty fulfilled only after her marriage\r\nC. It is as though he were teaching her lechery\r\nD. It is a practice commended for scribes' daughters\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker quotes rabbinic writings saying the law's words should be burnt rather than committed to a woman, and that teaching a daughter the law is as though teaching her lechery.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [04:29]\r\n\r\nQ37. How does the speaker read Jesus's insistence that Martha stay and hear the word rather than remain in the kitchen?\r\nA. As a rebuke teaching that practical service in the home is unimportant to God\r\nB. As a special exemption granted to Mary alone because she had not yet married\r\nC. As an instruction that Martha should leave her household and follow Him as a disciple\r\nD. As a radical message that women were not confined to the kitchen but could learn\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Martha wanted to do what her culture allowed, staying in the kitchen; Jesus said Mary had chosen the good part, which the speaker calls a radical message to women.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [04:29; 05:10]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+4/>\r\n\r\nQ38. What does the rabbinic writing quoted by the speaker say about a man who talks to a woman in public?\r\nA. He brings evil upon himself and should not so much as greet her\r\nB. He must first obtain the permission of her husband or father\r\nC. He is permitted to speak only about the law of Moses\r\nD. He must offer a sacrifice to be cleansed afterward\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The quoted writing says a man who talks to a woman in public brings evil upon himself and should not even greet her.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [05:10]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+4/>\r\n\r\nQ39. Why does the speaker say the Samaritan woman was shocked when Jesus engaged her in conversation in John 4?\r\nA. Rabbis were not permitted to travel through Samaria or to ask anyone for water\r\nB. Samaritan women were forbidden to speak to any man outside their own household\r\nC. She had been told beforehand that He was a prophet who would ask her for water\r\nD. Jews had no dealings with Samaritans, and a man would hardly engage a Samaritan woman\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Reading John 4 against its culture, the speaker notes that even she was shocked: a Jew and a Samaritan woman should have had no dealings.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [06:13]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+4/>\r\n\r\nQ40. What encouragement does the speaker draw from Jesus telling the woman she would have asked for living water if she knew the gift of God?\r\nA. Salvation was offered to Samaritans first, before it was ever extended to the Jews or Greeks\r\nB. She qualified for all God offers and should boldly ask without letting anyone count her out\r\nC. Only people who already understood the Law and its sacrifices could receive the gift\r\nD. Women should wait for their husbands to ask God for such gifts on their behalf\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reads Jesus's words as saying 'you qualify for everything God has to offer. Be bold to ask. Don't let anyone count you out.'\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [06:13]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+4/>\r\n\r\nQ41. What reason does the speaker suggest for Jesus sending His disciples away before speaking with the woman at the well?\r\nA. He wanted to test whether the disciples could find food without Him\r\nB. He needed privacy because the woman asked to speak with Him alone\r\nC. He suspected their cultural bias would hinder His evangelism of the woman\r\nD. He wanted the disciples to preach in the nearby town first\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the disciples marveled that He talked with a woman (John 4:27) and states his suspicion that Jesus sent them away because He knew their cultural bias would inhibit His evangelism.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [06:59; 07:58]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 4:27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+4%3A27/>\r\n\r\nQ42. How does the speaker connect Romans 10:9–10 to Jesus appearing first to women after His resurrection?\r\nA. Salvation rests on confessing the resurrection, and women were the first to announce it\r\nB. Salvation rests on confessing Christ publicly, which women were free to do in church\r\nC. The resurrection proved that women could serve as reliable witnesses in any court\r\nD. Paul wrote these verses to show that women were the first to be saved by faith\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Genuine salvation is predicated on faith and confession in the resurrection, and when Jesus rose He appeared to women and told them to go tell Peter and the disciples.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [08:36]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 10:9–10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+10%3A9%E2%80%9310/>\r\n\r\nQ43. Why does the speaker argue that choosing a woman as the first witness of the resurrection was deliberate rather than driven by culture?\r\nA. Roman law required that the first witness to any public event be a woman of standing\r\nB. Rabbinical writing said a woman's witness did not count, so pandering would have avoided it\r\nC. The male disciples were still in hiding and no one else was available to go\r\nD. Only women were permitted to enter the garden tomb according to Jewish custom\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He notes the rabbinical teaching that a woman's witness does not count, then says that if Jesus were pandering to the culture He would not have done this; he calls it deliberate and tied to divine kairos.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [08:36; 09:38]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 28:1–10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+28%3A1%E2%80%9310/>\r\n\r\nQ44. What standard rule of Bible interpretation does the speaker apply to the difficult texts about women?\r\nA. Begin with the most difficult passages to set the pattern\r\nB. Follow the interpretation most widely held in the culture\r\nC. Always move from what is known to what is unknown\r\nD. Read each text literally before considering its context\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says that in Bible interpretation you move from what is known to what is unknown, and repeats the principle later when concluding.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [10:38]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Galatians 3:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+3%3A28/>; 1 Corinthians 14:34 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+14%3A34/>; 1 Timothy 2:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+2%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ45. Why does the speaker say 1 Corinthians 11:5 counts against reading 1 Corinthians 14:34 as total silence for women?\r\nA. Chapter 11 was written to a different church from the one addressed in chapter 14\r\nB. Chapter 11 shows that women could prophesy only in private homes, not in the assembly\r\nC. Chapter 11 permits women to prophesy but forbids them to teach in any setting\r\nD. In chapter 11 Paul tells women how to prophesy, so he cannot demand they be mute in chapter 14\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Because Paul gives instructions about how women should prophesy (hair covering), the speaker argues he could not mean silence altogether in chapter 14.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [11:25]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Corinthians 11:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+11%3A5/>; 1 Corinthians 14:34 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+14%3A34/> | Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Timothy 2:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+2%3A15/>; Acts 21:8, 9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+21%3A8%2C+9/>\r\n\r\nQ46. How does the speaker use the word translated 'servant' in Romans 16:1 against the argument based on 'husband of one wife' in 1 Timothy 3?\r\nA. It shows Phoebe served only as a messenger, not as an officer of the church\r\nB. It is the same word translated 'deacon,' so Phoebe was openly a deaconess, not a secret\r\nC. It proves that 'husband of one wife' applied only to bishops and not to deacons\r\nD. It shows Paul used the word differently for women than he did for men\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The word for servant is the one translated deacon; the speaker says Phoebe was a deaconess, 'not a scandal, not a secret', so the 1 Timothy 3 argument fails.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [12:21]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 16:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+16%3A1/>; 1 Timothy 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+3/> | Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Corinthians 11:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+11%3A5/>; Acts 21:8, 9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+21%3A8%2C+9/>\r\n\r\nQ47. What further problem does the speaker raise with reading 'husband of one wife' strictly to mean a deacon must be a man?\r\nA. It would require every deacon to have first served as a bishop in the church\r\nB. It would prevent anyone who had been widowed or remarried from ever serving\r\nC. It would make the whole instruction apply to bishops and never to deacons\r\nD. It would also require a deacon to be married, disqualifying Paul himself\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says that if the phrase is read strictly, a deacon would also have to be married, which disqualifies Paul, who wrote it; he calls it too big a leap.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [19:11; 19:59]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Timothy 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+3/>; 1 Timothy 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+2/>\r\n\r\nQ48. What does the speaker infer from Paul naming Priscilla before Aquila in Romans 16?\r\nA. It was a customary courtesy in letters of the time with no significance\r\nB. It shows she hosted the house church while Aquila did the teaching\r\nC. It was unlikely to be accidental and implies she was more prominent\r\nD. It indicates that Aquila was not yet a believer at the time Paul wrote\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says it is unimaginable that mentioning the woman first was an accident in that context, so it implies she was more prominent.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [13:20]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+16/> | Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Timothy 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+3/>; Acts 18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+18/>\r\n\r\nQ49. What does the speaker conclude from Acts 18, where Priscilla and Aquila taught Apollos?\r\nA. Aquila alone taught Apollos while Priscilla only hosted and prepared the meeting\r\nB. Priscilla was a teacher, not merely arranging chairs, since both expounded the word to him\r\nC. Apollos was later corrected by the apostles after Priscilla made her report to them\r\nD. The teaching of Apollos was only a private conversation and not part of ministry\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The Bible says they expounded the word of God more perfectly to Apollos, 'both of them did', so the speaker says both were teachers.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [13:20; 14:21]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+18/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+16/>; 1 Timothy 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+3/>\r\n\r\nQ50. What significance does the speaker draw from Paul calling Priscilla a 'fellow worker' or 'co-laborer'?\r\nA. It is a title Paul used only for couples who hosted a church\r\nB. It distinguishes a volunteer helper from a recognized leader\r\nC. It was a phrase Paul reserved for those who had been imprisoned\r\nD. It is the same phrase Paul used to describe Timothy, who was a pastor\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He notes it is the exact same phrase used for Timothy, a pastor, and leaves the listener to connect the dots.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [14:21]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+18/>\r\n\r\nQ51. How does the speaker treat the reference to Andronicus and Junia in Romans 16:7?\r\nA. He insists it proves that Junia was an apostle equal in rank to the Twelve\r\nB. He calls it controversial, noting that at least it shows Junia was notable\r\nC. He dismisses the verse as irrelevant to any discussion of women in ministry\r\nD. He says it shows they were only Paul's relatives and nothing more than that\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He flags that the verse is controversial and that people disagree; at the base level, he says, it shows Junia was notable and did great work for the Lord.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [14:21; 15:25]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 16:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+16%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ52. Why does the speaker say Priscilla and Aquila teaching Apollos was 'no different from church'?\r\nA. Apollos had been formally commissioned to attend their meeting that week\r\nB. Their teaching was given before the entire congregation gathered in the city\r\nC. Churches met in people's houses because there were no church buildings\r\nD. Priscilla and Aquila held an official teaching office in the synagogue\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He reminds listeners that there were no church buildings and churches were literally held in people's houses.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [15:25]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 18:24–26 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+18%3A24%E2%80%9326/>\r\n\r\nQ53. In what specific setting does the speaker say 'let your women keep silent' was given in 1 Corinthians 14?\r\nA. The leading of congregational worship songs and the choice of hymns in the assembly\r\nB. Paul's instructions about marriage, family order and the care of widows\r\nC. A general discussion of dress and head coverings for both men and women in worship\r\nD. The orderly use of spiritual gifts, especially taking turns to interpret prophecy\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The chapter concerns administering spiritual gifts so there is no confusion; the speaker says the instruction comes in the context of interpreting prophecy, not talking or sharing the word generally.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [16:18; 17:15]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Corinthians 14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+14/>\r\n\r\nQ54. How do the scholars the speaker cites (Mike Winger and Anthony Thiselton) understand the word 'silence' in this passage?\r\nA. As abstaining from every form of speech for the whole duration of the gathered assembly\r\nB. As abstaining from speech only during prayer and the reading of the Scriptures\r\nC. As refraining from a particular kind of speech, such as rowdily speaking over others\r\nD. As a temporary rule that was meant to expire once Paul visited the church again\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says these scholars hold that 'silence' must be interpreted in context and means refraining from a particular type of speech.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [18:14]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Corinthians 14:34–35 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+14%3A34%E2%80%9335/>; 1 Timothy 2:11–15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+2%3A11%E2%80%9315/>; 1 Corinthians 11:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+11%3A5/>\r\n\r\nQ55. How does the speaker explain the instruction for women to ask their husbands at home?\r\nA. It teaches that wives need their husbands' permission before learning doctrine\r\nB. Men were educated and women were not, so it was a practical step toward order\r\nC. It reflects that only husbands were ever permitted to interpret prophecy in church\r\nD. It applied only to women whose husbands were recognized church leaders in the city\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He explains that in that culture men were educated and women were not, so asking at home was 'not theological degree' but a way to ensure order in God's house.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [18:14; 19:11]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 14:35 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+14%3A35/>\r\n\r\nQ56. How does the speaker read Paul's instruction about modest apparel in 1 Timothy 2:9?\r\nA. As a call for propriety and moderation, not a ban on wearing such items\r\nB. As an absolute prohibition on gold, pearls, braided hair and costly clothing\r\nC. As a dress code intended only for women who held leadership roles in the church\r\nD. As a symbolic instruction with no connection to the culture of the time\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Paul is calling for a clear distinction in an ostentatious culture and is 'not saying don't even wear these things'; there must be more to a woman than what she wears.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [19:59; 21:10]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Timothy 2:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+2%3A9/>\r\n\r\nQ57. What does the Greek word gune allow the speaker to argue about the instruction on subjection in 1 Timothy 2?\r\nA. Since gune can mean a wife, the subjection concerns a woman's husband rather than all men\r\nB. Since gune is the word for a widow, the instruction applies to older women\r\nC. Since gune refers to women generally, the verse governs women's role in every church\r\nD. Since gune appears only in this passage, the instruction cannot be interpreted\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Gune can refer to a married wife or women in general; translating it 'woman' gives the wrong impression. He says the instruction is addressed to her husband, which is why Adam is mentioned.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [23:22; 24:20]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Timothy 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+2/>\r\n\r\nQ58. Which detail in 1 Timothy 2 does the speaker say shows the passage concerns family life rather than roles in church?\r\nA. The instruction about modest apparel and braided hair in verse 9\r\nB. The mention of childbearing, which he calls family talk\r\nC. The reference to women learning in silence and in submission\r\nD. The mention of good works that suit women professing godliness\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says 'the fact that he says childbearing just tells you he's not talking about roles in church. This is family talk.'\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [25:16]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Timothy 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+2/>\r\n\r\nQ59. What does the speaker say about Paul's partnership with women in ministry relative to the culture of his day?\r\nA. It was normal practice in the Greek and Roman households of the time, so it drew no notice\r\nB. It was not normative; it was countercultural for Paul to partner with and commend women\r\nC. It was common among the rabbis, whose writings the speaker cites, in that period\r\nD. It began only after Paul wrote 1 Timothy and corrected his earlier practice\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He calls out 'Paul slander' and says it was not culturally normative for Paul to have women partnering with him and openly commending them.\r\nREF: JESUS AND WOMEN — [23:22]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 16:1–7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+16%3A1%E2%80%937/>; Philippians 4:2–3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+4%3A2%E2%80%933/>\r\n\r\nQ60. Which recurring phrase in the Book of Matthew does the speaker say will begin to jump out to the reader?\r\nA. 'Thus says the Lord'\r\nB. 'The kingdom of heaven is at hand'\r\nC. 'Verily I say unto you'\r\nD. 'That it might be fulfilled'\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that once taught this, the phrase 'that it might be fulfilled' stands out in Matthew.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS. — [00:15]\r\n\r\nQ61. In the speaker's account of Matthew 1:20–23, which prophecy is cited and what does the name in it mean?\r\nA. Isaiah 7:14; Immanuel, interpreted as 'God with us'\r\nB. Micah 5:2; Immanuel, interpreted as 'God with us'\r\nC. Isaiah 7:14; Jesus, interpreted as 'God with us'\r\nD. Hosea 11:1; Immanuel, interpreted as 'God with us'\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: After the angel appears to Joseph, Matthew says this was done to fulfill Isaiah 7:14: a virgin shall conceive and they shall call him Immanuel, 'God with us'.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS. — [01:17]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Matthew 1:20–23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+1%3A20%E2%80%9323/>; Isaiah 7:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+7%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ62. When Herod asked where the Messiah was to be born, how did the high priests find their answer?\r\nA. They questioned the wise men, who had already learned the location\r\nB. They recalled the angel's announcement to the shepherds in the fields\r\nC. They went to the Scriptures and found Micah 5:2, naming Bethlehem of Judea\r\nD. They consulted Hosea, who names Egypt as the child's place of origin\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker stresses that they did not know about Jesus's birth; they went to the Scriptures and found Micah 5:2, 'in Bethlehem of Judea'.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS. — [02:05; 02:51]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Micah 5:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Micah+5%3A2/>; Matthew 2:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+2%3A5/>\r\n\r\nQ63. Which Old Testament prophecy does Matthew connect with Jesus's time in Egypt after the angel warned Joseph?\r\nA. Jeremiah 31:15, 'Rachel weeping for her children'\r\nB. Micah 5:2, 'Out of you shall come a governor'\r\nC. Hosea 11:1, 'Out of Egypt I have called my son'\r\nD. Isaiah 7:14, 'A virgin shall be with child'\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Joseph stayed in Egypt until Herod's death so that what was spoken by the prophet might be fulfilled: 'out of Egypt I have called my son' (Hosea 11:1).\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS. — [02:51]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hosea 11:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hosea+11%3A1/>; Matthew 2:13, 15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+2%3A13%2C+15/>\r\n\r\nQ64. Which event does the speaker link to Jeremiah's prophecy of Rachel weeping for her children?\r\nA. The family's flight to Egypt to escape the king's threat\r\nB. The wise men's decision not to return to Herod with news\r\nC. Joseph's initial plan to put Mary away quietly and privately\r\nD. Herod's order to kill every child two years old and under\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: After being mocked by the wise men, Herod ordered every child two and under killed, which the speaker says fulfilled Jeremiah's prophecy (Matthew 2:17–18).\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS. — [03:42]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Matthew 2:17–18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+2%3A17%E2%80%9318/>; Jeremiah 31:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+31%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ65. What does the speaker say becomes possible when the scriptures Matthew cites (Isaiah, Micah, Hosea, Jeremiah) are arranged in chronological order?\r\nA. You can identify exactly which prophet wrote first\r\nB. You can retell the story of Christ from the Old Testament\r\nC. You can show that Matthew copied earlier gospel accounts\r\nD. You can prove that each prophet knew the others' writings\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says arranging these specific scriptures in order lets you retell the story of Christ from the Old Testament, because it was told in bits and pieces.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS. — [04:35]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Matthew 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+2/>; Isaiah 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+7/>; Micah 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Micah+5/>; Hosea 11:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hosea+11%3A1/>; Jeremiah 31:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+31%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ66. According to the speaker, why is Christ often not seen in the Old Testament even though knowledge of Him is plentiful there?\r\nA. It was told in bits and pieces, at various times and in various ways\r\nB. The prophets deliberately wrote in code to hide Him from their readers\r\nC. The Old Testament speaks of Christ only in the Psalms and a few other poems\r\nD. Jewish priests gradually removed references to Him over the centuries\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Citing Hebrews 1:1, he says it was told in various times, so Bible study works like a jigsaw puzzle to bring the pieces together.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS. — [04:35; 05:29]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Matthew 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+2/>; Isaiah 7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+7/>; Micah 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Micah+5/>; Hosea 11:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hosea+11%3A1/>; Jeremiah 31:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+31%3A15/>; Luke 24:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A25/> | Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ67. What was Jesus's point to the two on the road to Emmaus in Luke 24, as the speaker explains it?\r\nA. That the prophets had spoken only of a conquering political deliverer for Israel\r\nB. That the Christ had to suffer and enter His glory, as the Old Testament showed\r\nC. That they should set aside the Old Testament in light of what they had seen\r\nD. That the meaning of the resurrection would be explained to them later by the apostles\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Jesus called them foolish and slow of heart to believe all the prophets said; the speaker paraphrases that the Old Testament should have taught that Christ would suffer and enter His glory.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS. — [05:29]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Luke 24:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A25/>\r\n\r\nQ68. In Luke 24:27, what did Jesus do 'beginning at Moses and all the prophets'?\r\nA. He rebuked them for having rejected the writings of Moses and the prophets\r\nB. He quoted only from the prophets to prove His identity to them\r\nC. He expounded in all the Scriptures the things concerning Himself\r\nD. He read the law aloud to the assembled people at the synagogue\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reads verse 27: He expounded to them in all the Scriptures the things concerning Himself, showing many Old Testament passages concern Christ.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS. — [06:10]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Luke 24:27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A27/>\r\n\r\nQ69. Besides reading in context and applying rules like single, double and emphatic mention, what does the speaker say you must know to truly understand the Bible?\r\nA. Church history: tracing how doctrine developed after the apostles\r\nB. Eschatology: mapping the sequence of last-day events\r\nC. Hebrew grammar: reading the Old Testament in its original language\r\nD. Christology: seeing that the point of the whole Old Testament is Christ\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He calls Christology, the study of Christ, the one thing without which you don't really understand the Bible: the Old Testament's point is Christ, and the New Testament helps reveal veiled prophecies.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS. — [06:10; 07:03]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 24:27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A27/>\r\n\r\nQ70. What did John the Baptist's words 'Behold the Lamb of God' imply about the lambs brought to the temple, according to the speaker?\r\nA. They were to be replaced by a different animal offering\r\nB. They were meant to honor John's own ministry\r\nC. They were figures, a type and shadow of what Christ would do\r\nD. They were being abolished because the people had misused them\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says John's statement intimated that every literal lamb slaughtered at the temple was a figure, type and shadow of Christ's work.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS. — [07:03]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 1:29 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A29/>; Hebrews 9:11–14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+9%3A11%E2%80%9314/>\r\n\r\nQ71. In the speaker's explanation, what did the high priest taking the lamb's blood into the most holy place signify?\r\nA. That the people would be allowed into the most holy place\r\nB. That Christ would die privately, seen only by the priests\r\nC. That the lamb's death had already removed the people's sins\r\nD. That after dying in public, Christ would enter heaven on our behalf\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lamb slaughtered in the outer courts pointed to Christ dying in public; the blood entering the most holy place pointed to His entering heaven on our behalf.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS. — [07:03; 08:03]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 9:11–12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+9%3A11%E2%80%9312/>\r\n\r\nQ72. What does Hebrews 10:1, as read by the speaker, say the law has?\r\nA. The full image of the heavenly realities themselves, already present\r\nB. The power to make worshipers perfect through yearly offerings\r\nC. A shadow of good things to come, not the very image of them\r\nD. A temporary status that ends when the prophets arrive\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Hebrews 10:1 says the law has the shadow of good things to come and not the very image, so it can never make worshipers perfect with the same yearly sacrifices.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS. — [08:03; 09:03]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 10:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+10%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ73. What does the speaker use the story of a Nollywood actor playing a wicked man to illustrate?\r\nA. That Old Testament characters were fictional figures with no historical reality\r\nB. That Christ merely acted out a part rather than truly suffering for sin\r\nC. That wicked people in the Bible were merely playing roles God had assigned\r\nD. That sacrificial animals were roles pointing to what Christ would truly do\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The actor was blamed for his role because people couldn't separate the symbolism from the real; likewise the slaughtered rams were 'actors of Christ' pointing to what He would actually do.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS. — [09:03; 09:59]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+10/>\r\n\r\nQ74. According to the speaker, which Old Testament object was actually Christ?\r\nA. The ark of the covenant\r\nB. The rock that Moses struck\r\nC. The tablets of testimony\r\nD. The burning bush on Sinai\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Hebrews tells you that the rock Moses struck was actually Christ, as one of the Bible's metaphors.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS. — [10:48]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 10:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+10%3A4/>\r\n\r\nQ75. In the speaker's picture of salvation drawn from Israel's journey, which sequence is correct?\r\nA. Sinai as new birth; Red Sea as receiving the Holy Spirit; Promised Land entered under Moses\r\nB. Red Sea as receiving the Holy Spirit; Sinai as baptism; Promised Land entered under Joshua\r\nC. Promised Land as new birth; Sinai as baptism; Red Sea as the call to discipleship\r\nD. Red Sea: baptism and new birth; Sinai: Holy Spirit and discipleship; Promised Land: Joshua\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He treats crossing the Red Sea as salvation with baptism and new birth, Sinai as receiving the Holy Spirit and a call to discipleship, then entering the Promised Land under Joshua, whose name means savior.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS. — [10:48]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Exodus 14:21–31 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+14%3A21%E2%80%9331/>; 1 Corinthians 10:1–4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+10%3A1%E2%80%934/>; Hebrews 4:8–10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+4%3A8%E2%80%9310/>; Joshua 1:1–2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Joshua+1%3A1%E2%80%932/>\r\n\r\nQ76. What did the first disciples tell their company in John 1, according to the speaker?\r\nA. 'Come and see the prophet whom Elijah foretold'\r\nB. 'Come and see the one who will restore the temple'\r\nC. 'Come and see the person of whom Moses wrote in the Law'\r\nD. 'Come and see the teacher who explains the Law better than Moses'\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He recounts that they ran to Jesus and then to their company saying to come and see the person Moses wrote about in the Law, showing the Old Testament is full of knowledge about Christ.\r\nREF: DID OLD TESTAMENT AUTHORS KNOW JESUS. — [11:51]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1/>\r\n\r\nQ77. What does the speaker say Ruth's request, 'spread your garment over me because you are my redeemer,' amounted to in its cultural setting?\r\nA. A request for protection from a famine\r\nB. A formal payment of a family debt\r\nC. A ritual of mourning for her late husband\r\nD. A subtle invitation to marriage\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says that, read through its culture, Ruth's request to Boaz was a subtle invitation to marriage.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — [01:16]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ruth 3:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ruth+3%3A9/>\r\n\r\nQ78. What does the speaker say the Bible's honest portrayal of people's weaknesses and frailties suggests?\r\nA. That the authors lacked the skill to present their heroes in a better light\r\nB. That later editors inserted the stories to teach humility to future readers\r\nC. That it must be God's word, since a human writer would have edited them out\r\nD. That the stories were meant to be read as cautionary fiction, not history\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says that reading stories like this convinces him the Bible must be God's word, because if only a man were writing, some of these things would have been edited and weaknesses wouldn't show.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — [01:16; 02:13]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ruth 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ruth+3/>; Isaiah 6:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+6%3A2/>; 2 Corinthians 1:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+1%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ79. How does the speaker answer the question 'Do I need a degree to understand the Bible?'\r\nA. No, because Scripture is equally clear to every reader without any help\r\nB. Both yes and no: the writers did not intend to confuse, but barriers to interpretation remain\r\nC. Yes, because the Bible was deliberately written in a hidden esoteric language\r\nD. Yes, but only for the Old Testament, since the New Testament is plain\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the answer is both yes and no: no because the writers did not intend to confuse, yes because of difficulties that arise from time and context.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — [02:13]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 1:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+1%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ80. What does the speaker take from 2 Corinthians 1:13 about Paul's intent in writing?\r\nA. Paul deliberately wrote deeply so that only mature believers would understand\r\nB. Paul was not trying to be mysterious; he wrote what readers could understand\r\nC. Paul intended his letters to be explained only by the apostles themselves\r\nD. Paul wrote that some of his teaching would stay hidden until a later age\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Paul was not deliberately mysterious or trying to confuse; he wrote nothing other than what can be read, and (Ephesians 3) wrote for clarity, to unveil what was veiled.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — [02:13; 03:02]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 2 Corinthians 1:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+1%3A13/>; Ephesians 3:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+3%3A10/>\r\n\r\nQ81. Which set lists the barriers to Bible interpretation the speaker discusses?\r\nA. Language, figures of speech, and culture\r\nB. Language, genre, and chronology\r\nC. Culture, authorship, and translation committees\r\nD. Figures of speech, manuscripts, and geography\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He names the language barrier, figures of speech, and a culture barrier, all arising because the Bible has an immediate audience and time has passed.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — [03:02; 04:56; 07:52]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 1:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+1%3A13/>; Ephesians 3:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+3%3A10/>; Matthew 10:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+10%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ82. What does the speaker say about praying in the style of 'thou' and 'thee'?\r\nA. It is the original Hebrew idiom of prayer preserved in the translations we read\r\nB. It is King James English, not biblical language, and doesn't make you spiritual\r\nC. It is the style Jesus used when He prayed in the temple and synagogues\r\nD. It is required if one's prayers are to be heard and answered by God\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says that isn't biblical language but King James language, Old English; Jesus never spoke English, and speaking that way doesn't make you spiritual.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — [03:02; 03:56]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 1:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+1%3A13/>; Ephesians 3:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+3%3A10/>\r\n\r\nQ83. In the speaker's example 'evil communication corrupts good manners,' what did 'communication' mean to the writer?\r\nA. Conversation\r\nB. Association\r\nC. Instruction\r\nD. Correspondence\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Today communication means sharing information, but the writer meant association, showing how word usage changes over time.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — [03:56]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 15:33 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15%3A33/>\r\n\r\nQ84. How does the speaker distinguish a simile from a metaphor?\r\nA. A simile exaggerates a truth for effect, while a metaphor simply states it plainly\r\nB. A simile names both things being compared, while a metaphor names only one of them\r\nC. A simile appears only in poetry and prophecy, while a metaphor appears in narrative\r\nD. Similes compare with 'like' or 'as'; metaphors compare by direct representation\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Similes use words like 'like' and 'as'; metaphors are comparison by actual representation (e.g., 'he is a clumsy fox').\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — [04:56; 05:54]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Matthew 10:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+10%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ85. What does the speaker say Jesus means by telling the disciples to be 'wise as serpents and innocent as doves'?\r\nA. A figure of speech about attributes, being smart and gentle, not literal behavior\r\nB. A literal command to imitate the movements and habits of the animals named\r\nC. A warning that believers would be hunted and driven out like wild animals\r\nD. A promise that the disciples would always be protected from every danger\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Jesus is not being literal; it is simply a figure of speech referring to specific attributes: be gentle and be smart.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — [05:54]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Matthew 10:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+10%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ86. What does the speaker say Jesus is communicating when He says 'I am the door'?\r\nA. A lesson on the types of wood used in ancient doors and gates\r\nB. Access to the Father and to salvation through faith in His name\r\nC. A claim that He was guarding the entrance of the temple courts\r\nD. A reference to a narrow entrance reserved only for the Jewish people\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He calls it a metaphor about access to the Father and to salvation through faith in His name, not about types of wood or upholstery.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — [06:54]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 10:7–9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+10%3A7%E2%80%939/>\r\n\r\nQ87. Why does the speaker classify Jesus's saying about hating father, wife and one's own life as hyperbole?\r\nA. Jesus wanted His disciples to cut themselves off from their families\r\nB. The saying applies only to people who had rejected the gospel message\r\nC. In comparison with love for the Lord, love for family should look like hate\r\nD. It was a metaphor that equated family members with obstacles to the kingdom\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Jesus did not mean it literally because the same Jesus preached love; it is hyperbole meaning that, in comparison with love for the Lord, what you have for family is like hate.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — [06:54; 07:52]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 14:26 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+14%3A26/>; Matthew 10:37 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+10%3A37/>\r\n\r\nQ88. How many times does the speaker say the instruction to greet one another with a holy kiss appears in the Bible?\r\nA. Twice\r\nB. Four times\r\nC. Seven times\r\nD. Three times\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the idea is repeated four times in the Bible, and yet it is not practiced.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — [07:52]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 16:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+16%3A16/>; 1 Corinthians 16:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+16%3A20/>; 2 Corinthians 13:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+13%3A12/>; 1 Thessalonians 5:26 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A26/>\r\n\r\nQ89. What does the speaker say the holy-kiss instruction actually teaches?\r\nA. That a physical greeting must open every church service\r\nB. That cultural customs of greeting are unimportant to believers\r\nC. That the practice should be revived in every culture\r\nD. The principle of being cordial, not the kissing itself\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the idea was culture and that the principle behind it is being cordial: 'it is not the kissing that is the Bible teaching... let brotherly love continue.'\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — [08:34; 09:06]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 13:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+13%3A1/>; Romans 16:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+16%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ90. What attitude does the speaker commend from the Ethiopian eunuch's response to being asked whether he understood what he was reading?\r\nA. Confidence in his own ability to study the Scriptures alone\r\nB. Eagerness to debate Philip's interpretation of the passage\r\nC. The humility to admit he needed someone to explain it\r\nD. Reluctance to accept any help from a stranger on the road\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker urges the humility of the Ethiopian eunuch, who said, in effect, how can I understand unless someone teaches me.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — [09:53]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 8:26–31 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+8%3A26%E2%80%9331/>\r\n\r\nQ91. What does the speaker say is 'man's job,' which the God of the Bible will scarcely do Himself?\r\nA. Writing down the Scriptures for each generation\r\nB. Confirming the gospel with signs and miracles everywhere\r\nC. Teaching the fundamentals, that is, discipleship\r\nD. Interpreting people's dreams and visions in every season\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the Holy Spirit can lead you to materials, but God will scarcely teach the fundamentals Himself, since teaching and discipleship are man's job (as with Cornelius being led to Peter).\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — [10:48]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10/>; Matthew 28:19–20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+28%3A19%E2%80%9320/>\r\n\r\nQ92. What closing warning does the speaker give about being part of a church?\r\nA. Make sure you are growing, not just playing church or religion\r\nB. Avoid commentaries so that you rely only on the Holy Spirit\r\nC. Prefer private study at home over attending any teaching classes\r\nD. Wait for kairos before you look for a teacher to guide you\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He closes by urging listeners to get a good pastor and make sure they are not just playing church or religion but growing and understanding the text better.\r\nREF: CAN I UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE ON MY OWN. — [10:48; 11:46]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 4:11-13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+4%3A11-13/>\r\n\r\nQ93. What point does the speaker make with Papa Hagin's statement that there are no Bible schools in the Bible?\r\nA. Doctrine is meant to be learned in the local church, though Bible schools aren't wrong\r\nB. Bible schools are unbiblical, so believers should avoid them and study only at home\r\nC. Doctrine should be learned only through private study of Scripture, never in groups\r\nD. The local church exists mainly for miracles and favor, not for doctrinal teaching\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the local church, the pillar and ground of truth, is where doctrine ought to be learned; this doesn't mean Bible schools are wrong, only that this is God's original design.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [00:15; 01:05]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Timothy 3:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+3%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ94. What does the speaker identify as one of the true signs of conversion?\r\nA. Receiving miracles and favor\r\nB. Preferring sermons about what God will do\r\nC. Eagerness to attend Bible school\r\nD. A genuine love for the word of God\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says one of the true signs of conversion is that you love the word of God.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [01:05]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Peter 2:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+2%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ95. Which three aspects of the Bible does the speaker say he is addressing?\r\nA. Its inspiration, canon and translation\r\nB. Its inspiration, inerrancy and reliability\r\nC. Its authorship, authority and application\r\nD. Its inerrancy, history and prophecy\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the month's discourse addresses the inspiration, the inerrancy, and the reliability of the Bible.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [01:48]\r\n\r\nQ96. Which ways of divine leading does the speaker say people usually think of first?\r\nA. An audible voice or an inward witness\r\nB. Dreams interpreted by prophets or angelic visitations\r\nC. Scripture reading and wise counsel\r\nD. Circumstances and providence\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says people think of God's audible voice or an inward witness, because they think only in ways a man can receive communication, while the realm of the spirit is deeper.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [01:48; 02:46]\r\n\r\nQ97. Why does the speaker say providence is such a powerful way for God to lead?\r\nA. It works entirely without any involvement of the human will or choices\r\nB. It leaves a person completely unaware of any spiritual activity around them\r\nC. The devil cannot know the plan beforehand, unlike when God speaks audibly\r\nD. It makes angelic encounters and visions unnecessary for believers today\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He explains that with an audible voice the devil has information to work with (as with 'If you are the Son of God...'), but providence is a way even the devil will not know until the end.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [04:59; 05:45]\r\n\r\nQ98. What does the speaker draw from the statement that if the rulers had known, they would not have crucified the Lord of glory?\r\nA. The devil unknowingly became part of God's plan while trying to destroy it\r\nB. God hides His plans from believers just as He hides them from the devil\r\nC. The rulers bore no responsibility for the crucifixion since God planned it\r\nD. The crucifixion was a mistake that God later turned around and corrected\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the devil is oblivious and even sponsors God's plan, thinking he is trying to destroy you; if you oppose what God is about to do, His sovereignty and providence make you part of the plan.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [05:45]\r\n\r\nQ99. How does the speaker say Pharaoh unknowingly served God's purposes regarding Moses?\r\nA. By ordering the Israelites released immediately after Moses first asked\r\nB. By appointing Moses as the ruler over all of the Hebrew people\r\nC. By raising, feeding and educating the deliverer of the people he hated\r\nD. By sending Moses to learn leadership among his own people the Israelites\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Pharaoh paid for Moses's education, raised and fed him, supplying the exposed, educated leader God needed to deliver the people he opposed.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [06:24; 07:06]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 7:21-22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+7%3A21-22/>\r\n\r\nQ100. How does the speaker say God resists the proud?\r\nA. By making sure their replacement is someone they thought would never rise\r\nB. By hiding His plan from them until it is far too late to respond\r\nC. By withdrawing every gift He once gave them and humbling them publicly\r\nD. By raising up an enemy from a foreign nation to attack them\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the way God resists the proud is to ensure that their replacement is someone they thought would never rise, and illustrates it with a boy raised in a home who later declares 'let my people go'.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [07:06]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: James 4:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+4%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ101. What does the speaker say about most of the Bible's authors?\r\nA. They knew they were writing Scripture and followed instructions to produce it\r\nB. They didn't know they were Bible authors and wrote on matters of their own day\r\nC. They wrote together as a committee to produce one single unified book of law\r\nD. They wrote only during moments of direct revelation from God, never otherwise\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says most authors did not know they were Bible authors; they wrote what they thought was relevant for their time, not knowing it would become the inerrant word of God.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [08:49]\r\n\r\nQ102. Approximately how many authors does the speaker say wrote the Bible?\r\nA. Twelve\r\nB. Seventy\r\nC. Forty\r\nD. Four hundred\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says 40 authors from different walks of life, genres and languages wrote what became the Bible.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [09:44]\r\n\r\nQ103. Why does the speaker say the Bible is a miracle even though God did not dictate it to the authors?\r\nA. God prepared them through their exposure and learning so their writing fit His plan\r\nB. God overrode their personalities and natural gifts whenever they picked up the pen\r\nC. God allowed them to edit one another's work until the books all agreed with each other\r\nD. God ensured that only recognized prophets were ever allowed to write Scripture\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says God did not have to dictate it; He prepared the writers with their exposure and learning so their writing was relevant to what God was going to do.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [10:47]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Peter 1:20-21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Peter+1%3A20-21/>\r\n\r\nQ104. What does the speaker's Marvel and Avengers illustration show about the Bible?\r\nA. Bible stories are best understood as entertainment, like popular films, for modern audiences\r\nB. The Bible's authors all worked for a single publisher on a carefully planned series\r\nC. The Bible's books were all planned in advance by one editor from the very beginning\r\nD. Separate stories by different storytellers merging into one would be a miracle, like the Bible's authors\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He compares individual character stories merging into one end game with the Bible: different storytellers whose pieces fit together in one interdependent story would be a miracle.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [11:41; 12:40]\r\n\r\nQ105. Which prophet does the speaker cite for the prediction that the Messiah would ride on a donkey?\r\nA. Isaiah\r\nB. Micah\r\nC. Hosea\r\nD. Zechariah\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He lists prophecies Jesus fulfilled, saying Zechariah 9 foretold that He would ride on a donkey, and that the prophecies fit together like a jigsaw puzzle.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [13:36]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Zechariah 9:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Zechariah+9%3A9/>\r\n\r\nQ106. How does the speaker answer the objection 'If the Bible is the word of God, why doesn't it talk about dinosaurs?'\r\nA. It is not a zoology book; it is for salvation and shouldn't be held to other standards\r\nB. Dinosaurs are described in the Bible under other names that require careful study\r\nC. Such questions show that the Bible was edited by later authors who left things out\r\nD. Such questions can only be answered by modern scientists and never by Scripture\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He calls this a strawman: people hold the Bible to standards it doesn't set for itself; it is not a book on zoology but Scripture able to make one wise unto salvation.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [14:30; 15:40]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Micah 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Micah+5/>; Zechariah 9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Zechariah+9/>\r\n\r\nQ107. What does the speaker say about Joshua's command for the sun to stand still?\r\nA. Joshua understood astronomy well and deliberately meant that the earth stood still for a day\r\nB. The command was only a figure of speech, and no real miracle took place at that time\r\nC. Joshua misled the people with a faulty command, though God later corrected the record\r\nD. Joshua spoke from his own understanding and experienced a real miracle; God worked with that\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Joshua had no geography degree to know the sun does not move, but experienced a miracle; God worked with his level of understanding, and dismissing the Bible on that basis misunderstands it.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [15:40; 16:34]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Joshua 10:12-14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Joshua+10%3A12-14/>\r\n\r\nQ108. According to the speaker, what does inspiration (being 'God-breathed') not mean?\r\nA. Selecting and preparing the writers for the task they would do\r\nB. The writers' natural gifts being used in what they wrote\r\nC. Scripture being free from errors of truth in what it teaches\r\nD. Dictation, with God speaking and the writers simply writing it down\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says inspiration does not mean dictation: God was not speaking the way you hear him while the authors wrote.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [18:37]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Peter 1:21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Peter+1%3A21/>; Luke 1:1-4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+1%3A1-4/>\r\n\r\nQ109. What does the speaker's example of asking his P.A. to write a letter illustrate?\r\nA. Secretaries were regularly used to compile the Gospels from eyewitness reports\r\nB. The author of a letter must always be the one who physically writes it\r\nC. A scribe is the writer but not the author, and even that isn't how Scripture mostly came\r\nD. Bible authors needed trained assistants in order to write their books\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the PA would be the writer but not the author, and that even this is not mostly the case with the Bible, since God can use providence to prepare what they write.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [18:37; 19:34]\r\n\r\nQ110. In what way does the speaker say the Bible is primarily inspired?\r\nA. God dictated every word of it to the writers through angels\r\nB. God commissioned a council to edit and approve the writings\r\nC. God hand-picked the people whose writings would be Scripture\r\nD. God confirmed the books only after they had been gathered together\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says that primarily God hand picked the people whose writings would become Scripture.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [21:10]\r\n\r\nQ111. What is the point of the speaker's illustration of a woman who leaves breadcrumbs so a man feels the decision to pursue her was his own?\r\nA. Women should take the initiative in relationships whenever culture allows it\r\nB. People can manipulate God's plan by dropping subtle signs at the right moment\r\nC. The Bible writers deliberately hid their intentions from their first readers\r\nD. God can lead people to decisions that feel like their own, as He led the writers\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He uses it to show leading without categorical instruction: the guy feels he is taking initiative, and 'the Lord can lead you... that's how he led the Bible writers.'\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [20:15; 21:10]\r\n\r\nQ112. How does the speaker respond to the claim that the Bible was written only by men?\r\nA. God Himself made the first writing, on the tablets Moses brought down\r\nB. Joshua inscribed the first tablets on the mountain at God's command\r\nC. Angels wrote the first tablets and then handed them to Moses on the mountain\r\nD. Moses wrote the first tablets himself under direct dictation from God\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He cites Exodus 32:15–16: the tablets were the work of God and the writing was the writing of God, engraved on the tablets.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [22:29; 23:19]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Exodus 32:15–16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+32%3A15%E2%80%9316/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Deuteronomy 10:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+10%3A5/>; Joshua 24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Joshua+24/>\r\n\r\nQ113. What does the speaker say Joshua's recording of the words of the law in Joshua 24 shows?\r\nA. The tradition of writing was continued from generation to generation\r\nB. The tablets in the ark were replaced by Joshua's scroll\r\nC. Joshua was the sole author of the whole law of Moses\r\nD. Writing was forbidden for anyone outside the sanctuary and its priests\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: After the tablets were kept in the ark (Deuteronomy 10:5), God instructed Joshua to write; the speaker says the tradition of writing continued from generation to generation.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [23:19; 24:10]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Deuteronomy 10:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+10%3A5/>; Joshua 24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Joshua+24/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Exodus 32:15, 15, 16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+32%3A15%2C+15%2C+16/>\r\n\r\nQ114. What does the speaker point out about Luke to show how God worked with an author's background?\r\nA. He was a fisherman who needed help to write well in Greek\r\nB. He was a medical doctor and researcher, as his opening shows\r\nC. He was an eyewitness of every event in his Gospel account\r\nD. He was a tax collector trained in keeping careful records\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He calls Luke a medical doctor, more voluminous like a literature review, who opens with 'perfect understanding of all things'; God worked with their exposures.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [24:10]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Deuteronomy 10:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+10%3A5/>; Joshua 24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Joshua+24/>\r\n\r\nQ115. What does the speaker say inspiration does not do to the writers?\r\nA. It does not leave them free to choose which events they would record\r\nB. It does not require them to know the language they are writing in at all\r\nC. It does not bypass their personalities, idiosyncrasies and natural gifts\r\nD. It does not apply to books written by the more educated of the authors\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says inspiration doesn't bypass their personalities, idiosyncrasies or natural gifts; they weren't in a trance with animated hands.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [25:04]\r\n\r\nQ116. According to the speaker, what chronology argument answers the claim that the Bible was corrupted?\r\nA. The Quran came at least 300 years after Christ, when the canon was already complete\r\nB. Bible manuscripts were rewritten in the 300 years after Christ to fit later beliefs\r\nC. The Quran confirmed every passage of the Bible, so no further proof is needed\r\nD. The Bible was compiled and finished only after the Quran had been written\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He argues the Quran came at least 300 years after Christ, when the canon was already complete; manuscripts that predate it are identical to those today, so there is no change.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [26:11; 27:15]\r\n\r\nQ117. What two objective tests does the speaker name for judging whether an ancient writing is authentic?\r\nA. The time gap to the earliest manuscripts, and the number of manuscripts\r\nB. The popularity of the author and the length of the text itself\r\nC. The number of languages it was translated into and the age of its translators\r\nD. The number of copyists and the quality of the writing material\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He names the distance in time between original writing and the earliest manuscripts, and the number of manuscripts.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [27:15; 28:09]\r\n\r\nQ118. Why does the speaker say a larger number of manuscripts helps?\r\nA. More copies show that the text was widely popular among the scribes of its day\r\nB. More copies allow translators to choose the easiest and clearest wording available\r\nC. More copies prove that the original author personally approved the final text\r\nD. Discrepancies are easy to spot, so the original can be told from changed copies\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says 'the more copies, the merrier' because any discrepancies make it easy to tell which one is original and which has been changed.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [28:09]\r\n\r\nQ119. Why does the speaker find 1 Corinthians 15's mention of 500 witnesses powerful for reliability?\r\nA. Many were still alive when Paul wrote, so a false claim could be debunked\r\nB. The 500 had each written down their own separate accounts of the event\r\nC. The witnesses were later gathered together to verify Paul's letter in person\r\nD. Paul named every witness so that Roman officials could question each of them\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Paul said Christ appeared to 500 at once, many still alive then, so if false it could have been debunked.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [29:01]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Corinthians 15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15/>\r\n\r\nQ120. What does the speaker say Socrates's words on his deathbed show about eternal life?\r\nA. Brilliant people couldn't speak decisively on it; he said no one is sure\r\nB. Socrates was completely certain that he would reach good men after death\r\nC. Socrates rejected the idea of any afterlife and mocked those who held it\r\nD. Socrates pointed his students to the altar of the unknown God in Athens\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says brilliant people could not tell us decisively about eternal life, quoting Socrates, who hoped to go to good men but said that of that no one is sure; only God knows.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [30:10; 31:05]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Corinthians 15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15/>; Acts 17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17/>\r\n\r\nQ121. Why does the speaker present Paul's arrival in Athens in Acts 17 as an example of providence?\r\nA. Paul was sent to Athens by a direct audible command that he obeyed at once\r\nB. Paul landed where Socrates taught and declared the resurrection to worshipers of an unknown God\r\nC. Paul was invited by the philosophers of the city to come and answer their questions\r\nD. Paul was brought to Athens as a prisoner of the Romans and defended himself there\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Paul found himself in Greece, the region where Socrates taught, and told them about the 'unknown God' and the death and resurrection of Jesus, showing eternal life is open to all who believe.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [31:05; 31:48]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17/>\r\n\r\nQ122. What does the speaker conclude from the statement that God works in you both to will and to do?\r\nA. God only acts after you have already decided for yourself what you want to do\r\nB. God can inspire desire in you and influence you toward where He wants you\r\nC. God overrides your will completely so that you no longer make any choices\r\nD. God works only on your outward actions and never on your inner desires\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says God works not just to do but to will: He can inspire a desire and influence your propensities so you end up where He wants.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [32:40]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Philippians 2:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+2%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ123. What change does the speaker say this month's study should bring to your 'library'?\r\nA. Replacing every devotional book with commentaries and Bible dictionaries only\r\nB. Reading only recent books written by modern authors within the last century\r\nC. Moving from pragmatic how-to books to learning about your faith and who God is\r\nD. Collecting more material about what God can do and what He can give you\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says your library must change from just pragmatic things like how to get what you want, to learning about your faith, what God has done, and who He is, not just what He can give.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [32:40; 33:59]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 17:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+17%3A3/>\r\n\r\nQ124. What does the speaker use Abraham to show about God's word?\r\nA. God's promises must be partly fulfilled in history before they become fully real\r\nB. God's word depends on a person's response before it can take effect at all\r\nC. God's word to Abraham was only a symbolic image with no literal fulfillment\r\nD. God needn't give literally; He just speaks it, as He made Abraham a father of nations\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says when God wants to give you something He just has to speak it: He told Abraham 'I have made you a father of many nations', making him by words.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [33:59]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 17:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+17%3A5/>\r\n\r\nQ125. What does the speaker conclude from John 1:1 about God and His Word?\r\nA. There is no differentiation between God and His Word\r\nB. The Word is a created agent that God sends out\r\nC. God's Word carries less authority than His name\r\nD. The Word became God only at the incarnation of Christ\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says there is no differentiation between God and His Word: 'The Word of God is God. What God can do, His Word can do.'\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [35:01]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ126. What does the speaker say about how the story of this age ends?\r\nA. Believers win: mortality is swallowed up by immortality, per written prophecy\r\nB. The outcome depends on how effectively believers influence the world around them\r\nC. The ending is not revealed in Scripture and remains unknown to everyone\r\nD. The ending requires God to remove human free will from every person\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says there is a written prophecy of how it all ends ('We win'), citing the trump of God, mortality swallowed by immortality, and the dead in Christ rising first.\r\nREF: THE INSPIRATION OF SCRIPTURES — [35:01; 36:01]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Thessalonians 4:16–17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+4%3A16%E2%80%9317/>; 1 Corinthians 15:51–54 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15%3A51%E2%80%9354/>\n\nDECK: Discipleship Experience (Section 9) Evangelism\r\n\r\nQ1. What is the problem the lesson raises when pantheism says that everything is divine?\r\nA. It means that people can no longer distinguish between actions that are socially acceptable and those that are not.\r\nB. It makes it difficult to explain why people disagree about what is good and evil.\r\nC. It means that good and evil become dependent on individual opinions rather than being understood through spiritual truth.\r\nD. If everything is divine and inherently good, it becomes difficult to account for real evil and suffering that is objectively wrong.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Pantheism’s claim that all is divine and good conflicts with the reality of genuinely wrong evil and suffering.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY II — 08:19–11:14\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 1:18-25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+1%3A18-25/>\r\n\r\nQ2. According to the Mormon doctrine described in the lesson, how is God the Father understood?\r\nA. As a glorified physical being who passed through a mortal experience before attaining His present exalted state.\r\nB. As an eternal, non-created God who has always existed as God.\r\nC. As an exalted human who became God only after creating the physical universe.\r\nD. As the first spirit being created by Jesus before the creation of humanity.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson describes the Mormon doctrine of the Father as a glorified physical being who experienced mortality before exaltation.\r\nREF: MORMONS II — 00:17–01:14\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Isaiah 43:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+43%3A11/>; Isaiah 44:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+44%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ3. Which of these is the most important reason we should share the gospel?\r\nA. Christians have been given the responsibility of convincing people that Christianity is true.\r\nB. People are more likely to believe when Christians consistently speak about their faith.\r\nC. There is death and life, and life is found in Christ and Christ alone.\r\nD. People need to hear Christian beliefs before they can make informed decisions about faith.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson gives the contrast between death and life, and identifies Christ as the source of eternal life, as the reason to share the gospel.\r\nREF: HOW TO EVANGELIZE — 01:38–02:31\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 6:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6%3A23/>\r\n\r\nQ4. Why does the lesson use John 1:1-3 and Colossians 1:16-17 in challenging the Mormon understanding of Jesus?\r\nA. They show that Jesus became equal with God after He completed His earthly ministry.\r\nB. They show that Jesus was the first spirit being created by God.\r\nC. They show that Jesus became God's Son when He was born as a human being.\r\nD. They show that Jesus was with God in the beginning, is God, and that all things were created through Him.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson uses these passages to present Jesus as divine, present with God from the beginning, and involved in creation.\r\nREF: MORMONS II — 03:08–05:07\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 1:1–3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1%E2%80%933/>; Colossians 1:16–17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+1%3A16%E2%80%9317/> | Also cited in lesson segment: John 8:58 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+8%3A58/>; John 20:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+20%3A28/>; Genesis 1:26 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+1%3A26/>\r\n\r\nQ5. According to the lesson, which activity is specifically evangelism?\r\nA. Sharing about your faith and telling someone about Jesus.\r\nB. Serving people with the resources and opportunities God has given you.\r\nC. Learning Christian doctrine so you can respond when someone challenges your faith.\r\nD. Using your conduct to demonstrate what it looks like to live as a Christian.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson defines evangelism as sharing one’s faith and telling someone about Jesus.\r\nREF: HOW TO EVANGELIZE — 02:31\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Galatians 2:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+2%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ6. What does the lesson identify as a major issue with the Seventh-day Adventist understanding of atonement?\r\nA. They believe the Old Testament sacrifices were sufficient.\r\nB. They believe the atonement is unfinished and that Jesus is presently making atonement.\r\nC. They believe Jesus did not die for sin.\r\nD. They believe sin has already completely ended.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson characterizes the Adventist position as an unfinished atonement with Jesus presently making atonement.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS IV — 08:51–12:19\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Daniel 8:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Daniel+8%3A14/>; Hebrews 10:10, 18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+10%3A10%2C+18/>\r\n\r\nQ7. What does the lesson identify as a major difference between Christianity and Islam concerning humanity's relationship with God?\r\nA. Christians believe everyone is automatically a child of God, while Muslims believe only prophets are children of Allah.\r\nB. Christians believe God's children cannot sin, while Muslims believe they can.\r\nC. Christians believe God created humanity, while Muslims believe Allah created only Muslims.\r\nD. Christians can speak of being adopted into God's family and called children of God, while Islam does not present this same relationship.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson contrasts Christian adoption into God’s family with the relationship it describes in Islam.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS I — 10:28–11:07\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Galatians 4:4-7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+4%3A4-7/>\r\n\r\nQ8. What does the lesson describe as the connection between the New Age promise of control and Genesis 3?\r\nA. Both present the possibility of becoming like God by gaining a higher form of knowledge.\r\nB. Both encourage people to seek knowledge so they can understand the difference between good and evil.\r\nC. Both present the idea of becoming like God and defining good and evil for yourself.\r\nD. Both teach that human beings can become wiser by discovering their own spiritual nature.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson links the New Age promise of control with Genesis 3’s offer of becoming like God and defining good and evil oneself.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY I — 14:06–14:52\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+3/>\r\n\r\nQ9. Why is Isaiah 44:24 important to the argument about Jesus as Creator?\r\nA. It teaches that Jehovah created the world through an angel.\r\nB. It says Jehovah created the heavens and earth \"by myself,\" making Jesus' role as Creator significant.\r\nC. It teaches that Jehovah created only the heavens.\r\nD. It teaches that Jesus created the world before His incarnation.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson contrasts Isaiah’s statement that Jehovah created alone with New Testament passages presenting Jesus as Creator.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS IV — 02:34–04:05\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Isaiah 44:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+44%3A24/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Isaiah 48:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+48%3A12/>; Revelation 1:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+1%3A17/>; Revelation 22:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+22%3A12/>; Colossians 1:16–17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+1%3A16%E2%80%9317/>; Isaiah 48:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+48%3A13/>; John 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A3/>; Isaiah 9:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+9%3A6/>; Isaiah 10:21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+10%3A21/>\r\n\r\nQ10. A Muslim keeps asking a new question immediately after the Christian answers the previous one. Based on the lesson, what should the Christian do?\r\nA. Change the subject to another Islamic doctrine before answering.\r\nB. Answer every question immediately so the Muslim cannot change the subject.\r\nC. Stop the conversation because asking multiple questions means the Muslim is not interested.\r\nD. Take the steering of the conversation by identifying the actual stumbling block and asking whether resolving it removes that objection.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson advises identifying the actual objection and checking whether resolving it would remove the person’s stumbling block.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS III — 12:14–13:22\r\n\r\nQ11. Which sequence represents the Mormon teaching about humanity described in the lesson?\r\nA. Humans existed as premortal spirits, entered earthly life, and are expected to progress toward exaltation and become gods.\r\nB. Humans are created physically, die, become spirits, and may eventually become gods.\r\nC. Humans existed as angels, became physical beings, and return to angelic form after death.\r\nD. Humans were created in God's image, become spiritually separated from Him, and regain God's nature through good works.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson describes a premortal existence, earthly life, and progression toward becoming gods.\r\nREF: MORMONS II — 05:07–06:11\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A3/>; Colossians 1:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+1%3A16/>; John 8:58 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+8%3A58/>; John 20:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+20%3A28/>; Genesis 1:26 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+1%3A26/>; Psalm 8:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+8%3A4/>; Acts 17:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17%3A25/>; Acts 17:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ12. How does the lesson use Colossians 2:16-17 and Acts 15 in his argument about the Sabbath?\r\nA. To show that the Jerusalem Council transferred the Sabbath requirement from Jewish Christians to Gentile Christians.\r\nB. To establish Sunday as the new Christian Sabbath.\r\nC. To argue that the Sabbath belonged to the shadows pointing toward Christ and that the Mosaic requirement was not imposed upon Gentile believers.\r\nD. To establish that the seventh-day Sabbath was never commanded by God.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson uses Colossians 2 and Acts 15 to argue that the Sabbath pointed to Christ and was not imposed as a Mosaic requirement on Gentile believers.\r\nREF: MORMONS III — 02:07–02:59\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Colossians 2:16–17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+2%3A16%E2%80%9317/>; Acts 15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+15/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+2/>; Exodus 28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+28/>\r\n\r\nQ13. What does the lesson say there is something in every person that causes them to look for God. What important distinction is made about this search?\r\nA. People can genuinely be yearning for the divine while still searching for God in a misguided form.\r\nB. People may search for God through different spiritual beliefs, but the search itself shows that they have recognized the truth about God.\r\nC. People have a natural desire for the divine, but that desire does not necessarily mean they have correctly understood who God is.\r\nD. People who are searching for God are already moving in the right direction, even when their understanding of God is incomplete.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson distinguishes the desire to seek God from correctly understanding who God is.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY I — 00:56–03:23\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+1/>; Acts 17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17/>\r\n\r\nQ14. What do Jehovah's Witnesses believe about death, according to the lesson?\r\nA. The spirit remains consciously alive after death.\r\nB. Everyone immediately appears before God after death.\r\nC. Death is the complete end of both body and soul until the resurrection.\r\nD. The soul immediately enters heaven or hell.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says Jehovah’s Witnesses teach that death ends both body and soul until the resurrection.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS II — 09:03–10:02\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Thessalonians 5:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A23/>; Job 32 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Job+32/>; Proverbs 20:27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Proverbs+20%3A27/>; Romans 8:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A16/>; James 1:21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A21/>\r\n\r\nQ15. What is the lesson's central objection to the Adventist chronology of Christ's heavenly ministry?\r\nA. Hebrews teaches that Christ entered the heavenly sanctuary only after the investigative judgment had already been completed.\r\nB. Hebrews teaches that Christ entered the holy place once for all by His own blood, securing eternal redemption, rather than returning later to begin another atoning phase.\r\nC. Hebrews teaches that Christ's priesthood ended immediately after His resurrection.\r\nD. Hebrews teaches that Christ is not the High Priest in heaven.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson contrasts Christ’s once-for-all entrance and eternal redemption with the Adventist chronology of a later investigative judgment.\r\nREF: MORMONS II — 14:50–15:39\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 9:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+9%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ16. Why does the lesson challenge the statement \"the real religion is love\"?\r\nA. Because saying that love is the real religion does not explain which religious practices are correct.\r\nB. Because asking what \"love\" means reveals that different religions can give very different definitions of what love is.\r\nC. Because love is only properly understood through Christianity.\r\nD. Because some religions place more emphasis on love than others.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson challenges the statement by noting that religions can define love differently.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY III — 02:32–03:31\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 4:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+4%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ17. According to the lesson, what is presented as the central theme that holds every Muslim together?\r\nA. Allah is the creator and Muhammad is the greatest prophet.\r\nB. There is no god but Allah and Muhammad is the messenger of Allah.\r\nC. Allah is one and Muslims must follow the five major practices.\r\nD. Jesus is the Messiah and Muhammad is the final messenger.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson identifies this confession as Islam’s central theme and says it is called the shahada.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS I — 02:48–04:01\r\n\r\nQ18. The Lesson uses Jude 1:3 to establish that Christians are to \"contend for the faith that was first delivered unto the saints.\" What specific idea does he draw from this?\r\nA. Christianity is definite because anyone who sincerely identifies with Jesus is necessarily holding the same faith.\r\nB. Christianity has a recognizable pattern of belief that can be traced to what was originally delivered and believed.\r\nC. Christians must defend every practice that has been associated with Christianity throughout history.\r\nD. Christianity is authoritative because all Christians throughout history have belonged to the same denomination.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson draws from Jude 1:3 that the Christian faith has a definite, recognizable pattern traceable to what was originally believed.\r\nREF: MORMONS I — 00:00–01:54\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Jude 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jude+1%3A3/>\r\n\r\nQ19. What is the purpose of meditation as described in the lesson?\r\nA. To focus deeply on God's Word until we become spiritually sensitive enough to receive what God wants to say.\r\nB. To meditate on Scripture until the truth of God's Word becomes a reality in our lives.\r\nC. To retain the consciousness of what God's Word says so that we may observe and do according to it.\r\nD. To repeatedly speak God's Word until it becomes deeply established in the mind.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson describes Christian meditation as retaining awareness of God’s Word in order to observe and do what it says.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY III — 08:20–09:35\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Joshua 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Joshua+1%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ20. Which statement most accurately captures the definition of pantheism given in the lesson?\r\nA. Everything in creation contains something divine because the universe is an extension of God's presence.\r\nB. All reality is one divine substance of consciousness; God is not separate from creation, and everything created is divine.\r\nC. God exists throughout creation and can be experienced through everything that exists.\r\nD. God created the universe and remains present within it, making creation a reflection of God's divine nature.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson defines pantheism as one divine reality in which God is not separate from creation.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY II — 08:19–09:22\r\n\r\nQ21. What does the lesson identify as one of the beauties of Christianity when it comes to human pain?\r\nA. Christianity introduces us to a God who identifies with our pain and comforts us in our suffering.\r\nB. Christianity teaches that emotional pain is evidence that someone has sinned.\r\nC. Christianity gives people a way to avoid pain completely.\r\nD. Christianity teaches that people should understand the reason behind every painful experience.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says Christianity presents a God who empathizes with human pain and comforts people in suffering.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY II — 02:51–05:00\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+4/>; 2 Corinthians 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+1/>\r\n\r\nQ22. Why does the lesson reject the argument that \"God\" and \"Allah\" are simply different language terms for the same God?\r\nA. Because Allah is presented as a God of judgment while the biblical God is presented only as a God of love.\r\nB. Because Muslims and Christians use different names for Jesus.\r\nC. Because we can only know God by how God introduces himself.\r\nD. Because the names Abraham, Moses and David are also different in Islam.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson argues that the names alone do not establish that the two faiths refer to the same God; it compares their descriptions of God.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS I — 05:31–08:11\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A8/>; Acts 13:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+13%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ23. What is the distinction the lesson makes between manifestation and faith?\r\nA. Manifestation seeks something through spiritual means, while faith seeks something through God's means.\r\nB. Manifestation looks to the universe for something to happen, while faith looks to God for something to happen.\r\nC. Manifestation is based on what a person wants to receive, while faith is based on what a person believes God can provide.\r\nD. Manifestation is about trying to conjure something into existence, while faith is a response and alignment to what God has already said.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson contrasts attempts to manifest or conjure outcomes with faith as alignment to what God has said.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY III — 09:56–11:09\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17/>\r\n\r\nQ24. Which passages are used to connect \"the first and the last\" with both Jehovah and Jesus?\r\nA. Isaiah 9:6 and Matthew 3:3\r\nB. Hebrews 10:10-18 and Revelation 19:16\r\nC. Isaiah 44:6 and Revelation 1:17-18\r\nD. John 1:1 and Colossians 1:17\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson pairs Isaiah’s description of Jehovah with Revelation’s description of Jesus as “the first and the last.”\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS IV — 00:16–02:34\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Isaiah 44:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+44%3A6/>; Revelation 1:17–18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+1%3A17%E2%80%9318/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Isaiah 48:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+48%3A12/>; Revelation 22:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+22%3A12/>; Colossians 1:16–17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+1%3A16%E2%80%9317/>; Isaiah 44:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+44%3A24/>; Isaiah 48:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+48%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ25. A believer thinks, \"I don't want to share the gospel because I don't enjoy socially awkward conversations.\" What does the lesson say this believer needs to do?\r\nA. Ask the Holy Spirit to remove the possibility of an awkward conversation.\r\nB. Die to that preference because they have been crucified with Christ.\r\nC. Wait until they are comfortable enough to initiate spiritual conversations.\r\nD. Develop a less direct way of communicating the gospel.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says believers should die to the preference to avoid awkwardness and share the gospel.\r\nREF: HOW TO EVANGELIZE — 02:31–03:24\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Galatians 2:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+2%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ26. What question does the lesson raise when someone says, \"The universe is telling me something\"?\r\nA. Why would the universe want to communicate with human beings?\r\nB. If everything is one impersonal divine existence, who is speaking to whom?\r\nC. How can people know whether the universe is speaking in the correct language?\r\nD. How can the universe physically produce a voice?\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson questions how communication is possible if the universe is an impersonal divine force.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY II — 11:48–13:13\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+3/>\r\n\r\nQ27. Which statement correctly combines the three Adventist doctrines and the biblical responses presented in the lesson?\r\nA. Ellen White's writings concern only the Sabbath; soul sleep teaches conscious existence after death; conditional immortality teaches that the wicked remain immortal forever.\r\nB. Ellen White's writings are treated as prophetic guidance; soul sleep teaches unconsciousness until resurrection; conditional immortality teaches that the wicked are ultimately destroyed and the lesson challenges these claims using Scripture.\r\nC. Ellen White's writings replace Scripture; soul sleep teaches that the dead immediately enter heaven; conditional immortality teaches eternal conscious punishment.\r\nD. Ellen White's writings are regarded only as historical commentary; soul sleep teaches temporary judgment after death; conditional immortality teaches that everyone eventually receives eternal life.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson describes these Adventist teachings and challenges them using biblical passages.\r\nREF: MORMONS III — 02:59–09:02\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Colossians 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+2/>; Acts 15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+15/>; Galatians 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+1%3A8/>; John 4:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+4%3A1/>; Philippians 1:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+1%3A23/>; 2 Corinthians 5:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A8/>; Luke 23:43 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+23%3A43/>; Matthew 25:46 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+25%3A46/>; Mark 9:48 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+9%3A48/>; Matthew 13:42 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+13%3A42/>; Revelation 14:10–11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+14%3A10%E2%80%9311/>; 2 Thessalonians 1:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Thessalonians+1%3A9/>\r\n\r\nQ28. Why does the lesson describe a \"God-sized hole that nothing else but God can fill\"?\r\nA. People are searching for meaning because they need to find the right combination of spiritual beliefs to fill what is missing.\r\nB. People continue searching because they have not yet found the right spiritual practice to satisfy their deepest desires.\r\nC. People can keep adding things to their lives in an attempt to satisfy a longing that ultimately can only be satisfied by God.\r\nD. People search for different spiritual experiences because God has created everyone with different ways of finding satisfaction.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says people’s continued searching does not satisfy them; satisfaction is found in God.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY I — 07:05–07:38\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Jeremiah 2:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jeremiah+2%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ29. The Lesson on Mormons gives three biblical considerations for evaluating a prophetic claim. Which combination best represents the framework?\r\nA. Whether the prophet has a large following, whether his teachings are morally appealing, and whether his followers experience spiritual growth.\r\nB. Whether the prophet claims to have received revelation, whether his message is new, and whether his followers sincerely believe him.\r\nC. Whether the prophet performs miracles, whether people follow him, and whether he has a supernatural experience.\r\nD. Whether the prophet leads people to the true God, whether his fruit is consistent with the faith, and whether his message agrees with the gospel already given.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson evaluates prophetic claims by their direction, fruit, and agreement with the gospel already given.\r\nREF: MORMONS I — 09:29–11:04\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Timothy 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+3%3A16/>; Deuteronomy 13:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+13%3A1/>; Matthew 7:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+7%3A15/>; Galatians 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+1%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ30. What question does the lesson use to develop its argument about Islam calling Jesus “the Word of Allah”?\r\nA. Whether Jesus knew the entire Quran.\r\nB. Whether Allah spoke the Word through Muhammad or Jesus.\r\nC. Whether Allah's Word is eternal or created.\r\nD. Whether the Word of Allah is written in Arabic.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson develops its argument by asking whether Allah’s Word is eternal or created.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS II — 04:09–05:20\r\n\r\nQ48. How does Pastor Joshua apply Galatians 2:20 to the practice of evangelism?\r\nA. It means believers should physically suffer to share the gospel.\r\nB. It means believers must die to their personal preference of avoiding socially awkward situations.\r\nC. It means believers should wait until they feel completely ready before preaching.\r\nD. It means the gospel is only for those who have experienced near-death situations.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Pastor Joshua uses Galatians 2:20 (\"I have been crucified with Christ\") to explain that believers should not live for their own preferences. The tendency to avoid socially awkward situations must be put to death so that Christ can live and evangelize through them.\r\nREF: HOW-TO-EVANGELIZE-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-3a2279a1-b926.md (03:24)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Galatians 2:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+2%3A20/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+10/>\r\n\r\nQ49. According to the lesson, what is the significance of the Holy Spirit in evangelism as demonstrated by the apostles in Acts 4?\r\nA. The Holy Spirit provides a rigid script so believers know exactly what to say.\r\nB. The Holy Spirit guarantees that the listener will automatically be converted.\r\nC. The Holy Spirit grants believers the boldness to speak the word despite threats and fear of rejection.\r\nD. The Holy Spirit allows believers to avoid all physical persecution.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The transcript references Acts 4, noting that after the apostles prayed for boldness to speak God's word despite threats, they were filled with the Holy Spirit and \"spoke the word of God with boldness\".\r\nREF: HOW-TO-EVANGELIZE-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-3a2279a1-b926.md (07:26)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 4:29 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+4%3A29/>\r\n\r\nQ50. How does the lesson describe the initial encounter that began the religion of Islam?\r\nA. Muhammad had a peaceful vision of Jesus in the desert.\r\nB. Muhammad found ancient scrolls buried in a cave in Mecca.\r\nC. Muhammad had an unpleasant, violent encounter with a spirit being in the cave of Hira.\r\nD. Muhammad was visited by an angel who gave him golden plates to translate.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Islamic sources describe the encounter in the cave of Hira as a very unpleasant one, where a spirit being pressed on Muhammad violently and forcibly three times, leaving him fearful and traumatized.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-b745133b-8ff5.md (02:02-02:48)\r\n\r\nQ51. What distinction is made between the Christian and Islamic understanding of God's relationship with humanity?\r\nA. The God of the Bible is introduced as a Triune God who loves sinners, while Allah is one person who does not unconditionally love sinners.\r\nB. Both religions teach that God is a solitary being, but Christianity emphasizes love more.\r\nC. Islam teaches that everyone is automatically a child of Allah, while Christianity teaches only believers are children of God.\r\nD. The God of the Quran is described as a father, whereas the God of the Bible is described only as a king.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that the God of the Bible introduces Himself as triune in nature and loves sinners, doing something to bring them to Himself. In contrast, the God of the Quran is one person who categorically rejects sonship and does not love sinners; Muslims must \"work to try to earn his love\".\r\nREF: MUSLIMS-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-b745133b-8ff5.md (08:11-09:04)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A8/>; Acts 13:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+13%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ52. How does the lesson use the Islamic title \"the Word of Allah\" for Jesus to present a dilemma for Muslims?\r\nA. It shows that Jesus was the only prophet who could read the Quran.\r\nB. If Allah's word is eternal, then Jesus (the Word) must also be eternal, effectively confirming His deity.\r\nC. It proves that Jesus came to deliver the Quran before Muhammad.\r\nD. It indicates that Jesus was created before the angels to speak for Allah.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker poses a philosophical question based on the Quran calling Jesus \"the word of Allah\": Is Allah's word eternal or created? If eternal, and Jesus is the Word, then Jesus is eternal. Since nothing is eternal except Allah, Jesus must therefore be Allah/God.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a159b588-f6d8.md (04:55-05:20)\r\n\r\nQ53. How does the speaker address the Muslim objection that Jesus never explicitly said \"I am God\"?\r\nA. By admitting that Jesus hid His identity to avoid being killed prematurely.\r\nB. By pointing out that early Christians added those claims to the Bible later.\r\nC. By arguing that Jesus made implicit claims to deity through His actions and titles, such as claiming to give life and calling Himself the \"Alpha and Omega.\"\r\nD. By showing that the Quran contains a hidden verse where Jesus says He is God.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses logical inference, stating that Jesus didn't have to say it verbatim because of what He claimed to be able to do (giving life, forgiving sins) and the names He called Himself, such as the \"Alpha and the Omega,\" a title reserved only for God.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a159b588-f6d8.md (01:57-03:06)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 8:58 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+8%3A58/>; Exodus 3:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+3%3A14/>; John 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1/>; Isaiah 9:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+9%3A6/>; Revelation 22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+22/>\r\n\r\nQ54. According to Islamic sources like Sahih Muslim cited in the lesson, what is taught about a person's eternal destiny?\r\nA. A person's destiny is based strictly on their final act before death.\r\nB. Allah predetermines whether a person goes to paradise or hell while they are still unborn in their father's loins.\r\nC. Everyone who follows Muhammad is guaranteed an immediate place in paradise upon death.\r\nD. People can change their eternal destiny by completing the Hajj pilgrimage.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Quoting Sahih Muslim 2662, the speaker notes that Allah created people for paradise or for hell \"while they were yet in their Father's loins,\" indicating strict predeterminism regardless of a person's earthly deeds.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-6fbf7f97-a2ea.md (00:53-01:51)\r\n\r\nQ55. When evangelizing Muslims, what two things does the speaker warn Christians never to intentionally disrespect?\r\nA. The Kaaba and the city of Mecca.\r\nB. The five daily prayers and the month of Ramadan.\r\nC. The Prophet Muhammad and the Quran.\r\nD. The Hadith and the Islamic scholars.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker emphasizes that Muslims hold the Prophet Muhammad and the Quran completely sacred. Disrespecting or dishonoring them carelessly in conversation will cause you to \"lose them completely\".\r\nREF: MUSLIMS-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-6fbf7f97-a2ea.md (07:35)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Colossians 4:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+4%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ56. What does Jehovah's Witness theology teach about the Holy Spirit and Jesus?\r\nA. The Holy Spirit is God, but Jesus is merely a human prophet.\r\nB. Jesus is a lesser God who became the Archangel Michael, and the Holy Spirit is a force.\r\nC. The Holy Spirit and Jesus are two separate Gods who rule alongside Jehovah.\r\nD. Jesus is the Father incarnate, and the Holy Spirit is an angel.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson outlines that Jehovah's Witnesses believe Jesus is a lesser God who returned to the position of the Archangel Michael, and they believe the Holy Spirit is an impersonal force rather than a person.\r\nREF: JEHOVA-S-WITNESS-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-73da37cc-923f.md (04:32-05:48)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 1:1-3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1-3/>; Hebrews 1:5-8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A5-8/>; Acts 5:3-4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+5%3A3-4/>\r\n\r\nQ57. How does the lesson demonstrate that the \"fatherhood\" of God was a New Testament revelation?\r\nA. It was first revealed through the prophets in Babylon.\r\nB. The fatherhood of God was first discerned in the sonship of Jesus at His baptism.\r\nC. Abraham was the first person to explicitly call God \"Father.\"\r\nD. It was established when the Temple was rebuilt in Jerusalem.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that in the Old Testament, no one (not even Abraham or David) called God \"Father.\" The fatherhood of God was first discerned in the sonship of Jesus during His baptism in Matthew 3, when God spoke from heaven saying, \"this is my beloved Son\".\r\nREF: JEHOVA-S-WITNESS-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-73da37cc-923f.md (08:25-09:01)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Matthew 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+3/>\r\n\r\nQ58. What is the New World Translation's rendering of John 1:1, as highlighted in the lesson?\r\nA. \"...and the Word was God.\"\r\nB. \"...and the Word was a God.\"\r\nC. \"...and the Word was a force.\"\r\nD. \"...and the Word was Michael.\"\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Because Jehovah's Witnesses believe Jesus is not God, their specific translation (the New World Translation) alters John 1:1 to read \"in the beginning was the world and the world was with God and the world was a God\".\r\nREF: JEHOVA-S-WITNESS-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-c7fd3ce6-4d0d.md (03:19)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ59. In contrast to the Jehovah's Witness belief that man is only a body and soul that ceases to exist at death, what does the lesson teach based on 1 Thessalonians 5:23?\r\nA. Man is a tripartite being consisting of spirit, soul, and body.\r\nB. Man is purely a physical being until the final resurrection.\r\nC. Man is an immortal soul trapped in a temporary physical shell.\r\nD. Man is a spirit that eventually loses its consciousness and merges with God.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson counters the Jehovah's Witness view by citing 1 Thessalonians 5:23, which prays for God to sanctify the \"whole spirit, soul and body,\" proving that man is a tripartite being.\r\nREF: JEHOVA-S-WITNESS-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-c7fd3ce6-4d0d.md (10:02-11:09)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Thessalonians 5:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A23/>\r\n\r\nQ60. What is the Watchtower organization's definition of the \"good works\" required for salvation?\r\nA. Giving to the poor and taking care of orphans exclusively.\r\nB. Preaching door-to-door at least once a month.\r\nC. Serving the society with faithfulness and absolute obedience to the society.\r\nD. Reading the Bible and praying daily without fail.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker points out the danger of the JW belief that salvation requires a combination of faith and good works, specifically noting that good works are defined by the Watchtower organization as \"absolute obedience to the society\".\r\nREF: JEHOVA-S-WITNESS-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-caebe60b-013a.md (02:23-03:30)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 2:8-9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+2%3A8-9/>\r\n\r\nQ61. How did Jesus implicitly claim to be God in the Beatitudes (Matthew 5:11-12)?\r\nA. He explicitly demanded the crowds build Him a new temple.\r\nB. He compared those persecuted for His sake to the prophets who were persecuted for God.\r\nC. He told the disciples He would personally judge their enemies.\r\nD. He claimed He was older than the Roman Empire.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: By telling His followers they are blessed when persecuted \"because of me,\" and comparing it to how the prophets were persecuted (who were killed for God), Jesus implicitly places Himself in the position of God.\r\nREF: JEHOVA-S-WITNESS-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-caebe60b-013a.md (07:06-08:22)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Matthew 5:11–12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+5%3A11%E2%80%9312/>\r\n\r\nQ62. How does the speaker use John the Baptist to defend the deity of Jesus using the New World Translation?\r\nA. John the Baptist declared Jesus to be the Archangel Michael.\r\nB. John the Baptist applied Isaiah's prophecy about preparing the way for \"Jehovah\" directly to Jesus.\r\nC. John the Baptist baptized Jesus in the name of the Trinity.\r\nD. John the Baptist stated that Jesus was a lesser God sent to earth.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker shows that Isaiah 40:3 prophesied a voice calling to prepare the way of Jehovah. In Matthew 3, John the Baptist fulfills this by preparing the way for Jesus, demonstrating that John identified Jesus as Jehovah.\r\nREF: JEHOVA-S-WITNESS-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-d7319bce-23ff.md (11:34-12:12)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Isaiah 40:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+40%3A3/>; Matthew 3:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+3%3A3/>\r\n\r\nQ63. What is the significance of comparing Isaiah 9:6 and Isaiah 10:21 in the New World Translation?\r\nA. Both verses refer to Jehovah as \"Almighty God,\" proving Jesus is inferior.\r\nB. Isaiah 9:6 calls Jesus \"Mighty God\" and Isaiah 10:21 calls Jehovah \"Mighty God,\" showing consistency in their divine title.\r\nC. They both state that the Messiah will be a prophet, not a king.\r\nD. They prove that Jesus and the Archangel Michael are the same being.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Jehovah's Witnesses argue that \"Mighty God\" in Isaiah 9:6 (referring to Jesus) means He is a lesser god, not \"Almighty.\" However, the speaker points out that Isaiah 10:21 uses the exact same phrase, \"Mighty God,\" to refer to Jehovah in their own translation.\r\nREF: JEHOVA-S-WITNESS-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-d7319bce-23ff.md (04:05-05:19)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Isaiah 9:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+9%3A6/>; Isaiah 10:21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+10%3A21/>\r\n\r\nQ64. How does the speaker interpret the historical fact that every human civilization has built some form of temple or worshipped gods?\r\nA. It proves that all religions eventually merge into one.\r\nB. It shows that humans have an instinctive, natural craving to search for God and the divine.\r\nC. It indicates that humans are naturally atheistic but are forced into religion by cultural leaders.\r\nD. It proves that New Age spirituality is the oldest religion on earth.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses Plutarch's quote about cities without temples never existing to show that \"there is a craving to look For God... an aspect in people who would always need to find out about God\".\r\nREF: NEW-AGE-SPIRITUALITY-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-05165e51-eaca.md (02:19-03:17)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+1/>; Acts 17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17/>\r\n\r\nQ65. What does the lesson teach about the origins of New Age spiritual practices like divination and astrology?\r\nA. They were recently invented in the 20th century by modern philosophers.\r\nB. They are ancient practices often borrowed from Eastern religions and explicitly condemned in Deuteronomy 18.\r\nC. They were originally Christian practices that were later corrupted by society.\r\nD. They are scientifically proven psychological techniques.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson clarifies that there is \"nothing new about New Age\" and that practices like divination, astrology, and consulting spirits are ancient, borrowed from Eastern religions, and directly warned against in Deuteronomy 18.\r\nREF: NEW-AGE-SPIRITUALITY-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-05165e51-eaca.md (08:26-09:21)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Deuteronomy 18:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+18%3A10/>\r\n\r\nQ66. How does New Age spirituality explain the concept of emotional pain?\r\nA. It teaches that pain is a punishment from God for past sins.\r\nB. It describes emotional pain as negative energy or trauma that creates energetic blockages in the body that need to be released.\r\nC. It states that emotional pain is an illusion that does not actually exist.\r\nD. It believes pain is caused by failing to perform enough good works for the universe.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: New Age spirituality teaches that emotional pain is \"stored up in the body as negative energy or trauma, fear, anger, past experiences that have created energetic blockages that need to be released\".\r\nREF: NEW-AGE-SPIRITUALITY-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-09813dcc-1718.md (00:55-01:24)\r\n\r\nQ67. How does the Christian view of healing contrast with New Age coping mechanisms, according to the lesson?\r\nA. Christianity ignores emotional pain and focuses solely on physical healing.\r\nB. Christianity introduces a God who actively stepped into human pain, suffered, and truly empathizes with our weaknesses.\r\nC. Christianity requires believers to perform rituals to physically cut spiritual cords with past traumas.\r\nD. Christianity teaches that emotional trauma is a permanent state until heaven.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Unlike New Age coping mechanisms, the Christian faith introduces a God who \"identifies with our pain\" (Hebrews 4:15) and who actually \"stepped into the pain himself\" by suffering a gruesome death, allowing Him to truly understand human suffering.\r\nREF: NEW-AGE-SPIRITUALITY-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-09813dcc-1718.md (02:51-04:07)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 4:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+4%3A15/> | Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+1/>\r\n\r\nQ68. What is a major moral contradiction with the New Age belief of pantheism (the belief that all is divine)?\r\nA. It enforces strict, unachievable moral laws on its followers.\r\nB. If everything is divine, there is no distinction between good and evil, making real suffering merely an illusion.\r\nC. It demands that followers sacrifice their physical possessions to the universe.\r\nD. It teaches that humanity is inherently evil and unworthy of divine connection.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker points out that if all is God and all is divine, then \"nothing is evil.\" This contradicts the objective moral law and means that real evil and suffering must be viewed as an illusion, which is a moral contradiction.\r\nREF: NEW-AGE-SPIRITUALITY-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-1f31fc28-b074.md (10:17-11:15)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17/>\r\n\r\nQ69. Why is the New Age claim of \"human divinity\" (you are a god) so appealing to people?\r\nA. It forces them to be accountable to a higher moral standard.\r\nB. It requires them to constantly serve their community.\r\nC. It flatters human pride, removes guilt and shame by redefining sins as \"lessons,\" and eliminates accountability.\r\nD. It provides a structured, organized religious community with clear rules.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The claim is appealing because it \"flatters the human pride,\" \"eliminates accountability,\" and \"removes guilt and shame. You are not sinful, you are divine. What you've done in your life are just lessons\".\r\nREF: NEW-AGE-SPIRITUALITY-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-1f31fc28-b074.md (14:13-15:00)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 1:21-25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+1%3A21-25/>\r\n\r\nQ70. Why does the speaker emphasize presenting the historical facts of Jesus's resurrection to New Age adherents?\r\nA. Because New Age beliefs often rely purely on subjective feelings and experiences, so bringing in historical facts challenges their lack of objective evidence.\r\nB. Because New Age adherents already believe in the physical resurrection but attribute it to cosmic energy.\r\nC. Because it proves that reincarnation is scientifically impossible.\r\nD. Because the resurrection is the only Christian doctrine they are willing to accept without argument.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that New Age adherents base their beliefs on subjective experience (\"I felt was real to me\"). Presenting Christianity as a faith backed by \"hardcore evidence\" and \"factual sense\" (like the historically traceable resurrection) forces them to confront objective truth.\r\nREF: NEW-AGE-SPIRITUALITY-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-d04e2675-e11b.md (00:00-01:34)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+4/>\r\n\r\nQ71. According to the lesson, why is Christianity uniquely equipped to address the human condition compared to New Age spirituality?\r\nA. It offers a structured meditation program that guarantees physical wealth.\r\nB. It perfectly explains the origin of human evil and offers a proven savior who brings true transformation, calm, and peace.\r\nC. It allows believers to define their own moral truths while still feeling deeply spiritual.\r\nD. It promises that believers will eventually evolve into divine beings themselves.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Christianity is described as the only faith that truly explains why humans behave the way they do (the desperate heart of man) and provides the ultimate solution through Christ, bringing a \"deep sense of calm\" and transformation that no therapy or New Age practice can match.\r\nREF: NEW-AGE-SPIRITUALITY-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-d04e2675-e11b.md (04:38-07:22)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 3:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A23/>; John 14:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+14%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ72. What common theological characteristic is shared across various African Traditional Religions regarding the \"Supreme Being\"?\r\nA. The Supreme Being actively interacts with humans daily to provide guidance.\r\nB. The Supreme Being is viewed as transcendent but detached, having handed over creation and interaction to lesser deities.\r\nC. The Supreme Being is believed to be an evil force that must be constantly appeased.\r\nD. The Supreme Being is considered to be a physical object, like a mountain or river.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson explains that across African traditions, there is a common belief in a Supreme Being, but this being is viewed as \"transcendence and handed over everything to lesser deities,\" leaving the people more familiar with the lesser gods than the Supreme Being.\r\nREF: NEW-AGE-SPIRITUALITY-V-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-3e5f7740-2c47.md (12:39-13:46)\r\n\r\nQ73. According to Paul in 1 Corinthians 10:19-21, what is the spiritual reality behind sacrifices made to pagan idols?\r\nA. They are offered to imaginary concepts that have no actual spiritual power.\r\nB. They are inadvertently offered to the true God under a different cultural name.\r\nC. They are offered to real demons, and associating with them has genuine spiritual consequences.\r\nD. They are offered to deceased human ancestors who have become angels.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Paul acknowledges the spiritual reality behind idols, stating that \"what pagans sacrifice, they sacrifice to demons and not to God,\" indicating that there are real demonic spirits sponsoring these activities and receiving the worship.\r\nREF: NEW-AGE-SPIRITUALITY-V-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-3e5f7740-2c47.md (04:32-06:19)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Corinthians 10:19–21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+10%3A19%E2%80%9321/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Psalm 86:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+86%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ74. How does the speaker refute the claim that Christianity is merely a \"white man's religion\" imported through colonialism?\r\nA. By proving that Jesus was born in Egypt and lived his entire life in Africa.\r\nB. By highlighting that early North African theologians like Augustine and Tertullian shaped the faith centuries before European colonialism.\r\nC. By showing that all early Christian texts were originally written in Swahili.\r\nD. By arguing that European colonizers actually learned Christianity from African tribes.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker debunks this idea by pointing out that between the first and fourth centuries—long before the slave trade or European dominance—Africa produced strong theological leaders like St. Augustine of Hippo and Tertullian.\r\nREF: NEW-AGE-SPIRITUALITY-VI-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-4114fade-60bb.md (03:42-07:39)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 26:39 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+26%3A39/>\r\n\r\nQ75. How does the lesson view the relationship between adopting Christianity and maintaining African culture?\r\nA. Christians must completely abandon all African cultural practices and adopt European customs.\r\nB. Culture evolves; believers can maintain the core identity and positive aspects of their culture while discarding practices that conflict with the gospel.\r\nC. African culture and Christianity are entirely incompatible and cannot be mixed in any way.\r\nD. Believers should adopt traditional religious sacrifices into their church services to honor their ancestors.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that culture naturally evolves and that you don't lose your culture by embracing Christianity. Believers can maintain the core of their culture (like using great words of praise) while giving it a new, godly direction and discarding harmful practices.\r\nREF: NEW-AGE-SPIRITUALITY-VI-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-4114fade-60bb.md (09:08-11:47)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 9:19-23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+9%3A19-23/>\r\n\r\nQ76. According to Joseph Smith's claims, what happened during his \"first vision\"?\r\nA. An angel named Moroni gave him the Book of Mormon on golden plates.\r\nB. God the Father and Jesus Christ appeared to him and told him not to join any existing church because they were all corrupt.\r\nC. He was transported to heaven and saw the Apostle Peter.\r\nD. He received a vision of the apocalypse and the destruction of America.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The transcript states that in his first vision, Joseph Smith \"saw God the Father and Jesus Christ... and they told him not to join the existing church because they were all in error [corrupt]\".\r\nREF: MORMONS-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-c51534a0-82ed.md (03:37-04:23)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: James 1:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A5/>\r\n\r\nQ77. What issue does the lesson raise regarding the language used in the Book of Mormon?\r\nA. It was written in an extinct Egyptian dialect that no one could verify.\r\nB. It contains extensive 17th-century King James Bible phrasing and New Testament language, despite supposedly being written in 600 B.C.\r\nC. It frequently uses Greek words that Joseph Smith could not have known.\r\nD. It perfectly matches the linguistic style of ancient Hebrew poetry, proving its authenticity.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson highlights a chronological inconsistency: a book supposedly compiled in 600 B.C. contains \"extensive material corresponding to the King James Bible, including languages resembling New Testament formulation,\" which fails critical historical tests.\r\nREF: MORMONS-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-c51534a0-82ed.md (07:47-08:45)\r\n\r\nQ78. What does Mormon theology teach about the identity of Jesus?\r\nA. He is the eternal, uncreated God who took on human flesh.\r\nB. He is God's first spirit child, a distinct being from the Father, and the spirit brother of Lucifer.\r\nC. He is an ordinary human who achieved divinity strictly through good works.\r\nD. He is an angel who temporarily possessed a human body.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Mormon theology teaches that Jesus is an \"entirely distinct being from the Father\" and that He is \"God's first spirit child\" and our \"spirit brother, and that is also brother with Lucifer\".\r\nREF: MORMONS-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-75362ad6-5bb0.md (03:08-04:07)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1/>; John 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A3/>; Colossians 1:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+1%3A16/>; John 8:58 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+8%3A58/>; John 20:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+20%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ79. How does Mormon doctrine describe the ultimate destiny of human beings?\r\nA. Humans will either spend eternity in heaven praising God or be annihilated.\r\nB. Humans will be reincarnated until they reach spiritual perfection.\r\nC. Humans existed as premortal spirits and are expected to progress toward exaltation to become gods themselves.\r\nD. Humans will merge back into a single universal consciousness.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Mormon doctrine teaches that human beings existed as a premortal spirit before earthly life, and they are supposed to \"progress towards exhortation and become gods\".\r\nREF: MORMONS-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-75362ad6-5bb0.md (05:07-06:11)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A3/>; Colossians 1:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+1%3A16/>; John 8:58 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+8%3A58/>; John 20:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+20%3A28/>; Genesis 1:26 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+1%3A26/>; Psalm 8:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+8%3A4/>; Acts 17:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17%3A25/>; Acts 17:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ80. According to the lesson, how should Christians view the writings and visions of Ellen G. White (Seventh-day Adventism)?\r\nA. As an infallible addition to the Bible that must be obeyed.\r\nB. As historically interesting but completely irrelevant to modern faith.\r\nC. They should be tested against Scripture; because they lead to extra-biblical doctrines, they ought to be disregarded.\r\nD. They should be accepted because they accurately predicted the end of the world.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker references 1 John 4:1 and Galatians 1:8, stating that prophecies must be tested. Because Ellen White's visions lead to \"extra biblical doctrines,\" the lesson concludes that \"therefore we ought to disregard them\".\r\nREF: MORMONS-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8d8dedbb-7c27.md (04:12-06:00)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Galatians 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+1%3A8/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+15/>; Philippians 1:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+1%3A23/>; 2 Corinthians 5:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A8/> | Added from online research: 1 John 4:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+4%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ81. How does the lesson refute the Adventist doctrine of \"conditional immortality\" (annihilationism)?\r\nA. By showing that the Bible rarely mentions hell or punishment.\r\nB. By citing passages like Revelation 14:10-11 and Matthew 25:46, which describe ongoing punishment, unquenchable fire, and conscious eternal suffering.\r\nC. By arguing that a loving God would never punish anyone permanently.\r\nD. By proving that the concept of hell was invented by the early Catholic Church.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson refutes annihilationism by quoting multiple scriptures (Matthew 25:46, Mark 9:48, Revelation 14:10-11) that speak of \"eternal punishment,\" \"unquenchable fire,\" and being \"tormented day and night, forever and ever,\" pointing to ongoing conscious suffering.\r\nREF: MORMONS-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8d8dedbb-7c27.md (07:48-10:02)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Matthew 25:46 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+25%3A46/>; Mark 9:48 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+9%3A48/>; Revelation 14:10–11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+14%3A10%E2%80%9311/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 23:43 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+23%3A43/>; Matthew 13:42 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+13%3A42/>; 2 Thessalonians 1:9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Thessalonians+1%3A9/>; Ephesians 4:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+4%3A15/>; Matthew 22:29 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+22%3A29/>\r\n\r\nQ82. In the story that opens the lesson, what does magician Penn Gillette say about believers who never share their faith?\r\nA. He admires them because they refuse to pressure other people about what they believe.\r\nB. He cannot trust someone who believes in heaven and hell yet stays silent to avoid social awkwardness.\r\nC. He accepts their silence as long as they live morally upright lives in public.\r\nD. He says they should speak only after an unbeliever first asks about their faith.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Gillette said he does not respect people who don't proselytize and cannot trust someone who thinks others may miss eternal life but keeps quiet because it is awkward.\r\nREF: HOW TO EVANGELIZE — [01:38]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 10:14-15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+10%3A14-15/>\r\n\r\nQ83. How does the lesson define \"proselytize\"?\r\nA. Sharing your faith hoping others will come to believe what you believe.\r\nB. Teaching Christian doctrine only to people who already attend church.\r\nC. Defending Christian beliefs whenever someone attacks them in public.\r\nD. Inviting people to a church service without discussing what you believe.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker defines proselytizing as sharing your faith in the hope of changing someone's mind so they can believe what you believe.\r\nREF: HOW TO EVANGELIZE — [00:59]\r\n\r\nQ84. What does the lesson mean by \"live like a dead man\" using Galatians 2:20?\r\nA. You withdraw from social life so that prayer can take the place of conversations.\r\nB. You no longer live for your own preferences, because Christ lives through you.\r\nC. You wait passively for Christ to act without making any effort to speak.\r\nD. You treat yourself as spiritually dead until you have shared the gospel with someone.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Because believers have been crucified with Christ, they should die to the preference of avoiding socially awkward situations and share the gospel as Christ lives through them.\r\nREF: HOW TO EVANGELIZE — [02:31; 03:24]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Galatians 2:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+2%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ85. What does the lesson draw from the story of the good Samaritan about evangelism?\r\nA. Believers can use their career, platform and giftings to share the gospel.\r\nB. Evangelism should be limited to people who are in obvious physical need.\r\nC. Believers should first gain more resources before they begin to witness.\r\nD. Kind deeds can replace speaking about Jesus because actions are enough.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The Samaritan helped using the resources he had, his donkey and money; likewise believers should use their career, platform and giftings.\r\nREF: HOW TO EVANGELIZE — [03:24]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 10:25–37 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+10%3A25%E2%80%9337/>\r\n\r\nQ86. What does Philip's question to the Ethiopian eunuch illustrate about preaching the gospel intentionally?\r\nA. It shows that evangelism should begin with a debate over how Isaiah should be interpreted.\r\nB. It shows that believers should wait until a person asks for help before speaking.\r\nC. It shows that a ready conversation starter opens the way to explaining the gospel.\r\nD. It shows that a memorized speech is more effective than asking any question.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Philip asked, \"Do you understand what you're reading?\" and then preached the gospel from that point; the speaker urges having conversation starters ready.\r\nREF: HOW TO EVANGELIZE — [04:20]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 8:30-35 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+8%3A30-35/>\r\n\r\nQ87. Why does the lesson suggest following up with \"What do you mean by that?\" during a gospel conversation?\r\nA. So the person is pushed to defend their belief in front of others.\r\nB. So you gain time to recall a Bible verse before replying.\r\nC. So you don't assume you understand when you may be mistaken.\r\nD. So you can show them at once that their view is inconsistent.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says to ask this so you don't run off thinking you understand what they are saying when you may have misunderstood them.\r\nREF: HOW TO EVANGELIZE — [05:06]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Proverbs 18:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Proverbs+18%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ88. What point does the lesson make from Acts 1:8 about evangelism?\r\nA. The Holy Spirit gives believers power to witness beyond their own strength.\r\nB. Witnessing is reserved for believers who receive a special gift before they speak.\r\nC. Believers should wait until all fear has gone before they begin to witness.\r\nD. Evangelism works once a believer has completed enough training in doctrine.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Citing Acts 1:8, the speaker says power comes when the Holy Spirit comes upon you, enabling you to witness, so believers should partner with the Spirit.\r\nREF: HOW TO EVANGELIZE — [05:52]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+1%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ89. According to the lesson, what happened after the apostles prayed for boldness in Acts 4?\r\nA. The authorities withdrew their threats and the arrests of the apostles stopped.\r\nB. The apostles stopped preaching until the threats against them had passed.\r\nC. The apostles received a command to speak only to people who invited them.\r\nD. The place shook, they were Spirit-filled, and spoke the word with boldness.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: After praying, the place where they were gathered was shaken, they were filled with the Holy Spirit and spoke God's word with boldness.\r\nREF: HOW TO EVANGELIZE — [07:26]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 4:29–31 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+4%3A29%E2%80%9331/>\r\n\r\nQ90. What test does the lesson give for having mastered the gospel message well enough to evangelize?\r\nA. Being able to quote the key gospel verses from memory without any notes.\r\nB. Having completed formal theological training before speaking to anyone.\r\nC. Being able to explain salvation simply enough for a small child to follow.\r\nD. Being able to win every argument raised by a skeptical listener.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that if you cannot explain something difficult to a 3-, 4- or 5-year-old you have not mastered it, so learn to explain salvation, repentance and new life.\r\nREF: HOW TO EVANGELIZE — [07:26]\r\n\r\nQ91. Which of the following does the lesson present as a tool for evangelizing rather than as one of its key steps?\r\nA. Preaching the gospel intentionally\r\nB. Relying on the power of the Holy Spirit\r\nC. Using what you have, such as your career\r\nD. Praying for boldness\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: After listing the key steps (including preaching intentionally and relying on the Holy Spirit), the speaker introduces \"a few tools,\" beginning with praying for boldness.\r\nREF: HOW TO EVANGELIZE — [03:24; 05:52]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 4:29-31 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+4%3A29-31/>\r\n\r\nQ92. Why does the lesson advise having both a short and a longer version of the gospel ready?\r\nA. The short version is for fellow believers and the longer version is for strangers.\r\nB. A short version suits limited time; a longer one suits time with a friend.\r\nC. The short version is for social media and the longer version is for church services.\r\nD. The short version is for the first meeting and the longer one should replace it later.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says to be ready with a short form when you have a limited time frame and a longer version when you have more time with someone.\r\nREF: HOW TO EVANGELIZE — [09:09]\r\n\r\nQ93. What does the story of the man who warns drivers on a foggy, curved road illustrate?\r\nA. Believers should avoid people who insult them when they share the gospel.\r\nB. The gospel should be shared only after a person has asked for it.\r\nC. Danger can be avoided when a believer prepares well before the journey.\r\nD. Fear of insults should not stop us from warning people of real danger.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Drivers insulted the man until they saw the accident he saved them from; the speaker says refusing to share the gospel because of what people will say is like not jumping in front of the cars.\r\nREF: HOW TO EVANGELIZE — [11:21]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Corinthians 5:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ94. Why does the speaker begin the Muslim evangelism series by asking how Islam came to be?\r\nA. Knowing Islam's history lets you prove quickly that its founder was not a prophet.\r\nB. Muslims will only listen to a Christian who can recite the Quran from memory.\r\nC. A short history of Islam is required before any gospel conversation is permitted.\r\nD. Understanding their worldview is the only way to break through to their hearts.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says you must understand the worldview of the people you talk to, because that is the only way to present something that breaks through their hearts.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS I — [00:43]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 17:22-23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17%3A22-23/>\r\n\r\nQ95. According to the lesson, what did Muhammad's relative Waraqa say about the being he encountered in the cave?\r\nA. It was an evil spirit that Muhammad should refuse to listen to.\r\nB. It must have been the same spirit that was sent to Moses from Allah.\r\nC. It was the angel who had earlier announced the birth of Jesus.\r\nD. It was the Holy Spirit who had inspired the Christian Gospels.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Waraqa, said to be a Christian or familiar with Christian tradition, told Muhammad the being must be the same spirit sent to Moses from Allah.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS I — [02:48]\r\n\r\nQ96. Which pairing of a Muslim practice and its description matches the lesson?\r\nA. Zakat: giving a certain amount of money\r\nB. Sawm: making a pilgrimage to Mecca\r\nC. Hajj: fasting during a particular period of the year\r\nD. Salat: reciting that there is no God but Allah\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Pastor Tomiwa's colleague lists the shahada, salat (prayer facing Mecca five times a day), fasting, zakat (giving money) and the pilgrimage to Mecca.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS I — [04:01]\r\n\r\nQ97. What does the teacher analogy (one who flogs and one who does not) show about the claim that Christians and Muslims worship the same God?\r\nA. Contradictory self-descriptions cannot describe the same person, teacher or God.\r\nB. Different names for the same teacher show that language alone explains the difference.\r\nC. A teacher who punishes and one who does not are equally reliable guides for students.\r\nD. Students are the ones who decide which description of a teacher is correct.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Since we cannot know God apart from how He introduces Himself, a God who describes Himself differently in each book cannot be dismissed as the same teacher under another name.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS I — [06:24; 07:10]\r\n\r\nQ98. How does the lesson describe the way the God of the Bible introduces Himself, in contrast to the Quran?\r\nA. As one person with no partners, which is the same self-description found in the Quran.\r\nB. As three separate gods who share one purpose, unlike the single God of the Quran.\r\nC. As one God who has no Son, which agrees with the Quran on the nature of God.\r\nD. As triune: three co-equal, co-eternal persons in one being, unlike the Quran's one person.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says the Bible's God is triune, three persons, one being, while the Quran's God introduces Himself as one person with no partners.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS I — [07:10]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 28:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+28%3A19/>; 2 Corinthians 13:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+13%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ99. What do Hebrews 1:8 and Acts 13:2 show, according to the speaker?\r\nA. Jesus and the Holy Spirit are created servants who act on the Father's behalf.\r\nB. The Father calls the Son God, and the Holy Spirit speaks as God.\r\nC. The Father speaks only about the Son and never directly to Him in Scripture.\r\nD. The Holy Spirit is a power from God rather than someone who can speak.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: In Hebrews 1:8 the Father says to the Son, \"Your throne, O God,\" and in Acts 13:2 the Holy Spirit speaks, supporting that all three function as God.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS I — [08:11]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A8/>; Acts 13:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+13%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ100. What objection to divine sonship does the lesson attribute to the Quran?\r\nA. God cannot have a son because the Son would then be older than the Father.\r\nB. God has many sons, so calling Jesus the Son would make Him unremarkable.\r\nC. Sonship is possible but only for prophets who have kept the five practices.\r\nD. God cannot have a son without a wife, since sonship comes only by procreation.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the Quran rejects sonship and cannot see how it could happen without normal procreation: \"how can God have a son when he has no wife?\"\r\nREF: MUSLIMS I — [08:11]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 1:12-14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A12-14/>\r\n\r\nQ101. How does the lesson contrast the God of the Bible and the God of the Quran regarding sinners?\r\nA. Both love sinners unconditionally, but only Christianity requires a response.\r\nB. The God of the Bible loves only the righteous, while Allah loves all sinners equally.\r\nC. The Bible's God loved sinners and acted; Muslims must earn Allah's love.\r\nD. Neither God is said to love sinners; both simply demand obedience.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the God of the Bible loved sinners and did something to bring them to Himself, while even Muslims cannot say God loves them and must work to earn His love.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS I — [08:11]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 5:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ102. What problem does the speaker raise from the Hebrew-based names of Quranic figures such as John?\r\nA. The names raise the question of who Yahweh is if there is no God but Allah.\r\nB. The names show that the Quran copied the Bible word for word without changes.\r\nC. The names prove that Allah and Yahweh are simply two languages' words for God.\r\nD. The names show Muslims were forbidden to use the names of Hebrew prophets.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: God told Moses His name is \"I am that I am\" (Yahweh); the Quran uses names built on Yahweh, which raises the question of who Yahweh is if Allah alone is God.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS I — [09:07; 09:55]\r\n\r\nQ103. What does the lesson say Islam teaches about the crucifixion of Jesus?\r\nA. That he was crucified but did not rise, which is the same as the Gospel account.\r\nB. That it was only made to appear he was crucified, though history records his death.\r\nC. That he was crucified in secret so that Jewish and Roman records never mention it.\r\nD. That he died on the cross as a ransom, but only for the people of Israel.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Islam alone rejects the well-recorded public death of Jesus, claiming it was only made to appear that he was crucified.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS I — [13:20]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 15:3-4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15%3A3-4/>\r\n\r\nQ104. Which statement of Jesus does the speaker connect to God's introduction of Himself to Moses in Exodus 3:14?\r\nA. \"Before Abraham was, I am\" (John 8:58)\r\nB. \"Your throne, O God\" (Hebrews 1:8)\r\nC. \"Separate unto me Paul and Barnabas\" (Acts 13:2)\r\nD. \"I am the way, the truth and the life\" (John 14:6)\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says in John 8:58 Jesus used the same \"I am\" introduction God gave Moses in Exodus 3:14.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS II — [01:57]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Exodus 3:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+3%3A14/>; John 8:58 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+8%3A58/>\r\n\r\nQ105. Why does the speaker cite Revelation 22:13 (\"the first and the last\") when talking with Muslims?\r\nA. Islam has no title for Allah, so the Bible must supply one.\r\nB. The title shows Jesus was the first prophet and Muhammad the last.\r\nC. The title is reserved for Allah in Islam, yet Jesus claims it.\r\nD. The title appears in the Quran only for angels and prophets.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that in Islam the title \"the first and the last\" is reserved only for Allah, so Jesus' use of it is a claim to deity.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS II — [03:06]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Revelation 22:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+22%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ106. In the \"Word of Allah\" argument, what difficulty arises if Allah's Word is said to be created?\r\nA. It would mean Allah once lacked his Word and had to add something to himself.\r\nB. It would mean the Quran is older than Jesus, so the Gospel cannot be Allah's Word.\r\nC. It would mean the Word was written in Arabic before any human had learned to read.\r\nD. It would mean Allah first spoke through Muhammad and only afterward through Jesus.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker argues a created Word implies a time when Allah's Word wasn't, so he would have needed to add something to himself, which is problematic.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS II — [04:09]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 1:1-3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1-3/>\r\n\r\nQ107. Which sequence best represents the \"Word of Allah\" argument as presented?\r\nA. Jesus is the Word of Allah; Allah's Word is created; therefore Jesus is created; so Jesus is a prophet.\r\nB. Allah's Word is eternal; the Quran is Allah's Word; therefore the Quran is eternal; so Muhammad is God.\r\nC. Jesus is Allah's Word; the Word is eternal; so Jesus is eternal; only Allah is eternal; so Jesus is God.\r\nD. Jesus is a prophet; prophets speak Allah's Word; therefore Jesus is eternal; so Jesus is Allah's servant.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reasons that if Jesus is Allah's Word and Allah's Word is eternal, Jesus is eternal, and nothing eternal is not Allah, so by definition Jesus is God.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS II — [04:55]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 1:1-3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1-3/>\r\n\r\nQ108. What do the speakers infer from the Islamic belief that the Quran will testify for believers on the Day of Judgment?\r\nA. It suggests personhood and a distinct eternal entity, resembling the Trinity.\r\nB. It shows the Quran is a human document that was written after the Day of Judgment was announced.\r\nC. It proves only Muslims will be judged, because the Quran has no testimony about Christians.\r\nD. It shows the Quran is the same person as Allah with no distinction between them.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speakers say a Quran that knows each person and testifies to Allah displays personhood, distinct from Allah, which they compare to Trinitarian doctrine.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS II — [06:27]\r\n\r\nQ109. What philosophical dilemma does the speaker raise about the claim that the Bible has been corrupted?\r\nA. Allah, the faithful guardian, either could not or would not preserve his earlier scriptures.\r\nB. Allah preserved the earlier scriptures but Christians chose to ignore their message.\r\nC. Allah allowed corruption of earlier scriptures but promised that no one could corrupt the Gospel.\r\nD. Allah did not know the earlier scriptures existed when he sent the Quran.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: One of Allah's names is the guardian; the speaker says that if the scripture was corrupted either Allah wanted to preserve it but couldn't, or could but wouldn't.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS II — [07:38]\r\n\r\nQ110. What does the speaker conclude from Allah telling Muhammad to \"go and ask the people who have been reading the scriptures before you\"?\r\nA. Muhammad was told to ignore earlier scriptures because they had been corrupted.\r\nB. Muhammad was told to consult only Jewish scholars because the New Testament was not yet written.\r\nC. Muhammad was told to go to Christians holding the Old and New Testaments, giving them authority.\r\nD. Muhammad was told to rely entirely on the Quran, with no outside confirmation.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the New Testament was canonized before Muhammad's birth, so the people reading scripture were Christians; sending him to them places authority with them.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS II — [09:47]\r\n\r\nQ111. What is the \"Islamic dilemma\" as the speaker describes it?\r\nA. Muslims must obey the Quran yet cannot read it in its original language.\r\nB. Muslims believe Jesus will return but are not allowed to discuss his teaching.\r\nC. Muslims are told earlier scriptures hold authority, yet they show the Quran false.\r\nD. Muslims claim the Bible is true but cannot say which version is the genuine one.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says apologists call it the Islamic dilemma: Muslims are told to trust earlier scriptures, but going back to them shows that the Quran is false.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS II — [13:02]\r\n\r\nQ112. If someone claims the Bible was corrupted after Muhammad's time, what does the speaker say can be done?\r\nA. Ask Muslims to prove which verses were added after the time of Muhammad.\r\nB. Show that the Quran itself quotes the Bible to confirm each corrupted passage.\r\nC. Appeal to Allah's promise to preserve every scripture in its original form.\r\nD. Compare manuscripts from Muhammad's time frame with today's Bible and find no difference.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says manuscripts exist within Muhammad's time frame, so you can compare them to the current Bible and see there is no difference.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS II — [11:50]\r\n\r\nQ113. Which central Christian belief does the speaker say Islam attacks when it denies that Jesus died on the cross?\r\nA. That Jesus was born of a virgin in Bethlehem\r\nB. That Jesus was a Jewish teacher who lived before Muhammad\r\nC. That Jesus' life was laid down as a ransom for forgiveness\r\nD. That Jesus was called the Messiah by his followers\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker calls the cross the bedrock of Christian faith, where Jesus' life was laid down as ransom; Muslims do not believe in the atonement.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS II — [14:00]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Mark 10:45 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+10%3A45/>; 1 Corinthians 15:3-4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15%3A3-4/>\r\n\r\nQ114. Why does the speaker say no Muslim can know for certain, while on earth, that they will enter paradise?\r\nA. The five practices must be completed perfectly before any assurance is given.\r\nB. Only the prophets are told in advance whether they will enter paradise.\r\nC. Hadith teachings say Allah predetermined each person's destination, paradise or hell, before birth.\r\nD. Allah reveals each person's destination only on the Day of Judgment.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker argues that Islamic theology holds that nobody can know for a fact whether they will go to paradise or hell because Allah has predetermined it.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS III — [00:14]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 John 5:11-13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+5%3A11-13/>\r\n\r\nQ115. What does Sahih Muslim 2662, as quoted in the lesson, say about those created for paradise and hell?\r\nA. God decides each person's destination only after weighing their lifelong deeds.\r\nB. God made some for paradise and some for hell before they were born.\r\nC. Children who die young are sent to hell unless a parent has prayed for them.\r\nD. Angels decide a person's destination on the day that person dies.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The Hadith about Aisha and a child's funeral says God created for paradise and hell those who were fit for them while yet in their fathers' loins.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS III — [00:53]\r\n\r\nQ116. According to Sahih Muslim 2643 as quoted, which things does the angel write down after conception?\r\nA. His livelihood, debts, deeds, and fortune or misfortune.\r\nB. His name, parentage, prayers, and pilgrimage.\r\nC. His beliefs, sins, repentance, and rewards.\r\nD. His faith, fasting, almsgiving, and obedience.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The quoted Hadith says Allah sends an angel with instructions concerning four things: livelihood, debts, deeds, and fortune and misfortune.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS III — [01:51]\r\n\r\nQ117. Why does the speaker ask Muslims, \"What are the deeds of paradise?\"\r\nA. To show that the deeds of paradise are the same as the deeds of Christians.\r\nB. To show that the deeds of paradise are too difficult to perform consistently.\r\nC. To show that Muslims do not agree on which deeds are required for paradise.\r\nD. To show a person doing paradise's deeds could still end in hell.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker argues a Muslim doing paradise's deeds could still be written for hell and a Christian could be written for paradise, so nobody can be sure.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS III — [02:45]\r\n\r\nQ118. What contrast does the speaker draw with the image of \"casting a die\"?\r\nA. Both Christianity and Islam leave salvation to chance, but Christianity explains it better.\r\nB. Islam leaves paradise to chance, but faith in the risen Jesus gives certainty.\r\nC. Christians must cast lots to decide moral matters, whereas Muslims follow fixed rules.\r\nD. Christian assurance depends on keeping the law, whereas Islam depends on faith alone.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Islam is like casting a die to know whether you go to paradise, while Christianity says that placing faith in the crucified and risen Jesus gives assurance.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS III — [04:20]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 John 5:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+5%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ119. Which two things does Pastor Tomiwa say Muslims hold completely sacred in conversation?\r\nA. The Kaaba and the month of fasting\r\nB. The Prophet Muhammad and the Quran\r\nC. The five daily prayers and the pilgrimage\r\nD. The names of Allah and the Hadith\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Muhammad, regarded as the Prophet and best man, and the Quran must not be disrespected in conversation or you lose the listener.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS III — [06:34; 07:35]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Peter 3:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+3%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ120. Where does Pastor Tomiwa say he usually begins a conversation with a Muslim?\r\nA. With the concept of judgment: what will happen when I leave this earth?\r\nB. With the moral character of Muhammad before anything else.\r\nC. With the textual history of the Quran and its manuscripts.\r\nD. With the question of whether Allah and the God of the Bible are the same.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He starts with judgment, a question every Muslim has pondered, then shares that God took on flesh and paid for sin.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS III — [07:35]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Corinthians 5:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A10/>; John 1:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ121. In the question about whether a prophet should know right and wrong, what is Pastor Tomiwa trying to achieve?\r\nA. To prove Muhammad could not have known right from wrong because he was unlettered.\r\nB. To help them see a clash between their inner morality and Muhammad's record.\r\nC. To pressure the Muslim to say publicly that they no longer follow Islam.\r\nD. To show that moral knowledge comes from the Quran alone and not from conscience.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He asks them whether a prophet bringing the final revelation should know what is right, then raises Muhammad's marriage to Aisha, aiming to help them see a contradiction.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS III — [09:50]\r\n\r\nQ122. Why does Pastor Tomiwa's colleague urge compassion when evangelizing Muslims?\r\nA. Most Muslims already know Christianity is true, so compassion removes their fear.\r\nB. Compassion lets a Christian avoid discussing any difference between the two faiths.\r\nC. Muslims are more open to debate when they sense that the Christian is gentle.\r\nD. Most were born into Islam, so imagine being told your upbringing is false.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says a large majority of Muslims are born into Islam, so you should come with compassion and not as one trying to win a debate.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS III — [10:57]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Timothy 2:24-25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+2%3A24-25/>\r\n\r\nQ123. How does the lesson distinguish between Muslims and Islam?\r\nA. Muslims are the enemy and Islam is merely their culture.\r\nB. Muslims are not the enemy; Islam is the spiritual problem.\r\nC. Both Muslims and Islam are enemies to be defeated through debate.\r\nD. Neither is a spiritual problem because both worship the same God.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Citing Ephesians 6, the speaker says Muslims are not the enemies; Islam is the problem, and it has been instilled in many from birth.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS III — [14:31]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 6:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+6%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ124. Why does the lesson say a Christian should pray before evangelizing Muslims?\r\nA. Because prayer replaces the need to learn what Muslims believe.\r\nB. Because Muslims will only listen if they see the Christian praying aloud.\r\nC. Because prayer guarantees that Muslims will answer every question asked.\r\nD. Because the god of this world has blinded their eyes, so head knowledge is not enough.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites Paul that the god of this world has blinded their eyes and says you can't go with head knowledge alone but must pray before you evangelize.\r\nREF: MUSLIMS III — [13:42]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Corinthians 4:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+4%3A4/>; Ephesians 6:18-20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+6%3A18-20/>\r\n\r\nQ125. Who does the lesson say founded the Jehovah's Witness organization, and what did he decide about the Bible?\r\nA. Charles Taze Russell, who decided the Bible as we have it is corrupt and wrote a new translation.\r\nB. William Miller, who decided the Bible was correct but wrongly dated Christ's return.\r\nC. Joseph Smith, who decided the Bible was incomplete and added another testament.\r\nD. Ellen G. White, who decided the Bible was correct but needed prophetic commentary.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Charles Taze Russell started the organization around 1869 in Pennsylvania after deciding the Bible as we have it is corrupt, then produced a new translation.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS I — [00:16]\r\n\r\nQ126. How does the lesson answer the Jehovah's Witness belief that God is a spirit being with a body, though not a human one?\r\nA. The Bible says God has a glorified body that humans will see after the resurrection.\r\nB. The Bible says God is a spirit who takes on a body only when speaking to prophets.\r\nC. The Bible says God is a force that fills creation rather than a person.\r\nD. The Bible says God is spirit (John 4:24), and no man has seen or can see him (1 Tim 6:16).\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says God is a spirit, not a spirit with a body, citing John 4:24 and 1 Timothy 6:15-16, \"whom no man has seen or can see.\"\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS I — [02:36; 03:46]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 4:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+4%3A24/>; 1 Timothy 6:15–16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+6%3A15%E2%80%9316/>\r\n\r\nQ127. How does the lesson respond to the Jehovah's Witness accusation that Trinitarians worship three gods?\r\nA. It agrees that Christians worship three gods but says each god deserves worship.\r\nB. It says the Old Testament never mentions that God is one, only that He is great.\r\nC. It says Christians worship only the Father and honor the Son and Spirit as servants.\r\nD. It says God is one (Deut 6:4; Isa 45:5) and Christians worship one God who is also three.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker states the first of four points about the Trinity: God is one, citing Deuteronomy 6:4 and Isaiah 45:5, and that Christians worship one God, not three.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS I — [06:52]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Deuteronomy 6:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+6%3A4/>; Isaiah 45:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+45%3A5/>\r\n\r\nQ128. What does the lesson say Matthew 3:16-17 shows about God?\r\nA. God is one person who appears in three different roles at different times.\r\nB. God is three: Father speaks, Son is baptized, and the Spirit descends.\r\nC. God is a spirit who needs a body in order to speak to people.\r\nD. God is the Father only, and the dove and the Son are His servants.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses the baptism scene, where the voice from heaven calls Jesus beloved Son as the Spirit descends, to show God as Father, Son and Holy Spirit.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS I — [08:25]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 3:16–17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+3%3A16%E2%80%9317/>\r\n\r\nQ129. What does the lesson observe about the Old Testament and calling God \"Father\"?\r\nA. Abraham and David often called God Father, so Jesus' use of the name was familiar.\r\nB. God was called Father throughout the Old Testament, but never by the Gentiles.\r\nC. Even Abraham and David never called God Father; the Jews objected when Jesus did.\r\nD. Only the prophets could call God Father, and Jesus was not recognized as one.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that no one in the Old Testament, not even Abraham or David, is recorded calling God Father; this was a Jewish issue with Jesus.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS I — [07:38]\r\n\r\nQ130. How does the lesson explain how Jesus can be both God and the Son of God?\r\nA. He became God's Son at his baptism, when God adopted him for ministry.\r\nB. He is the Son of God as the first being God created before the world began.\r\nC. He was God's Son only in his divine nature and God only in his human nature.\r\nD. His sonship begins in the incarnation, through the virgin birth.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that in his birth, by the power of the Most High, Jesus is called the Son of God, answering how he is God and Son at once.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS I — [13:43]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 1:35 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+1%3A35/>\r\n\r\nQ131. What does the lesson say Philippians 2:5-8 teaches about Jesus?\r\nA. Though only a created being, he was exalted to equality with God after his death.\r\nB. Though truly man, he gave up his deity permanently when he took human form.\r\nC. Though in the form of God, he came as a spirit with a body that only some could see.\r\nD. Though in the form of God, he took the form of a servant to save us as a man.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reads Philippians 2 to say that to save us God became a man, and that man is Jesus, God in human form.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS I — [11:38]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Philippians 2:5–8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+2%3A5%E2%80%938/>\r\n\r\nQ132. Why does the lesson say Jesus could not be born in the usual way through a man?\r\nA. Because Jewish law forbade a prophet from being born to a married couple.\r\nB. Because sin is inherited through men, a sinless child needed God's power in Mary's womb.\r\nC. Because the Father wanted to avoid any link between Jesus and Israel's kings.\r\nD. Because angels could not be born through human fathers in the ordinary way.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says we inherited Adam's sin, so to solve it Jesus could not be born through a man; God's power formed him in the woman, making him the new Adam.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS I — [13:23]\r\n\r\nQ133. What reason does the lesson give for saying the Holy Spirit is not a force?\r\nA. Jesus said the Spirit would come only when the Father sends angels.\r\nB. Jesus said the Spirit is the same person as Jesus in another form.\r\nC. Jesus used the pronoun \"he\" for the Spirit, showing he is a person.\r\nD. Jesus said the Spirit is power given to believers only for healing.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Reading John 14, the speaker says Jesus used the personal pronoun he for the Spirit, meaning he is a person and not a force.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS II — [00:16]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 14:16–17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+14%3A16%E2%80%9317/>; John 14:26 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+14%3A26/>\r\n\r\nQ134. What does the lesson say is the issue with the New World Translation of Titus 2:13?\r\nA. It removes Jesus' name from the verse so that only the Father is called Savior.\r\nB. It splits God and Savior into two persons instead of Jesus Christ as both.\r\nC. It changes \"Savior\" to \"Redeemer\" so the verse no longer mentions salvation.\r\nD. It moves the verse to a different chapter so its meaning is altered.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the original means God and Savior are the same person, Jesus Christ, while the New World Translation splits them.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS II — [04:25]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Titus 2:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Titus+2%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ135. How does the New World Translation render Hebrews 1:8 according to the lesson?\r\nA. \"God is your throne\" instead of \"Your throne, O God\"\r\nB. \"Your throne, O Lord, is forever\" instead of \"Your throne, O God\"\r\nC. \"The Son sits on God's throne\" instead of \"Your throne, O God\"\r\nD. \"Your angels' throne is forever\" instead of \"Your throne, O God\"\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker contrasts \"Your throne, O God\" with the New World Translation's \"God is your throne\" for the Son.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS II — [05:25]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ136. Why does the lesson say it is difficult to preach to a Jehovah's Witness from a normal Bible?\r\nA. They accept every translation but reject any verse that mentions the Trinity.\r\nB. They think the Bible is wrong and only their translation is correct.\r\nC. They believe only their elders may read the Bible aloud to others.\r\nD. They believe the Bible was lost and is now known only through oral tradition.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says you can't open a normal Bible to them because they believe it is wrong and that only their translation is correct.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS II — [03:19]\r\n\r\nQ137. What argument does the lesson give that the Bible has been properly preserved?\r\nA. Scripture was written by many authors, so errors in copying would have been obvious.\r\nB. Scripture is God-inspired (2 Tim 3:16), God can preserve it, and believers died for it.\r\nC. Scripture has been preserved by church councils, who decided which texts were correct.\r\nD. Scripture is preserved only in the original languages, not in translations.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says all Scripture is inspired by God, who is able to preserve it, and cites people such as Tyndale who died so the Bible would be available.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS II — [06:36]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Timothy 3:16-17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+3%3A16-17/>; 1 Peter 1:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+1%3A25/>\r\n\r\nQ138. What does the lesson say Jehovah's Witnesses believe happens at death?\r\nA. The soul enters heaven or hell immediately, awaiting the resurrection of the body.\r\nB. The spirit returns to God for judgment while the body waits for the resurrection.\r\nC. Death ends body and soul until Jesus resurrects; the unsaved cease to exist.\r\nD. The soul is reborn into another life until it has become fully righteous.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says they believe man is body and soul, death is the complete end of both, and the unsaved cease to exist, with no hell.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS II — [09:03]\r\n\r\nQ139. In the lesson's view of man as spirit, soul and body, which part is called the \"true man\"?\r\nA. The soul\r\nB. The mind\r\nC. The spirit\r\nD. The body\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Citing 1 Thessalonians 5:23, the speaker says the spirit is the true man and the body is just a container.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS II — [10:02]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Thessalonians 5:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A23/>\r\n\r\nQ140. How does the lesson connect the tenses \"saved, being saved, will be saved\" to the spirit, soul and body?\r\nA. The body is made new (saved), the soul is renewed (being saved), and the spirit is resurrected (will be saved).\r\nB. The soul is made new (saved), the spirit is renewed (being saved), and the body is resurrected (will be saved).\r\nC. The spirit is resurrected (saved), the body is renewed (being saved), and the soul is made new (will be saved).\r\nD. The spirit is made new (saved), the soul is renewed (being saved), and the body is resurrected (will be saved).\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says your spirit is made new, your mind is being renewed, and your body will be resurrected on the last day.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS II — [11:09]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Thessalonians 5:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A23/>\r\n\r\nQ141. According to the lesson, what renews the soul?\r\nA. The resurrection, when the soul is made new at Christ's return\r\nB. God's word, received with meekness (James 1:21)\r\nC. The sacraments, which wash away the sin of the soul\r\nD. Good works, which earn the renewal of the soul\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the soul needs renewal, and that it is the word of God that renews it, citing James 1:21 and Hebrews 4:12.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS II — [12:04]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: James 1:21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A21/>; Hebrews 4:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+4%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ142. Against the claim that nothing happens after death, what does the lesson cite from Ecclesiastes 12:7?\r\nA. The spirit returns to God who gave it, for judgment.\r\nB. The body returns to the earth and the soul sleeps until the resurrection.\r\nC. The spirit returns to the earth and the body goes to God.\r\nD. The spirit is reborn until it has been fully purified.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the spirit returns to God who gave it, answering the afterlife question and pointing to judgment.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS III — [00:20]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ecclesiastes 12:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ecclesiastes+12%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ143. According to John 3:16-18 as taught in this lesson, what is the basis of judgment?\r\nA. Faith in Jesus, since believers are not condemned.\r\nB. Good works measured against the standard of the Law.\r\nC. Obedience to the church that teaches the gospel.\r\nD. Knowledge of Christian doctrine and the Scriptures.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reads John 3:16-18 and says the judgment is faith in Jesus.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS III — [01:26]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 3:16–18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A16%E2%80%9318/>\r\n\r\nQ144. How does the lesson say Jehovah's Witnesses understand salvation and good works?\r\nA. Salvation is by faith alone, and good works are optional signs of gratitude.\r\nB. Salvation is by good works alone, defined as personal acts of charity.\r\nC. Salvation requires faith plus works, defined as absolute obedience to the Watchtower.\r\nD. Salvation is by membership in the organization with no works required.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says they believe faith and good works save, and that good works are defined by the Watchtower as serving the society and absolutely obeying it.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS III — [02:23]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 2:8-9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+2%3A8-9/>\r\n\r\nQ145. Why does the speaker keep repeating the figure of 8 million Jehovah's Witnesses?\r\nA. To show that a religion with 8 million followers must be teaching the truth.\r\nB. To show that Jehovah's Witnesses are the largest group in the church today.\r\nC. To suggest that Christians should not talk to them until they are prepared.\r\nD. To create urgency to tell Witnesses you know, so you are not found silent.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says to think of 8 million people in danger of hell when meeting Jehovah's Witnesses so it will propel you to tell them.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS III — [03:30]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Corinthians 5:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ146. What does Hebrews 1:4 show, according to the lesson?\r\nA. Jesus was made equal to the angels after his resurrection.\r\nB. Jesus was an angel who became greater than the others by obedience.\r\nC. Jesus is better than the angels and has a more excellent name, so he is not an angel.\r\nD. Jesus is the greatest angel who took on the name of God.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reads that Christ became so much better than the angels, using it to show that Jesus is not an angel.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS III — [03:57]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 1:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A4/>\r\n\r\nQ147. What contrast does the lesson draw between Jesus and angels regarding worship?\r\nA. Jesus told Thomas to worship the Father alone, as angels had commanded.\r\nB. Jesus and the angels both accepted worship but only from Jewish believers.\r\nC. Angels rebuked worshippers, but Jesus never stopped anyone from worshipping him.\r\nD. Jesus and the angels both rebuked worshippers because worship belongs to Jehovah.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Jesus accepted worship, as when Thomas called him my Lord and my God, while angels stopped those who tried to worship them.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS III — [08:22]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 20:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+20%3A28/>; Hebrews 1:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A6/>; Revelation 19:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+19%3A10/>\r\n\r\nQ148. Why did the Jews pick up stones when Jesus said, \"Before Abraham was, I am\"?\r\nA. They believed he had insulted Abraham by claiming to be older than him.\r\nB. They understood \"I am\" as Jesus claiming to be God.\r\nC. They believed he had broken the Sabbath by speaking in the temple.\r\nD. They believed he was claiming to be Abraham's descendant.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the Jews knew he was referring to God, so they took up stones to throw at him for blasphemy.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS III — [10:17]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 8:58-59 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+8%3A58-59/>\r\n\r\nQ149. What does the speaker say about the New World Translation's wording of John 8:58, \"I have been\"?\r\nA. The wording matches the Greek and shows Jesus was speaking of his age.\r\nB. The wording departs from the Greek, which per the lexicon is \"I am,\" as God said to Moses.\r\nC. The wording is correct, but the phrase does not refer to God's name.\r\nD. The wording was used because the Greek is unclear and cannot be translated.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the New World Translation reads \"before Abraham was I have been,\" yet the Greek translation is \"I am,\" as God said to Moses.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS III — [08:22]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 8:58 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+8%3A58/>\r\n\r\nQ150. What approach does the speaker take in defending the deity of Christ in the later part of this lesson?\r\nA. Using only early church writings, which Jehovah's Witnesses already accept.\r\nB. Using only the original Greek, so Jehovah's Witnesses cannot object.\r\nC. Using the King James Version, since Jehovah's Witnesses respect it.\r\nD. Using Jehovah's Witnesses' own New World Translation to make the argument.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says he will defend the deity of Christ from the New World Translation and uses four points.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS III — [10:26]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 1:1-3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1-3/>; Colossians 1:15-17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+1%3A15-17/>; Hebrews 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ151. What question does the lesson ask Jehovah's Witnesses about the Isaiah 40:3 \"way of Jehovah\" passage?\r\nA. Why does Isaiah say that Jehovah will come after John the Baptist?\r\nB. Why does John the Baptist say Jehovah will baptize with the Holy Spirit?\r\nC. Why does Isaiah refer to Jesus as the voice crying in the wilderness?\r\nD. Why does John the Baptist apply to Jesus what Isaiah said of Jehovah?\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker asks why John the Baptist applied to Jesus the passage Isaiah used for Jehovah, which is repeated in all the Gospels in the New World Translation.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS IV — [00:16]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Isaiah 40:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+40%3A3/>\r\n\r\nQ152. How does the lesson answer the objection that Revelation 22:12-13 (\"first and last\") may not be Jesus speaking?\r\nA. The passage is quoted from Isaiah, so Jehovah is the speaker.\r\nB. The passage is a vision, so it cannot be applied to anyone in particular.\r\nC. The context shows it is Jesus, since verse 20 says he is coming quickly.\r\nD. The speaker is an angel who is repeating a message from Jehovah.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the objection fails in context since verse 20 says Jesus is coming quickly.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS IV — [02:34]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Revelation 22:12–13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+22%3A12%E2%80%9313/>; Revelation 22:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+22%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ153. What conclusion does the lesson draw from John 1:3 (\"apart from him, not even one thing came into existence\") with Isaiah 48:13?\r\nA. Jehovah created the world alone, so Jesus had no role in creation.\r\nB. Only one person created the world, so Jesus the Creator must be God.\r\nC. Jesus created part of the world while Jehovah created the rest.\r\nD. Two persons created the world together, so Jehovah and Jesus are equal.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker argues \"apart from him\" means him alone, so it's not two people creating the world; if the New Testament calls Jesus Creator, Jesus is God.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS IV — [04:05]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A3/>; Isaiah 48:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+48%3A13/>\r\n\r\nQ154. How does the lesson answer the claim that \"Mighty God\" in Isaiah 9:6 is not \"Almighty God\"?\r\nA. Isaiah 9:6 refers to a human king, and Isaiah 10:21 refers to a different king.\r\nB. Isaiah 10:21 uses \"Mighty God\" for angels, so the title does not require deity.\r\nC. Isaiah 9:6 is a later addition to Isaiah and so should not be used.\r\nD. Isaiah 10:21 applies \"Mighty God\" to Jehovah, so it is no lesser title.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Isaiah applies \"Mighty God\" to Jehovah in Isaiah 10:21, so it would be inconsistent to treat it as a lesser title in Isaiah 9:6.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS IV — [04:05]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Isaiah 9:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+9%3A6/>; Isaiah 10:21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+10%3A21/>\r\n\r\nQ155. What was the \"Great Disappointment\" in the Adventist history described in the lesson?\r\nA. The day Ellen G. White admitted that her visions were mistaken.\r\nB. The moment the church abandoned the Sabbath for Sunday worship.\r\nC. The decision of Adventists to leave the Baptist church.\r\nD. Miller's predicted date for Jesus' return passed with no return.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Miller said Jesus would come between March 1843 and March 1844; Jesus did not, and this became known as the Great Disappointment.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS IV — [07:47]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Daniel 8:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Daniel+8%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ156. What did Hiram Edson conclude after the Great Disappointment?\r\nA. That Miller was right about the place but wrong about the time.\r\nB. That Miller was right about the time but wrong about the place.\r\nC. That Miller had been right about both and Jesus had come in secret.\r\nD. That Miller was wrong about both and the prophecy should be abandoned.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Edson claimed a vision of Jesus at the altar of heaven and concluded Miller was right about the time but wrong about the place.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS IV — [08:51]\r\n\r\nQ157. What tension does the lesson note in the Adventist view of authority?\r\nA. They call the Bible the only creed but also follow Ellen G. White.\r\nB. They say the Bible is the only creed, yet do not follow any other writings.\r\nC. They say Ellen G. White is the only creed, yet also follow the Bible.\r\nD. They say tradition is the only creed, yet rely only on the Bible.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says they claim the Bible is the only creed but also follow Ellen G. White's teachings and writings.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS IV — [08:51]\r\n\r\nQ158. How does Hebrews 10:10-18 contrast Jesus with the Jewish priests, according to the lesson?\r\nA. The priests offered sacrifices once a year, but Jesus offered sacrifices daily.\r\nB. The priests stood daily offering sacrifices, but Jesus offered one and sat down.\r\nC. The priests offered sacrifices for sin, but Jesus offered none and only prayed.\r\nD. The priests stood to pray, and Jesus stood to teach until his return.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the priests stood daily offering the same sacrifices, but Jesus offered one sacrifice for sin and sat down at God's right hand.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS IV — [13:26]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 10:10–18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+10%3A10%E2%80%9318/>\r\n\r\nQ159. What does the name \"Seventh Day Adventist\" refer to, according to the lesson?\r\nA. Observing Sunday worship and the belief in Jesus' resurrection on the seventh day.\r\nB. Observing seven festivals a year and the belief in seven judgments.\r\nC. Counting the days to Christ's return and observing the seventh month as holy.\r\nD. Observing the biblical Sabbath on Saturday and the belief that Jesus will soon return.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the name comes from Sabbath observance on the seventh day, Saturday, and Advent, meaning coming, referring to Christ's return.\r\nREF: JEHOVA’S WITNESS IV — [06:41]\r\n\r\nQ160. What observation does the quote from the historian Plutarch make about human societies?\r\nA. Cities have been found without walls, kings or wealth, but never a city without a temple or gods.\r\nB. Cities without kings and wealth have always collapsed within a few generations.\r\nC. Cities that built temples were always wealthier than cities that did not.\r\nD. Cities that rejected the gods eventually returned to worship them.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Plutarch said you may find cities lacking walls, letters, kings, houses, wealth, theaters, but a city without a temple or gods no one ever saw.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY I — [01:48]\r\n\r\nQ161. How does Paul begin his address in Athens, according to the speakers?\r\nA. He says their religion is an illusion that must be abandoned before they can hear him.\r\nB. He says they are far from the truth because they worship many gods.\r\nC. He says he perceives they are deeply religious, rather than condemning them.\r\nD. He says their religion is the same as his, with a different name for God.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speakers say Paul commended them, \"I perceive that you are deeply religious,\" before pointing them to the true God.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY I — [03:23]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 17:22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17%3A22/>\r\n\r\nQ162. How does Paul use the Athenian poet's line that we are the \"offspring of God\"?\r\nA. He argues that the poet proves all people are already worshipping the true God.\r\nB. He argues that the poet was a prophet who foretold the coming of Christ.\r\nC. He argues that the poet's line proves the gods are made by human hands.\r\nD. He argues that if we are God's offspring, God cannot be an idol made of silver or gold.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Paul used their own poet and asked how those who are God's offspring can come from an idol made of silver or gold.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY I — [05:10]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 17:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ163. Why does Pastor Ayokunle say people searching for the divine are closer to the truth than many atheists?\r\nA. They already worship the true God, though they use a different name for Him.\r\nB. They see the need for belief and admit we are not creatures of chance.\r\nC. They have rejected organized religion and are therefore free from error.\r\nD. They follow a defined religion that teaches the truth about God.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says they understand the need for something to believe and admit we are not creatures of happenstance, which puts them nearer the truth than atheists.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY I — [04:54]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 17:27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17%3A27/>; Romans 1:19-20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+1%3A19-20/>\r\n\r\nQ164. What happened to many who embraced the new atheism, according to Pastor Praiseworth?\r\nA. Their search for God ended, and they returned to organized church life.\r\nB. The void remained, and many now turn to New Age spirituality.\r\nC. Their need for God faded, and they lost interest in any spirituality.\r\nD. Their beliefs matured, and they began to reject all supernatural claims.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says that after embracing these beliefs, the void for the search for God remained, and many are moving to New Age spirituality seeking something transcendent.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY I — [06:06]\r\n\r\nQ165. How do the speakers describe New Age spirituality?\r\nA. A single religion founded recently by a group of Eastern teachers.\r\nB. A modern movement with new practices that Scripture has never addressed.\r\nC. A collection of ancient beliefs, not one defined religion.\r\nD. A Christian movement that mixes biblical teaching with local customs.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Pastor Ayokunle says it is not a defined religion but a group of spiritual beliefs that are quite ancient, such as divination, astrology and reincarnation.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY I — [08:26]\r\n\r\nQ166. Why does the speaker cite Deuteronomy 18:10 when discussing New Age practices?\r\nA. To show that the Bible permits some divination if it is practiced with prayer.\r\nB. To show that the New Age movement is mentioned only in the New Testament.\r\nC. To show that magical practices began after the time of Moses.\r\nD. To show practices like divination are ancient and already named in Scripture.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He reads the verse listing divination, enchanters and necromancers to show these practices are ancient and the Bible itself referenced them.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY I — [09:21]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Deuteronomy 18:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+18%3A10/>\r\n\r\nQ167. What does the \"50 naira bike\" remark illustrate?\r\nA. A worldview of nothingness leaves people seeking meaning, so New Age is a short step away.\r\nB. A person must travel a long way to move from atheism to New Age belief.\r\nC. A person who leaves atheism must pass through many religions first.\r\nD. A person who leaves church is almost certain to become an atheist.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says popularizing a view that emphasizes nothingness leaves people with a search for meaning; his friend likened atheism to a short bike ride from New Age.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY I — [12:07]\r\n\r\nQ168. According to Pastor Praiseworth, why does the idea of a divine essence in each person appeal to some?\r\nA. It offers them a clear set of rules and a defined moral code to follow.\r\nB. It flatters ego: you are more than physical, and who wouldn't want to be a god?\r\nC. It gives them an explanation of suffering that no other religion has provided.\r\nD. It offers them a simple way to earn forgiveness for the wrongs they have done.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the New Age idea that you have a divine essence appeals to ego, recalling a New Ager who said \"we are all gods,\" and asks who doesn't want to be a god.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY I — [11:42]\r\n\r\nQ169. How do the speakers respond to the New Age promise of control, using Proverbs 3:5-6?\r\nA. The verse says not to lean on your own understanding, so New Age promises what it can't deliver.\r\nB. The verse says people should control their lives by trusting their own understanding.\r\nC. The verse says control is possible only for those who have received higher knowledge.\r\nD. The verse says control belongs to angels, who direct the paths of those who ask.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Pastor Ayokunle says Proverbs 3:5-6 shows we can't direct our own paths with a higher form of knowledge, so New Age sells the promise but doesn't deliver.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY I — [13:48]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Proverbs 3:5–6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Proverbs+3%3A5%E2%80%936/>\r\n\r\nQ170. Why do the speakers say that telling a New Age practitioner \"you have sinned\" feels like a personal attack?\r\nA. They believe sin is only an idea and have never considered what it means.\r\nB. They believe sin is real but only the leaders of their group can name it.\r\nC. They believe sin is something done only by people outside their group.\r\nD. They believe nothing is wrong, so the claim exposes their lack of control.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speakers say New Age followers think there's nothing wrong and every craving is to be satisfied, so an accusation hits to the core and exposes a lack of control.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY II — [00:00]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 3:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A23/>\r\n\r\nQ171. What is \"cord cutting,\" as described in the lesson?\r\nA. A ritual meant to release negative energy stored in the body through meditation.\r\nB. A ritual meant to communicate with the spirit of a person who has died.\r\nC. A ritual meant to bind two people together so they never part.\r\nD. A ritual to spiritually sever an unhealthy attachment to a person.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker defines it as a ritual to symbolically or spiritually sever a connection with an unhealthy attachment, such as after a bad breakup.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY II — [02:11]\r\n\r\nQ172. What does the speaker say about many who embrace New Age healing practices?\r\nA. Many do not heal; the practices are coping mechanisms and escapes.\r\nB. Many heal fully and then go on to teach the practices to others.\r\nC. Many heal outwardly but fall back into the old pain after a few months.\r\nD. Many gain emotional healing but lose their faith as a result.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says many don't necessarily heal and that these are coping mechanisms and escapes, which can result in a life without accountability.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY II — [05:00]\r\n\r\nQ173. How does the lesson say the sense of belonging in New Age often begins?\r\nA. Through formal membership in a temple that requires vows of commitment.\r\nB. Through yoga or meditation groups of the \"spiritual but irreligious.\"\r\nC. Through public rituals that new members are required to attend each week.\r\nD. Through online courses that promise secret knowledge to those who pay.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says people join a yoga, meditation, hiking or travel group and say, \"we are spiritual, but we are irreligious.\"\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY II — [06:48]\r\n\r\nQ174. What does Pastor Praiseworth say about the difference between the symptom and the problem?\r\nA. Rebellion against the faith is the problem, so you should confront the symptom first.\r\nB. Rebellion against the faith is a symptom of ignorance, so you should explain doctrine.\r\nC. Rebellion against the faith is a symptom of pride, so you should challenge the person.\r\nD. Rebellion may be a symptom of deeper hurt, so you should address the cause with love.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says to diagnose the problem and not the symptom, noting that someone hurt by the church may show it by accepting these ideologies, and God's love can restore them.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY II — [07:28]\r\n\r\nQ175. According to the lesson, why is pantheism appealing in terms of judgment?\r\nA. If all is divine, evil is real but is punished only after death.\r\nB. If all is divine, judgment is left to each person's conscience.\r\nC. If all is divine, only sacred people are held to account.\r\nD. If all is divine, nothing is evil, so judgment disappears.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker lists first that pantheism eliminates judgment: if all is divine then nothing is evil and everything is sacred.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY II — [08:19]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 9:27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+9%3A27/>\r\n\r\nQ176. What does the example of the speaker's sons taking turns with a toy illustrate?\r\nA. Children need to be taught rules because they have no sense of fairness.\r\nB. Children disagree about fairness because every person has a different standard.\r\nC. Children appeal to a moral standard, pointing to a moral lawgiver.\r\nD. Children show that morality is created by agreements among people.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Using C.S. Lewis's argument, he says his sons appeal to what is fair without being taught, showing an instinctive moral law and a lawgiver, which pantheism contradicts.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY II — [09:59]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 2:14-15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+2%3A14-15/>\r\n\r\nQ177. What is the \"problem of creation\" for pantheism, as the speakers state it?\r\nA. If God and the universe are one, pantheism cannot explain why people die.\r\nB. If God and the universe are one, pantheism cannot explain why people pray.\r\nC. If God and the universe are one, pantheism cannot explain how the universe came to exist.\r\nD. If God and the universe are one, pantheism cannot explain why evil exists.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says pantheism has no answer to how the universe came to exist or why something exists rather than nothing, and the universe began to exist.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY II — [11:15]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+1%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ178. What is the \"problem of change and time\" for pantheism?\r\nA. If all is God and God changes, then God must be growing along with creation.\r\nB. If all is God and God is in time, then God cannot know the future.\r\nC. If all is God, time is only an illusion that science has already proven false.\r\nD. If God is eternal and unchanging, nothing could change, yet people die.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that if all is God and God is unchanging, how can anything change, yet people come, go and die.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY II — [11:48]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Malachi 3:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Malachi+3%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ179. How does the claim \"you are God\" deal with guilt, according to the speakers?\r\nA. It increases guilt by telling people they are responsible for creating reality.\r\nB. It deals with guilt through rituals that remove sins from the soul.\r\nC. It admits guilt but says each person must work it off through reincarnation.\r\nD. It removes guilt: everything done is just a lesson, neither good nor bad.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the claim removes guilt and shame, saying what you've done are just lessons, neither good nor bad.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY II — [13:16]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 3:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A23/>\r\n\r\nQ180. What problem does the question \"If you are a God, why can't you fly?\" raise for the claim of human divinity?\r\nA. It cannot account for the moral law that people appeal to.\r\nB. It cannot account for why people search for a supreme being.\r\nC. It cannot account for human limitations, such as inability to fly and death.\r\nD. It cannot account for the existence of many religions.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the theology ignores the reality of sin and cannot account for limitations: if you are a God, why can't you fly and why do you die?\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY III — [00:01]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 6:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6%3A23/>\r\n\r\nQ181. What does the zodiac example (\"I did this because I am a Leo\") show?\r\nA. Wrong behavior is seen but excused until the person has changed.\r\nB. Wrong behavior is accepted only when the stars support the action.\r\nC. Wrong behavior can't be challenged, since the ideology justifies it.\r\nD. Wrong behavior is judged more harshly for those who believe in signs.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says you can't really challenge wrong behavior because it is already justified by an ideology.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY III — [00:22]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: James 1:14-15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A14-15/>\r\n\r\nQ182. How do the speakers show that \"all paths lead to God\" is a logical contradiction?\r\nA. The religions make exclusive claims, such as Acts 4:12 and \"no God but Allah.\"\r\nB. The religions teach the same things in different words, so no contradiction arises.\r\nC. The religions agree on the nature of God but differ only in their rituals.\r\nD. The religions disagree on moral questions, but not on the nature of God.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He cites Acts 4:12 (no other name for salvation) and Islam's statement that there is no God but Allah as exclusive claims.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY III — [01:35]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 4:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+4%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ183. According to the lesson, what does the claim that all religions are the same imply about those religions?\r\nA. It dismisses their teachings and implies they are self-deceived.\r\nB. It respects what each religion teaches by finding the truth in all of them.\r\nC. It proves that each religion has discovered a different part of the truth.\r\nD. It shows that each religion has a right to its own definition of God.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that claim speaks down on religions, calling them liars or self-deceived, because people don't want the intellectual and emotional rigor to weigh them.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY III — [02:42]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 14:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+14%3A6/>; Acts 4:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+4%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ184. What does the \"two different maps\" remark add to the argument against \"all paths lead to God\"?\r\nA. Different maps can lead to the same place if the traveler is patient.\r\nB. Different maps show that every religion has a guide that is equally trustworthy.\r\nC. Different maps can't all lead to one destination; even heaven differs.\r\nD. Different maps prove that each person must draw their own map to God.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Christianity says there is one God and Hinduism says many, so they are different maps leading to different destinations, even in their view of heaven.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY III — [03:37]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Deuteronomy 6:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+6%3A4/>; 1 Corinthians 8:4-6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+8%3A4-6/>\r\n\r\nQ185. How does the lesson distinguish the prophetic pictures in Scripture from New Age vision boards?\r\nA. In Scripture, believers create the vision; in New Age, the universe gives it.\r\nB. In Scripture, the vision is private; in New Age, the vision is shown to others.\r\nC. In Scripture God gives the vision (Genesis 15); in New Age it is self-driven.\r\nD. In Scripture, the vision comes through dreams; in New Age, it comes through pictures.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says in Scripture God is the one who gives the vision (Abraham and the stars), whereas the New Age asks what you want and is self-driven.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY III — [05:32]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 15:1–6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+15%3A1%E2%80%936/>\r\n\r\nQ186. How does the lesson contrast Christian confessions with New Age affirmations such as \"money comes to me easily\"?\r\nA. Christians declare what they want God to do; New Age declares what is already true.\r\nB. Christians declare what God has said; New Age affirmations come from self.\r\nC. Christians declare their own goals; New Age declares what the universe has promised.\r\nD. Christians declare their feelings; New Age declares scientific facts.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says our confession is what God has said about us, not what we think or what is propelled by self.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY III — [08:07]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Corinthians 1:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+1%3A20/>\r\n\r\nQ187. What does the lesson say about the Hebrew sense of \"meditate\" in Joshua 1:8?\r\nA. It means to empty the mind: sitting still until a spiritual state is reached.\r\nB. It means to dream: waiting for visions that confirm the word.\r\nC. It means to chant: repeating a single phrase until the body relaxes.\r\nD. It means to mutter: declaring the words quietly so the mind retains the word.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the Hebrew use of the word actually means to mutter, informing your mind what the word says, unlike the New Age.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY III — [09:14]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Joshua 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Joshua+1%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ188. Why does Pastor Ayokunle say you should begin with questions instead of criticism when speaking with New Age people?\r\nA. Questions save time, because New Age people do not like long explanations.\r\nB. Attacking beliefs loses them early, as they quickly feel you are biased.\r\nC. Questions are required by Scripture before any person can be evangelized.\r\nD. Questions prove the person's beliefs are wrong without needing an argument.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says starting with questions, not criticism, is probably the most important principle, because attacking beliefs triggers quick defenses like \"What about Muslims?\"\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY III — [12:00]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Peter 3:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+3%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ189. What does Pastor Praiseworth say most New Agers' answers rest on, and what question follows?\r\nA. Ancient texts; then ask which translation of the texts they follow.\r\nB. Personal experience; then ask how that experience is validated.\r\nC. Scientific research; then ask which experiments support their claims.\r\nD. Community teaching; then ask who has the authority to teach.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says most of their responses come from \"I felt it was real to me,\" which is experience, and then asks what makes the interpretation true and how it is validated.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY III — [14:11]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Thessalonians 5:21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+5%3A21/>\r\n\r\nQ190. What does the speaker take from Jesus' conversation with the woman at the well in John 4?\r\nA. Begin with correction of the person's sin so that they feel the need for repentance.\r\nB. Begin by comparing religions so that the person can see the difference.\r\nC. Begin by finding their deepest desire and meeting it in Christ, as with living water.\r\nD. Begin by quoting Scripture until the person asks about its meaning.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Jesus met her where she was, asking for water, exposing her need and offering living water.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY IV — [02:06]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 4:7–15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+4%3A7%E2%80%9315/>\r\n\r\nQ191. What is the purpose of asking a New Ager, \"What does your spirituality give you that you were looking for?\"\r\nA. To uncover the underlying desire so the gospel can address it.\r\nB. To show them that their spirituality is not giving them what they want.\r\nC. To put them on the defensive so they will explain their practices.\r\nD. To test whether they are sincere about their spiritual beliefs.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker lists desires such as meaning, community, peace, healing, control and belonging, and says to ask what their spirituality gives them so you can present the gospel.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY IV — [02:06]\r\n\r\nQ192. What happens, according to the speaker, when you press a New Ager for evidence that their belief is true?\r\nA. They realize they only \"know\" it, which opens a door for the historical Christ.\r\nB. They produce evidence from experience that is as strong as the Christian evidence.\r\nC. They realize their belief is false and immediately accept Christianity.\r\nD. They end the conversation and refuse to discuss the matter further.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says that when pressed for tangible evidence they realize they just know and believe, and this is a good place to introduce Christ, who lived at a traceable time.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY IV — [00:00]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 15:3-8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15%3A3-8/>\r\n\r\nQ193. How does the speaker say the death and resurrection of Christ differ from other religious claims?\r\nA. They are spiritual claims that cannot be investigated, so they must be accepted.\r\nB. They are historical claims that can be investigated, not just believed.\r\nC. They are legendary claims that were added to the story after the apostles' time.\r\nD. They are personal claims that are valid only for those who have experienced them.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the death and resurrection are historically traceable and next week will show how to defend the resurrection, just as we can investigate a murder.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY IV — [01:20]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 15:3-8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15%3A3-8/>\r\n\r\nQ194. How does the speaker say a Christian should challenge \"all spiritual paths lead to God\"?\r\nA. By insisting on logical consistency, which forces the question \"which one then?\"\r\nB. By insisting that a person must choose a religion on the basis of their feelings.\r\nC. By insisting that each religion has some truth, so the person must test each one.\r\nD. By insisting that the person's own religion is the only one that can be tested.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the logical consistency demand moves the person from \"every path can lead to God\" to \"which one then?\"\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY IV — [08:04]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 14:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+14%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ195. Why does the lesson say prayer is effective with New Agers, according to an ex-New Ager?\r\nA. Prayer is like a debate to them, and it brings them to prove their own claims.\r\nB. Prayer is like meditation to them, and it brings them to the same state they know.\r\nC. Prayer is like a spa treatment to them and exposes their need for God (Psalm 100:3).\r\nD. Prayer is like therapy to them, and it brings them the healing they were seeking.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says an ex-New Ager called prayer like a spa treatment and that Psalm 100:3 (God made us) brings them to vulnerability.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY IV — [08:29]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Psalm 100:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+100%3A3/>\r\n\r\nQ196. What does the lesson say about the devil and a person's pain, using 2 Corinthians 4:3-4?\r\nA. He creates the pain so that the person turns away from God.\r\nB. He uses legitimate pain, such as church hurt, to veil their eyes.\r\nC. He uses pain only when a person has chosen to practice New Age rituals.\r\nD. He uses a person's pride to veil the truth, and not their pain.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the devil will use your legitimate pain to achieve his goal, placing a veil over the eyes.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY IV — [09:44]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Corinthians 4:3–4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+4%3A3%E2%80%934/>\r\n\r\nQ197. What does the lesson say African traditional religions have in common regarding the Supreme Being?\r\nA. Each one denies a supreme being and worships only lesser deities.\r\nB. Each one holds that the supreme being is the same under different names.\r\nC. Each one acknowledges a supreme being, e.g. Olodumare or Chukwu.\r\nD. Each one holds that the supreme being is a human ancestor.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker lists names for the Supreme Being among the Yoruba, Igbo, Ghanaian, Zulu and Maasai peoples and says every one acknowledges one.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY IV — [11:50]\r\n\r\nQ198. How does the lesson say Africans have tended to view the Supreme Being?\r\nA. As near and active, having handed all creation to the ancestors.\r\nB. As angry and distant, having withdrawn after humans sinned.\r\nC. As one among many equal gods, all of whom are worshipped together.\r\nD. As detached, having handed matters over to lesser deities.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Africans view the Supreme Being as transcendent and handed over everything to lesser deities, so people are more familiar with the lesser deities.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY IV — [13:00]\r\n\r\nQ199. What question does the speaker raise from Shango being traced to a historical ruler?\r\nA. Who was worshipped before him, and did people deify themselves?\r\nB. Why did Shango's people stop worshipping the Supreme Being?\r\nC. Why did Shango's people not worship the lesser deities?\r\nD. Why was Shango regarded as a king and not as a god?\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Shango is said to have been the third Alaafin of Oyo, so before his time who were they worshipping, and his partner asks if they deified themselves.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY IV — [14:24]\r\n\r\nQ200. What does the speaker infer from the similarities between deities such as Shango and Thor, or Esu and Loki?\r\nA. The deities were separately revealed by the Supreme Being to each people.\r\nB. The deities are the same figure, with names that differ by language alone.\r\nC. Either one spirit motivates the pattern or there was copying over time.\r\nD. The deities prove that all traditions arise from one original religion.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says that either a spirit is motivating the same thing across traditional religions or copying is happening, showing the knowledge developed over time.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY V — [01:30]\r\n\r\nQ201. What shared traits does the lesson give for Ekwensu in Igbo belief and Esu in Yoruba belief?\r\nA. Both are supreme beings who created the world and gave moral laws.\r\nB. Both are ancestors who became deities after the death of a king.\r\nC. Both are guardians of children who protect twins from harm.\r\nD. Both are policemen of the spirit realm and trickster spirits.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says both are like policemen in the spirit realm who effect justice and vengeance, and trickster spirits like Loki.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY V — [00:42]\r\n\r\nQ202. Why do some people find African traditional religion attractive, according to Pastor Praiseworth?\r\nA. Its simple creed seems to require no moral effort from followers.\r\nB. Its use of one supreme God seems to be more consistent than Christianity.\r\nC. Its strong moral codes and swift consequences seem more accountable.\r\nD. Its lack of rules seems to give people more freedom than Christianity.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says some argue that because Christianity promises forgiveness while traditional religion brings immediate consequences, it holds people more accountable.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY V — [03:40]\r\n\r\nQ203. What position does the lesson take from 1 Corinthians 10:19-21 on the gods of traditional religion?\r\nA. They are real spirits (demons) behind idols, not God and not imaginary.\r\nB. They are only symbols, so offering sacrifices to them is harmless.\r\nC. They are lesser gods who deserve respect when they are properly honored.\r\nD. They are the spirits of ancestors who help their families.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Paul didn't treat idols as nothing but said what pagans sacrifice they sacrifice to demons, acknowledging spirits behind them.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY V — [04:32]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 10:19–21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+10%3A19%E2%80%9321/>\r\n\r\nQ204. What does Psalm 86:8, \"among the gods there is none like you,\" show, according to the lesson?\r\nA. These gods do not exist, so none can be compared with Yahweh.\r\nB. These gods are equal to Yahweh in their power over the nations.\r\nC. These spiritual forces are real, but none is like Yahweh.\r\nD. These gods are the same as Yahweh with different names.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the verse means these gods or spiritual elements are present, but there is no God like Yahweh.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY V — [06:19]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Psalm 86:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+86%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ205. What does the confrontation of Elijah with the prophets of Baal show, according to the lesson?\r\nA. Baal was treated as an invention, so the choice was between truth and falsehood.\r\nB. Baal was a real entity; the choice was Yahweh or Baal, not Yahweh or nothing.\r\nC. Baal was treated as a lesser god, so the choice was to worship both.\r\nD. Baal was treated as a prophet, so the choice was between prophets.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Elijah did not tell the people to choose between Yahweh and serving no one; Baal was a real entity, and they had to choose.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY V — [07:47]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Kings 18:21-39 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Kings+18%3A21-39/>\r\n\r\nQ206. What was the Abobaku practice, as described in the lesson?\r\nA. People who died with the king, who could not enter the underworld alone.\r\nB. People sent away from the king so that the king's heirs could rule in peace.\r\nC. People chosen to guard the king's grave for the rest of their lives.\r\nD. People who served the king and were freed when the king died.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says it means \"to die with the king\": a group treated well all their lives so they would die with the king, who could not go to the underworld alone.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY V — [08:50]\r\n\r\nQ207. What does the lesson say about the killing of twins in Calabar?\r\nA. Twins were thought to be gods and were killed as an offering to the Supreme Being.\r\nB. Twins were thought to be a sign of favor and were separated by the king.\r\nC. Twins were thought to be a bad omen and the practice was ended by local kings alone.\r\nD. Twins were thought to be a bad omen and were killed until missionaries like Slessor intervened.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says many were killed because twins were thought a bad omen, and that this continued until the missionaries came, with Mary Slessor a prominent figure.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY V — [10:46]\r\n\r\nQ208. According to the lesson, what truth from the Bible changed the viewpoint on human life?\r\nA. That every person belongs to a tribe that is chosen by God for a special task.\r\nB. That every person must prove their worth through service to the king.\r\nC. That every person has an ancestor who protects them in the afterlife.\r\nD. That everyone is made in God's image, with no slave or free in Christ.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the idea that everybody is made in the image of God changed the central viewpoint on human life, and cites that all are one in Christ.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY V — [11:56]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 1:27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+1%3A27/>; Galatians 3:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+3%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ209. What does the lesson say about some traditional adherents who claim they \"used to be a Christian\"?\r\nA. Some were never saved: they attended church but never heard the gospel.\r\nB. Some were saved but fell away, so we must only warn them about judgment.\r\nC. Some were saved but left because of persecution, so we must apologize for it.\r\nD. Some were taught well but rejected the church, so we must avoid them.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that when you engage them you realize the person was never saved; they attended church but never heard the gospel.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY VI — [01:10]\r\n\r\nQ210. How does the speaker begin to answer the claim that Christianity is a \"white man's religion\"?\r\nA. Jesus was a white European, but the gospel was meant for every nation.\r\nB. Jesus was born in Africa, so the faith began among African peoples.\r\nC. Jesus was Middle Eastern, not European, as were the patriarchs.\r\nD. Jesus was a Roman citizen, so the faith began in the Roman Empire.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says first that Jesus was not a white man or a European; Jesus was Middle Eastern, as were the patriarchs.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY VI — [02:58]\r\n\r\nQ211. What do the speakers say the Ethiopian eunuch's example shows?\r\nA. An African had no religion and was taught the gospel by Philip alone.\r\nB. An African rejected the faith when he read Isaiah and left the church.\r\nC. An African was forced to accept the gospel by missionaries.\r\nD. An African was reading Isaiah and was open to question what he believed.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says he was reading Isaiah, open enough to question what he believed despite his traditional religion, and many adherents refuse to do the same.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY VI — [04:46]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 8:30-35 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+8%3A30-35/>\r\n\r\nQ212. What point does the lesson make by mentioning Augustine, Athanasius and Tertullian?\r\nA. Strong Christian theology existed in Africa before slavery and European dominance.\r\nB. Strong Christian theology began in Africa only after the missionaries arrived.\r\nC. Strong Christian theology came mainly from European leaders in North Africa.\r\nD. Strong Christian theology in Africa was a reaction to colonial rule.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says that between the first and fourth century there was no slave trade or European dominance, yet strong theologians such as Augustine, Athanasius and Tertullian came from Africa.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY VI — [06:01]\r\n\r\nQ213. What does the delivery-man illustration teach about those who blame Christianity for the failures of Christians?\r\nA. Don't accept a package if the delivery man does not know what is inside it.\r\nB. Don't throw away the package, the gospel, because the delivery man behaved badly.\r\nC. Don't judge the delivery man, because he is not responsible for the package.\r\nD. Don't expect the delivery man to behave better than the person who sent him.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the delivery man may be ill-behaved, but don't throw away the package because of his bad behavior; the gospel remains the power of God to salvation.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY VI — [08:04]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 1:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+1%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ214. How does the lesson respond to the fear that embracing Christianity means losing one's culture?\r\nA. Culture does not change, so Christianity must adapt to every cultural practice.\r\nB. Culture is always wrong, so a Christian should abandon every part of it.\r\nC. Culture is private, so it should not be mixed with a person's faith.\r\nD. Culture evolves and borrows, so you can update it without losing its essence.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He gives examples of seats, clothes and Ankara to show culture evolves, and says you can bring new positive features and not lose the essence.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY VI — [11:32]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 9:19-23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+9%3A19-23/>\r\n\r\nQ215. How does the speaker illustrate keeping one's culture while embracing the Christian faith?\r\nA. Yoruba Christians keep their culture of praise-singing, giving it a new direction.\r\nB. Yoruba Christians abandon their culture of praise and replace it with new customs.\r\nC. Yoruba Christians keep their culture only in private, away from the church.\r\nD. Yoruba Christians keep their culture but avoid using it in worship.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the Yoruba culture of using great words and praise-singing continues among those who embraced Christianity, giving a direction to it.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY VI — [09:52]\r\n\r\nQ216. What does the account of the Ephesians who brought their books to be burned (Acts 19) illustrate?\r\nA. Seeing Paul's arguments led worshippers of Diana to debate with him.\r\nB. Seeing the church's wealth led worshippers of Diana to give to the poor.\r\nC. Seeing the city's laws led worshippers of Diana to give up their idols.\r\nD. Seeing God's power led Diana's worshippers to burn their occult items.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says in Ephesus, where they worshipped Diana, people saw Paul and brought their curious arts and burned them.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY VI — [13:01]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 19:18–20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+19%3A18%E2%80%9320/>\r\n\r\nQ217. What does the speaker's testimony about his father's conversion illustrate?\r\nA. God's power can open a door to Christ's authority over darkness.\r\nB. A person's family history is the strongest argument for the faith.\r\nC. A person's fear of death is the only way to bring them to faith.\r\nD. A person's emotional need for community is the main path to faith.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: His father sought a power strong enough to break the hold of what killed his own father and was drawn by a display of power in Jesus' name; the speaker calls it a strong entry point.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY VI — [15:26]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 3:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+3%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ218. How does the speaker answer the argument that a practice is right because it is embedded in culture, such as taboos?\r\nA. Embedding in culture makes it right, but only for those who practice it.\r\nB. Embedding in culture does not make it right, since real people suffer real consequences.\r\nC. Embedding in culture makes it right, unless it is practiced in secret.\r\nD. Embedding in culture does not matter, because taboos do not affect anyone.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He asks whether being practically embedded in culture ultimately makes it right, and says there are real people suffering real consequences.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY VI — [00:53]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 12:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+12%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ219. What does the lesson mean by calling the Mormon and Seventh Day Adventist movements \"restorationist\"?\r\nA. They believe their task is to restore the original Christian faith from corruption.\r\nB. They believe their assignment is to restore the Jewish law that Christians have set aside.\r\nC. They believe their assignment is to unite all denominations into one church.\r\nD. They believe their assignment is to restore the apostles' authority through new leaders.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says restorationists believe their goal is to restore the Christian faith to its original form, claiming it has lost track or become corrupt.\r\nREF: MORMONS I — [01:54]\r\n\r\nQ220. What prompted Joseph Smith's first vision, according to the lesson?\r\nA. Troubled by the death of his family, he read the Psalms and asked God for comfort.\r\nB. Troubled over which denomination to join, he studied James 1:5 and asked God for wisdom.\r\nC. Troubled by his work, he studied Genesis 1 and asked God for a calling.\r\nD. Troubled by his sins, he studied Romans and asked God for forgiveness.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Smith was troubled by competing denominations, studied James 1:5 (\"if anyone lacks wisdom, let him ask\") and asked which church was true.\r\nREF: MORMONS I — [03:37]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: James 1:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A5/>\r\n\r\nQ221. According to the lesson, what did the Father and Jesus tell Smith in the first vision?\r\nA. To join the Baptist church because it was the closest to the truth.\r\nB. To join the church in his town and reform it from within.\r\nC. Not to join any of the existing denominations, because they were all in error.\r\nD. To wait for the Book of Mormon before joining any church.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says that in the vision they told him not to join the existing church because they were all in error.\r\nREF: MORMONS I — [03:37]\r\n\r\nQ222. How does the lesson describe the roles of Mormon and Moroni in the origin of the Book of Mormon?\r\nA. Moroni compiled the record with his son Mormon, who appeared to Smith as an angel.\r\nB. Mormon wrote the record alone, and Moroni translated it into English.\r\nC. Mormon compiled it with Moroni, who later appeared to Smith as an angel.\r\nD. Mormon and Moroni were Jewish prophets who left Jerusalem in 600 BC.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the record was compiled by Mormon and his son Moroni, and that Moroni later appeared to Smith as an angel and led him to the buried book.\r\nREF: MORMONS I — [05:56]\r\n\r\nQ223. How is the Book of Mormon presented in Mormonism, according to the lesson?\r\nA. As another testament of Christ, equal to the Bible in authority.\r\nB. As a commentary on the Bible that has less authority than Scripture.\r\nC. As a history of the early church that is helpful but not inspired.\r\nD. As a replacement for the Bible, which is no longer reliable.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the book is central because it is presented as another testament of Jesus Christ, held on equal authority as the Bible, and evidence of Smith's calling.\r\nREF: MORMONS I — [05:13]\r\n\r\nQ224. What does Galatians 1:8 teach about supernatural encounters, according to the lesson?\r\nA. The appearance of an angel validates a prophet if the angel is from heaven.\r\nB. The appearance of an angel is a sign that the gospel needs to be restored.\r\nC. The appearance of an angel does not validate a prophet whose teaching differs.\r\nD. The appearance of an angel shows God is speaking, but only to the church.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says an angel from heaven preaching another gospel is accursed, so a supernatural encounter doesn't validate a prophet if his teaching conflicts with the gospel.\r\nREF: MORMONS I — [11:04]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Galatians 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+1%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ225. What does Deuteronomy 13:1-3 provide, according to the lesson?\r\nA. A test of a true prophet: he must not lead people to other gods but point them back to the true God.\r\nB. A test of a true prophet: he must perform miracles that confirm his message.\r\nC. A test of a true prophet: he must be accepted by the leaders of the nation.\r\nD. A test of a true prophet: he must be able to predict future events accurately.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the passage gives a test of a true prophet, who must not lead people to other gods, as Aaron did with the idol.\r\nREF: MORMONS I — [09:29]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Deuteronomy 13:1–3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+13%3A1%E2%80%933/>\r\n\r\nQ226. What does the lesson say about Joseph Smith's use of a seer stone?\r\nA. After Mormonism began, he used a seer stone to translate the Book of Mormon in public.\r\nB. Before Mormonism he claimed seer-stone powers and faced a 1826 proceeding over money.\r\nC. Before Mormonism he claimed to heal the sick with a seer stone but was never charged.\r\nD. He used the seer stone to find the buried plates before he saw Moroni.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says he claimed he could use a seer stone to find hidden objects and, in 1826, appeared in an illegal proceeding for collecting money on the claim.\r\nREF: MORMONS I — [12:00]\r\n\r\nQ227. What does the lesson say about the origin of polygamy in Mormonism?\r\nA. It was not original doctrine; Smith practiced it privately while denying it.\r\nB. It was part of the doctrine from the start, and Smith taught it openly.\r\nC. It was introduced after Smith's death by the leaders in Utah.\r\nD. It was taught to Smith by an angel, and he announced it publicly.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says polygamy wasn't part of the LDS doctrine when it began, but was first practiced privately by Smith while he kept denying it publicly.\r\nREF: MORMONS I — [12:00]\r\n\r\nQ228. How does the lesson describe the Mormon view of Jesus' relationship to God and to us?\r\nA. Jesus is the same being as the Father, and he is our elder brother only in his human nature.\r\nB. Jesus is a created angel who became God's Son at his birth, and he is not our brother.\r\nC. Jesus is a distinct being, God's first spirit child, and our spirit brother, and also brother with Lucifer.\r\nD. Jesus is a man who became God at his resurrection, and he is not the Son of God.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Mormons teach that Jesus is an entirely distinct being, God's first spirit child, our spirit brother, and also a brother of Lucifer.\r\nREF: MORMONS II — [03:08]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 1:1-3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1-3/>; Colossians 1:15-17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+1%3A15-17/>; Hebrews 1:5-8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A5-8/>\r\n\r\nQ229. How does the lesson use Isaiah 43:11 and 44:6 against the idea that God the Father once had a God?\r\nA. God says he is a savior among many gods, so he could have had a God.\r\nB. God says he is the first of the gods, so others were exalted after him.\r\nC. God says he will be succeeded by others, so he is not the last.\r\nD. God says none is beside him, first and last, so he had no God.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the verses declare there is no savior or God besides the Lord, who is first and last, and that God did not have a God.\r\nREF: MORMONS II — [01:14]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Isaiah 43:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+43%3A11/>; Isaiah 44:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+44%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ230. What do John 8:58 and John 20:28 show, according to the lesson?\r\nA. Jesus is an exalted human who was worshipped by Thomas after his resurrection.\r\nB. Jesus is not an exalted human but has always been God, co-equal and co-eternal with the Father.\r\nC. Jesus is a created being who became God after his earthly ministry.\r\nD. Jesus is a prophet whom Thomas honored as a servant of God.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites \"Before Abraham was I am\" and Thomas's \"my Lord and my God\" to say Jesus is not an exalted human but has always been God.\r\nREF: MORMONS II — [04:07]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 8:58 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+8%3A58/>; John 20:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+20%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ231. What do Acts 17:25 and 17:28 teach about humans, according to the lesson?\r\nA. God gives life and we depend on him, rather than becoming like him.\r\nB. God gives life, but we are able to become like God by our own effort.\r\nC. God gives life, but only to those who have been chosen before birth.\r\nD. God gives life, but we existed earlier as spirits before we were born.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says Acts 17 shows God as the giver of life and that we are dependent on God for our existence, rather than evolving to become like God.\r\nREF: MORMONS II — [06:11]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 17:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17%3A25/>; Acts 17:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ232. How does the lesson respond to the Mormon belief that revelation received by church presidents becomes scripture?\r\nA. The words of church presidents are Scripture only if the whole church accepts them.\r\nB. The words of church presidents are not Scripture, since human words don't become Scripture.\r\nC. The words of church presidents are Scripture if they agree with the Book of Mormon.\r\nD. The words of church presidents are Scripture only after the president's death.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that revelation received by presidents is treated as scripture in their canon, but human words don't become scripture, citing 2 Timothy 3:16-17.\r\nREF: MORMONS II — [07:13]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Timothy 3:16–17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+3%3A16%E2%80%9317/>\r\n\r\nQ233. What principle does 1 Corinthians 14:29 establish about prophecy, according to the lesson?\r\nA. Prophetic claims must be accepted if two or three prophets agree.\r\nB. Prophetic claims must be weighed and tested rather than accepted blindly.\r\nC. Prophetic claims must be accepted if they are spoken in the church.\r\nD. Prophetic claims must be tested only if they conflict with the leaders.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the verse, \"let others weigh what is said,\" shows that all prophetic claims are to be tested.\r\nREF: MORMONS II — [08:17]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 14:29 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+14%3A29/>\r\n\r\nQ234. What does Hebrews 1:1-2 imply about later claims of authority, according to the lesson?\r\nA. God spoke by the prophets and will speak again through new prophets.\r\nB. God spoke by his Son, and the apostles then received equal authority to him.\r\nC. God has spoken in these last days by his Son, so no one after Jesus can claim new authority.\r\nD. God spoke by his Son, and the church may now add authority as needed.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says God spoke through prophets before but now through his Son, so there is no other authority after Jesus.\r\nREF: MORMONS II — [09:13]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 1:1–2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A1%E2%80%932/>\r\n\r\nQ235. How did William Miller arrive at his date for Christ's return, according to the lesson?\r\nA. He applied the 70 weeks of Daniel 9 using the principle of a week for a year.\r\nB. He applied the 1,000 years of Revelation 20 using the principle of a day for a year.\r\nC. He applied the 2,300 days of Daniel 8:14 using the principle of a day for a year from Ezekiel 4:6.\r\nD. He applied the 2,300 days of Daniel 8:14 using the dates of the Jewish feasts.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Miller used Daniel 8:14's 2,300 days and applied Ezekiel 4:6, \"each day of a year,\" to calculate the return.\r\nREF: MORMONS II — [10:59]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Daniel 8:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Daniel+8%3A14/>; Ezekiel 4:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ezekiel+4%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ236. What do Adventists teach about the investigative judgment, according to the lesson?\r\nA. Beginning at the cross, Jesus examines the records of all people to condemn the wicked.\r\nB. Beginning in 1844, Jesus examines believers' records to decide who gets eternal life.\r\nC. Beginning in 1844, Jesus returned to earth to judge believers and the wicked at once.\r\nD. Beginning after the Second Coming, Jesus examines the records of believers to give rewards.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Adventists teach Jesus began an investigative judgment in 1844, assessing believers' records to determine who receives eternal life, before the Second Coming.\r\nREF: MORMONS II — [14:50]\r\n\r\nQ237. What do Adventists believe about Sunday worship, according to the lesson?\r\nA. That it is part of the Antichrist's work to change the day of worship and deceive many.\r\nB. That it is acceptable, though the seventh day is a better day for worship.\r\nC. That it is required, because the Sabbath was moved to Sunday by Jesus.\r\nD. That it is acceptable only for Gentiles, but not for Jewish believers.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Adventists believe one of the Antichrist's actions will be to pervert the day of worship and deceive many into worshipping on Sunday.\r\nREF: MORMONS III — [00:17]\r\n\r\nQ238. According to the speaker, what is the real question in the Sabbath debate?\r\nA. Whether God rested on the seventh day, which Adventists deny.\r\nB. Whether the Sabbath is on Saturday, which Christians deny.\r\nC. Whether the Sabbath was part of creation, which the Bible denies.\r\nD. Whether Christians must keep the Mosaic Sabbath as Israel did.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He concedes the seventh-day Sabbath is biblical and says the question is whether Christians must observe the Mosaic commandment as Israel did.\r\nREF: MORMONS III — [01:17]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 2:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+2%3A2/>; Colossians 2:16–17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+2%3A16%E2%80%9317/>; Acts 15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+15/>\r\n\r\nQ239. What standard does the lesson give for evaluating prophecy such as Ellen White's visions?\r\nA. Its agreement with the church matters, since its leaders are the ones who recognize true prophecy.\r\nB. Its fulfillment matters, since a true prophet will always be proven right.\r\nC. Its agreement with the apostolic faith matters, even if an angel brings it.\r\nD. Its source matters, since a prophet who sees a vision must be sent by God.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says the source of prophecy does not make it true; its agreement with the apostolic faith is what matters, so Ellen White's visions are tested by Scripture.\r\nREF: MORMONS III — [05:16]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Galatians 1:8-9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+1%3A8-9/>; 1 John 4:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+4%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ240. How does Luke 23:43 answer the Adventist teaching of soul sleep, according to the lesson?\r\nA. Jesus told the thief he would sleep until the resurrection in paradise.\r\nB. Jesus told the thief he would be judged on the last day in paradise.\r\nC. Jesus told the thief he would be with him after a time of purification.\r\nD. Jesus told the thief he would be with him that day in paradise.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says Jesus said \"today you'll be with me in paradise,\" not thousands of years later, so death is not soul sleep.\r\nREF: MORMONS III — [06:56]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Luke 23:43 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+23%3A43/>\r\n\r\nQ241. How does the lesson use Matthew 25:46 against annihilationism?\r\nA. It contrasts \"eternal life\" with \"temporary punishment,\" which ends in destruction.\r\nB. It contrasts \"eternal punishment\" with \"eternal sleep,\" which is a state of rest.\r\nC. It contrasts \"eternal life\" with \"destruction of the body,\" which is not the soul.\r\nD. It contrasts \"eternal punishment\" with \"eternal life,\" which is not ceasing to exist.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says the verse's \"eternal punishment\" versus \"eternal life\" is not annihilationism or ceasing to exist.\r\nREF: MORMONS III — [07:48]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 25:46 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+25%3A46/>\r\n\r\nQ242. What is the first guideline given for evangelizing Mormons and Adventists?\r\nA. Separate the person from the system and speak the truth in love.\r\nB. Show them the failures of their founders before anything else.\r\nC. Show them the differences in their doctrine as quickly as possible.\r\nD. Show them that their church is not recognized by other Christians.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: He says to see the person within the system and cites Ephesians 4:15 to speak the truth in love, not to put anger against the doctrine on the person.\r\nREF: MORMONS III — [10:02]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 4:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+4%3A15/>\r\n\r\nQ243. Why does the lesson say you should create psychological safety when evangelizing?\r\nA. If people feel attacked or mocked they won't open up; your goal is to win their soul, not an argument.\r\nB. If people feel attacked, they will quickly leave, and there will be no future chance.\r\nC. If people feel safe, they will agree with you and give up their beliefs.\r\nD. If people feel safe, they will be convinced that you are always right.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says people who feel attacked, mocked or threatened won't open up, so create a safe environment where they feel heard and seen.\r\nREF: MORMONS III — [10:49]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Colossians 4:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+4%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ244. Why does the speaker say to focus on the gospel and not on debate?\r\nA. Debates will save them if you win, so prepare for them with great care.\r\nB. Debates are necessary for the gospel to be understood, so use them often.\r\nC. Debates are useful only for correcting people within the church.\r\nD. Debates won't save; the gospel will, so lead back to Jesus.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: He says debates won't save them but the gospel will, and you should always lead back to who Jesus is and what he accomplished.\r\nREF: MORMONS III — [11:45]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 2:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+2%3A2/>\r\n\r\nQ245. What did Nabeel Qureshi say was important to him in the person who reached him?\r\nA. He could sense that the person was an expert who could answer every question.\r\nB. He could sense that the person sincerely loved him, not just scored points.\r\nC. He could sense that the person had given up everything to share the gospel.\r\nD. He could sense that the person was from the same culture and background.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says he could sense the love, that the guy honestly and sincerely loved him and was not trying to score a punch.\r\nREF: MORMONS III — [14:18]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 13:1-3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+13%3A1-3/>\r\n\r\nQ246. What does the speaker say about the sincerity of Mormons and Adventists, and how should Christians respond?\r\nA. Sincerity makes a doctrine true enough, so Christians should accept it and be indifferent.\r\nB. Sincerity is a sign of deception, so Christians should be hostile and warn others.\r\nC. Sincerity doesn't make a doctrine true; be neither hostile nor indifferent.\r\nD. Sincerity is rare in these groups, so Christians should be cautious and avoid them.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: He says these movements arose from sincere convictions, but sincerity does not make a doctrine true, so the response should be neither hostile nor indifferent.\r\nREF: MORMONS III — [14:18]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 17:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ247. How does the speaker describe African traditional religion as a category?\r\nA. One uniform religion whose beliefs and practices are the same everywhere.\r\nB. A combination of indigenous beliefs and practices that are not necessarily the same.\r\nC. A religion limited to one ethnic group in West Africa.\r\nD. A set of practices with no characteristics shared across communities.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker describes African traditional religion as a combination of indigenous beliefs and practices that are not necessarily identical, while saying that some similar characteristics can be traced.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY IV — [11:07]\r\n\r\nQ248. Which name does the speaker give for the Supreme Being among the Maasai of Kenya?\r\nA. Enkai\r\nB. Chukwu\r\nC. Yame\r\nD. Olodumare\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: At this point in the transcript, the speaker names Enkai for the Maasai of Kenya.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY IV — [11:50]\r\n\r\nQ249. Which name does the transcript record the speaker using for the Supreme Being in Ghana?\r\nA. Enkai\r\nB. Chukwu\r\nC. Yame\r\nD. Olodumare\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The transcript records the speaker saying “Yame” when naming the Supreme Being in Ghana; this item follows the transcript wording.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY IV — [11:50]\r\n\r\nQ250. In the speakers’ description of Yoruba theology, how are the orishas related to worshippers?\r\nA. They are described as closer to people, who offer sacrifices to them and fear their wrath.\r\nB. They are described as identical to the Supreme Being and having no separate role.\r\nC. They are described as distant memories who have no present connection with people.\r\nD. They are described as figures whom worshippers neither approach nor fear.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says orishas are closer to the people who offer sacrifices to them and fear their wrath.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY V — [01:30]\r\n\r\nQ251. Why does the speaker say listeners should investigate where beliefs about lesser deities come from?\r\nA. Because acknowledging a Supreme Being does not, by itself, place every other god in the same category.\r\nB. Because the speaker says every named deity is simply another name for the Supreme Being.\r\nC. Because the speaker says African traditions have no belief in a Supreme Being.\r\nD. Because the speaker says traditions cannot change over time.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says that even if there has long been a belief in a Supreme God, people should investigate the origins of other beliefs rather than place every god in the same category.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY IV — [12:49]\r\n\r\nQ252. How does the speaker qualify the claim that Shango was a historical ruler?\r\nA. As a rumor or a claim attributed to some scholars.\r\nB. As a fact that the speaker says has been proven beyond dispute.\r\nC. As a claim that the transcript says was invented by missionaries.\r\nD. As a claim that the speaker says applies to every Yoruba deity.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker introduces the claim with the qualification “rumored, or at least according to some scholars,” rather than presenting it as certain.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY IV — [14:24]\r\n\r\nQ253. What caution does the speaker add after comparing recurring patterns among deities in different traditions?\r\nA. Similarities do not mean the deities are exactly the same; differences remain.\r\nB. Similarities prove every tradition uses the same names for one deity.\r\nC. Similarities show that all traditions have identical rituals.\r\nD. Similarities mean that the traditions have no historical development.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explicitly clarifies that the comparison is not to say the deities are exactly the same, and notes that differences remain.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY V — [01:30]\r\n\r\nQ254. Which practice does the speaker identify as a prominent feature of African traditional religion?\r\nA. Reverence for ancestors.\r\nB. Rejection of all moral codes.\r\nC. Worship of only one local deity in every community.\r\nD. Avoidance of all ritual practices.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker calls reverence for ancestors another prominent feature of African traditional religion.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY V — [01:30]\r\n\r\nQ255. According to the speaker’s description of ancestral beliefs, what happens after a person dies?\r\nA. The person is believed to cease to exist and have no further role.\r\nB. The person is believed to continue working as an ancestor.\r\nC. The person is believed to become the Supreme Being.\r\nD. The person is believed to return immediately as the same person.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker describes a belief that people do not simply cease to exist at death and says that ancestors continue their work.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY V — [02:33]\r\n\r\nQ256. Which two positions does the speaker describe as extremes in the discussion of whether African traditional deities exist?\r\nA. Overemphasizing their existence and denying that they exist at all.\r\nB. Treating them as ancestors and treating them as historical rulers.\r\nC. Worshipping them publicly and worshipping them privately.\r\nD. Comparing them with Greek deities and comparing them with Norse deities.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker says there are two extremes: some people overemphasize the deities’ existence, while others say they do not exist at all.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY V — [04:32]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Corinthians 10:19–21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+10%3A19%E2%80%9321/>\r\n\r\nQ257. In Speaker 2’s Dahomey example, which groups did he say were among those sacrificed during ceremonies honoring deceased kings and gods?\r\nA. Prisoners and enslaved people.\r\nB. Priests and elected officials.\r\nC. Traders and visiting musicians.\r\nD. Children selected from every family.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Speaker 2 refers to prisoners and enslaved people when describing the sacrifices he discusses. This question reports the speaker’s claim in the transcript.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY V — [09:56]\r\n\r\nQ258. What range of deaths did Speaker 2 cite for the Dahomey ceremonies he discussed?\r\nA. 5 to 40.\r\nB. 50 to 400.\r\nC. 500 to 4,000.\r\nD. 5,000 to 40,000.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Speaker 2 cites figures ranging from 500 to 4,000. This tests the number stated in the lesson transcript and is not an independent historical verification.\r\nREF: NEW AGE SPIRITUALITY V — [09:56]\n\nDECK: Discipleship Experience (Section 10) Apologetics\n\nQ1. Which pair of questions does the lesson associate with the biographical test?\nA. How many authors produced the document, and how many people witnessed the events described in it?\nB. How many manuscripts are available and whether their contents are the same or similar, and how long the interval is between the original and earliest available manuscript?\nC. Whether archaeology supports the document, and whether later communities accepted the document as authoritative?\nD. Whether the document contains prophecy, and whether the prophecy was fulfilled within the period it describes?\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The lesson assesses manuscript evidence by considering the number and similarity of manuscripts and the interval from the original to the earliest available copies.\nREF: IS THE BIBLE TRUE I — approx. 01:37–06:01\n\nQ2. What does the lesson mean by saying, \"A dead God cannot give eternal life\"?\nA. Eternal life depends on Jesus having conquered death rather than remaining under the power of death.\nB. Eternal life can only be received by those who physically witness Jesus' resurrection.\nC. Eternal life is possible only when believers are able to prove the resurrection through historical documents.\nD. Eternal life depends on believers becoming physically immortal before they can receive salvation.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The lesson connects Jesus’ resurrection with believers’ redemption and future resurrection.\nREF: DID JESUS RISE FROM THE DEAD II — 04:03–05:02\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Thessalonians 4:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+4%3A14/>\n\nQ3. What does the internal evidence test examine?\nA. Whether archaeological discoveries confirm the locations and events described within the Bible.\nB. Whether writings outside the Bible confirm the people, places and events described within the Bible.\nC. Whether the information within the Bible is coherent and free from known contradictions.\nD. Whether the Bible contains more surviving manuscripts than other ancient documents from the same period.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The lesson defines the internal evidence test as examining the Bible’s own testimony for coherence and known contradictions.\nREF: IS THE BIBLE TRUE I — 06:41–07:44\n\nQ4. Why does the lesson describe the resurrection as more than simply getting the facts of the past right?\nA. It establishes a guarantee concerning the future of those who believe in Jesus.\nB. It establishes that every historical detail concerning Jesus can now be independently verified.\nC. It establishes that believers will never experience suffering or death after becoming Christians.\nD. It establishes that every person who hears the gospel will automatically receive eternal life.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents Jesus’ resurrection as assurance that believers will rise as well.\nREF: DID JESUS RISE FROM THE DEAD II — 04:03–05:46\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Thessalonians 4:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+4%3A14/>; John 11:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+11%3A25/>\n\nQ5. Why does the lesson refer to Isaiah's writings about Jesus?\nA. Isaiah was written during Jesus' lifetime and records eyewitness testimony about His ministry.\nB. Isaiah was discovered as part of the earliest complete New Testament manuscript and confirms the Gospel accounts.\nC. Isaiah contains predictions about Jesus' life and death that were written over 700 years before Jesus was born and were later fulfilled.\nD. Isaiah proves that Matthew, Mark, Luke and John wrote their Gospel accounts together from the same source.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The lesson cites Isaiah’s predictions about Jesus’ life and death as prophecies written centuries before his birth and later fulfilled.\nREF: IS THE BIBLE TRUE I — 10:31–11:39\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Isaiah 53 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53/>\n\nQ6. Why does the lesson regard the fact that women were the first witnesses of the empty tomb as significant evidence?\nA. Women were considered the most reliable legal witnesses in the first-century Jewish world.\nB. Women were treated as highly respected public authorities whose testimony was preferred over that of men.\nC. Women were treated as second-class citizens in that cultural context, making their inclusion as first witnesses an unlikely choice if the story were fabricated to be convincing.\nD. Women were the only people permitted to enter tombs and were automatically considered reliable witnesses.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The lesson argues that reporting women as the first witnesses would have been an unlikely choice for a fabricated account.\nREF: DID JESUS RISE FROM THE DEAD IV — approx. 06:51–09:42\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 24:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A10/>\n\nQ7. Which statement best captures the idea that what appears evil or chaotic may not be ultimate?\nA. If an event eventually produces something good, the original event should no longer be regarded as evil.\nB. If God has a purpose, every event must have been directly caused by Him.\nC. A painful or chaotic reality can be genuinely difficult while still not representing the final outcome of God's purpose.\nD. Human beings should stop judging circumstances because only God can determine whether something is good.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says present evil or chaos can be real without being the final outcome of God’s purpose.\nREF: THEODICY II — 08:26–09:12\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 8:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A28/>\n\nQ8. Why does the lesson consider Jesus' teaching about Himself from Scripture significant?\nA. Because Jesus' use of Scripture proves that every surviving biblical manuscript has been preserved perfectly.\nB. Because Jesus wrote the Old Testament Himself and could therefore explain every part of it from personal authorship.\nC. Because Jesus' teaching about Scripture means that external evidence is no longer necessary for examining the Bible.\nD. Because Jesus was the person prophesied about and could teach about Himself from the Scriptures that spoke about Him.\nANSWER: D\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says Jesus could teach about himself from the Scriptures that prophesied about him.\nREF: IS THE BIBLE TRUE I — 10:31–11:39\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Isaiah 53 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53/>\n\nQ9. What distinction does Hebrews 9 make between Christ's sacrifice and the sacrifices offered by the Old Testament high priest?\nA. Christ entered the Most Holy Place repeatedly with His own blood, while the Old Testament high priest entered once a year with animal blood.\nB. Christ entered the earthly sanctuary once with His own blood, while the Old Testament high priest entered the heavenly sanctuary repeatedly with animal blood.\nC. Christ entered heaven once and for all by His own blood, while the Old Testament high priest repeatedly entered the Most Holy Place with the blood of animals.\nD. Christ entered the Most Holy Place every year with His own blood, while the Old Testament high priest offered animal blood only once for Israel.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The lesson contrasts the high priest’s repeated entry with animal blood and Christ’s once-for-all entry into heaven with his own blood.\nREF: DID JESUS RISE FROM THE DEAD II — 00:15–02:35\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+9/>\n\nQ10. A believer is suffering despite trusting God and wonders whether the suffering means Christ has failed to defeat evil. Which response best reflects the framework presented?\nA. The suffering proves that Christ's victory applies only after death and has no present relevance.\nB. The suffering shows that the believer has not yet exercised enough faith to appropriate Christ's victory.\nC. The suffering exists within the present reality of a fallen world, while Christ's victory guarantees that suffering and death will not have the final word.\nD. The suffering should be understood as God's direct method of teaching the believer whatever lesson they have not yet learned.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The lesson connects present suffering with creation’s corruption and presents Christ’s victory as assurance that death and suffering will not have the final word.\nREF: THEODICY IV — approx. 00:49–02:59; 14:42–18:23\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8/>; John 11:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+11%3A25/>; 2 Timothy 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+4/>; Revelation 21:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+21%3A4/>\n\nQ11. What does the lesson mean when it says that the different parties in the Day of Atonement represented one person, Jesus?\nA. It means that the high priest, the goats and the other elements were separate sacrifices that were eventually replaced by Jesus.\nB. It means that the high priest and the goats were historical figures who physically became Jesus during His ministry.\nC. It means that the different elements of the ceremony served as a type and shadow of what Jesus Christ would come to accomplish.\nD. It means that Jesus performed the Day of Atonement ceremony in the same form before beginning His earthly ministry.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents the Day of Atonement’s elements as a type and shadow of what Jesus would accomplish.\nREF: DID JESUS RISE FROM THE DEAD I — 12:06–13:42\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+9/>; Leviticus 16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Leviticus+16/>\n\nQ12. What does the lesson mean when it describes money as a \"spiritual stethoscope\"?\nA. The amount of money a person has reveals the level of spiritual maturity that person has attained.\nB. The way a person responds to money and giving can reveal the condition of that person's heart.\nC. The level of financial success a person experiences reveals the strength of that person's relationship with God.\nD. The amount of money a person gives determines whether God accepts that person's faith and worship.\nANSWER: B\nREF: Not found in the provided sources — N/A\n\nQ13. Why has the problem addressed by theodicy continued to generate questions among Christians and non-Christians?\nA. Because Scripture gives no examples of people questioning God's justice\nB. Because the existence of evil creates questions about how God's goodness, power and justice relate to human experience\nC. Because Christians have historically disagreed about whether God possesses absolute sovereignty\nD. Because the Bible presents evil as a mystery that human beings are forbidden to investigate\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The lesson connects the problem of evil with questions about God’s goodness, knowledge, power, and justice.\nREF: THEODICY I — approx. 01:18–03:28\n\nQ14. Why does the lesson argue that the Islamic substitution theory conflicts with the earliest historical testimony about Jesus?\nA. Because the theory appeared several hundred years after the Christian accounts and conflicts with both Christian and non-Christian testimony concerning His crucifixion.\nB. Because the theory appeared before the Christian accounts and therefore contradicts the eyewitnesses who recorded Jesus' crucifixion.\nC. Because the theory was developed by Roman authorities who were attempting to explain why Jesus' disciples continued preaching after His death.\nD. Because the theory depends entirely on the Gospel accounts while rejecting the historical sources outside the Christian tradition.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The lesson argues that the substitution theory came later and conflicts with earlier Christian and non-Christian testimony about Jesus’ crucifixion.\nREF: DID JESUS RISE FROM THE DEAD II — approx. 08:15–10:59\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 15:3-8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15%3A3-8/>\n\nQ15. Why does the lesson consider the question \"Is the Bible true?\" important to the Christian?\nA. Because the Bible contains more historical information than every other ancient document.\nB. Because the Bible is the oldest religious document available to Christians.\nC. Because the Bible is the standard for Christian belief, so questioning its origin and validity raises a question about the foundation of the faith.\nD. Because the Bible must be confirmed by archaeological discoveries before Christians can accept its teachings.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says the Bible is the standard for belief, so questions about its origin and validity affect Christian faith.\nREF: IS THE BIBLE TRUE I — 00:29–01:14\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Timothy 3:16-17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+3%3A16-17/>\n\nQ16. What confidence does the resurrection of Jesus give believers about their own future?\nA. It gives believers confidence that they will avoid physical death if they maintain their faith until the end.\nB. It gives believers confidence that they will rise again because Jesus Himself rose from the dead.\nC. It gives believers confidence that they will experience resurrection only if they have never struggled with doubt.\nD. It gives believers confidence that physical death has already ceased to exist for everyone who believes in Jesus.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents Jesus’ resurrection as assurance that believers will rise too.\nREF: DID JESUS RISE FROM THE DEAD II — 04:03–05:46\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 17:30 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17%3A30/>; 1 Thessalonians 4:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+4%3A14/>; John 11:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+11%3A25/>\n\nQ17. What does the lesson highlight about the different backgrounds of the Bible's authors?\nA. Their different backgrounds show that the books developed from several unrelated salvation stories.\nB. Their different backgrounds suggest that the books were written independently without a common theological direction.\nC. Their different backgrounds mean that the books reflect different understandings of salvation that cannot be brought together.\nD. Their different backgrounds show that, despite their differences in countries, classes and professions, their writings form a unified salvation story.\nANSWER: D\nEXPLANATION: The lesson highlights that authors from varied backgrounds contributed to a unified salvation story.\nREF: IS THE BIBLE TRUE I — 01:14\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Peter 1:20-21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Peter+1%3A20-21/>\n\nQ18. Why was the flood understood as an act of preservation as well as judgment?\nA. The corruption of humanity had become so extensive that intervention was necessary to preserve the human race\nB. Humanity had become incapable of receiving God's forgiveness and therefore needed to be completely destroyed\nC. God needed to demonstrate His authority before establishing His covenant with Noah\nD. The flood was primarily intended to punish those who had rejected Noah's preaching\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents the flood as an intervention intended to preserve the human race amid extensive evil.\nREF: THEODICY III — approx. 01:49–04:16\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+6/>\n\nQ19. What did the scapegoat represent in the Day of Atonement picture presented in the lesson?\nA. It represented the animal whose blood was brought behind the veil to make atonement for the high priest.\nB. It represented the one upon whom the sins and rebellious acts of the Israelites were placed before being sent into the wilderness.\nC. It represented the high priest who entered the Most Holy Place and returned to announce that the sacrifice had been accepted.\nD. It represented the people of Israel who remained outside the tabernacle while the high priest offered sacrifices for their sins.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says the scapegoat carried the Israelites’ sins away into the wilderness.\nREF: DID JESUS RISE FROM THE DEAD I — 11:18–12:55\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Leviticus 16:8–10, 20–22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Leviticus+16%3A8%E2%80%9310%2C+20%E2%80%9322/>\n\nQ20. Why does the lesson consider the time interval between an original document and the earliest surviving manuscript?\nA. Because the age of the surviving manuscript determines whether its original author was inspired by God.\nB. Because a longer interval between manuscripts automatically makes the original document historically unreliable.\nC. Because the date of the surviving manuscript determines whether the document's message is doctrinally correct.\nD. Because the interval helps us consider how close the surviving evidence is to the period in which the original was written.\nANSWER: D\nEXPLANATION: The lesson uses the interval to assess how close surviving manuscripts are to the original documents.\nREF: IS THE BIBLE TRUE I — 01:37–02:26\n\nQ21. What pattern of divine intervention is identified throughout Scripture?\nA. God intervenes whenever His people experience suffering for an extended period\nB. God intervenes primarily in ways that advance His overarching will and purpose\nC. God intervenes only when human beings have exhausted their own ability to solve a problem\nD. God intervenes whenever evil becomes visible enough for humanity to recognize it\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The lesson describes God’s interventions as advancing his overarching will and purpose.\nREF: THEODICY III — approx. 00:16–01:49\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+6/>\n\nQ22. What were the differences found in the approximately 5% that did not match in the Isaiah comparison?\nA. They involved major changes to the story and theology presented throughout the book of Isaiah.\nB. They involved spelling differences, omitted letters and misspelled words that did not change the core meaning.\nC. They involved entire sections of Isaiah that had been removed from the later manuscript tradition.\nD. They involved different accounts of the life, ministry and death of Jesus within the book of Isaiah.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The lesson characterizes the differences as spelling and copying errors that do not change the core meaning.\nREF: IS THE BIBLE TRUE I — 04:02–05:04\n\nQ23. What does Christ's resurrection change about the way death is understood?\nA. Death remains the final outcome of human existence but is no longer something Christians should fear\nB. Death is no longer the final word for those who belong to Christ because resurrection gives hope beyond physical death\nC. Death is removed from the experience of every believer who exercises sufficient faith\nD. Death becomes spiritually beneficial because every Christian is guaranteed immediate resurrection after physical death\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents resurrection as hope that death is not the end for believers.\nREF: DID JESUS RISE FROM THE DEAD II — 04:03–05:46\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 17:30 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17%3A30/>; 1 Thessalonians 4:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Thessalonians+4%3A14/>; John 11:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+11%3A25/>\n\nQ24. What does the lesson say Christianity would become without the resurrection?\nA. Christianity would become a less effective form of Christianity because its moral teachings would remain unchanged.\nB. Christianity would become a mere religion because the resurrection is what distinguishes the Christian faith from a system of moral teaching.\nC. Christianity would become a historical movement because the teachings of Jesus would still provide a complete path to eternal life.\nD. Christianity would become a different form of Judaism because the death of Jesus would still fulfil the purpose of the Christian faith.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says the risen Savior, rather than moral teaching alone, distinguishes Christianity.\nREF: DID JESUS RISE FROM THE DEAD II — 06:27–07:18\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 15:14–19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15%3A14%E2%80%9319/>\n\nQ25. What is the main difference between internal and external evidence in the lesson?\nA. Internal evidence examines the Bible's own testimony, while external evidence looks to material outside the Bible to see whether it confirms or denies that testimony.\nB. Internal evidence examines the Old Testament, while external evidence examines the New Testament and the events surrounding its development.\nC. Internal evidence examines archaeological discoveries, while external evidence examines the prophecies contained within the biblical text.\nD. Internal evidence examines Christian belief, while external evidence examines whether non-Christians accept the Bible's historical claims.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The lesson distinguishes evidence drawn from within the Bible from evidence outside it that may confirm or deny its testimony.\nREF: IS THE BIBLE TRUE I — 06:41; IS THE BIBLE TRUE II — 00:15–01:10\n\nQ26. What is the lesson's main historical objection to the Islamic substitution theory?\nA. The theory emerged several hundred years after Christianity and conflicts with earlier Christian and non-Christian testimony concerning Jesus' crucifixion.\nB. The theory emerged before the earliest Christian accounts and therefore represents a competing eyewitness tradition concerning Jesus' death.\nC. The theory was based primarily on Roman records that the Christian writers deliberately excluded from their accounts of Jesus.\nD. The theory agrees with the earliest historical testimony about Jesus but differs from later Christian interpretations of His death.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The lesson argues that the substitution theory came later and conflicts with earlier testimony about Jesus’ crucifixion.\nREF: DID JESUS RISE FROM THE DEAD II — approx. 08:15–10:59\n\nQ27. What is the central purpose of the concept of theodicy as presented?\nA. To demonstrate that every form of suffering has an immediately identifiable purpose\nB. To defend the goodness and justice of God in response to objections arising from evil\nC. To establish that evil exists independently of God's sovereignty\nD. To explain why human beings are morally responsible for every form of suffering\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents theodicy as addressing objections to God’s goodness and justice in light of evil.\nREF: THEODICY I — approx. 02:11–04:19\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+18/>; Exodus 33 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+33/>\n\nQ28. What is the basic claim of the ontological argument as explained in the lesson?\nA. God is the greatest possible being, and such a being cannot exist only in the imagination but must exist in reality.\nB. God is the first cause of the universe, and therefore everything that exists must ultimately depend on Him.\nC. God is the intelligent designer of nature, and the complexity of creation demonstrates that He exists.\nD. God is the source of objective morality, and therefore universal moral values demonstrate His existence.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents the argument that the greatest possible being cannot exist only in the imagination and must exist in reality.\nREF: IS THERE A GOD III — approx. 06:00–09:51\n\nQ29. Why does Christ's incarnation matter to the question of suffering?\nA. God entered human experience and personally experienced hunger, temptation, rejection, suffering and death\nB. God entered human experience primarily to demonstrate that suffering is necessary for spiritual maturity\nC. God entered human experience so that He could understand human beings despite previously being separated from them\nD. God entered human experience to demonstrate that divine power could completely eliminate suffering during earthly life\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The lesson says Christ experienced human pain and temptation, so God is not portrayed as detached from human suffering.\nREF: THEODICY IV — approx. 11:12–12:28\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 2:14–18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+2%3A14%E2%80%9318/>; Hebrews 4:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+4%3A15/>\n\nQ30. What is the broader purpose of studying apologetics according to the conclusion of the lesson?\nA. To equip Christians with enough intellectual arguments to win debates and demonstrate the superiority of Christianity over every opposing belief.\nB. To help Christians replace simple faith with intellectual certainty so that difficult questions about Christianity no longer need to be asked.\nC. To equip Christians to understand why they believe, engage their minds with their faith, defend it reasonably, and help others come to Christ.\nD. To prepare Christians to become academic scholars who can provide technical answers to every philosophical objection against Christianity.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The lesson presents apologetics as preparing Christians to reason about and defend their faith and to help others come to Christ.\nREF: IS THERE A GOD III — approx. 10:36–15:41\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Exodus 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+3/>; John 8:58 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+8%3A58/>; John 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1/>\n\nQ31. How does the lesson define \"apologetics\"?\nA. Apologetics is the religious discipline of defending religious doctrines through systematic argumentation and discourse, derived from the Greek word *apologia* meaning \"defense.\"\nB. Apologetics is the practice of repeatedly asking for God's forgiveness for one's doubts.\nC. Apologetics is the study of different religions to prove they all worship the same God.\nD. Apologetics is the process of apologizing to people of other faiths for historical Christian mistakes.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The speaker defines apologetics, noting it comes from the Greek word \"apologia\" meaning \"defense,\" and formally defines it as \"the religious discipline of defending religious doctrines through systematic argumentation and discourse\".\nREF: IS-THERE-A-GOD-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-c834ed76-9fb4.md (01:56-03:21)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+25/>; 1 Peter 3:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+3%3A15/>\n\nQ32. According to 1 Peter 3:15, as cited in the lesson, who is commanded to give a defense of the faith?\nA. Only ordained pastors and church leaders.\nB. Only academics and Bible scholars who debate constantly.\nC. Only those who are falsely accused in a court of law.\nD. Every believer should be ready to give a defense to everyone who asks a reason for their hope.\nANSWER: D\nEXPLANATION: The speaker quotes 1 Peter 3:15, stating it instructs believers to be ready to give a defense \"to everyone who asks you,\" emphasizing that this applies to everyone \"on the streets, in your office, at home,\" not just academics or scholars.\nREF: IS-THERE-A-GOD-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-c834ed76-9fb4.md (03:21-04:11)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Peter 3:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+3%3A15/>\n\nQ33. What is the core premise of the cosmological argument?\nA. The universe is eternal and has always existed in its current form.\nB. Whatever begins to exist has a cause, meaning the universe must have a first, uncaused cause that is timeless, changeless, and immaterial.\nC. The universe was created by a random combination of gases and matter out of absolute nothingness.\nD. The complexity of human DNA proves that the universe was designed by an intelligent being.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The cosmological argument states \"whatever begins to exist... has a cause.\" To avoid an infinite regression, there must be a \"first uncaused cause\" that is \"timeless,\" \"changeless,\" and \"immaterial,\" which points to God.\nREF: IS-THERE-A-GOD-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a563e42b-2b42.md (01:14-03:29)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Colossians 1:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+1%3A16/>\n\nQ34. How does the teleological argument use the concept of the \"Goldilocks zone\" to argue for the existence of God?\nA. It shows that Earth's perfect distance from the sun and specific atmospheric gases to sustain life demonstrate precise, intelligent design rather than random chance.\nB. It argues that the universe was created from nothing, like a fairy tale.\nC. It proves that human fingerprints and DNA evolved naturally over billions of years.\nD. It suggests that there are many other habitable planets just like Earth waiting to be discovered.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The teleological argument (intelligent design) uses the \"Goldilocks zone\" to show that Earth is perfectly positioned—not too hot, not too cold—with a specific combination of gases to support life, arguing that this order cannot result from a random \"big bang\".\nREF: IS-THERE-A-GOD-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a563e42b-2b42.md (10:36-11:42)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 1:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+1%3A20/>\n\nQ35. According to the lesson, how does the moral argument demonstrate the existence of God?\nA. It proves that morality is entirely subjective and depends solely on an individual's culture.\nB. It suggests that humans only behave morally because they fear punishment from human laws.\nC. It argues that the existence of objective, universal moral values (like knowing murder is wrong) requires a transcendent moral lawgiver.\nD. It claims that humans evolved moral behaviors simply to ensure the survival of the fittest.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The moral argument states that \"objective moral values and duties exist,\" and the reason humanity has a universal moral compass (knowing certain things are universally bad) is because that morality comes from a \"transcendent moral law giver\".\nREF: IS-THERE-A-GOD-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-32b1015e-f0f6.md (00:16-01:10)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 2:14-15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+2%3A14-15/>\n\nQ36. What is the fundamental conclusion of the ontological argument?\nA. God exists only as a comforting psychological construct in the human imagination.\nB. Because God is defined as the greatest possible being, and a being existing in reality is greater than one existing only in imagination, God must exist in reality.\nC. God's existence can only be proven through physical, archaeological evidence.\nD. God is a universal energy that permeates all living things, rather than a specific being.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The ontological argument defines God as the \"greatest possible being imaginable.\" Because a being that exists in reality is greater than one only in imagination, the conclusion is that if you can conceive of this greatest being, \"God must exist in reality\".\nREF: IS-THERE-A-GOD-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-32b1015e-f0f6.md (06:59-09:44)\n\nQ37. What point does Paul make in 1 Corinthians 15:12-14 regarding the resurrection?\nA. That the resurrection is a helpful but optional belief for mature Christians.\nB. That if Christ is not risen, Christian preaching is vain and faith is in vain, leaving believers still in their sins.\nC. That the resurrection only applies to the spirit, not the physical body.\nD. That Jesus only appeared to resurrect to inspire the disciples' moral teachings.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: Quoting 1 Corinthians 15:12-14, the speaker notes Paul's argument: \"if Christ be not risen, then our preaching is in vain and your faith is also in vain... you are still in your sins,\" showing the resurrection is the foundation of the faith.\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8fb9b97d-ffc1.md (03:05-03:50)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Corinthians 15:12–14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15%3A12%E2%80%9314/>\n\nQ38. In the Old Testament Day of Atonement (Leviticus 16), what did the \"scapegoat\" symbolize in relation to Christ?\nA. It represented the Jewish leaders who rejected Jesus.\nB. It represented the believers who would spread the gospel to the wilderness.\nC. It represented the sacrifice for the high priest's personal sins before he could enter the Holy of Holies.\nD. It prefigured Christ carrying the iniquities and rebellious acts of the people away into a solitary place.\nANSWER: D\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that on the Day of Atonement, the high priest confessed the iniquities of Israel over the live goat (the scapegoat), which carried those sins away into the wilderness. This served as a type and shadow representing Jesus taking away the sins of the world.\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8fb9b97d-ffc1.md (11:18-12:06)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Leviticus 16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Leviticus+16/>\n\nQ39. According to Hebrews 9, how is Christ’s sacrifice different from the Old Testament high priest’s sacrifice?\nA. Christ entered the holy place once and for all by His own blood, rather than entering yearly with the blood of animals.\nB. Christ required the blood of a perfect bull to atone for His human weaknesses before entering heaven.\nC. Christ did not offer blood, but instead offered moral teachings to secure redemption.\nD. Christ must return to heaven repeatedly to renew His sacrifice for new sins.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: Referencing Hebrews 9, the speaker explains that unlike the Old Testament high priest who entered yearly with animal blood, Christ \"entered the most holy place once and for all by his own blood, thus obtaining... eternal redemption\".\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a8a24fd0-8a8b.md (00:51-01:48)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+9/>\n\nQ40. How does the Islamic \"substitution theory\" explain the crucifixion of Jesus?\nA. It claims Jesus willingly substituted Himself for Barabbas on the cross.\nB. It claims Jesus was substituted with a sacrificial lamb at the last minute.\nC. It claims Jesus was crucified, but substituted His divine spirit for a human one before dying.\nD. It claims Jesus was not crucified at all; instead, someone else (often suggested as Judas) was made to resemble Him and was killed in His place.\nANSWER: D\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites Surah 4:159, explaining the Islamic substitution theory claims \"they did not kill him nor did they crucify him, but another was made to resemble him,\" with some interpretations suggesting Judas was crucified in His place.\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a8a24fd0-8a8b.md (08:15-10:03)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 15:3-4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15%3A3-4/>\n\nQ41. What does the \"Swoon Theory\" claim about the resurrection of Jesus?\nA. It claims Jesus was never nailed to the cross, but only tied to it.\nB. It claims Jesus lost consciousness and fainted on the cross, but later revived in the tomb and survived.\nC. It claims Jesus died, but the disciples hallucinated His resurrection because they swooned from grief.\nD. It claims the Roman soldiers swooned and fell asleep, allowing thieves to steal the body.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The Swoon theory (from Ahmadiyya Islam) claims \"Jesus was crucified, but he didn't actually die, that he merely swooned. He lost consciousness and he fainted... later he was revived in the tomb,\" arguing no resurrection miracle occurred.\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8a8d1adc-a011.md (04:08-05:06)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A1/>\n\nQ42. Why does the \"Conspiracy or Fraud Theory\" fail regarding the disciples' claims of the resurrection?\nA. The disciples were too uneducated to invent a complex lie.\nB. The Romans immediately found the stolen body, exposing the fraud.\nC. People do not willingly die for a lie they invented themselves, and the early apostles faced execution without recanting.\nD. The disciples were eventually paid by the Romans to confess they stole the body.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker refutes the conspiracy theory by noting that while people might die for a lie they were deceived into believing, they do not die for a lie they made up. The apostles \"went to their graves refusing to recant\" and faced execution for their claims, proving they genuinely believed they saw the resurrected Christ.\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8a8d1adc-a011.md (08:32-09:29)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+1%3A3/>; 1 Corinthians 15:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15%3A6/>\n\nQ43. How does the response of the Jewish authorities in Matthew 28:12 support the reality of the empty tomb?\nA. They admitted the tomb was empty by bribing the guards to say the disciples stole the body, rather than producing the body.\nB. They immediately launched a massive Roman investigation to find the stolen body.\nC. They confessed publicly that Jesus had resurrected and converted to Christianity.\nD. They claimed the Romans had moved the body to a different graveyard.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The speaker points out that instead of producing the body to crush Christianity, the authorities held a meeting and \"decided to give the soldiers a large bribe\" to claim \"Jesus disciples came during the night... and they stole his body.\" This serves as a \"crucial admission\" that the tomb was indeed empty.\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-ec3ff305-758b.md (03:13-04:06)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Matthew 28:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+28%3A12/>\n\nQ44. Why is it highly significant that the Gospels record women as the first eyewitnesses of the empty tomb?\nA. It fulfilled a specific Old Testament prophecy about women discovering the Messiah.\nB. It proved that the Roman guards were afraid of women.\nC. In the first-century Jewish context, the testimony of women was considered invalid in court, meaning writers fabricating a story would never have used women as their primary witnesses.\nD. The disciples were busy hiding in Galilee and sent the women to check the tomb.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that due to \"widespread misogyny\" in the ancient world, women's testimony was often considered invalid (as noted by Josephus and the Talmud). Therefore, \"the last detail that a first century writer who is trying to fabricate a convincing story would have invented\" is having women as the first witnesses, suggesting it was recorded simply because \"it actually happened\".\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-ec3ff305-758b.md (06:51-09:42)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 24:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A10/>\n\nQ45. How does the conversion of Jesus’s brothers provide evidence for the resurrection?\nA. They were bribed by the Roman government to say Jesus had risen.\nB. They did not believe in Jesus during His ministry, but became devoted followers and leaders (like James and Jude) only after encountering the resurrected Christ.\nC. They were the ones who secretly stole His body and later confessed to the early church.\nD. They always believed in Him and used their family status to gain political power in Jerusalem.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that before the resurrection, Jesus's brothers did not believe in Him (John 7:5). However, after the resurrection, James calls himself a \"servant of Jesus,\" and Jude also wrote an epistle, showing that encountering the resurrected Christ transformed His skeptical siblings into believers.\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-V-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-cd747752-7160.md (01:42-03:21)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 7:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+7%3A5/> | Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 15:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15%3A7/>\n\nQ46. How did Peter use the tomb of David in Acts 2 to argue for the resurrection of Jesus?\nA. He claimed that David had also resurrected and ascended to heaven alongside Jesus.\nB. He noted that David's tomb was still sealed and his body saw decay, proving David's prophecy about the \"Holy One\" not seeing corruption was actually about the resurrected Christ.\nC. He argued that Jesus was buried in David's exact tomb, which was found empty.\nD. He stated that David's bones were stolen by the Romans, just like Jesus's body.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: According to Acts 2, Peter points out that \"David's sepulcher is with us... David died and was buried.\" Because David's body saw decay, his prophecy in Psalm 16 that God would not \"let your Holy One see corruption\" was actually foreseeing the resurrection of the Messiah, Jesus.\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-V-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-cd747752-7160.md (08:02-09:33)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Psalm 16:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+16%3A10/>; Acts 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+2/>\n\nQ47. What does the \"bibliographical test\" seek to determine about the Bible?\nA. Whether the Bible contains any contradictions with modern science.\nB. How many manuscript copies are available and the time interval between the original document and the earliest available copies, to prove it was properly copied.\nC. Whether the original authors of the Bible were mathematically literate.\nD. How many different languages the Bible has been translated into over the last century.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that the bibliographical test tries to answer \"how many manuscripts are available... and how short is the interval between the date of the original documents and... the earliest available manuscripts\" to establish if the Bible was properly copied.\nREF: IS-THE-BIBLE-TRUE-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-2-mp4-4de941bf-ab47.md (02:26-03:09)\n\nQ48. How does the lesson explain the apparent discrepancy between Matthew and Acts regarding the death of Judas Iscariot?\nA. Matthew and Acts were describing two completely different people named Judas.\nB. Matthew recorded that Judas hung himself, and Acts recorded that he fell headlong; research shows he hung himself near a cliff, and later fell, making both accounts complementary angles of the same event.\nC. The book of Acts was corrupted by early translators who hated Judas.\nD. Matthew's account was a metaphor, while Acts provided the literal historical truth.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The lesson addresses the supposed contradiction by explaining that they are stories from different angles: Judas hung himself (Matthew), and because the location was beside a cliff, the rope or branch eventually gave way, causing him to fall headlong and burst open (Acts).\nREF: IS-THE-BIBLE-TRUE-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-2-mp4-4de941bf-ab47.md (09:27-10:31)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Isaiah 53 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+53/>\n\nQ49. How do the historical writings of Tacitus support the Biblical account?\nA. Tacitus wrote a detailed biography of the Apostle Paul's missionary journeys.\nB. Tacitus claimed that Jesus was a mythical figure invented by the Jews.\nC. Tacitus confirmed that a person named Christus was crucified by Pontius Pilate during the reign of Emperor Tiberius.\nD. Tacitus documented the exact location of the original Ten Commandments.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that Tacitus's writings \"help us understand that a person by the name of Christus, that's Christ, was crucified by Pontius Pilate during the reign of emperor Tiberius,\" which corroborates the Gospels' account.\nREF: IS-THE-BIBLE-TRUE-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-2-mp4-8439008d-8508.md (01:10-02:15)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Kings 20:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Kings+20%3A20/>; 2 Chronicles 32:30 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Chronicles+32%3A30/>; Judges 5:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Judges+5%3A19/>; 1 Kings 9:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Kings+9%3A15/>\n\nQ50. What is the significance of the mosaic found in Megiddo?\nA. It provides a detailed map of David's kingdom.\nB. It contains an inscription reading \"God, Christ Jesus,\" proving that early Christians worshipped Jesus as God long before the Council of Nicaea.\nC. It is the only surviving physical portrait of what Jesus looked like.\nD. It proves that the Israelites worshipped multiple gods during the time of the Judges.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that a mosaic was found in Megiddo with the inscription \"God, Christ Jesus.\" Because this mosaic predates the Council of Nicaea, it refutes the claim that Jesus's deity was invented later, showing \"that Jesus was truly believed to be God by the early Christians\".\nREF: IS-THE-BIBLE-TRUE-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-2-mp4-8439008d-8508.md (05:08-06:02)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 5:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+5%3A1/>; Judges 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Judges+5/>; 1 Kings 9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Kings+9/>\n\nQ51. How is the term \"theodicy\" defined in the lesson?\nA. The study of how natural disasters are formed.\nB. A Greek term meaning the justification of God against human objections concerning evil.\nC. The belief that God does not exist because evil exists.\nD. The theological study of why humans commit moral evil.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that \"Theodicy is a Greek word from two words, Theo and dc. Theo meaning God. DC meaning just so we are trying to justify... God against human objections\".\nREF: THEODICY-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-2-mp4-54713388-2eb8.md (03:24-03:28)\n\nQ52. According to the lesson, why does God allow \"moral evil\" to occur rather than stopping it immediately?\nA. God is not powerful enough to stop human beings from making bad choices.\nB. God uses moral evil to test how much humans truly love Him before rewarding them.\nC. God granted humans free will to create a genuine family marked by love, which means humans also have the capacity to choose evil.\nD. God does not notice moral evil because He is too transcendent to intervene in human matters.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that moral evil stems from \"the concept of free will.\" God granted humans the \"capability to choose\" because He \"wanted to create an extension of heaven... to build a family... marked by love.\" Without free will, love would be \"robotic,\" but this freedom also means creatures who can choose good can also choose evil.\nREF: THEODICY-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-2-mp4-54713388-2eb8.md (07:08-08:39)\n\nQ53. How does the lesson explain God's perspective on human suffering compared to a human's perspective?\nA. Humans have a perfect understanding of the past, while God only knows the future.\nB. Humans interpret events myopically within the constraints of time and the present, whereas God dwells outside of time and has a holistic, \"bird's eye view\" of all possible permutations.\nC. God sees suffering but chooses to ignore it until humans prove they have enough faith to overcome it.\nD. Humans and God have the exact same perspective on suffering, but God has more power to stop it.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker states that human viewpoints are \"pretty myopic\" because \"we're only ever entrenched in the present.\" In contrast, the Creator is \"not restricted to time, space or matter,\" giving Him a \"bird's eye view\" and a \"holistic\" counsel that sees all outcomes.\nREF: THEODICY-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-345e7215-afe4.md (01:12-01:47)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Isaiah 55:8-9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+55%3A8-9/>\n\nQ54. What broader divine purpose was accomplished by Joseph's prolonged suffering and eventual rise to power in Egypt?\nA. God used Joseph's suffering primarily to punish his brothers for their jealousy.\nB. Joseph's suffering was an accident that God later decided to fix by making him a ruler.\nC. God allowed Joseph's suffering to preserve Judah's lineage, ultimately preserving the line of Jesus Christ for the salvation of humanity.\nD. Joseph's suffering was a test to see if he would worship the Egyptian gods.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The lesson explains that during Joseph's 12-year trial, God had a much bigger picture in mind. \"God actually did not even have Joseph majorly in mind. He had Judah in mind... the lion of the tribe of Judah... Jesus had to be preserved because we had to be saved\".\nREF: THEODICY-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-345e7215-afe4.md (05:15-05:58)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 50:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+50%3A20/>\n\nQ55. According to the lesson, what was God's primary motivation for sending the global flood in Genesis 6?\nA. He was angry that humans stopped offering animal sacrifices to Him.\nB. He needed to wash away the sinful cities of Sodom and Gomorrah.\nC. He wanted to demonstrate His power over the Canaanite storm gods.\nD. He intervened to destroy the giants (Nephilim) and preserve a pure human race, as fallen angels had contaminated humanity.\nANSWER: D\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that fallen angels (\"sons of God\") slept with human women, producing giants. If God had not sent the flood, \"the true humans will be completely wiped out.\" God sent the flood \"to protect human beings... so that the human race will be saved\" and a pure human lineage could bring forth the Savior.\nREF: THEODICY-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8b09d75d-6ba0.md (01:49-04:43)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+6/>\n\nQ56. Why does the speaker bring up the Canaanite worship of Molech when discussing God's judgment?\nA. To show that the Canaanites possessed advanced metalworking skills that the Israelites needed.\nB. To demonstrate the extreme moral depravity and evil—such as sacrificing children on burning hot iron—that necessitated God's intervention and judgment.\nC. To prove that the Canaanites were secretly worshipping the true God under a different name.\nD. To argue that God's judgments in the Old Testament were overly harsh and unjustified.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker describes how the Canaanites sacrificed children to the god Molech, placing them \"literally on hot burning iron\" and drumming so they wouldn't hear their cries. This extreme evil, along with other practices, illustrates why God commanded their destruction to prevent the Israelites from being corrupted.\nREF: THEODICY-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8b09d75d-6ba0.md (09:00-11:22)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Deuteronomy 12:31 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+12%3A31/>\n\nQ57. Based on Romans 8, how does the lesson explain the origin of \"natural evil\" such as diseases and natural disasters?\nA. God created the earth with natural disasters to routinely test human survival skills.\nB. The devil created natural disasters before Adam and Eve were ever placed in the Garden.\nC. Natural evil is a consequence of Adam's sin and the fall, which brought death and corruption not only to humans but subjected all of creation to futility and decay.\nD. Natural disasters only occur in regions where people do not believe in God.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker references Romans 8, explaining that creation was \"subject to futility\" and is \"in bondage to corruption.\" This occurred when Adam sinned, causing death to enter the world. As a result, \"the world too began to die,\" meaning natural evil (like sickness and disasters) is a direct result of the fall, not God's original design.\nREF: THEODICY-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-3877d1c1-564b.md (00:49-04:42)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+5/>\n\nQ58. In Luke 13, how did Jesus diagnose the condition of the woman who had been bent over for 18 years?\nA. He attributed her condition solely to a genetic mutation passed down from her parents.\nB. He stated that God had bent her over to teach her a lesson in humility.\nC. He identified the devil as the agent, saying that Satan had bound her for 18 years.\nD. He explained that her illness was a natural consequence of the fallen environment.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker points out that Jesus diagnosed the woman's condition not as a natural illness or moral failure, but specifically named the devil, saying, \"ought not this woman, a daughter of Abraham whom Satan has bound for 18 years, be loosed\".\nREF: THEODICY-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-3877d1c1-564b.md (05:57-06:44)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Luke 13:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+13%3A16/>\n\nQ59. According to the lesson, what are the three major schools of thought regarding the existence of God?\nA. Atheism, Agnosticism, and Theism.\nB. Polytheism, Monotheism, and Pantheism.\nC. Gnosticism, Docetism, and Theism.\nD. Apologetics, Theology, and Philosophy.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The speaker outlines three schools of thought: Atheism (rejection of belief in God), Agnosticism (the view that the existence of God cannot be known), and Theism (belief that God exists).\nREF: IS-THERE-A-GOD-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-c834ed76-9fb4.md (07:44-08:48)\n\nQ60. How does Paul’s experience in Acts 17 demonstrate that Christian faith is reasonable?\nA. He performed a series of physical miracles to force the crowds to believe without explanation.\nB. He stood in the Areopagus and logically drew examples from their culture and poets to point them to the one true God.\nC. He refused to answer their questions and told them to simply have blind faith.\nD. He apologized for his beliefs and promised not to preach publicly anymore.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The lesson highlights Paul in Acts 17 as an example of apologetics, noting he \"stood there drawing examples from their culture, quoting from their poets to defend the Christian faith... applying what he saw in culture, but using it to point their heart... back to the one truth\".\nREF: IS-THERE-A-GOD-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-c834ed76-9fb4.md (05:29-05:56)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17/>\n\nQ61. How does the lesson address the \"Big Bang\" theory in relation to the cosmological argument?\nA. It fully accepts the Big Bang as the first uncaused cause that needs no explanation.\nB. It argues that a Big Bang naturally produces order, such as a beautiful garden.\nC. It questions where the elements that \"banged\" together originally came from, arguing they still require a first uncaused cause.\nD. It states that the Big Bang is entirely a myth and never happened in any capacity.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker addresses the Big Bang by asking, \"the things that bang together, where did they, where did they come from?... You must always be able to trace it back to a source,\" reaffirming the need for an uncaused first cause.\nREF: IS-THERE-A-GOD-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a563e42b-2b42.md (01:14-01:48)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+1%3A1/>\n\nQ62. How does the speaker counter the quantum physics argument that \"something can come out of nothing\"?\nA. By proving that scientists fabricated the theory of quantum physics.\nB. By clarifying that the \"nothing\" referred to in quantum physics is actually multiple gases and molecules working together, not \"absolute nothingness.\"\nC. By showing that quantum physics only applies to spiritual realities, not physical matter.\nD. By agreeing that something can come from absolute nothingness, but only once.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that in quantum physics, what is called \"nothing\" or \"emptiness\" is \"actually multiple gases, multiple molecules working together... it is not absolute nothingness\".\nREF: IS-THERE-A-GOD-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a563e42b-2b42.md (05:47-06:44)\n\nQ63. How does the concept of *Imago Dei* (made in the image of God) relate to the moral argument?\nA. It provides the fundamental basis for human rights and the abolitionist movement, because humans are not mere puppets but carry God's image and inherent value.\nB. It proves that all humans physically look exactly like God.\nC. It suggests that humans are allowed to decide for themselves what is morally right or wrong.\nD. It indicates that humans have the right to evolve and dominate weaker species.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that because every human being is made in the image of God (*Imago Dei*), we cannot harm others. This belief is why \"the abolitionist movement was able to thrive\" and why we recognize human rights and atrocities.\nREF: IS-THERE-A-GOD-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-32b1015e-f0f6.md (04:56-06:00)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 1:27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+1%3A27/>\n\nQ64. In the debate mentioned between Douglas Wilson and Christopher Hitchens, what flaw was pointed out regarding the atheist worldview?\nA. Atheists rely too heavily on the Old Testament for their moral standards.\nB. Atheists cannot physically see God, which makes their science invalid.\nC. If there is no God and only evolutionary \"survival of the fittest,\" an atheist has no objective basis to condemn religious violence or champion human rights.\nD. Atheists believe in the teleological argument but reject the cosmological argument.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: Douglas Wilson pointed out that Hitchens was making \"confident moral pronouncements... championing human rights,\" but asked \"On what basis?\" because if there is no God, and evolution is just \"survival of the fittest,\" there is nothing objectively making it wrong to kill the weak.\nREF: IS-THERE-A-GOD-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-32b1015e-f0f6.md (04:25-04:50)\n\nQ65. Why does the speaker emphasize that Jesus’s humanity is an integral part of the Christian faith?\nA. Because a divine God cannot perform miracles on Earth.\nB. Because it ensures we have a High Priest who can relate to our infirmities and struggles, such as hunger during fasting.\nC. Because it proves that Jesus was not actually God, but only a great moral teacher.\nD. Because the Roman Empire only executed human citizens, not divine beings.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: Using the story of his daughter fasting, the speaker notes that explaining Jesus's humanity showed her that \"we don't have a high priest that cannot be taught by the fingers of our infirmity\" and that Jesus can truly relate to us.\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8fb9b97d-ffc1.md (01:44-02:15)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 4:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+4%3A15/>\n\nQ66. What mystery of godliness is declared in 1 Timothy 3:16, as cited in the lesson?\nA. That God created the heavens and the earth in six days.\nB. That God destroyed the wicked with a flood.\nC. That God was manifest in the flesh, justified in the Spirit, seen of angels, preached unto the Gentiles, and received up into glory.\nD. That God requires animal sacrifices to forgive sins.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker quotes 1 Timothy 3:16 directly: \"without controversy, great is the mystery of Godliness... that God was manifest in the flesh... justified in the spirit sin of angels, preached unto Gentiles, believed on in the world, received up into glory\".\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8fb9b97d-ffc1.md (04:33)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Timothy 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+3%3A16/>\n\nQ67. How does the lesson apply John 1:29 (\"Behold the Lamb of God\") to the Old Testament imagery discussed?\nA. It proves that Jesus only preached to agricultural communities.\nB. It connects Jesus directly to the animal sacrifices and the scapegoat that took away the sins of the people.\nC. It shows that John the Baptist did not believe in the resurrection.\nD. It demonstrates that Jesus was not human, but an angelic being.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: After detailing the Old Testament Day of Atonement sacrifices (the sin offering and the scapegoat), the speaker notes, \"when John says, behold the Lamb of God who takes away the sins of the world. You see, it's making more sense now\".\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8fb9b97d-ffc1.md (11:18-12:06)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 1:29 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A29/>\n\nQ68. According to Acts 17:30 (cited in the lesson), how has God given assurance to all people that He will judge the world in righteousness?\nA. By writing the Ten Commandments on stone tablets.\nB. By sending a massive flood in the days of Noah.\nC. By allowing the destruction of the Jerusalem temple.\nD. By raising the appointed man (Jesus) from the dead.\nANSWER: D\nEXPLANATION: The speaker quotes Paul in Acts 17:30, which states that God has fixed a day to judge the world by a man He appointed, \"and of this he has given assurance to all by raising him from the dead\".\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a8a24fd0-8a8b.md (05:02)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 17:30 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+17%3A30/>\n\nQ69. What is \"Docetism,\" which is closely related to the Gnostic illusion of separation theory?\nA. The belief that Jesus was just a normal human who became a god after He died.\nB. The belief that Jesus only appeared to have a physical body and was not really human.\nC. The belief that Jesus was actually the Archangel Michael in disguise.\nD. The belief that the physical world is good, but the spiritual world is evil.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that Gnosticism is closely related to Docetism, \"which meant that Jesus only appeared to have a physical body, that he wasn't really human, he only appeared to have a physical body\".\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8a8d1adc-a011.md (01:10)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: John 1:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A14/>\n\nQ70. How does the timeline of Paul's letter in 1 Corinthians 15 refute the \"Legend Theory\"?\nA. It was written 20 to 24 years after the resurrection, proving that the belief was reported almost immediately and was not a folk tale developed gradually over centuries.\nB. It was written 400 years after Jesus died, proving the legend took a long time to form.\nC. It was written before Jesus was even born, making it a prophetic text rather than a historical one.\nD. It proves that Paul invented the story himself to gain political power in Rome.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The \"legend theory\" claims the resurrection was a gradually built folk tale. The speaker counters this by pointing out that 1 Corinthians 15 was written \"maybe 20 to 24 years after the resurrection... it was a belief that almost immediately was already reported\".\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8a8d1adc-a011.md (06:57-07:49)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Corinthians 15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15/>\n\nQ71. What specific physical details made the \"Roman Scourge\" a lethal punishment before the crucifixion even began?\nA. The whip was made of soft leather to slowly exhaust the victim without breaking the skin.\nB. The whip had iron balls, sharp steel bones, and nails tied into it, causing severe contusions and lacerations.\nC. The victim was forced to drink poisonous water during the scourging.\nD. The victim was repeatedly thrown into a freezing river.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker describes the brutal Roman scourge as a whip that \"had iron balls tied a few inches from the ends... with sharp steel bones and Nails, it could cause bruising, contusions, cuts into the skin\".\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8a8d1adc-a011.md (11:24-12:23)\n\nQ72. What was the primary medical cause of death for someone undergoing crucifixion, according to the lesson?\nA. Bleeding to death from the nail wounds in the hands and feet.\nB. A heart attack caused by extreme fear and psychological trauma.\nC. Suffocation/asphyxiation, as the weight on the arms and ribs made breathing increasingly difficult.\nD. Starvation and dehydration over the course of several weeks.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that once the legs gave out, weight transferred to the arms and rib cage, meaning \"the person will have trouble breathing\" and \"after a while, they would suffocate,\" with lack of oxygen damaging tissues and impairing the heart.\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-ec3ff305-758b.md (00:15-01:12)\n\nQ73. How is the Apostle Paul's conversion used as evidence for the reality of the resurrection?\nA. Paul was one of the Roman guards at the tomb who witnessed the angels.\nB. Paul was a former terrorist and enemy of the church who drastically changed his life, willing to die for the gospel after encountering the risen Christ.\nC. Paul was Jesus's biological brother who previously doubted Him.\nD. Paul was a Greek philosopher who was convinced by the cosmological argument alone.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker highlights that Paul was \"an enemy of the Church\" who went from \"writing letters to get permission for followers of Jesus to be killed\" to being willing to be \"killed for the gospel\" after encountering the resurrected Jesus.\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-V-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-cd747752-7160.md (03:21-04:23)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 9:1–9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+9%3A1%E2%80%939/>; 1 Corinthians 15:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15%3A8/>\n\nQ74. In John 12:23-24, how did Jesus prophetically describe the necessity of His death and resurrection?\nA. He compared it to a shepherd losing a sheep and finding it three days later.\nB. He said He must be swallowed by a great fish for three days, like Jonah.\nC. He stated that unless a kernel of wheat falls to the ground and dies, it remains a single seed, but if it dies, it produces many seeds.\nD. He compared His body to the temple that would be destroyed and rebuilt in forty years.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites John 12:23, where Jesus explains His impending death and resurrection by saying, \"except a kernel of which falls to the ground and dies, it remains a single seed. But if it dies, it produces many seeds\".\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-V-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-cd747752-7160.md (07:12)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 12:23–24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+12%3A23%E2%80%9324/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 24:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A25/>; John 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+2/>\n\nQ75. How does the lesson define the origin and composition of the Bible?\nA. It is a single book written by one author in Latin over a period of 50 years.\nB. It is a collection of books derived from the Latin word *biblia*, written by over 40 authors across 2000 years, forming a unified salvation story.\nC. It is an exclusively Greek document compiled by the Council of Nicaea.\nD. It is a set of historical archives maintained entirely by Roman historians.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: Pastor Dolapo defines the Bible: \"from the Latin word tabiblia, and it means a collection of books... written by over 40 authors. And within the space of 2000 years... their writings still form a unified salvation story\".\nREF: IS-THE-BIBLE-TRUE-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-2-mp4-4de941bf-ab47.md (01:14-01:37)\n\nQ76. What was the significance of the Book of Isaiah scroll found among the Dead Sea Scrolls?\nA. It proved that Isaiah had originally predicted the rise of the Roman Empire.\nB. It contained completely different theological teachings compared to modern Bibles.\nC. It predated the previously oldest available manuscript by 1100 years yet maintained 95% accuracy, proving the reliable transmission of the text.\nD. It was the only scroll in the cave written in perfect Greek.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The lesson notes that the Dead Sea Scroll of Isaiah (dating to 75 BC) was compared with the previously earliest manuscript (which was 1100 years later), and they discovered \"it maintains 95% accuracy,\" with the 5% being mere spelling differences.\nREF: IS-THE-BIBLE-TRUE-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-2-mp4-4de941bf-ab47.md (04:02-05:04)\n\nQ77. What information does the Babylonian Talmud provide regarding Jesus that supports the Biblical account?\nA. It confirms that He travelled to India during His teenage years.\nB. It details the execution of a man named Yeshua, suggesting he was hung on the eve of the Passover.\nC. It states that He successfully led a Jewish rebellion against the Romans.\nD. It claims He was never crucified but lived to an old age in Egypt.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker mentions that the Babylonian Talmud (Jewish rabbinic writing) helps us know about \"the execution of a man called Yeshua... and suggests that he was hung on the eve of the Passover,\" which aligns with the New Testament.\nREF: IS-THE-BIBLE-TRUE-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-2-mp4-8439008d-8508.md (01:10-02:15)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Kings 20:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Kings+20%3A20/>; 2 Chronicles 32:30 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Chronicles+32%3A30/>; Judges 5:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Judges+5%3A19/>; 1 Kings 9:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Kings+9%3A15/>\n\nQ78. What Old Testament archaeological evidence is mentioned that validates the events recorded during King Hezekiah's reign?\nA. The discovery of Goliath's armor in the valley of Elah.\nB. The preservation of Noah's Ark on Mount Ararat.\nC. The discovery of Hezekiah's tunnel, built during an Assyrian siege to protect Jerusalem's water supply.\nD. The ruins of the Tower of Babel in modern-day Iraq.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker points to Hezekiah's tunnel (2 Kings 20:20), explaining that during an Assyrian siege against Jerusalem, \"King Hezekiah built a tunnel to protect the water supply in Jerusalem. That exact tunnel... still exists today\".\nREF: IS-THE-BIBLE-TRUE-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-2-mp4-8439008d-8508.md (04:15)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 2 Kings 20:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Kings+20%3A20/>; 2 Chronicles 32:30 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Chronicles+32%3A30/>\n\nQ79. How does Abraham’s intercession in Genesis 18 demonstrate that God's character is fundamentally good and just?\nA. Abraham reminded God of a strict legal contract they had signed.\nB. Abraham challenged God on His justice, knowing His reputation, by asking, \"shall the judge of the world not do what is right?\"\nC. Abraham rebuked God for causing the flood.\nD. Abraham proved that God had no right to judge the people of Sodom.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The lesson highlights that people in the Bible have challenged God based on His justice. Abraham, when negotiating for Sodom and Gomorrah, relied on God's integrity, asking, \"shall the judge of the world not do what is right?\".\nREF: THEODICY-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-2-mp4-54713388-2eb8.md (04:19-04:24)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+18/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Exodus 33 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+33/>\n\nQ80. What parallel does the lesson draw between Abraham’s negotiation and Moses's actions in Exodus 32/33?\nA. Both men requested that God completely destroy the nations around Israel.\nB. Both men used their knowledge of God's goodness to intercede for rebellious people when God expressed anger.\nC. Both men were punished by God for asking too many questions.\nD. Both men wrote lengthy philosophical treatises on theodicy.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: Similar to Abraham, when the Israelites were \"stiff necked\" and built the golden calf, Moses interceded on their behalf. The speaker notes Moses told God, \"I know you. You're a good guy... You are not wicked,\" appealing to God's inherent goodness.\nREF: THEODICY-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-2-mp4-54713388-2eb8.md (04:35-05:14)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Exodus 32:1–14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Exodus+32%3A1%E2%80%9314/>\n\nQ81. How does the lesson contextualize Romans 8:28 (\"all things work together for good\") in light of suffering?\nA. It means that every bad thing that happens is actually good and shouldn't cause pain.\nB. It means that God promises believers will eventually become wealthy in this lifetime to make up for their pain.\nC. It clarifies that while not all things are inherently good, an all-knowing, sovereign God can work all outcomes toward His ultimate good for those who love Him.\nD. It suggests that suffering is an illusion that believers must ignore through positive confession.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker emphasizes that \"the verse did not say all things are good\" or that \"all suffering is immediately understandable.\" Rather, because God is sovereign, all-knowing, and all-good, \"he can work all those outcomes for his ultimate good\".\nREF: THEODICY-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-345e7215-afe4.md (11:24)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 8:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A28/>\n\nQ82. How does the speaker use the analogy of a \"building site\" to explain God's plan amidst perceived chaos?\nA. A building site is always clean and orderly, just as God's plans are always clearly understood by humans.\nB. A building site is chaotic with noise, dust, and incongruous materials, showing that current disorder is not the absence of design, but the presence of an unfinished design.\nC. A building site frequently collapses, representing how human free will destroys God's plans.\nD. A building site proves that humans must labor in sweat and pain to achieve salvation.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker compares suffering to a building site filled with \"noise,\" \"dust,\" and materials that \"look absolutely nothing like the final structure.\" The key point is that \"disorder is not necessarily the absence of design. Many times it's the presence of a design you cannot yet see\".\nREF: THEODICY-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-345e7215-afe4.md (08:05-08:26)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 50:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+50%3A20/>\n\nQ83. What highly troubling details about Sodom and Gomorrah does the lesson cite to justify God’s judgment of the cities?\nA. They had abandoned agriculture to focus on making weapons of war.\nB. They worshipped fallen angels and built towering idols to them.\nC. The entire male population possessed such a severe reprobate and violent sexual nature that they sought to assault visiting strangers, right from the city gates.\nD. They charged exorbitant taxes to travelers crossing their borders.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker describes the depth of Sodom's depravity, noting that \"all the men in the city\" had the proclivity to assault strangers. \"They saw angels at the gates and wanted to rape them,\" illustrating the extreme wickedness that prompted God's judgment.\nREF: THEODICY-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8b09d75d-6ba0.md (07:13-07:56)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 19:4-11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+19%3A4-11/>\n\nQ84. What historical event illustrates the danger of Israel failing to fully obey God's commands to drive out corrupt nations (like the Amalekites)?\nA. The destruction of the second temple by the Romans in 70 AD.\nB. The rise of Haman the Agagite during the Babylonian exile, who attempted to annihilate the entire Jewish race.\nC. The enslavement of Israel in Egypt under Pharaoh.\nD. The splitting of the kingdom into Israel and Judah.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses Saul's failure to completely destroy the Amalekites (sparing Agag) as an example. Later, during the exile, \"Haman the Agagite\" wanted to \"come and finish the work\" and destroy the Jews, which would have prevented Jesus from coming to save humanity.\nREF: THEODICY-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8b09d75d-6ba0.md (10:17-10:57)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 Samuel 15:7–9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Samuel+15%3A7%E2%80%939/>; Esther 3:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Esther+3%3A1/>\n\nQ85. According to the lesson, why does human labor often involve intense struggle and frustration today?\nA. Because humans forgot the original farming techniques used by Adam.\nB. Because God desired for humans to build physical endurance.\nC. It is a direct result of the curse in Genesis where God said, \"In the sweat of thy face shalt thou eat bread,\" following Adam's fall.\nD. It is due entirely to modern economic systems and capitalism.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker connects the struggle of human labor to the fall in Genesis, explaining that when God said, \"cursed is the ground for thy sake... In the sweat of thy face, thou shalt eat bread,\" it meant that human labor now involves struggle as part of natural evil.\nREF: THEODICY-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-3877d1c1-564b.md (03:56-04:42)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 3:17–19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+3%3A17%E2%80%9319/>\n\nQ86. How does the lesson frame the \"now and not yet\" reality of Christ's victory over evil and death?\nA. Christ has won the final victory over death, but believers still live in a world with \"pockets of resistance\" (like sickness) while awaiting the complete physical manifestation of that victory.\nB. Christ's victory will only begin once all believers become completely sinless.\nC. Christ has not actually defeated evil yet; He is waiting for the Church to defeat it through political action.\nD. Believers currently experience zero sickness or pain if their faith is genuine.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The speaker compares the current reality to winning a war where \"there are still pockets of resistance... rebels still kicking up.\" Death is defeated, but on earth right now, \"you might have to wake up every day and carry your cross,\" awaiting the final day when God wipes away every tear.\nREF: THEODICY-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-3877d1c1-564b.md (14:42-16:17)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Timothy 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+4/>\n\nQ87. Which point does the speaker use to challenge the hallucination theory of the resurrection appearances?\nA. The theory says that one person saw Jesus once, at the tomb.\nB. The theory requires the disciples to have stolen Jesus' body.\nC. The reports describe a single appearance to the disciples in Jerusalem.\nD. The speaker points to appearances over 40 days, including one to 500 people at once.\nANSWER: D\nEXPLANATION: The speaker argues that a hallucination does not account for the reported appearances over 40 days, to different groups and locations, including the appearance to 500 people at one time.\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-III — [09:29]–[10:29]\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 1:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+1%3A3/>; 1 Corinthians 15:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15%3A6/>\n\nQ88. How did the apostles initially respond to the women's report that Jesus had risen?\nA. They immediately believed it and went to Galilee.\nB. They assumed the authorities had moved Jesus' body.\nC. They dismissed the report as idle tales and did not believe.\nD. They accused the women of stealing the body.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that Luke says the women's words seemed like idle tales to the apostles, who did not believe them at first.\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-IV — [09:42]–[10:32]\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Luke 24:10–11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A10%E2%80%9311/>\n\nQ89. Which detail from the lesson supports the claim that Jesus' resurrection appearances were physical?\nA. The disciples heard a voice but did not see Jesus.\nB. Jesus showed his wounds and ate with the disciples.\nC. The women returned with another report from the tomb.\nD. The authorities announced that Jesus was alive.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker points to Jesus showing Thomas his wounds and to Jesus eating with the disciples as evidence that they encountered him physically.\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-IV — [10:32]\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 20:24–28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+20%3A24%E2%80%9328/>; Luke 24:36–43 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A36%E2%80%9343/>\n\nQ90. What did Jesus rebuke the Emmaus disciples for?\nA. Being slow to believe what the prophets had said about the Messiah.\nB. Expecting the Messiah to raise an army and expel Rome.\nC. Returning to Jerusalem before the other disciples arrived.\nD. Claiming that the tomb had never been empty.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The speaker recalls Jesus calling them foolish and slow to believe the prophets, who had taught that the Messiah must suffer and then enter his glory.\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-V — [05:27]; [07:12]\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Luke 24:25–27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+24%3A25%E2%80%9327/>\n\nQ91. Why does the lesson emphasize that Jesus' brothers originally doubted Him during His earthly ministry?\nA. To demonstrate that those closest to Jesus served as a rigorous, natural litmus test for the historical reality of His resurrection.\nB. To show that only strangers and political authorities could recognize Jesus' messianic credentials before His death.\nC. To argue that personal familiarity with a prophet guarantees immediate spiritual enlightenment without evidence.\nD. To explain why the early apostles refused to allow any members of Jesus' biological family into the Jerusalem church.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: John 7:5 records that Jesus' brothers did not believe in Him during His ministry; their post-resurrection transformation into worshipful servants and leaders proves the undeniable impact of encountering the risen Christ.\nREF: DID JESUS RISE FROM THE DEAD. V — 01:19–02:05\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 7:5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+7%3A5/>; James 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A1/>\n\nQ92. According to the lesson's summary on the historical case for the resurrection, what medical fact confirms Jesus truly died?\nA. Pontius Pilate refused to release Jesus' body to Joseph of Arimathea until three independent physicians conducted an autopsy.\nB. Temple guards immediately carried Jesus' body away to an unrecorded burial chamber before official certification.\nC. The high priest verified Jesus' death by requiring blood from the scourging to be sprinkled on the altar.\nD. Roman executioners confirmed His death on the cross and a soldier's spear produced a sudden flow of blood and water.\nANSWER: D\nEXPLANATION: The historical and medical evidence of crucifixion confirms Jesus truly died; Roman executioners confirmed He was already dead and pierced His side, producing blood and water.\nREF: DID JESUS RISE FROM THE DEAD. V — 04:54–05:18\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 19:33–34 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+19%3A33%E2%80%9334/>\n\nQ93. In James 1:1, how does Jesus' earthly brother James identify his primary relationship to Jesus after the resurrection?\nA. As the chief biological heir entitled to royal succession over the Davidic dynasty.\nB. As an apostle who possessed greater spiritual authority than Peter because of shared childhood memories.\nC. As a servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ, rather than relying on his biological family status.\nD. As the exclusive custodian of Jesus' private, unrecorded family teachings.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: James does not boast of being Jesus' brother; after witnessing the risen Lord, he humbly introduces himself in James 1:1 as a bondservant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ.\nREF: DID JESUS RISE FROM THE DEAD. V — 01:54–02:05\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: James 1:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A1/>\n\nDECK: Discipleship Experience (Section 11) Believers' Authority/Charisma\r\n\r\nQ1. \"Not everything which is physical in its manifestation is physical in its source.\" What is the main point of this statement?\r\nA. Spiritual problems never show up physically\r\nB. Only physical problems are real\r\nC. Something can show up physically while its root cause is spiritual\r\nD. Physical problems should never be treated by doctors alone\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: While a problem may have a physical manifestation, its underlying root cause can often be spiritual.\r\nREF: HOW TO CAST OUT DEMONS I — [08:42], [14:27]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Luke 13:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+13%3A11/>; Mark 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+5/>\r\n\r\nQ2. In Mark 5, which two effects of the demon on the man are mentioned in the teaching?\r\nA. He lived in a graveyard and had supernatural strength\r\nB. He lived in a mountain and could not sleep\r\nC. He was bent over and had supernatural strength\r\nD. He could not speak and lived in a graveyard\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The man in Mark 5 lived in a graveyard and possessed supernatural strength capable of breaking chains.\r\nREF: HOW TO CAST OUT DEMONS I — [15:37] - [16:38]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Mark 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+5/>\r\n\r\nQ3. Which account correctly reflects the sequence that led to Saul's tormented state?\r\nA. He grew envious of David, the Spirit of God left him, then he disobeyed and wanted to kill David\r\nB. An evil spirit entered him and this caused the Spirit of God to leave him\r\nC. He disobeyed, and an evil spirit entered him while the Spirit of God remained upon him\r\nD. He disobeyed, the Spirit of God left him, and an evil spirit then entered him\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Saul's disobedience caused the Spirit of God to leave him, allowing an evil spirit to enter.\r\nREF: HOW TO CAST OUT DEMONS II — [00:16]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Samuel 15:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Samuel+15%3A23/>; 1 Samuel 16:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Samuel+16%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ4. A believer says, \"Anger is just anger, it is only human nature.\" Which response aligns with the teaching?\r\nA. Ordinary envy becomes demonic once it is acted upon publicly\r\nB. Demon spirits can only exploit envy in people who are not believers\r\nC. Envy is always demonic in origin\r\nD. Some anger is ordinary, but where an inroad exists, demon spirits can take advantage of it\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: While natural anger exists, holding onto it creates an inroad that demon spirits can exploit.\r\nREF: HOW TO CAST OUT DEMONS I — [05:22] - [06:35]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+4/>\r\n\r\nQ5. Which statement about believers and demon spirits is true?\r\nA. Meeting a demon spirit is a sign that the Spirit of God has left the believer\r\nB. Believers can avoid demon spirits entirely if they understand the devil's tricks\r\nC. Believers only meet demon spirits after giving an inroad through sin or unforgiveness\r\nD. Believers will encounter demon spirits in this world whether they like it or not, so they must be ready to act\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Believers will inevitably encounter demon spirits in the world and must recognize their operations to act accordingly.\r\nREF: HOW TO CAST OUT DEMONS II — [07:09] - [08:06]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 6:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+6%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ6. Which statement is consistent with what was taught about pride, humility and honour?\r\nA. Honour people in your own way, since the realm of the spirit ignores social context\r\nB. Stop honouring people, since the realm of the spirit differs from our culture\r\nC. Find a way to honour people in a way their social context perceives honour, while remembering the realm of the spirit differs from our culture\r\nD. Let our social ideas of humility decide how we speak about God's power in warfare\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: We should honor people according to their social context, while keeping in mind that spiritual laws regarding pride and humility differ from cultural norms.\r\nREF: HOW TO CAST OUT DEMONS III — [05:45] - [06:36], [09:57]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Peter 2:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+2%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ7. Which best describes a believer's relationship with boasting?\r\nA. They may boast in Christ, but only in warfare\r\nB. They boast in Christ and not in their own efforts\r\nC. Boasting has no place in their life, since salvation is by grace alone\r\nD. They may boast in their own efforts, provided they give Christ credit afterwards\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Believers are called to boast, but their boasting should be centered in Christ Jesus rather than their own fleshly efforts.\r\nREF: HOW TO CAST OUT DEMONS III — [03:49]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 2:8, 9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+2%3A8%2C+9/>; Romans 3:27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+3%3A27/>; Philippians 3:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+3%3A3/>; Romans 15:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+15%3A17/>; Romans 2:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+2%3A17/>; Hebrews 3:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+3%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ8. A beginner has made bold declarations, stepped in and worshipped, yet the demons have not left. What's the next step to take?\r\nA. Keep repeating the declarations until the demons leave, since glory touch is for experienced ministers\r\nB. Go to glory touch privately, so the person is not embarrassed in front of others\r\nC. Go to glory touch with people around, because demons only leave when a group is present\r\nD. Go to glory touch with people around, because those being delivered can become violent\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: If earlier steps fail, a beginner should proceed to the glory touch with others present, as the person being delivered may become physically violent.\r\nREF: HOW TO CAST OUT DEMONS IV — [05:16]\r\n\r\nQ9. What point is made through Jesus feeling the touch of the woman with the issue of blood?\r\nA. Jesus released power only after deciding to, so glory is never transferred unknowingly\r\nB. The woman's faith, not the glory in Jesus, produced the transfer of power\r\nC. Jesus felt the touch, showing a believer can be conscious of the glory of God within them\r\nD. Christ entered the Most Holy Place every year with His own blood, while the Old Testament high priest offered animal blood only once for Israel.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Jesus feeling the touch demonstrates that believers can be consciously aware of the glory of God flowing through them.\r\nREF: HOW TO CAST OUT DEMONS IV — [07:36] - [08:29]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Mark 5:30 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+5%3A30/>\r\n\r\nQ10. A believer perceives a demon in a colleague who is awake and alert. Which approach is appropriate?\r\nA. Ask or offer to pray for them, without telling them directly or rudely that they have a demon\r\nB. Tell them plainly that they have a demon so they can consent to prayer\r\nC. Wait until they are asleep, then cast the demon out without ever telling them\r\nD. Say nothing and pray privately, since offering prayer risks offence\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Rather than bluntly telling someone they have a demon, which might cause offense, it is better to politely offer to pray for them.\r\nREF: HOW TO CAST OUT DEMONS IV — [13:46] - [14:32]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Colossians 4:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+4%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ11. God moves in a person's life through two streams. Which pair names them?\r\nA. The word and the anointing\r\nB. Faith and glory\r\nC. Faith and the anointing\r\nD. Faith and worship\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: God operates in a believer's life through the dual streams of faith and the anointing.\r\nREF: MANIFESTING THE PRESENCE OF GOD I — [00:15] - [01:02]\r\n\r\nQ12. Which option best describes how the two streams relate?\r\nA. Symbiotic: each supports the other, and walking consistently in glory means learning how they work together\r\nB. Sequential: One opens the way, and the other takes over afterwards\r\nC. Competing: a believer should choose the stronger stream and stay in it\r\nD. Independent: one meets some needs and the other meets others\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Faith and the anointing have a symbiotic relationship; walking consistently in God's power requires understanding how to partner with both.\r\nREF: MANIFESTING THE PRESENCE OF GOD I — [01:02] - [01:52]\r\n\r\nQ13. Which sequence describes what the stream of faith requires of a believer?\r\nA. Believe, know, practise\r\nB. Know, practise, believe\r\nC. Know, believe, teach\r\nD. Know, believe, practise\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The stream of faith requires a believer to first know the Word, then believe it, and finally practice it.\r\nREF: MANIFESTING THE PRESENCE OF GOD I — [02:43]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 10:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+10%3A17/>; James 1:22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A22/>\r\n\r\nQ14. A believer handles scripture casually but expects it to transform their life. Which statement applies to them?\r\nA. The word transforms anyone who hears it, whatever their disposition\r\nB. The word has sacred impact on the lives of those who hold sacred reverence for it\r\nC. The word transforms those who know it thoroughly\r\nD. The word transforms only those who first receive the anointing\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The Word of God delivers its powerful impact to those who treat it with sacred reverence, rather than casual familiarity.\r\nREF: MANIFESTING THE PRESENCE OF GOD I — [02:43] - [03:31]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Revelation 19:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+19%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ15. A believer declares in general terms that \"God's word works\" over a problem but sees no change. What is missing?\r\nA. Greater intensity in declaring the word, since volume releases faith\r\nB. Agreement with others, since faith can be released corporately\r\nC. Because the Bible is the standard for Christian belief, so questioning its origin and validity raises a question about the foundation of the faith.\r\nD. Because the Bible must be confirmed by archaeological discoveries before Christians can accept its teachings.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The Bible sets the standard for everything believers hold to be true, meaning any question regarding its origin and validity directly questions the foundation of the Christian faith.\r\nREF: IS THE BIBLE TRUE. I – Celebration Church International - LMS_2.mp4 — [00:29] - [01:14]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Timothy 3:16-17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Timothy+3%3A16-17/>\r\n\r\nQ16. A believer says, \"I know the word and I believe it, so I have done everything faith requires.\" What is missing?\r\nA. Practising the word, since knowing and believing are only two of the three requirements\r\nB. Nothing, since knowing and believing are what faith requires\r\nC. Teaching the word to others, as faith is completed by teaching\r\nD. The anointing, since the stream of faith ends once the word is believed\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Knowing and believing are insufficient on their own; true faith also requires practicing (doing) the Word.\r\nREF: MANIFESTING THE PRESENCE OF GOD I — [10:52]; MANIFESTING THE PRESENCE OF GOD II — [01:18] - [01:49]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: James 1:22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A22/>\r\n\r\nQ17. A believer senses the anointing strongly in a meeting, yet the situation is not changing. Which response fits how the two streams work together?\r\nA. Wait quietly, since the anointing carries out everything by itself\r\nB. Drop faith, since the anointing has taken over\r\nC. Take a step of faith, since the two streams work together and sometimes faith must step forward\r\nD. Suspect the anointing is just goosebumps, since it should have settled the matter\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Faith and the anointing work symbiotically; when one stream seems insufficient alone, taking a bold step of faith can trigger the manifestation.\r\nREF: MANIFESTING THE PRESENCE OF GOD I — [01:02] - [01:52]\r\n\r\nQ18. Becoming a doer of the word of God indicates which of the following?\r\nA. You have gained enough knowledge of the word of God to do it\r\nB. You have enough reverence for the word of God to do it\r\nC. You have spent enough time building confidence, now you are capable of doing it\r\nD. You have spent enough time watching your pastor do it, now you are confident enough to do it\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Acting on the Word of God is the ultimate proof that a believer holds sacred reverence for it.\r\nREF: MANIFESTING THE PRESENCE OF GOD II — [01:53]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: James 1:22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A22/>\r\n\r\nQ19. You try to cast out a demon, but nothing dramatic happens, what should your conclusion be?\r\nA. Demons don't exist\r\nB. You need to brush up on your exorcism skills\r\nC. You don't have the requisite anointing for the task\r\nD. The Word, not the evidence, settles the act\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Faith dictates that the believer's confidence remains in the Word of God, regardless of immediate physical evidence or dramatic manifestations.\r\nREF: MANIFESTING THE PRESENCE OF GOD II — [02:47] - [03:56]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Corinthians 5:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+5%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ20. When casting out a demon, which of the following best indicates the effectiveness of the anointing?\r\nA. An energy level that zings like electricity at the tip of your fingers\r\nB. Violent shaking, jumping and shouting of the possessed\r\nC. Assurance of effectiveness comes by the Spirit\r\nD. The number of people praying at the same time\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: While physical signs may occur, the primary and most reliable assurance that a demon has been expelled comes through spiritual discernment.\r\nREF: MANIFESTING THE PRESENCE OF GOD III — [01:31] - [02:25]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+28/>\r\n\r\nQ21. The symbiotic relationship between faith and the anointing is represented by which of the following?\r\nA. Multiple confirmations of full manifestation\r\nB. A spiritual note of victory\r\nC. Learning to see, even if it's in seed form, what God is doing\r\nD. Treating faith and the anointing as unrelated to one another\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The partnership between faith and the anointing requires a believer to spiritually perceive and respond to what God is doing, even when it appears only in seed form.\r\nREF: MANIFESTING THE PRESENCE OF GOD III — [09:01] - [09:46]\r\n\r\nQ22. The Spirit realm operates like a courtroom which consists of the following except\r\nA. A Judge\r\nB. An Accuser\r\nC. An Attorney\r\nD. The Devil\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The spiritual courtroom is comprised of legal roles including a Judge, an Accuser, and a Defense Attorney; \"the devil\" is an entity, not the legal title of the role.\r\nREF: POWER IN THE BLOOD I — [02:26] - [09:03]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 18:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+18%3A25/>; Psalm 50:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+50%3A6/>; Zechariah 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Zechariah+3/>; Numbers 22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Numbers+22/>; Job 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Job+1/>; 1 John 2:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+2%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ23. The word \"Satan\" always has an evil personality.\r\nA. True\r\nB. False\r\nC. Partially false\r\nD. True, but with some caveats\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The word \"Satan\" means \"accuser\" or \"opposer\" and was even used to describe an angel of the Lord standing in opposition, devoid of an evil personality.\r\nREF: POWER IN THE BLOOD I — [05:43] - [06:36]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Psalm 50:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+50%3A6/>; Zechariah 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Zechariah+3/>; Numbers 22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Numbers+22/>; Job 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Job+1/>\r\n\r\nQ24. What role does Jesus play in the spirit realm?\r\nA. Propitiation\r\nB. Sanctification\r\nC. Justice\r\nD. Defense Attorney\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: In the spiritual courtroom, Jesus serves as our advocate and defense attorney.\r\nREF: POWER IN THE BLOOD I — [07:45] - [09:03]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Job 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Job+1/>; 1 John 2:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+2%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ25. What was the first recorded death in the bible?\r\nA. Abel\r\nB. Adam and Eve, spiritually\r\nC. A sacrifice\r\nD. The death of Cain\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The first physical death recorded in the Bible was not Abel's murder, but God sacrificing an animal to cover Adam and Eve.\r\nREF: POWER IN THE BLOOD I — [10:31] - [13:32]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 3:21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+3%3A21/>; Genesis 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+4/>\r\n\r\nQ26. What is one fact about spiritual legality that many do not know?\r\nA. They belong to every believer\r\nB. They are ratified by the blood\r\nC. They must be continually defended\r\nD. They depend on a person's personal merit\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: In the realm of spiritual legality, possessing a right does not automatically guarantee its enforcement; believers must actively stand on and defend their rights.\r\nREF: POWER IN THE BLOOD II — [04:59] - [07:21]\r\n\r\nQ27. Leviticus 17:11 best explains which of the following legal principles of the spirit realm?\r\nA. Substitutionary Atonement\r\nB. Propitiation\r\nC. The Principle of Substitution\r\nD. The principle of creation\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Leviticus 17:11 establishes that the life of the flesh is in the blood, which forms the legal basis for the principle of substitution.\r\nREF: POWER IN THE BLOOD III — [02:31] - [03:51]\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Leviticus 17:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Leviticus+17%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ28. The following are key terminologies that describe the principles in the Spirit realm\r\nA. Substitution, Ransom, Redemption\r\nB. Substitution, Petition, Revelation\r\nC. Assurance, Ratification, Penitentiary Licence\r\nD. Prayer, fasting, and worship\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The key terminologies that govern the legality of the Spirit realm include substitution, ransom, and redemption.\r\nREF: POWER IN THE BLOOD III — [02:31] - [03:51]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Leviticus 17:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Leviticus+17%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ29. An animal can play your part in a spiritual ritual to the degree that when the animal is offered, it is as if your life is offered. Which principle is this?\r\nA. Legality\r\nB. Ransom\r\nC. Substitution\r\nD. Consecration\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The principle of substitution allows an animal's life to legally take the place of a human's life in a spiritual ritual.\r\nREF: POWER IN THE BLOOD III — [02:31] - [03:51]\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Leviticus 17:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Leviticus+17%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ30. Which of the following statements best explain the principle of Ransom?\r\nA. The price paid to buy back or release someone that was lost or enslaved\r\nB. The price paid to ratify the release of someone that was held in bondage\r\nC. The price paid to justify the release of someone that was held in bondage\r\nD. The price paid to release someone that was held in bondage\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Ransom legally refers to the precise price paid to secure the release of someone who has been held in bondage.\r\nREF: POWER IN THE BLOOD III — [06:19] - [07:33]\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Mark 10:45 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+10%3A45/>\r\n\r\nQ31. What is the primary reason the sons of Sceva failed to cast out the demon, according to the lesson?\r\nA. They did not shout the name of Jesus loudly enough.\r\nB. They forgot to ask the demon for its name first.\r\nC. They had no personal relationship with the God of the believers.\r\nD. They were using the wrong spiritual garments.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that the sons of Sceva (Acts 19) failed because \"The sons of Sceva had no relationship with God. They were not born again. And you see, there are many people who are not born again who want to do what believers do\".\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-c3bc6668-b9d9.md (00:16-01:18)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+19/>\r\n\r\nQ32. According to the lesson, what is the ultimate goal of hearing the gospel?\r\nA. To receive insights for a happy home.\r\nB. To gain illumination for daily living.\r\nC. To be entertained by the preacher's trendy delivery.\r\nD. Salvation.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker emphasizes that \"the goal of hearing the gospel is not just illumination for you to get insights for living, insights for a happy home... The goal is salvation\".\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-c3bc6668-b9d9.md (01:58-02:50)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 1:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+1%3A16/>\r\n\r\nQ33. How does the lesson explain the preservation of the earth from total demonic destruction?\r\nA. The presence of God in believers preserves the world, similar to how Noah's presence delayed the flood.\r\nB. The government has enacted laws to restrain demonic activity.\r\nC. The demons are not powerful enough to cause global destruction.\r\nD. Angels physically guard the borders of every nation.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker asserts that \"it is the presence of God in believers, preserving the world... the moment we are raptured, it is the presence of God in the church that is keeping the earth together,\" citing Noah and Lot as historical parallels.\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-c3bc6668-b9d9.md (06:24-07:19)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 6–9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+6%E2%80%939/>; Genesis 19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+19/>\r\n\r\nQ34. What practical story does the speaker share to illustrate that spiritual power is not to be debated, but demonstrated?\r\nA. The story of a man whose stolen money was miraculously returned.\r\nB. The story of a student who passed an exam without studying.\r\nC. The story of a demon that was cast out via a text message.\r\nD. The story of a man whose missing testicles were supernaturally restored after prayer.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: To prove that power is demonstrated rather than debated, the speaker recounts a recent event in Abuja where he laid hands on a man whose two testicles had supernaturally disappeared after being touched in a public vehicle, and they began to grow back fully within twelve hours.\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-c3bc6668-b9d9.md (03:38-06:24)\r\n\r\nQ35. Why does the speaker warn believers against being \"no different from the sons of Sceva\"?\r\nA. Because they might accidentally start a professional exorcism business.\r\nB. Because playing church without a true spiritual identity leaves believers vulnerable to constant demonic oppression.\r\nC. Because the sons of Sceva were physically weak.\r\nD. Because the sons of Sceva did not know how to read the Scriptures.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker warns that some believers are no different from the sons of Sceva because they \"just play church\" without a true identity, resulting in them being \"constantly oppressed\" by nightmares and demonic attacks despite attending church.\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-c3bc6668-b9d9.md (08:54-09:50)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 19:13–16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+19%3A13%E2%80%9316/>\r\n\r\nQ36. What does Paul's prayer in Ephesians 1:15-18 reveal about a believer's inheritance?\r\nA. Believers need the spirit of wisdom and revelation to know what they already have, because having an inheritance doesn't matter if you don't know it.\r\nB. Believers automatically know everything they possess the moment they are saved.\r\nC. Believers must pray and fast for six months before they are granted an inheritance.\r\nD. Believers only receive their inheritance after they get to heaven.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses Ephesians 1:15-18 to explain that Paul prays for believers to receive revelation because \"just because you are saved doesn't mean you know who you are... If you don't know what you have. It doesn't matter that you have it\".\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-084525a9-0788.md (01:06-01:51)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Ephesians 1:15–18 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A15%E2%80%9318/>\r\n\r\nQ37. In the lesson, what analogy is used to explain \"inheritance by grace\" regarding authority?\r\nA. A soldier earning his rank through years of difficult combat.\r\nB. A son inheriting the estate and authority of his hard-working, wealthy father, even though the son didn't work for it.\r\nC. A student studying hard to pass an exam and receive a certificate.\r\nD. A farmer planting seeds and waiting patiently for the harvest.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses the analogy of the richest man in Ghana willing his estate to his son. The son might be \"unserious,\" but because of the inheritance, \"all the honor that belonged to his father must be given to him.\" This illustrates that authority is \"fundamentally an inheritance by grace\".\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-084525a9-0788.md (03:24-05:06)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 8:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ38. How does the speaker describe the relationship between prayer and identity?\r\nA. Prayer is the only thing needed for spiritual warfare.\r\nB. Identity is irrelevant as long as a person prays loud enough.\r\nC. Prayer is like a bullet, but knowing your identity (who you are) is the gun that gives it propelling power.\r\nD. Prayer and identity are completely unrelated concepts in the realm of the spirit.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker states that prayer is important but it is an addition. \"Prayer is a bullet, but without a gun. A bullet is not different from a stone... The real propelling power of a bullet is a gun... It is first an identity, an inheritance\".\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-084525a9-0788.md (05:06-05:55)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 2:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+2%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ39. What did God tell Pastor Adeboye after he fasted for a year asking for power?\r\nA. He told him to fast for another six months to prove his dedication.\r\nB. He told him that power was only for the original twelve apostles.\r\nC. He gave him a new mantle to cast out demons.\r\nD. He asked him why he was asking for what he already had, directing him to Acts 1:8.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker shares a story of Pastor Adeboye fasting for a year for power. God asked him, \"why are you asking for what you already have?\" and pointed him to Acts 1:8, emphasizing that believers must know what God has already done in the *now*.\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-084525a9-0788.md (07:54-08:51)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+1%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ40. According to the lesson, what happens if a believer doubts their authority in front of demons?\r\nA. The demons will respectfully leave to avoid conflict.\r\nB. The demons will know, because they can see if you don't know who you are.\r\nC. God will send an angel to speak on the believer's behalf.\r\nD. The believer will instantly lose their salvation.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explicitly warns, \"If you doubt your authority, demons know. Did you hear what I said? They can see it. If you don't know who you are, they know\".\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-084525a9-0788.md (06:47-07:54)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+1%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ41. According to Galatians 3:27 as explained in the lesson, what does it mean to be \"baptized into Christ\"?\r\nA. It means being physically submerged in water to join a local church.\r\nB. It means wearing a physical garment with a cross on it.\r\nC. It is a spiritual immersion where one puts on Christ as a \"garment of influence\" and operates in the spirit realm as though it were Christ.\r\nD. It refers to memorizing the four Gospels.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that Galatians 3:27 describes baptism as \"a garment of influence. You know what baptism does? It wears you with the influence of Christ. And now in the spirit realm, you begin to deal and do business as though it were Christ\".\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-5ac40e68-af28.md (00:15-01:22)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Galatians 3:27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+3%3A27/>\r\n\r\nQ42. How does the lesson explain a believer's connection to the redemptive work of Christ?\r\nA. Believers are spiritually immersed into Christ, meaning when Christ was crucified, buried, and raised, the believer was crucified, buried, and raised with Him.\r\nB. Believers must physically suffer on a cross to gain redemption.\r\nC. Believers earn redemption by continuously asking for forgiveness.\r\nD. Believers simply read about the historical event without it affecting their spiritual position.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that through baptism, God \"removed you from the lineage of the first Adam, and he messed you into Christ.\" Therefore, \"Christ did not just die for you, he died as you... as Jesus was here with his arms spread, God also sees you in him\".\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-5ac40e68-af28.md (02:14-03:12)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 6:3-5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6%3A3-5/>\r\n\r\nQ43. What does the speaker emphasize about the phrase \"in Christ\"?\r\nA. It is synonymous with the word \"Christian,\" which was invented by God in Antioch.\r\nB. It means striving daily to become more \"Christ-like.\"\r\nC. It is a deeper identity than being called \"Christian\" or \"Christ-like,\" representing a literal spiritual immersion and identity.\r\nD. It is a temporary title used only during times of prayer.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that the term \"Christian\" was invented by unbelievers in Antioch. The Bible instead says believers are \"in Christ.\" The speaker emphasizes, \"God did not call you Christ like you are more than Christ like you are in Christ... immersed into him\".\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-5ac40e68-af28.md (11:20-12:12)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 9:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+9%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ44. How does the story of Esau and Jacob (Romans 9) illustrate the concept of grace?\r\nA. It shows that God rewards the hardest worker, as Esau hunted for his blessing.\r\nB. It demonstrates that God's purpose according to election is not based on works or entitlement, but on His mercy and choice.\r\nC. It proves that the firstborn child always receives the spiritual inheritance.\r\nD. It teaches that humans must manipulate their way into God's presence.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Using Romans 9:11, the speaker explains that neither Esau nor Jacob had done good or evil when God chose Jacob, proving that \"the purpose of God according to election... When it comes to mercy, it's not an entitlement... it's his choice\".\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-5ac40e68-af28.md (12:12-16:15)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 9:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+9%3A11/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 25:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+25%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ45. In the analogy of Jacob receiving the blessing, what does wearing Esau's garments represent?\r\nA. A believer pretending to be something they are not to fool God.\r\nB. The deceitful nature of the human heart before salvation.\r\nC. The necessity of animal sacrifices in the New Covenant.\r\nD. The picture of the gospel, where a believer approaches the Father in a garment that is not theirs (Christ's) to receive a blessing they didn't work for.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker maps Jacob putting on the fur of the animal (the identity of his brother) to baptism, noting that Jacob walked to the father \"in a garment that was not his, with an identity that was not his. And he got the blessing. This is the picture of the gospel\".\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-5ac40e68-af28.md (18:28-19:30)\r\n\r\nQ46. Why does the speaker recount the story of the civilian putting on an army uniform to stop a car?\r\nA. To illustrate that spiritual authority comes from the \"garment\" you wear (putting on Christ), meaning demons respect the government of heaven you represent, not your physical body.\r\nB. To prove that physical appearance is the only thing that matters in society.\r\nC. To show that believers should involve law enforcement in spiritual warfare.\r\nD. To encourage believers to buy military clothing for prayer meetings.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses the analogy of a civilian putting on an army uniform. A driver stops for him because \"he has put on... the government you are respecting, not him.\" This illustrates what baptism is: \"You put on Christ and now... He says, in my name they shall cast out devils\".\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a9bdb497-b81d.md (03:33-04:46)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 13:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+13%3A14/>\r\n\r\nQ47. According to the lesson, how should a believer understand \"the name of Jesus\"?\r\nA. It is a magic phrase or mantra, similar to \"open sesame,\" used to unlock blessings.\r\nB. It is a historical title that should only be used by ordained ministers.\r\nC. It is a secret code that demons are unable to pronounce.\r\nD. It is an identity and a garment of influence that a believer becomes one with, not just a word to call out.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker clarifies that the name of Jesus is not a \"magic phrase\" like \"open sesame.\" Instead, \"The name of Jesus is a baptism. It's an identity. It's a garment of influence... not a magic phrase, you become One with the Name\".\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a9bdb497-b81d.md (05:33-06:47)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 19:13-16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+19%3A13-16/>\r\n\r\nQ48. How does the speaker interpret Jesus' statement in Luke 10:19, \"Behold, I give you authority to trample on serpents and scorpions\"?\r\nA. Believers have authority, but they must actively \"trample\" and resist the devil; the devil won't stay under their feet automatically.\r\nB. Believers should literally step on dangerous animals to prove their faith.\r\nC. The devil is defeated and will never attempt to attack a believer again.\r\nD. Believers should observe the devil but avoid engaging in spiritual warfare.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker emphasizes that \"The fact that the devil is under your feet does not mean he will stay there. You must trample.\" Believers are instructed to actively \"resist the devil\" and \"give no place to the devil\".\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a9bdb497-b81d.md (12:17-13:12)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Luke 10:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+10%3A19/>\r\n\r\nQ49. From what geographical or spiritual position is the believer instructed to pray against satanic influences?\r\nA. From a position of begging for mercy on Earth.\r\nB. From a position of equality with the devil.\r\nC. From a position \"far above\" all principalities and powers, seated in heavenly places.\r\nD. From the perspective of the Old Testament law.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: During the prayer session, the speaker instructs the congregation: \"you are praying from a position far above... Are you seated in this market? Where are you seated? Far above... All principalities and powers\".\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a9bdb497-b81d.md (13:36-14:14)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 1:20-22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+1%3A20-22/>\r\n\r\nQ50. What caution does the speaker give regarding cultural divisions (like Nigerian vs. Ghanaian) within the church?\r\nA. Believers should strictly worship with their own nationalities to avoid confusion.\r\nB. Cultural differences should dictate how spiritual authority operates.\r\nC. In Christ there is neither Jew nor Greek; the church must not adopt worldly divisions, because believers are one in Christ.\r\nD. Believers should prioritize their national identity over their spiritual identity.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Referencing Galatians 3:28 (\"there is neither Jew nor Greek\"), the speaker warns against division based on nationality (e.g., Nigerian or Ghanaian), stating, \"The church must not begin to think like the world. It's an embarrassment. We are one\".\r\nREF: WHO-ARE-YOU-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a9bdb497-b81d.md (00:16-01:36)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Galatians 3:28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Galatians+3%3A28/>\r\n\r\nQ51. How does the lesson describe the operation of the spiritual realm based on Zechariah 3?\r\nA. It operates like a courtroom, featuring a Judge, an Accuser, an Advocate, and a Law.\r\nB. It operates like a hospital, focusing solely on emotional healing.\r\nC. It operates like a battlefield with physical swords and shields.\r\nD. It operates like a marketplace where blessings are bought and sold.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: Using Zechariah 3, the speaker states, \"the spiritual actually operates like a courtroom. There is a judge, there is an accuser, there is a defense attorney. There is a law... And then you have the accused\".\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-cff7e4ba-d663.md (01:21-02:26)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Zechariah 3:1, 2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Zechariah+3%3A1%2C+2/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 18:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+18%3A25/>\r\n\r\nQ52. According to the lesson, what does the title \"Satan\" actually mean?\r\nA. A fallen angel of light.\r\nB. The ruler of the underworld.\r\nC. A job description meaning \"accuser\" or \"one who opposes.\"\r\nD. The creator of evil.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker clarifies that \"Satan is not a name. Satan is a job description... actually means accuser or one who opposes.\" The speaker gives an Old Testament example where an angel is called \"Satan\" because of his position of opposition.\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-cff7e4ba-d663.md (04:39-05:43)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Psalm 50:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+50%3A6/>; Zechariah 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Zechariah+3/>; Numbers 22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Numbers+22/>\r\n\r\nQ53. Why is God described as a \"righteous judge\" who listens to Satan in the Old Testament?\r\nA. Because God needed Satan's advice on human affairs.\r\nB. Because a righteous judge must hear all sides of a case to avoid being biased or unjust.\r\nC. Because Satan was God's equal in the Old Testament.\r\nD. Because God enjoyed watching humans suffer.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Addressing why God listened to Satan in Job, the speaker explains: \"because he's a righteous judge. He will hear all sides... If he does not hear all sides, he's biased. And if he's biased, he's not just\".\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-cff7e4ba-d663.md (06:36-07:45)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Numbers 22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Numbers+22/>; Job 1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Job+1/>; 1 John 2:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+2%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ54. What does the lesson identify as the *first* recorded physical death in the Bible?\r\nA. Cain murdering his brother Abel.\r\nB. The death of Adam after 930 years.\r\nC. The drowning of humanity during Noah's flood.\r\nD. An animal killed by God to make skin coats to cover Adam and Eve's nakedness.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker corrects the common assumption that Abel's death was the first, explaining that in Genesis 3:21, God made \"skin coat of animal\" to cover Adam and Eve. This necessitated the first physical death of an animal.\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-cff7e4ba-d663.md (10:31-12:11)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Genesis 3:21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+3%3A21/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+4/>\r\n\r\nQ55. What spiritual protocol is established by the first two deaths (the animal skin for Adam/Eve and Abel's animal sacrifice)?\r\nA. That farming is a less honorable profession than shepherding.\r\nB. That animals are morally evil and must be destroyed.\r\nC. That humans are naturally righteous and only need to try harder.\r\nD. That human works and efforts (like fig leaves or crops) cannot cover sin; blood must cover you to approach a holy God.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses these examples to show that Cain tried to use his \"works\" (produce of the ground) to cover his failure, just as Adam and Eve used leaves. God established a spiritual protocol: \"Blood must cover you... Your efforts can't cover you\".\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-cff7e4ba-d663.md (13:32-14:27)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+4/>\r\n\r\nQ56. What subtle error do believers make when approaching God after a period of \"good behavior\"?\r\nA. They pray with a sense of carnal confidence based on their own works, forgetting that the blood is their only access and justification, whether they fall or stand.\r\nB. They forget to pray for their enemies.\r\nC. They spend too much time worshiping instead of making petitions.\r\nD. They assume God is no longer listening to them.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that when believers haven't failed recently, they scan their behavior and say, \"I've not done anything wrong... And so you pray with some sense of carnal confidence.\" The speaker corrects this: \"Whether you fall or whether you stand, your righteousness is in the blood\".\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-6a88f6da-9926.md (00:16-01:43)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Genesis 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+4/>\r\n\r\nQ57. What is the meaning of the analogy regarding the LASMA official or a policeman?\r\nA. It illustrates that spiritual laws only apply in certain geographic locations.\r\nB. It shows that just because you have a right (or are free), the devil will still try to take advantage of you if you are ignorant of your rights; you must defend them.\r\nC. It proves that believers should argue with earthly authorities.\r\nD. It means that demons have the legal right to harass Christians.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses the example of LASMA officials seizing licenses even though the governor explicitly banned it, demonstrating that \"Just because is your right does not mean... you don't have to defend it... if you don't know your rights, he [the devil] will take advantage of you\".\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-6a88f6da-9926.md (05:43-06:43)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: James 4:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+4%3A7/>\r\n\r\nQ58. In spiritual warfare, what does the lesson state is the \"greatest and surest assurance of victory\"?\r\nA. The believer's personal fasting record.\r\nB. The volume of the believer's voice.\r\nC. The believer's good deeds over the past week.\r\nD. The blood of Jesus.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Quoting Revelation 12:11, the speaker emphatically states, \"The greatest and the surest assurance of victory as a believer is the blood. I'm telling you this. In warfare, the blood is the strongest assurance\".\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-6a88f6da-9926.md (09:26-09:46)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Revelation 12:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+12%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ59. How does the lesson differentiate between the power believers had during the Incarnation (Jesus' earthly ministry) versus after the Resurrection?\r\nA. The power during the Incarnation was stronger because Jesus was physically present.\r\nB. There is no difference; power has remained exactly the same since Genesis.\r\nC. After the blood was shed and Jesus resurrected, believers were translated to a new dimension of power and a completely different playing ground.\r\nD. The disciples lost their power after Jesus ascended.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker contrasts the power Jesus gave His disciples during the Incarnation with the post-Resurrection mandate (\"all power in heaven and earth have been given to me\"), stating that the shedding of blood and resurrection raised believers \"to a new dimension of power... a different playing ground\".\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-6a88f6da-9926.md (10:07-12:29)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+1%3A8/>\r\n\r\nQ60. According to the lesson, how does a believer put the devil in his place forever?\r\nA. By trusting in their own sincerity and good behavior.\r\nB. By mastering spiritual legalities, understanding the blood, and standing on their rights in the courts of heaven.\r\nC. By avoiding all confrontation and hiding from spiritual warfare.\r\nD. By relying entirely on the prayers of their local pastor.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: Throughout the lesson, the speaker stresses that believers must \"Master the legalities, learn how to petition, learn how to stand for your rights, to present your strong reasons and to do business in the courts of heaven\".\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-6a88f6da-9926.md (08:41-09:04)\r\n\r\nQ61. What does the lesson emphasize about Romans 6:23, \"The wages of sin is death\"?\r\nA. That humans earn death based on their behavior, and therefore cannot fix their brokenness through their own strength or works.\r\nB. That death is an unfair punishment invented by the devil.\r\nC. That physical death is the only consequence of sin.\r\nD. That believers should work harder to earn life.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses Romans 6:23 to show that death is a \"wage\" earned by our behavior. Because of this, \"If I go in my own strength, I'm in trouble. You sinned and you can't cover yourself... Your works can't cover you\".\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-902030b1-d072.md (00:16-01:16)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Romans 6:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6%3A23/>\r\n\r\nQ62. How did humans empower the \"accuser\" according to the lesson?\r\nA. By writing laws that gave the devil authority.\r\nB. By sinning morally through deception and surrendering their free will to him in false religious worship.\r\nC. By failing to read the Bible.\r\nD. By creating idols out of gold.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that \"when man sinned, we empowered the accuser,\" and furthermore, Satan deceived many people \"to surrender their will to him in false religious worship,\" submitting lineages to demonic claims.\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-902030b1-d072.md (01:16-02:31)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Romans 6:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6%3A23/>\r\n\r\nQ63. What does the spiritual principle of \"substitution\" mean?\r\nA. That God substitutes blessings for curses randomly.\r\nB. That someone takes the place of a sinner, bearing their penalty and consequences, just as an animal's life was offered in place of a human's in the Old Testament.\r\nC. That humans can substitute one sin for a lesser sin.\r\nD. That angels take the place of humans on Earth.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker defines substitution as \"the act of someone taking the place of a sinner, bearing their penalty, their judgment, their consequences,\" illustrating this with Old Testament animal sacrifices playing the part in a spiritual ritual.\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-902030b1-d072.md (03:51-06:19)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Leviticus 17:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Leviticus+17%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ64. How does the lesson define \"ransom\"?\r\nA. The price paid to secure the release of someone held in bondage.\r\nB. The price paid to buy back or reclaim something that was lost.\r\nC. The act of offering an animal sacrifice.\r\nD. The legal document proving innocence.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker provides precise definitions: \"Ransom is price paid to secure the release of someone held in bondage.\" (He then defines redemption as buying back or reclaiming something lost or enslaved).\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-902030b1-d072.md (06:19-07:33)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Timothy 2:6 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Timothy+2%3A6/>\r\n\r\nQ65. What does the blood of Jesus \"say\" in the spiritual courtroom compared to Abel's blood?\r\nA. It cries out for vengeance and immediate destruction of the enemies.\r\nB. It remains silent because the Judge already knows the facts.\r\nC. It speaks better things, stating that while the accused is guilty, the price has been fully paid and the individual now belongs to God.\r\nD. It demands that the believer perform penance for their sins.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker contrasts Abel's blood (which cried for vengeance) with Jesus's blood, which \"speaks better things... saying, yes, they are guilty, but I paid... It is true that his mom made this mistake, but he's my son now\".\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-902030b1-d072.md (11:44-13:03)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 12:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+12%3A24/>\r\n\r\nQ66. According to Revelation 12:10, what changed in the spiritual courtroom after Christ's sacrifice?\r\nA. Satan was promoted to the chief judge.\r\nB. The accuser of the brethren was cast down, meaning God no longer entertains the ministry of the accuser.\r\nC. The angels were given the job of accusing humans instead of Satan.\r\nD. God decided to forgive Satan's rebellion.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker quotes Revelation 12:10 regarding the casting down of the accuser of the brethren, stating that because of Christ's once-for-all crucifixion, \"God will never listen to what the enemy has to say... God no longer entertains the ministry of the accuser\".\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-6de96f2d-2ee1.md (00:57-01:30)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Revelation 12:10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+12%3A10/>\r\n\r\nQ67. If God no longer listens to the accuser, what trap does the speaker warn believers about?\r\nA. Believers often listen to the accuser themselves, letting him remind them of their past mistakes or making them think their failures caused their current problems.\r\nB. Believers might start accusing God.\r\nC. Believers might forget to repent.\r\nD. Believers might accidentally worship the accuser.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker warns that while \"God won't listen\" to the accuser anymore, \"there is still someone who can listen, and that's you... If God no longer entertains the ministry of the accuser, why are you doing it?\".\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-6de96f2d-2ee1.md (01:30-02:28)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 8:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+8%3A1/>\r\n\r\nQ68. What does 1 John 2:1-2 teach about Jesus, as highlighted in the lesson?\r\nA. Jesus is solely an example of good moral behavior.\r\nB. Jesus only forgives sins committed before baptism.\r\nC. Jesus is the propitiation for our sins, meaning all our sins have been fully paid for.\r\nD. Jesus requires us to advocate for ourselves before the Father.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker reads 1 John 2:1-2, emphasizing that Jesus \"is the propitiation for our sins,\" and follows it by declaring, \"All your sins have been paid for\".\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-6de96f2d-2ee1.md (06:47-07:17)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 John 2:1–2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+2%3A1%E2%80%932/>\r\n\r\nQ69. How is the blood of Jesus effectively \"applied\" in a believer's life, using the Passover analogy?\r\nA. It is applied automatically without the believer needing to do anything.\r\nB. It is applied by physically marking doors with red paint.\r\nC. It is applied solely by fasting for extended periods.\r\nD. It is applied through the word of their testimony, declaring and confessing what the blood has done (e.g., \"I am seated with Christ\").\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses the Passover lamb as an example: the lamb was slain, but the blood still needed to be applied to the lintel with hyssop. For believers, Revelation 12:11 shows application is made \"by the word of their testimony,\" meaning declaring with their words what Christ has done.\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-6de96f2d-2ee1.md (12:21-14:29)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Revelation 12:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+12%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ70. What does the phrase \"they loved not their lives unto the death\" imply for believers today?\r\nA. They must believe with absolute loyalty, allowing their belief to fundamentally change how they live and act.\r\nB. They must literally seek physical martyrdom.\r\nC. They must hate themselves and their bodies.\r\nD. They must isolate themselves from the modern world completely.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that \"they love not their lives unto death\" signifies loyalty. \"What really moves the needle is believe in loyalty... you must believe enough to Act... If you believe in Jesus, you must live for him\".\r\nREF: POWER-IN-THE-BLOOD-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-6de96f2d-2ee1.md (13:15-14:29)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Revelation 12:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+12%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ71. Why does the speaker warn trendy, modern Christians not to ignore the topic of casting out demons?\r\nA. Because demons only attack people wearing unbranded clothing.\r\nB. Because it is a popular topic that attracts large crowds.\r\nC. Because regardless of how fresh or educated they are, the devil hates them, and spiritual warfare is a serious reality they must face.\r\nD. Because modern technology is inherently demonic.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cautions \"educated\" and \"trendy\" Christians that despite their modern fashion, \"there is a devil out there that hates your guts... Spiritual... warfare is not a joke. You have to be serious\".\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a5c62313-ab4c_2.md (01:12-02:08)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 10:38 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10%3A38/>\r\n\r\nQ72. How does the lesson distinguish between normal human flaws and demonic influence?\r\nA. All human flaws are directly caused by demonic possession.\r\nB. Demons only influence physical sickness, while humans are solely responsible for moral sins.\r\nC. Demons cannot influence believers at all, so any flaw is strictly a medical issue.\r\nD. Man is fallen and can do wrong without the devil, but continuing in certain sins (like anger or stealing) can give an inroad to a spirit, making it a supernaturally influenced problem.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that while \"man is fallen... and does not always need the influence of the devil to do wrong,\" persistent sin (like holding onto wrath) gives \"an inroad to Satan... and now it becomes a spiritual problem\".\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a5c62313-ab4c_2.md (04:18-06:35)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Ephesians 4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+4/>\r\n\r\nQ73. What does Luke 13 illustrate about the spiritual cause behind the woman's physical condition?\r\nA. As suffering from a natural calcium deficiency.\r\nB. As bearing a cross of humility given by God.\r\nC. As being bound by a spirit of infirmity, showing a spiritual problem with a physical manifestation.\r\nD. As faking her illness for attention.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses Luke 13 to show that what \"looked like a normal physical condition\" was diagnosed by Jesus as a \"spirit of infirmity.\" It was a \"spiritual problem that had a physical manifestation\".\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a5c62313-ab4c_2.md (07:44-08:42)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Luke 13:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+13%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ74. In Mark 5, what evidence pointed to the Gadarene man's condition being demonic rather than just a natural psychological issue?\r\nA. He displayed supernatural strength, repeatedly breaking fetters and chains that nobody could tame.\r\nB. He suffered from frequent headaches.\r\nC. He spoke entirely in Latin.\r\nD. He requested a large sum of money to leave the city.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker highlights the Gadarene man's \"supernatural strength,\" noting that \"he had been bound many times with fetters and chains and he will pluck them asunder... nobody could tame him\".\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a5c62313-ab4c_2.md (15:37-16:38)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Mark 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+5/>\r\n\r\nQ75. What is the speaker's view on the use of doctors and medical science?\r\nA. Doctors are useless and should be avoided by true believers.\r\nB. Doctors are a blessing, but believers must recognize that some physical manifestations have spiritual sources that medicine cannot cure.\r\nC. Doctors can cure all spiritual problems with modern psychiatric drugs.\r\nD. Believers should only visit doctors if prayer fails after 40 days.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explicitly states, \"I love doctors. They're such a blessing to us... no sensible person will make derogatory remarks against doctors.\" However, he notes that \"not all these things, though physical in their manifestation, are physical in their source,\" meaning some issues require spiritual deliverance, not medicine.\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a5c62313-ab4c_2.md (14:27-15:37)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Mark 5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+5/>\r\n\r\nQ76. What served as the \"trigger\" for the evil spirit tormenting King Saul?\r\nA. He ate meat offered to idols.\r\nB. He listened to Goliath's boasts.\r\nC. He heard the women singing a song comparing his thousands of kills to David's ten thousands, sparking intense envy.\r\nD. He lost his armor in battle.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that Saul already had an evil spirit, \"but there was a trigger.\" That trigger occurred when a young lady sang, \"Saul has killed his thousands, David 10,000,\" sparking extreme envy that triggered the spirit.\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-738af920-21c6_2.md (01:18-02:20)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Samuel 18:6-10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Samuel+18%3A6-10/>\r\n\r\nQ77. How can unforgiveness act as an \"inroad\" for the devil, according to Paul in 2 Corinthians?\r\nA. Unforgiveness causes immediate physical death.\r\nB. Unforgiveness forces God to send an evil spirit to torment the believer.\r\nC. If believers do not forgive, Satan takes advantage of the situation to cause spiritual havoc.\r\nD. Unforgiveness nullifies a believer's salvation permanently.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Quoting 2 Corinthians, the speaker notes Paul tells the church to forgive an erring brother, \"let Satan has the advantage... If you don't forgive him, Satan will take advantage,\" highlighting unforgiveness as an inroad for demonic activity.\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-738af920-21c6_2.md (05:15-06:27)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 2:10–11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+2%3A10%E2%80%9311/>\r\n\r\nQ78. What does the story of the hostile university lecturer illustrate?\r\nA. That demon spirits can influence people to be unusually difficult or hostile, and addressing the spirit can bring immediate favor.\r\nB. That students should report difficult teachers to the police.\r\nC. That academic failure is always the result of a generational curse.\r\nD. That arguing loudly is the best way to get a passing grade.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker tells a story of a lecturer who repeatedly yelled at him. After the speaker commanded \"any spirit causing me to be out of favor... I cast it out,\" the lecturer immediately became polite and corrected his grade, illustrating that demons can influence interpersonal hostility.\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-738af920-21c6_2.md (08:06-10:21)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Ephesians 6:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+6%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ79. According to Mark 16:17, who is authorized to cast out devils?\r\nA. Only ordained pastors and prophets.\r\nB. Only believers who have fasted for at least 21 days.\r\nC. Every person who believes in the name of Jesus; it is not the esoteric reserve of the clergy.\r\nD. Only those who have attended a recognized seminary.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker emphasizes that casting out devils is a sign that \"shall follow them that believe. It's not for Pastor Irene... it's not the esoteric reserve of the clergy\".\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-738af920-21c6_2.md (11:19-12:10)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Mark 16:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+16%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ80. What are the three simple steps the speaker recommends for casting out demons?\r\nA. Fasting, shouting, and anointing with oil.\r\nB. Interrogation, binding, and sending into the abyss.\r\nC. Confidence in God, worship, and the glory touch.\r\nD. Diagnosing, prescribing, and laying on of hands.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker outlines three simple, effective steps that worked for him: \"Number one, I call it confidence in God... Number two, worship... And number three, what I call glory touch\".\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-738af920-21c6_2.md (14:56-15:47)\r\n\r\nQ81. Why does the lesson emphasize that believers must learn to \"boast in God\"?\r\nA. To intimidate other religions.\r\nB. Because boasting in God shows Him off, delights in His power, fuels audacity for warfare, and is a crucial part of devotion.\r\nC. Because boasting is required to receive financial prosperity.\r\nD. Because boasting distracts the devil from attacking.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker states that boasting in God is a \"crucial part of our devotion... You boast in God, you show him off to the world. You delight in his power... This is a crucial aspect of warfare\".\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-758530d2-b123_2.md (00:15-01:15)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Psalm 34:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+34%3A2/>; Psalm 44 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+44/>\r\n\r\nQ82. How does the spiritual perspective of humility differ from the natural social understanding, according to the teaching?\r\nA. Social humility requires speaking poorly of oneself, whereas spiritual humility embraces and boldly declares what the Word of God says about the believer's identity and power.\r\nB. Spiritual humility means never telling anyone you are a Christian.\r\nC. Social humility is always sinful, while spiritual humility demands physical prostration.\r\nD. There is no difference; both require avoiding any claims to power or authority.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker points out that while social contexts expect humility to look a certain modest way, in the kingdom, you must speak boldly: \"Don't you embrace who the word of God says you are. You talk big about your God.\" Failing to do so out of \"modesty\" misinterprets spiritual humility.\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-758530d2-b123_2.md (04:42-06:36)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Philippians 3:3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Philippians+3%3A3/>; Romans 15:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+15%3A17/>\r\n\r\nQ83. Why was Jesus’ reading of Isaiah 61 in the synagogue an example of spiritual confidence?\r\nA. He read it in a language no one else understood.\r\nB. He boldly declared, \"This day is this scripture fulfilled in your ears,\" actively embracing the identity and anointing described in the text.\r\nC. He read it quietly so as not to offend the Pharisees.\r\nD. He argued with the minister about the translation of the text.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker marvels at Jesus reading the profound claims of Isaiah 61 and then declaring, \"this day is this scripture fulfilled in your eyes.\" While culturally it might look like a \"show off,\" in the spiritual kingdom, \"anything you have to say it. You have to say it\".\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-758530d2-b123_2.md (08:14-09:57)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Isaiah 61:1–2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+61%3A1%E2%80%932/>; Luke 4:16–21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+4%3A16%E2%80%9321/>\r\n\r\nQ84. What role does worship play in spiritual warfare and manifesting the power of God?\r\nA. Worship distracts the believer from the pain of the problem.\r\nB. Worship is a \"power factor\" that stirs up the glory of God, changing the atmosphere so that demons leave.\r\nC. Worship is only meant to be used for giving thanks after the demon has already left.\r\nD. Worship must involve professional musicians to be effective against strong demons.\r\nANSWER: B\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker describes worship as a \"power factor\" that brings a \"stare of glory.\" When faced with violent demons, the speaker closed his eyes to worship, and \"the atmosphere in the room began to change,\" resulting in the demons leaving.\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-758530d2-b123_2.md (13:26-15:16)\r\n\r\nQ85. How does the lesson explain the concept of the \"glory touch\" using the analogy of electricity?\r\nA. You must shout to activate the power.\r\nB. Power is dangerous and should only be handled by experts.\r\nC. The power of God is present but needs to be transferred or \"plugged into\" via a medium, such as laying hands or using mantles/aprons.\r\nD. The glory touch only works if the person receiving it feels an electric shock.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses electricity as an analogy: \"there can be electricity supplied to a house and there is no sockets... You have to learn how to use power that is dead.\" Just as electricity transfers through conduction, God's power transfers through a medium like touch, mantles, or aprons.\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-758530d2-b123_2.md (15:16-16:19)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 19:11-12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+19%3A11-12/>\r\n\r\nQ86. What does the story of Paul’s aprons and handkerchiefs (Acts 19) demonstrate about casting out spirits?\r\nA. The spiritual science of casting out demons is not always dependent on speech; power can be transferred through a medium to expel demons.\r\nB. Demons are allergic to cotton fabric.\r\nC. Believers must always carry a handkerchief when preaching.\r\nD. Paul was too busy to pray for people personally.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker points to Acts 19:12 to show that \"the spiritual science behind is not always dependent on speech... Handkerchiefs don't talk... The demons left.\" This proves power can be transferred silently via a medium.\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a4e98998-978c_2.md (00:15-01:18)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 19:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+19%3A12/>\r\n\r\nQ87. Why does the speaker advise beginners to have other people around when casting out demons?\r\nA. Because demons only leave when a certain quota of believers is present.\r\nB. So the others can take notes on the demon's secrets.\r\nC. Because possessed individuals can sometimes become physically violent, and others can help hold them using the consciousness of God's glory.\r\nD. Because the beginner needs a choir to sing worship songs loudly.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker advises beginners to have at least two people around \"because sometimes these folks tend to get physical.\" If there is a physical struggle, those assisting can help hold the person using the \"glory of God flowing through your hands\" rather than engaging in a pure physical power tussle.\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a4e98998-978c_2.md (05:16-06:52)\r\n\r\nQ88. What should a believer do if a demon starts speaking and trying to have a conversation?\r\nA. Write down everything it says for theological research.\r\nB. Ask it how many other demons are in the person.\r\nC. Ignore it and ask it to leave, because demons lie and you do not need to enroll in their \"Bible school.\"\r\nD. Debate it using Old Testament scriptures until it surrenders.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: Reflecting on a past mistake of letting a demon speak for 45 minutes, the speaker warns, \"Demons lie... you don't need to enroll in their Bible school... I don't even ask questions. Just ask them to go\".\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a4e98998-978c_2.md (08:29-09:19)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Mark 1:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+1%3A25/>\r\n\r\nQ89. How can a believer often discern if someone has a demon, according to the speaker's experience?\r\nA. The person will have a foul physical odor.\r\nB. The person will be unable to enter a church building.\r\nC. The person will constantly speak in a deep voice.\r\nD. The person's eyes will often reveal a spiritual evasion, lacking contact, or supernatural perception will provide a \"knowing.\"\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker shares that alongside supernatural perception (\"a knowing\"), \"the eyes of every human being reveal all that there is to know... when someone is evasive... have little or no eye contact, the same thing spiritually\".\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a4e98998-978c_2.md (11:04-11:57)\r\n\r\nQ90. Why is it critical to put a person on a devotional program and ensure they are filled with the Spirit after a deliverance?\r\nA. Because it generates income for the church.\r\nB. Because it is a legal requirement for church membership.\r\nC. Because demons do not die; if the person's spiritual state is not improved and filled, the demons will return.\r\nD. Because they need to learn how to preach immediately.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker strongly advises putting the delivered person on a devotional program and making sure they are born again because \"Demons don't die... if the person... does not do better than the person has been doing before, all your work will be undone and even worse\".\r\nREF: HOW-TO-CAST-OUT-DEMONS-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a4e98998-978c_2.md (15:18-16:13)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Matthew 12:43-45 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+12%3A43-45/>\r\n\r\nQ91. What are the two streams by which God moves in the life of a man, according to the teaching?\r\nA. The stream of faith and the stream of the anointing.\r\nB. Prayer and Fasting.\r\nC. Praise and Worship.\r\nD. The Word and the Sacraments.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker declares, \"There are two streams by which God does whatever he will do in and through the life of a man... There is the stream of faith and there is the stream of the anointing\".\r\nREF: MANIFESTING-THE-PRESENCE-OF-GOD-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-916daa08-bd44_2.md (00:15-01:02)\r\n\r\nQ92. Why does the speaker say the Word of God has \"sacred impact in the lives of sacred people\"?\r\nA. Because the Bible only works for flawless individuals.\r\nB. Because only ordained ministers can read the Bible effectively.\r\nC. Because you must guard reverence for the Word and read it as God's true Word, not a fairy tale, since God and His Word cannot be separated.\r\nD. Because the Bible must be read in its original Greek or Hebrew to be sacred.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker emphasizes that people must \"guard the reverence for the Word of God\" and read it \"like it is indeed the Word of God. Not some fairy tale.\" Using Revelation 19, he shows that Jesus's name is the Word of God, meaning \"you cannot separate God from His Word\".\r\nREF: MANIFESTING-THE-PRESENCE-OF-GOD-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-916daa08-bd44_2.md (02:43-03:31)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Revelation 19:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Revelation+19%3A11/>\r\n\r\nQ93. What does the \"stream of faith\" require a believer to do?\r\nA. Know the Word, believe the Word, and practice the Word with specificity to the challenge.\r\nB. Wait passively for a feeling of power before acting.\r\nC. Speak in tongues for an hour before making a decision.\r\nD. Memorize the entire New Testament.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explicitly lists the requirements for the stream of faith: \"Number one, you must know the Word of God. Number two, you must believe the Word of God. And number three, you must practice the Word of God.\" Later, he adds that there \"must be a specificity to the release of your faith\".\r\nREF: MANIFESTING-THE-PRESENCE-OF-GOD-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-916daa08-bd44_2.md (02:43-03:31)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: James 1:22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/James+1%3A22/>\r\n\r\nQ94. What correction was Jesus making when He told Peter to \"have faith in God\" regarding the withered fig tree?\r\nA. He was correcting Peter's lack of agricultural knowledge.\r\nB. He was correcting Peter for speaking too loudly.\r\nC. He was scolding Peter for pointing at the tree.\r\nD. He was correcting the fact that Peter had to see the physical change (the dried-up tree) before he believed the Word. Faith means siding with the Word even when evidence seems contrary.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that Jesus's statement \"have faith in God\" was correcting Peter because \"Peter had to see the change to believe the Word.\" True faith means believing and siding with the Word \"even when the evidence seems contrary,\" rather than waiting for visible proof.\r\nREF: MANIFESTING-THE-PRESENCE-OF-GOD-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-7bba400c-ea54_2.md (03:08-04:13)\r\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Mark 11:22–24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+11%3A22%E2%80%9324/>\r\n\r\nQ95. How does the \"anointing\" differ from \"faith\" in how it deals with challenges?\r\nA. The anointing requires quoting ten specific scriptures, while faith requires only one.\r\nB. The anointing works much slower than faith.\r\nC. Faith addresses specific challenges directly; the anointing rises to an energy level where challenges simply cannot survive and bow on their own.\r\nD. The anointing is for physical healing, while faith is for financial provision.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker contrasts the two: \"faith... you apply it directly. And specifically, the anointing has a kind of indifference... The anointing simply rises to an energy level where those challenges cannot survive. And then the challenges on their own, weighing out\".\r\nREF: MANIFESTING-THE-PRESENCE-OF-GOD-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-7bba400c-ea54_2.md (04:49-06:03)\r\n\r\nQ96. What analogy is used to explain the anointing rising above challenges?\r\nA. A lion roaring to scare away hyenas.\r\nB. A submarine diving deep below a storm.\r\nC. A hawk attacking an eagle; the eagle does nothing but ascend to a height where the hawk cannot breathe or hold on.\r\nD. A tree shedding its leaves in winter to survive the cold.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: To illustrate the anointing, the speaker uses the analogy of a hawk attacking an eagle. The eagle didn't fight back; it \"just continued to ascend\" to a height where the hawk \"couldn't breathe\" and had to let go.\r\nREF: MANIFESTING-THE-PRESENCE-OF-GOD-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-7bba400c-ea54_2.md (06:09-06:45)\r\n\r\nQ97. What does the story of the viper in Acts 28 illustrate about the anointing?\r\nA. The anointing is like a fire that exposes strangers (demons) hiding in the environment, forcing them to manifest and leave.\r\nB. The anointing protects believers from snake bites.\r\nC. The anointing requires gathering physical wood to burn.\r\nD. The anointing is only effective on islands.\r\nANSWER: A\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses the story of the viper coming out of the wood when the fire came up to explain that \"The anointing exposes strangers... when you see someone who needs help, let them learn to stir up the environment... The demons will check out on their own\".\r\nREF: MANIFESTING-THE-PRESENCE-OF-GOD-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-bfa24448-378d_2.md (00:15-01:31)\r\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 28 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+28/>\r\n\r\nQ98. What are the primary ways to stir up the anointing, as taught in the lesson?\r\nA. Reading historical books, sleeping, and quiet contemplation.\r\nB. Fasting for 40 days, traveling to Israel, and changing your wardrobe.\r\nC. Prayer (to set the power in motion), bold confession (to pull the trigger), and spiritual response.\r\nD. Asking a pastor to pray for you daily.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker outlines three ways to stir the anointing: 1. Prayer (\"exercise to set it in motion\"), 2. Simple confession (\"pulls the trigger\"), and 3. Response (learning to see and jump at what God is doing).\r\nREF: MANIFESTING-THE-PRESENCE-OF-GOD-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-bfa24448-378d_2.md (03:36-09:01)\r\n\r\nQ99. What does the story of Elijah's servant seeing a cloud the size of a hand teach about \"response\"?\r\nA. You should ignore small signs and wait for massive miracles.\r\nB. You should report back exactly what you see without interpretation.\r\nC. Weather patterns are unpredictable and cannot be trusted.\r\nD. You must learn to see and jump at what God is doing even in seed form, receiving small manifestations with rejoicing to stir up a greater flow.\r\nANSWER: D\r\nEXPLANATION: Using the story of Elijah's servant, the speaker emphasizes that \"a man of God knows how to interpret\" the small cloud. He advises, \"never call the small signals. Small. There is a spiritual intelligence to jump when the river is dead... Receive it with rejoicing\" to fan the flames.\r\nREF: MANIFESTING-THE-PRESENCE-OF-GOD-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-bfa24448-378d_2.md (09:01-10:19)\r\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Kings 18:41-45 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Kings+18%3A41-45/>\r\n\r\nQ100. How does \"heartfelt worship\" function when stirring up the anointing in the face of a violent demonic manifestation?\r\nA. It provides background music to calm the victim's nerves.\r\nB. It is used to beg the demon to leave peacefully.\r\nC. It focuses entirely on God rather than the problem, charging the atmosphere with His presence until anything inconsistent with that presence (the demon) bows and leaves.\r\nD. It distracts the audience so they don't see the struggle.\r\nANSWER: C\r\nEXPLANATION: The speaker shares a story of closing his eyes to worship during a violent demonic manifestation. He explains that worship is \"not directed towards problems.\" By focusing purely on God, \"the atmosphere in the room began to change... Everything inconsistent with the presence of God bows to the presence of God\".\r\nREF: MANIFESTING-THE-PRESENCE-OF-GOD-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-bfa24448-378d_2.md (13:33-14:50)\n\nDECK: Discipleship Experience (Section 12) Church History\n\nQ1. Which distinction does the lesson on Baptism make between the Lutheran and Reformed positions?\nA. Lutherans reject infant baptism, while Reformed churches require it for salvation.\nB. Lutherans view baptism as a means of grace, while the Reformed view baptism as a seal of God's covenant.\nC. Both groups teach that baptism is purely symbolic and has no relationship to grace.\nD. Both groups reject infant baptism because personal faith must always precede baptism.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The Lutheran Church teaches that baptism is a means of grace, whereas the Reformed position views it as a symbolic sign and seal of God's covenant.\nREF: BAPTISM II — [04:52], [06:15] - [07:48]\n\nQ2. What did the Nicene Creed affirm in the controversy described in the lesson on Church History?\nA. That salvation comes through secret knowledge\nB. That Jesus was adopted as God's Son\nC. The divinity of Christ\nD. That the Father, Son and Holy Spirit are three modes of one person\nANSWER: C\nREF: JESUS-SON-OF-MAN-II — [04:47–05:43]\n\nQ3. What does Acts 10:2 contribute to the lesson's teaching about sacrifice?\nA. Cornelius' prayers and alms were presented as having come up as a memorial before God.\nB. Cornelius' financial giving was the only reason the angel appeared to him.\nC. Cornelius' sacrifice made him born again before the gospel was preached to him.\nD. Cornelius' alms were presented as more important than his prayer life.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: Acts 10 reveals that Cornelius' prayers and sacrificial giving (alms) came up together as a memorial before God, attracting angelic visitation.\nREF: HOW TO ATTRACT ANGELS V — [05:23] - [06:29]\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 10:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10%3A2/>\n\nQ4. What did Pelagius teach that the church rejected?\nA. Humans could live sinless lives without the aid of divine grace.\nB. Salvation could only come through hidden knowledge.\nC. Jesus was not truly human.\nD. God exists in three distinct persons.\nANSWER: A\n\nQ5. Cornelius had not been born again, was not full of the Spirit and most likely had never read a book about angels. What does his example establish?\nA. Knowledge about angels is not necessary for a life of reverence to God.\nB. Angelic encounters only happen to people outside the church.\nC. A person must first understand spiritual realities before God can engage with him.\nD. Being outside the church automatically makes a person more sensitive to angels.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: Cornelius proves that a person can attract angelic ministry purely through a lifestyle of genuine reverence and the fear of the Lord, without prior theological knowledge of angels.\nREF: HOW TO ATTRACT ANGELS I — [13:17] - [14:06]; HOW TO ATTRACT ANGELS II — [03:27] - [04:50]\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Psalm 34:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+34%3A7/>\n\nQ6. Which sequence best supports the lesson's argument that Christianity's roots in Africa go back beyond the colonial era?\nA. Portuguese explorers arrived in Africa, missionaries followed them, and African kingdoms adopted Christianity.\nB. Jesus fled to Egypt, Simon of Cyrene carried Jesus' cross, and the Ethiopian eunuch heard the gospel and was baptised.\nC. Constantine legalised Christianity, founded Constantinople, and missionaries later travelled to Africa.\nD. Martin Luther began the Reformation, the Jesuits expanded Catholicism, and missionaries then entered Africa.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: Biblical accounts of Jesus fleeing to Egypt, Simon of Cyrene carrying the cross, and the Ethiopian eunuch's conversion prove Christianity has deep African roots predating European colonialism.\nREF: EARLY CHURCH II — [06:16] - [07:29]\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Matthew 2:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+2%3A13/>; Hosea 11:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hosea+11%3A1/>; Acts 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+8/>\n\nQ7. How does Psalm 104:4 fit into the lesson's explanation of angels?\nA. It teaches that every manifestation of fire is an angel.\nB. It describes angels as spirits and ministers and is connected to the manifestation of fire.\nC. It teaches that angels can only minister through fire.\nD. It shows that angels are symbols rather than actual spiritual beings.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: Psalm 104:4 establishes that God makes His angels spirits and ministers, which the lesson connects directly to angelic manifestations of fire.\nREF: HOW TO ATTRACT ANGELS IV — [01:34] - [02:52]\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Psalm 104:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+104%3A4/>\n\nQ8. What does the lesson identify as the thing that truly points to the salvation of a person?\nA. Completion of a baptismal class\nB. Being baptized by immersion\nC. Believing loyalty\nD. Being recognized by a church leader as a believer\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: True salvation is evidenced not merely by a temporary profession of faith, but by a consistent \"believing loyalty\" that endures.\nREF: BAPTISM IV.mp4 — [06:41] - [08:02]\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 3:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+3%3A14/>\n\nQ9. What was Arius' main argument concerning Jesus?\nA. Jesus was not divine in the same way as the Father and was not co-eternal.\nB. Jesus was only a human prophet and never existed before His birth.\nC. Jesus was fully divine and co-eternal with the Father.\nD. Jesus became God's Son only after His resurrection.\nANSWER: A\nREF: JESUS-SON-OF-MAN-II — [04:47–05:43]\n\nQ10. What did Docetism teach about Jesus?\nA. Jesus was fully human but was adopted as God's Son later in His life.\nB. Jesus was one person appearing in three different modes.\nC. Jesus was divine but was not the same as the Father.\nD. Jesus only appeared to be human, so His body, suffering and death were considered illusions.\nANSWER: D\nEXPLANATION: Docetism is the heretical belief that Jesus only appeared to be human, treating His physical body, suffering, and death as mere illusions.\nREF: EARLY CHURCH I — [04:52]\nBIBLE: Added from online research: John 1:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A14/>\n\nQ11. What connection does Daniel 10:12 establish between prayer and angelic activity in the lesson?\nA. Daniel's prayer was heard, and the angel said he had come because of his words.\nB. Daniel's angel came because Daniel had achieved a high level of spiritual maturity.\nC. Daniel's experience shows that angels respond only to prophetic declarations.\nD. Daniel's experience shows that prayer becomes unnecessary once an angel is assigned to a person.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: Daniel 10:12 illustrates that angels respond to the prayers of believers, as the angel explicitly stated he came because of Daniel's words.\nREF: HOW TO ATTRACT ANGELS III — [12:57] - [14:24]\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Daniel 10:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Daniel+10%3A12/>\n\nQ12. Which set of questions does the lesson identify as the major areas of disagreement surrounding water baptism?\nA. Who performs baptism, where it is performed, how often it is performed, and what language is used\nB. The mode of baptism, who should be baptized, the purpose of baptism, and whether baptism is regenerative\nC. Whether baptism should happen before or after communion, who should administer communion, and whether communion saves\nD. Whether baptism should happen publicly, privately, annually, or only after a baptismal class\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: Historically, controversies on water baptism center around its mode, the proper subjects for it, its purpose, and whether it has a regenerative nature.\nREF: BAPTISM I — [01:35] - [02:50]\n\nQ13. What role did figures such as Ignatius of Antioch and Irenaeus play?\nA. They introduced Christianity to the Roman Empire for the first time.\nB. They replaced the apostles as the founders of the Christian faith.\nC. They defended Christian doctrine and affirmed the authority of the apostles.\nD. They led the Protestant Reformation against the Catholic Church.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: During the second century, early church figures like Ignatius and Irenaeus wrote to defend orthodox Christian doctrine and uphold apostolic authority.\nREF: EARLY CHURCH I — [03:46]\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Jude 3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Jude+3/>\n\nQ14. Why does the lesson describe Abraham's encounter as an \"honor test\"?\nA. Abraham already knew exactly who the visitors were before he welcomed them.\nB. Abraham had to respond honorably to what appeared to be an ordinary situation.\nC. Abraham was required to perform a special act of worship before the visitors could speak to him.\nD. Abraham's encounter depended on him recognising the visitors as angels immediately.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: Abraham's encounter functioned as an honor test because the divine visitors appeared as ordinary passersby, requiring Abraham to proactively demonstrate honorable hospitality.\nREF: HOW TO ATTRACT ANGELS II — [08:44] - [09:47]\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 18:1-8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+18%3A1-8/>\n\nQ15. Why did the early church have to define its beliefs more clearly as Christianity grew?\nA. The Roman Empire required every Christian church to use the same form of worship.\nB. Growing heresies and false teachings threatened the unity of the faith.\nC. The apostles had stopped teaching Christians about Jesus.\nD. Christianity had already become the official religion of the Roman Empire.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The early church was compelled to solidify its doctrinal beliefs to combat emerging heresies and false teachings that jeopardized their unity.\nREF: EARLY CHURCH I — [03:46]\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 20:29-31 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+20%3A29-31/>\n\nQ16. What is the significance of Colossians 2:12 within the lesson's discussion of baptism?\nA. It connects baptism with being buried and raised with Christ through faith.\nB. It teaches that water itself removes sin from the believer.\nC. It establishes infant baptism as a requirement of the New Covenant.\nD. It teaches that baptism is the means by which a person first receives faith.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: Colossians 2:12 is used to show that baptism is a spiritual operation depicting the believer being buried and raised to new life with Christ through faith.\nREF: BAPTISM III — [11:44] - [12:05]\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Colossians 2:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+2%3A12/>\n\nQ17. Why was the Council of Nicaea convened?\nA. To decide whether Christianity should spread into Africa\nB. To settle the controversy over the divinity of Christ\nC. To establish Constantinople as the new capital of the Roman Empire\nD. To begin the Protestant Reformation\nANSWER: B\nREF: JESUS-SON-OF-MAN-II — [04:47–05:43]\n\nQ18. According to Psalm 103:20 as it is used in the lesson, what are angels said to respond to?\nA. The strength of a believer's emotions\nB. The voice of God's Word\nC. Whatever a believer desires fervently\nD. The number of times a believer attends church\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: Psalm 103:20 reveals that angels excel in strength and execute their ministry by responding to and heeding the voice of God's Word.\nREF: HOW TO ATTRACT ANGELS III — [02:29] - [03:26]\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Psalm 103:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+103%3A20/>\n\nQ19. Which combination of passages is used in the lesson to support the description of baptism as immersion?\nA. Matthew 3:16, Acts 8:38-39 and John 3:23\nB. Romans 6:1-4, Ephesians 5:25-26 and Ezekiel 36:25-27\nC. Matthew 28:19, Acts 2:38 and Titus 3:5\nD. Colossians 2:11, John 7:37 and 1 Peter 3:18\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: These three passages are cited to argue for full immersion because they mention coming \"up out of the water,\" going \"down into the water,\" and the requirement for \"much water.\"\nREF: BAPTISM I — [06:16] - [07:24]\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Matthew 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+3%3A16/>; Acts 8:38–39 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+8%3A38%E2%80%9339/>; John 3:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A23/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Colossians 2:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+2%3A12/>\n\nQ20. What did Sabellianism teach about the Father, Son and Holy Spirit?\nA. They are three different gods working together.\nB. They are three distinct persons who are equal and eternal.\nC. They are three modes of the same God.\nD. The Son and Spirit are created beings who serve the Father.\nANSWER: C\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Deuteronomy 6:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Deuteronomy+6%3A4/>; Genesis 2:24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+2%3A24/>\nREF: TRINITY-II — [01:05; 02:41–03:40]\n\nQ21. Which of the following correctly identifies the four keys to engaging the angelic dimension?\nA. Faith, fasting, prophecy and sacrifice\nB. Reverence, honor, fasting and prayer\nC. Prayer, prophecy, sacrifice and worship\nD. Reverence, the Word, prayer and sacrifice\nANSWER: D\nEXPLANATION: The four keys to attracting and engaging angelic ministry outlined in the teaching are reverence, the Word of God, prayer, and sacrifice.\nREF: HOW TO ATTRACT ANGELS I — [12:12]; HOW TO ATTRACT ANGELS III — [01:27], [12:57]; HOW TO ATTRACT ANGELS V — [05:23] - [06:29]\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Daniel 10:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Daniel+10%3A12/>; Acts 10 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10/>; Judges 13:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Judges+13%3A19/>\n\nQ22. What did the Edict of Milan accomplish according to the lesson?\nA. It legalised Christianity and ended decades of persecution.\nB. It made Christianity the only religion in the Roman Empire.\nC. It divided Christianity into Eastern and Western churches.\nD. It established the Nicene Creed as the official Roman constitution.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The Edict of Milan in 313 AD officially legalized Christianity and brought an end to decades of state-sponsored persecution.\nREF: EARLY CHURCH I — [04:52]\n\nQ23. What sequence of events does the lesson give concerning Constantine?\nA. He legalised Christianity, then saw a cross, then became a Christian.\nB. He founded Constantinople, then legalised Christianity, then fought his pivotal battle.\nC. He saw a cross before a pivotal battle, won the battle, embraced Christianity, and later issued the Edict of Milan.\nD. He became a bishop, convened the Council of Nicaea, and then founded Constantinople.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: Constantine first saw a cross before a major battle, emerged victorious, embraced the Christian faith, and subsequently issued the Edict of Milan.\nREF: EARLY CHURCH I — [03:46] - [04:52]\n\nQ24. What is the significance of the Greek word baptizo?\nA. It is presented as meaning sprinkling, which supports the practice of pouring.\nB. It is presented as meaning to immerse or submerge, which supports immersion.\nC. It is presented as meaning to cleanse spiritually, which proves baptism regenerates.\nD. It is presented as meaning to dedicate an infant to God.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The original Greek word \"baptizo\" explicitly means to immerse, dip, or submerge, linguistically supporting the practice of full-body immersion.\nREF: BAPTISM I — [04:05]\n\nQ25. What did Gnosticism teach about salvation?\nA. Salvation comes through faith in the death and resurrection of Christ.\nB. Salvation comes through keeping the traditions of the apostles.\nC. Salvation comes through secret knowledge that frees the divine spark within a person.\nD. Salvation comes through human effort without divine grace.\nANSWER: C\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 1 John 1:1–3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+1%3A1%E2%80%933/>\nREF: DID-JESUS-RISE-FROM-THE-DEAD-III — [00:14; 03:15]\n\nQ26. According to the lesson, what are the two meanings given for the word \"seraph\"?\nA. Messenger and minister\nB. Burning one and serpent\nC. Spirit and flame\nD. Guardian and throne\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The teaching explains that the word \"seraph\" has a dual meaning: \"burning ones\" who protect God's presence, and \"serpents,\" as seen in fiery serpents used for judgment.\nREF: HOW TO ATTRACT ANGELS IV — [05:26] - [07:14], [13:14] - [14:47]\n\nQ27. What practice particularly provoked Martin Luther's objections to the Catholic Church in 1517?\nA. The sale of indulgences\nB. The use of monasteries\nC. The appointment of bishops\nD. The translation of Scripture\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: Martin Luther's primary objection in his 95 theses was directed against the corrupt practice of selling indulgences.\nREF: EARLY CHURCH II — [03:46] - [05:05]\n\nQ28. What is the significance of Ezekiel 36 and 1 Corinthians 6 in the lesson's explanation of the symbolism of water?\nA. To prove that physical water itself removes sin.\nB. To show that water is repeatedly used as a metaphor for the cleansing work of the Spirit in the believer.\nC. To establish that every reference to water in Scripture refers to baptism.\nD. To prove that baptism and regeneration are exactly the same event.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: These passages are cited to demonstrate that water is a biblical metaphor representing the inward, cleansing power of the Holy Spirit rather than a literal physical washing.\nREF: BAPTISM III — [16:19] - [18:47]\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Ezekiel 36:25–27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ezekiel+36%3A25%E2%80%9327/>; 1 Corinthians 6:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+6%3A11/> | Also cited in lesson segment: John 7:37 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+7%3A37/>; Ephesians 5:25, 26 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+5%3A25%2C+26/>; John 1:32–33 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A32%E2%80%9333/>\n\nQ29. What event does the lesson identify as setting the foundation for what would become Christianity?\nA. Jesus' ascension into heaven\nB. The resurrection of Jesus Christ three days after His crucifixion\nC. The descent of the Holy Spirit at Pentecost\nD. The spread of the apostles across the Roman Empire\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The lesson explicitly identifies the resurrection of Jesus Christ, three days after His crucifixion, as the foundational event of Christianity.\nREF: EARLY CHURCH I — [01:24] - [02:30]\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 15:14-17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+15%3A14-17/>\n\nQ30. Why is the ministry of angels relevant to the believer's work in Christ according to the lesson?\nA. Angels become more powerful when believers develop relationships with them.\nB. Angels take the place of the Holy Spirit in ministry.\nC. Angels are ministering spirits sent forth to minister for those who will inherit salvation.\nD. Every believer must see his assigned angel before he can minister effectively.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: Angels are highly relevant to the believer's work because God has provided them as ministering spirits to assist and work on behalf of the heirs of salvation.\nREF: HOW TO ATTRACT ANGELS I — [04:35] - [05:27]\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 1:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A14/>\n\nQ31. According to Hebrews 12 as explained in the lesson, what is the believer's \"new address\" or location in the realm of the Spirit?\nA. A physical mountain that burns with fire and darkness, similar to Sinai.\nB. Mount Zion, the city of the living God, which includes an innumerable company of angels.\nC. A literal, geographical recreation of the Garden of Eden in the Middle East.\nD. A solitary place where believers must operate completely alone.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker cites Hebrews 12:18-22, explaining that believers have not come to a physical mount that can be touched, but rather to \"Mount Zion, the city of the living God... And to what? An innumerable company of angels,\" defining this as the believer's \"new environment\" and \"new address\".\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-62b14bd4-1716.md (02:12-03:41)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 12:18–22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+12%3A18%E2%80%9322/>\n\nQ32. According to Hebrews 1:14, why are angels sent forth?\nA. To punish believers when they sin.\nB. To govern physical nations and their leaders.\nC. To minister for those who will inherit salvation.\nD. To replace the Holy Spirit in the New Testament.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: Quoting Hebrews 1:14, the speaker states, \"Are they not all ministering spirits sent forth to minister for those who will inherit salvation?\" The speaker emphasizes that God knows a believer's walk cannot be accomplished without angels, so He made them available.\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-62b14bd4-1716.md (04:35-05:27)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 1:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A14/>\n\nQ33. What does the speaker identify as the *first* of the four keys to activating the ministry of angels?\nA. Fasting for 40 days and nights.\nB. Reverence for the Lord.\nC. Learning the specific names of archangels.\nD. Studying ancient Hebrew texts.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explicitly lists the first key: \"all right, so four keys to activating the ministry of angels. Number one... Very simple. Reverence for the Lord. You want to see angels walk in your life, revere God\".\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-62b14bd4-1716.md (12:12-13:17)\n\nQ34. How does the story of Cornelius demonstrate the importance of reverence in attracting angels?\nA. Cornelius was a highly educated Jewish priest who studied angelology for years.\nB. Cornelius had to be fully born again and filled with the Spirit before an angel would visit him.\nC. Even though Cornelius wasn't born again or full of the Spirit at the time, his genuine fear and reverence for the Lord created a conducive environment for angelic visitation.\nD. Cornelius learned a secret chant from a herbalist to summon an angel.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses Cornelius as an example of reverence, noting that although he wasn't born again or full of the Spirit at the time, the Bible says he was \"one who feared the Lord with his household,\" proving that \"just the fear of the Lord\" and genuine reverence creates an environment for angels.\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-62b14bd4-1716.md (13:17-14:38)\n\nQ41. According to the lesson, what significant event happened around 312 AD that changed the course of Christian history?\nA. The Apostle Paul was executed in Rome.\nB. Emperor Nero blamed Christians for the Great Fire of Rome.\nC. Emperor Constantine had a vision of a cross in the sky, embraced Christianity, and later issued the Edict of Milan to legalize the faith.\nD. The Council of Nicaea officially established the New Testament canon.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The transcript states that in \"312 AD when Constantine... saw a cross in the sky and heard the words in this sign, conquer... he became the first Roman Emperor to embrace Christianity,\" leading to the Edict of Milan in 313 AD which legalized the faith.\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-e41e9f8e-c746.md (03:46-04:52)\n\nQ42. What was the core belief of the heresy known as \"Docetism\"?\nA. That Jesus only seemed human, and that His body, suffering, and death were all illusions because He was pure spirit.\nB. That Jesus was a regular human who achieved divinity through good works.\nC. That the God of the Old Testament was evil and different from the God of the New Testament.\nD. That believers must be physically circumcised to receive salvation.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The lesson defines Docetism as \"the belief that Jesus only seemed human, that his body, his suffering, his death were all illusions. Proponents said he was pure spirit, untouched by physical power\".\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-e41e9f8e-c746.md (04:52-05:07)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 John 4:2-3 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+John+4%3A2-3/>\n\nQ43. What major event in 1054 AD caused a defining split in the Christian world?\nA. The Protestant Reformation.\nB. The Council of Trent.\nC. The Great Schism, which divided the Eastern Orthodox Church and the Roman Catholic Church.\nD. The crowning of Charlemagne as Holy Roman Emperor.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that in \"1054 AD, a defining moment occurred. The Great Schism... caused this split between the Eastern Orthodox Church and the Roman Catholic Church\" due to theological disputes and disagreements over papal authority.\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-2a37207c-dbfb.md (02:35-03:46)\n\nQ44. What are two early, significant biblical connections between Africa and the Christian narrative mentioned in the lesson?\nA. The conversion of the Apostle Paul in Egypt and the writing of the Book of Revelation in Carthage.\nB. The flight of Joseph, Mary, and infant Jesus to Egypt, and Simon of Cyrene carrying the cross of Jesus.\nC. The construction of the first monastery in Sudan and the translation of the Bible into Swahili.\nD. The missionary journeys of Peter to Ethiopia and John to Libya.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The transcript highlights that the first significant connection happened when \"Joseph, Mary and the infant Jesus fled to Egypt\" (Matthew 2:13). Another presence is \"Simon of Cyrene, a man from eastern Libya who was compelled to carry the cross of Jesus\".\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-2a37207c-dbfb.md (06:16-07:29)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Matthew 2:13 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+2%3A13/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Hosea 11:1 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hosea+11%3A1/>; Acts 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+8/>\n\nQ45. How did the abolition of the transatlantic slave trade impact the spread of Christianity in West Africa?\nA. It caused European missionaries to completely abandon West Africa.\nB. It led rescued Africans, such as the Yoruba who settled in Sierra Leone (the \"Saroz\"), to embrace Christianity and return to their homelands as missionaries.\nC. It forced African rulers to revert entirely to traditional religions.\nD. It resulted in the immediate translation of the Bible into all 500 Nigerian languages.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that the abolition of the slave trade gave the movement wings. \"Rescued Africans. Many Yoruba settled in Sierra Leone, Embraced Christianity and returned home as missionaries. These were the Saroz,\" leading to figures like Bishop Samuel Ajayi Crowther.\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-29efed0f-8465.md (05:13-06:37)\n\nQ46. What was the significance of Josiah Olunowo Oshitelu's \"Church of the Lord Aladura\" founded in 1925?\nA. It was a European missionary strategy designed to control local populations.\nB. It successfully prevented the spread of Islam in northern Nigeria.\nC. It represented an authentically African response to faith, emphasizing healings, visions, and fervent prayer, independent of European invention.\nD. It translated the first complete Bible into the Yoruba language.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The lesson highlights that the Aladura churches were not European inventions. They were \"authentically African responses to faith... known for their white garments and deep emphasis on healings, visions and fervent prayer\".\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-29efed0f-8465.md (09:20-10:39)\n\nQ47. What specific event in 1985 catalyzed the formation of the Pentecostal Fellowship of Nigeria (PFN)?\nA. The burning of 152 churches during the Kafanchan crisis.\nB. The arrival of Pa Sidney Granville Elton from England.\nC. Reverend Uma Ukpai's Greater Lagos Crusade of thanks, which had an overwhelming turnout of prayer volunteers.\nD. The first publication of a Yoruba newspaper by Reverend Henry Townsend.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes that the PFN was founded after \"Reverend Uma Okbai's Greater Lagos Crusade of thanks... the overwhelming Turnout, more than 23,000 prayer volunteers, gave birth to something bigger... What began as prayer support soon metamorphosed into a national body\".\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-11fa9bac-f5a5.md (00:15-01:36)\n\nQ48. How does the lesson describe the current state of Christianity regarding \"reverse missions\"?\nA. African Christians are planting churches globally across Europe, Asia, and the Americas, transforming the evangelized into the evangelists.\nB. European missionaries are returning to Africa in record numbers to rebuild colonial churches.\nC. African nations are sending diplomats to the Vatican to ask for more priests.\nD. Christians are increasingly abandoning modern technology in favor of traditional village missions.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The speaker states that with independence, African Christians \"were no longer the evangelized. They were the evangelists. This reverse mission has seen Africans planting churches across Europe, Asia and the Americas\".\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-11fa9bac-f5a5.md (04:31-06:04)\n\nQ49. Why does the speaker argue that the linguistic meaning of the Greek word *baptizo* supports baptism by immersion?\nA. Because *baptizo* explicitly means \"to wash a newborn baby.\"\nB. Because *baptizo* means \"to immerse, to dip or to submerge,\" whereas the Greek word *rantizo* would have been used if sprinkling was intended.\nC. Because *baptizo* was originally a Latin word that meant \"to sprinkle holy water.\"\nD. Because *baptizo* translates directly to \"salvation.\"\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains the linguistic argument: the Greek word *baptizo* \"means to immerse, to deep or to submerge... if sprinkling was what was intended, they would have used that Greek word. Rantizo is the Greek word for sprinkling\".\nREF: BAPTISM-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-71aba375-058c.md (04:05-05:07)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Matthew 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+3%3A16/>; Acts 8:38, 39 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+8%3A38%2C+39/>\n\nQ50. According to the lesson, what is the Roman Catholic Church's official view on the regenerative nature of baptism?\nA. They believe baptism is purely symbolic and has no spiritual power.\nB. They believe baptism is only a public declaration for adults.\nC. They believe baptism is sacramental, washes away original sin, imparts sanctifying grace, and is necessary for entry into the eternal Beatitudes.\nD. They believe baptism should only be performed by full immersion.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker details the Catholic view: \"They believe that it is sacramental, meaning it imparts grace... that baptism washes away original sin... The Church does not know of any other means other than baptism that assures entry into the eternal Beatitudes\".\nREF: BAPTISM-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-71aba375-058c.md (09:44-10:35)\n\nQ51. What distinction is drawn in the lesson between the Reformed view of baptism and the Roman Catholic/Lutheran view?\nA. The Reformed view holds that baptism actually grants forgiveness of sins, while Catholics believe it is merely symbolic.\nB. The Reformed view believes baptism is a sign and a seal of God's covenant, but is not regenerative in itself, whereas Catholics and Lutherans believe baptism saves or grants forgiveness.\nC. The Reformed view insists that only adults can be baptized, rejecting infant baptism entirely.\nD. The Reformed view teaches that baptism must be performed by sprinkling, while Catholics insist on immersion.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker contrasts the views: Lutherans and Catholics believe baptism \"works Forgiveness of sins\" and \"washes away original sin.\" In contrast, the Reformed view states that baptism does not save, but \"is a sign and a seal of God's covenant, but is not regenerative in itself\".\nREF: BAPTISM-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8ecea2b2-7835.md (04:52-07:48)\n\nQ52. How does the Baptist view of baptism differ fundamentally from the Roman Catholic, Eastern Orthodox, and Reformed views regarding *who* should be baptized?\nA. Baptists believe that infants must be baptized on the eighth day.\nB. Baptists believe that only government officials can be baptized.\nC. Baptists believe that baptism requires personal faith and confession, so only believers are baptized (no infant baptism).\nD. Baptists believe that whole households must be baptized at the same time.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: While Catholics, Orthodox, and Reformed churches all practice infant baptism (for varying theological reasons), the speaker points out that in the Baptist view, \"Are infants to be baptized? No. Baptism requires personal faith and confession. Only believers are baptized\".\nREF: BAPTISM-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8ecea2b2-7835.md (09:17-10:18)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 2:38 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+2%3A38/>; Acts 8:36–38 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+8%3A36%E2%80%9338/>\n\nQ53. According to the video commentary by John Piper presented in the lesson, what is the imagery of baptism most fundamentally about?\nA. It is fundamentally about washing away physical dirt.\nB. It is fundamentally about burial, death, and resurrection, where the believer is united by faith to Christ's death and raised to walk in newness of life.\nC. It is fundamentally about becoming a formal member of a local church denomination.\nD. It is fundamentally about dedicating a child to God.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: In the video played during the lesson, John Piper states, \"So the imagery of BAPTISM is most fundamentally burial, death, resurrection. By faith, we're united to Him. And thus, as he died, was buried, was raised. We die, are buried, are raised\".\nREF: BAPTISM-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a7ea45f3-3f8a.md (01:59-03:29)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 6:3-5 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+6%3A3-5/>\n\nQ54. How does the lesson interpret Ezekiel 36:25 (\"I will sprinkle clean water on you\") and 1 Corinthians 6:11 (\"you are washed\") regarding salvation?\nA. It proves that physical water baptism is the exact moment salvation occurs.\nB. It demonstrates that sprinkling is the only biblically accurate mode of baptism.\nC. It shows that water is used as a metaphor to describe the spiritual cleansing power of the Holy Spirit and the Word in the heart of a believer.\nD. It indicates that believers must take daily physical baths to remain holy.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses Ezekiel 36 and 1 Corinthians 6 to explain that \"washing or water is used as a metaphor to describe the cleansing power of the Holy Spirit in the heart of the believer... it is not the water that saves\".\nREF: BAPTISM-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a7ea45f3-3f8a.md (16:19-19:40)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Ezekiel 36:25 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ezekiel+36%3A25/>; 1 Corinthians 6:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+6%3A11/> | Also cited in lesson segment: John 7:37 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+7%3A37/>; Ephesians 5:25, 26 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ephesians+5%3A25%2C+26/>; John 1:32–33 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A32%E2%80%9333/>; Luke 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+3%3A16/>\n\nQ55. What historical fact does the speaker highlight to challenge the idea that water baptism is an infallible proof of salvation?\nA. Several famous Christians refused to be baptized.\nB. Some of the most brutal enemies of the church and notable atheists (such as Adolf Hitler, Karl Marx, and Richard Dawkins) were baptized, proving that baptism does not automatically confirm true, lasting faith.\nC. Baptism was outlawed during the entire first century.\nD. Only people in the Middle East were allowed to be baptized.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker lists Adolf Hitler, Karl Marx, Christopher Hitchens, and Richard Dawkins to point out that \"some of the most brutal enemies of the church were baptized... There are people who were baptized and were not saved\".\nREF: BAPTISM-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS--mp4-b9dcb190-9b43.md (03:37-05:33)\n\nQ56. Why does the speaker emphasize that the \"real baptism\" is of the Holy Spirit rather than just water?\nA. Because water baptism is physically uncomfortable.\nB. Because it is impossible to find enough clean water for everyone.\nC. Because people can fake a physical water baptism (even acting foolishly immediately afterward), but no one can fake the true transformative work and discerning power of the Holy Spirit.\nD. Because the Holy Spirit specifically requested that water baptism be abolished.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker shares examples of people mocking physical baptism (e.g., twerking or smoking immediately after) to show that humans can fake a profession of faith. He contrasts this with the Spirit: \"don't think it flows flimsy when somebody says the real baptism is of the Spirit. That one nobody can fake, nobody can deceive\".\nREF: BAPTISM-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS--mp4-b9dcb190-9b43.md (08:02-11:29)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Matthew 3:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+3%3A11/>; Mark 1:8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Mark+1%3A8/>\n\nQ57. According to the speaker's interpretation of 1 Peter 3:18-21, how is water baptism a form of \"spiritual warfare\"?\nA. It requires the believer to verbally curse demons while submerged in water.\nB. It is a demonstration that the believer identifies with Christ's death and resurrection, separating their faith from the rebel spirits who were judged by God in the flood of Noah.\nC. It allows believers to physically wrestle angels underwater.\nD. It involves throwing holy water at political enemies.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker interprets 1 Peter 3 to argue that the flood judged rebel spirits, and \"baptism is now a demonstration that unlike those who rebelled against God and were judged by the flood, you, in Christ, you die and you are raised back to life... Water baptism is spiritual warfare because you are identifying with Christ\".\nREF: BAPTISM-V-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-145d20c7-d8f1.md (03:25-04:47)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Peter 3:18–22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+3%3A18%E2%80%9322/>\n\nQ58. How does the speaker address the practice of infant baptism and the concept of \"original sin\"?\nA. The speaker affirms that all infants must be baptized to erase original sin.\nB. The speaker states that infants should be baptized because they are automatically saved by their parents' faith.\nC. The speaker states that infants should not be baptized, and points out that arguments for it (like claiming Paul baptized infants in households) are based purely on silence and assumptions.\nD. The speaker demands that all infants be baptized by full immersion immediately after birth.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: When asked during the Q&A, the speaker explicitly states, \"I can tell you for a fact, Infants should not be baptized. And many of the people who argue for it argue from silence,\" using the example of the Philippian jailer's \"house\" to show that claiming infants were present is \"just gymnastics\" based on assumptions.\nREF: BAPTISM-V-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-145d20c7-d8f1.md (12:33-13:31)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 16:31–34 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+16%3A31%E2%80%9334/>\n\nQ59. Where is the new \"Eden\" located today according to the teaching?\nA. In a geographical location in the Middle East.\nB. In the believer, who is made an envoy of God's glory.\nC. In the third heaven where angels reside exclusively.\nD. In the physical buildings of local churches.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker asks \"Where is Eden today?\" and states that angels are \"ascending and descending, not just on the Son of Man, but in you. God has made you an envoy of his glory and of his presence\".\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-62b14bd4-1716.md (00:14-01:16)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+12/>\n\nQ60. What does the speaker claim is the measure of a believer's effectiveness in ministry, especially if it travels wide?\nA. The number of theological degrees attained.\nB. The amount of financial backing the ministry receives.\nC. The ability to work with and accommodate the ministry of angels.\nD. The frequency of fasting and isolation.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker emphasizes that \"You are effective in ministry to the degree of your ability to work with angels. This is what measures effectiveness. Especially if your ministry is supposed to travel wide\".\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-62b14bd4-1716.md (06:47-07:56)\n\nQ61. How does the speaker describe the \"Mount Zion\" mentioned in Hebrews 12?\nA. It is a physical mountain in Israel that believers must visit for angelic encounters.\nB. It is a spiritual environment that cannot be touched, serving as the believer's new address alongside an innumerable company of angels.\nC. It is a metaphor for the final judgment seat of God.\nD. It is a fiery mountain identical to Mount Sinai.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: Quoting Hebrews 12, the speaker explains that believers \"have not come to a mount that can be touched... It's no longer a geographical location... but you are come to Mount Zion... And to what? An innumerable company of angels. Listen, this is your new environment\".\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-62b14bd4-1716.md (02:12-04:04)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Hebrews 12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+12/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Hebrews 1:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+1%3A14/>\n\nQ62. What does the story of the speaker hearing his name called in a dark room illustrate about spiritual encounters?\nA. Angels only speak in riddles that must be deciphered.\nB. Missing a divine encounter because of fear or lacking a spiritual guide can cost years of spiritual progress, as God operates on His own timeline.\nC. Believers should always run away from disembodied voices to avoid demons.\nD. Angels will forcibly stop a believer who tries to run away.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker recounts running away from a voice calling his name and warns, \"You know, there are some encounters. If you miss, it will take you 15 years. Because God is a king. He won't work with your calendar... You have to be long suffering\".\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-ca63fecb-7512.md (01:17-02:11)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 10:2 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10%3A2/>; Psalm 34:7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+34%3A7/>\n\nQ63. What crucial \"honor test\" did Abraham pass in Genesis 18 that allowed him to experience an angelic encounter?\nA. He offered his son Isaac as a sacrifice on the altar.\nB. He welcomed and entertained strangers, demonstrating a culture of reverence and honor.\nC. He fasted for forty days before speaking to the visitors.\nD. He built a new tabernacle for the angels to rest in.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses Abraham to illustrate how honor attracts angels: \"God didn't come to his house. God did As if he was passing. If he was not the person of honor, he would have missed it... God will give you the honor test... Abraham will now say, please, just stay. Let me honor you\".\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-ca63fecb-7512.md (08:44-09:47)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Genesis 18:1–8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+18%3A1%E2%80%938/>\n\nQ64. Why does the speaker strictly warn against speaking against men of God, using the story of a politician opposing a church property?\nA. Because politicians have less authority than pastors in the spiritual realm.\nB. Because speaking against a man of God violates civil laws.\nC. Because the authority of the Word of God protects His servants, and attacking them rather than sticking strictly to biblical arguments can result in severe spiritual judgment.\nD. Because the church will sue the individual for defamation of character.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker shares a story where a politician who hated the church and blocked their property died weeks later after the pastors simply \"blew the trumpet, said a few things.\" He warns the generation to \"keep it strictly biblical. No attack on character. Keep it strictly biblical because the authority of the word of God is greater than the authority of the man of God\".\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-ca63fecb-7512.md (04:50-07:14)\n\nQ65. How does the Old Testament imagery of the cherubim over the mercy seat relate to attracting angels?\nA. It shows that angels are drawn to physical gold and incense.\nB. It shows that angels guard the Word of God jealously, so becoming a doer of the Word excites them and gives them something to work with.\nC. It shows that angels only operate inside physical temples and church buildings.\nD. It shows that angels require animal sacrifices to initiate movement.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that the cherubs over the mercy seat guarded the tablet of the commandments. \"They guard the word of God... jealously. And if you want to see angelic intervention in your life, become committed to knowing the Word, obeying the Word and doing the Word... you've given them something to work with\".\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-67c8f91a-f935.md (00:14-02:29)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Psalm 103:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+103%3A20/>\n\nQ66. According to Psalm 103:20, what specifically causes angels to excel in strength and perform their duties?\nA. They respond to the emotional weeping of the believer.\nB. They wait for specific commands from archangels.\nC. They heed the voice of God's Word.\nD. They react strictly to the volume of a believer's voice.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker quotes Psalm 103:20, \"you, his angels who excel in strength, who do his what? Heeding the voice of his word.\" He then advises, \"If you learn to speak the Word of God... You want angels to go on your behalf? Learn to talk. Talk the word of God\".\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-67c8f91a-f935.md (02:29-03:26)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Psalm 103:20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Psalm+103%3A20/>\n\nQ67. Based on Daniel 10:12, what does the lesson identify as the third key to stirring up angelic activity?\nA. Fasting and wearing sackcloth.\nB. Playing spiritual worship music constantly.\nC. Prayer, because angels actively respond to the words spoken in heartfelt prayer.\nD. Giving away all physical possessions.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: Using Daniel 10:12, the speaker emphasizes the necessity of a prayer life (the third key): \"Then he said to me, do not fear, Daniel, for from the first day that you set your heart to understand... your words were heard. And I have come because of your words... angels, they respond to your words\".\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-67c8f91a-f935.md (12:57-14:24)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Daniel 10:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Daniel+10%3A12/>\n\nQ68. What are the two literal meanings of the word \"seraph\" discussed in the lesson?\nA. Messenger and warrior.\nB. Burning ones and serpent.\nC. Holy one and judge.\nD. Healer and protector.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explicitly states the dual meaning: \"The word seraph means two things, number one, it means burning ones... The other meaning of the word seraph is actually serpent. Is actually serpents\".\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-cbe39ac5-4fa0.md (05:26-07:14), (13:14-14:47)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Isaiah 6:2–7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+6%3A2%E2%80%937/>; Numbers 21:6–9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Numbers+21%3A6%E2%80%939/>\n\nQ69. According to the speaker's teaching on Seraphim, what are the three possible outcomes when a person encounters one?\nA. Instant wealth, worldwide fame, or sudden death.\nB. Repentance, judgment, or the person's life is changed positively.\nC. Extreme fear, persistent doubt, or mental confusion.\nD. The gift of prophecy, speaking in new tongues, or physical healing.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker outlines the consequences of encountering this specific class of angels: \"When a seraph appears to you, it is either you repent or you are judged. You either repent, you are judged, or your life is changed positively. So there are three options\".\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-cbe39ac5-4fa0.md (08:38-09:46)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Isaiah 6:5–7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Isaiah+6%3A5%E2%80%937/>; Luke 1:19–20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+1%3A19%E2%80%9320/>; Daniel 10:15–19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Daniel+10%3A15%E2%80%9319/>\n\nQ70. How does the speaker interpret the \"fiery serpents\" that stung the Israelites in the wilderness?\nA. They were natural snakes native to the desert environment.\nB. They were a manifestation of the \"burning ones\" (Seraphim) operating in a judgment function.\nC. They were hallucinations caused by severe dehydration and fear.\nD. They were demons disguised as animals to test the Israelites' faith.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: Connecting the word \"seraph\" (meaning serpent and burning one), the speaker explains that when the Israelites strayed, \"fiery serpents came and stung them... it's another manifestation of the burning ones. And they operate for judgment\".\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-cbe39ac5-4fa0.md (13:14-14:47)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Numbers 21:6–9 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Numbers+21%3A6%E2%80%939/>\n\nQ71. What does the lesson identify as the fourth key to attracting the ministry of angels, as demonstrated by Cornelius and Manoah?\nA. Perfect moral behavior and sinless perfection.\nB. Speaking exclusively in heavenly tongues.\nC. Sacrifice and the giving of alms.\nD. Attending every single church service.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: Moving to the fourth key, the speaker reads Acts 10:2-4 showing Cornelius \"gave much alms,\" and the angel said, \"thine prayers and thine alms have come up for a memorial... The burning ones hover over sacrifice.\" He also cites Manoah offering a sacrifice in Judges 13.\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-V-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-748925d2-ab8a.md (05:23-06:29)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Acts 10:2–4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+10%3A2%E2%80%934/>; Judges 13:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Judges+13%3A19/>\n\nQ72. What spiritual phenomenon did the speaker observe regarding Pastor Nathaniel Bassey to illustrate the power of sacrifice?\nA. He saw him surrounded by a choir of singing angels holding swords.\nB. He saw bags of money leaving him but connected by strings, while a heavenly voice declared him \"a man of sacrifice.\"\nC. He saw him holding a flaming sword that repelled demonic forces.\nD. He saw him physically wrestling with a demonic serpent.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker recounts his first time seeing Pastor Nathaniel Bassey: \"I saw bags of money that have left him, but they were still connected to him by strings... and a voice saying, behold, a man of sacrifice... his generosity is speaking for him in the heavenly realm\".\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-V-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-748925d2-ab8a.md (07:50-08:51)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Judges 13:19 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Judges+13%3A19/>\n\nQ73. What happened when Manoah offered a sacrifice on a rock in Judges 13?\nA. Fire fell from the sky and consumed his enemies immediately.\nB. The angel ascended in the flame of the altar, demonstrating that sacrifices can encourage and strengthen angelic ministry.\nC. The rock split in two, revealing hidden scrolls of prophecy.\nD. It rained continuously for forty days to end a severe drought.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: Reading Judges 13:19-20, the speaker notes that when Manoah offered the sacrifice, \"the flame went up towards heaven from the altar. That the angel ascended in the flame of the altar,\" using this to argue that \"Your angels can get more powerful. There are things you will do that will encourage their ministry\".\nREF: HOW-TO-ATTRACT-ANGELS-V-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-748925d2-ab8a.md (08:51-11:37)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Judges 13:19–20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Judges+13%3A19%E2%80%9320/>\n\nQ74. Which Roman Emperor blamed Christians for the Great Fire of Rome in 64 AD?\nA. Emperor Constantine.\nB. Emperor Tiberius.\nC. Emperor Nero.\nD. Emperor Domitian.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The transcript identifies the specific emperor responsible for this persecution: \"By 64 A.D. emperor Nero blamed Christians for the Great Fire of Rome, launching brutal persecution\".\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-e41e9f8e-c746.md (02:30-03:46)\n\nQ75. What pivotal role did the Apostle Paul play in the development of the early church?\nA. He was the first Roman Emperor to officially convert to Christianity and end persecution.\nB. He traveled across the Roman Empire spreading the message, and his letters formed the New Testament, laying the foundation for Christian theology and practice.\nC. He founded the city of Constantinople to serve as a safe haven for believers.\nD. He personally presided over the Council of Nicaea to establish the Trinity.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The lesson highlights Paul as \"one of the most influential early missionaries,\" noting he \"traveled across the Roman Empire spreading the message of Jesus. His letters, many of which formed the New Testament, laid the foundation for Christian theology and practice\".\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-e41e9f8e-c746.md (02:30-03:46)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 9:15 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+9%3A15/>\n\nQ76. During which century were key doctrines such as the nature of Jesus Christ and the Trinity officially recognized by the Church?\nA. The 1st century.\nB. The 2nd century.\nC. The 4th century.\nD. The 10th century.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: While early defenders wrote in the second century, the transcript specifies that \"it wasn't until the 4th century that the key doctrines of the Church such as the nature of Jesus Christ and the Trinity were officially recognized\".\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-e41e9f8e-c746.md (03:46-04:52)\n\nQ77. What essential role did Catholic monasteries play during the \"Dark Ages\" following the fall of Rome?\nA. They trained elite soldiers to fight in the upcoming Crusades.\nB. They preserved knowledge by copying texts from the ancient world and kept learning alive.\nC. They conquered the remaining territories of the Western Roman Empire.\nD. They invented the printing press to mass-produce the Bible.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The transcript notes that after the fall of Rome, \"Monasteries played a crucial role. They preserved knowledge, copying texts from the ancient world and keeping learning alive through the Dark Ages\".\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-2a37207c-dbfb.md (00:15-01:29)\n\nQ78. What was the central focus of the \"Counter Reformation\" launched by the Catholic Church?\nA. To completely abandon the Pope's authority and adopt Protestant views.\nB. To launch the Crusades against the Muslim control of the Holy Land.\nC. To renew the church spiritually, reform the clergy, clarify doctrine at the Council of Trent, and spread Catholicism globally through new orders like the Jesuits.\nD. To forcibly integrate Protestant teachings into the official Catholic catechism.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The lesson states that in response to the Protestant Reformation, the Catholic Church launched the Counter Reformation. The key to this revival was \"The Council of Trent clarified Catholic doctrine and reformed the clergy. New religious orders, like the Jesuits... took on the mission of spreading Catholicism worldwide\".\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-2a37207c-dbfb.md (03:46-05:05)\n\nQ79. Which African nation is highlighted as remaining one of the oldest Christian nations in the world, having maintained a unique tradition through the Coptic Church?\nA. Egypt.\nB. Tunisia.\nC. Ethiopia.\nD. Congo.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The transcript points out that while Islam replaced Christianity in much of North Africa, \"there was one African kingdom Where Christianity thrived. Ethiopia... It remains one of the oldest Christian nations in the world... maintained a unique Christian tradition Through the coptic church\".\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-2a37207c-dbfb.md (08:39-09:57)\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+8/>\n\nQ80. How did the Atlantic slave trade create a major contradiction for early Portuguese Christian missions in the Kingdom of Congo?\nA. The Portuguese refused to teach the Bible to any Africans they encountered.\nB. Priests became complicit in the trade, and the church actively profited from baptizing enslaved people.\nC. The African rulers forced the Portuguese to abandon Christianity entirely.\nD. The slave trade prevented any church buildings from being constructed in the region.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The lesson explains that although King Afonso I embraced Christianity, \"the coming of the slave trade... created a major contradiction... Priests became complicit in the trade, and the church profited from baptizing enslaved people\".\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-29efed0f-8465.md (00:15-01:24)\n\nQ81. Who was Samuel Ajayi Crowther?\nA. A Portuguese explorer who mapped the Niger Delta region.\nB. A former slave who embraced Christianity, translated the Bible into Yoruba, and became the first African Anglican bishop in Nigeria.\nC. The indigenous founder of the Aladura prophetic movement.\nD. The European leader of the Sudan Interior Mission.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The transcript identifies Samuel Ajayi Crowther as a \"former slave who became the first African Anglican bishop in Nigeria... Crowther not only translated the Bible into Yoruba, but also established the foundations of Christian education in Nigeria\".\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-29efed0f-8465.md (05:13-06:37)\n\nQ82. How did Joseph Shanahan significantly increase Catholic numbers in eastern Nigeria?\nA. By relying on military force provided by the British crown.\nB. By using education to reach children, and through them, successfully reaching their parents.\nC. By promising substantial financial rewards for religious conversion.\nD. By blending Catholicism seamlessly with traditional religious sacrifices.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The lesson details that denominations sprang up in the 1850s and beyond, specifically noting \"Joseph Shanahan. Shanahan's approach using education to reach children and through them parents Saw Catholic numbers in eastern Nigeria grow from 4,000 to over 110,000\".\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-29efed0f-8465.md (06:37-08:00)\n\nQ83. Which statement accurately reflects the statistical growth of Christianity in Nigeria between 1953 and the 1970s?\nA. It grew from 22% in 1953 to 44.9% by the 1970s.\nB. It rapidly declined from 50% down to 10%.\nC. It remained completely stagnant at roughly 5%.\nD. It reached nearly 100% due to mandatory national conversion laws.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The transcript provides specific data on the expansion of indigenous expressions of faith: \"In Nigeria alone, Christianity grew from 22% in 1953 to 44.9% by the 1970s\".\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-11fa9bac-f5a5.md (00:15-01:36)\n\nQ84. What tragic events highlight the severe religious tensions and persecution faced by Christians in Nigeria since the late 1970s?\nA. The peaceful integration of all religious schools without conflict.\nB. The 1982 Kano violence, the Kafanchan crisis, and the violent murder of figures like Deborah Samuel and Eunice Olawale.\nC. The complete banning of Christianity in the southern regions of Nigeria.\nD. The forced deportation of all foreign Christian missionaries by the government.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker lists a series of violent events: \"By 1982, violence exploded in Kano... Then came the Kafanchan crisis, where 152 churches and five mosques were set ablaze... Deborah Samuel Yakubo, stoned and burned alive... Eunice Olawale, a street preacher, murdered\".\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-11fa9bac-f5a5.md (04:31-06:04)\n\nQ85. What does the term \"reverse mission\" refer to in the context of modern African Christianity?\nA. Africans abandoning Christianity to return en masse to traditional religions.\nB. European missionaries returning to Africa to rebuild colonial-era churches.\nC. African Christians planting churches across Europe, Asia, and the Americas, transitioning from the evangelized to the evangelists.\nD. The process of translating the English Bible back into its original Hebrew and Greek texts.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The lesson defines reverse mission: \"African Christians were no longer the evangelized. They were the evangelists. This reverse mission has seen Africans planting churches across Europe, Asia and the Americas,\" noting Nigerians lead the largest churches in England and Ukraine.\nREF: EARLY-CHURCH-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-11fa9bac-f5a5.md (04:31-06:04)\n\nQ86. What is the difference between baptism by sprinkling and baptism by pouring?\nA. Pouring is exclusively done in rivers, while sprinkling is done in churches.\nB. Sprinkling uses anointing oil, while pouring uses regular water.\nC. Sprinkling was reserved for adults, while pouring was designed for infants.\nD. Pouring essentially uses more water than sprinkling, often involving pouring a basin of water over the head while standing or sitting.\nANSWER: D\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains the difference in modes: \"baptism by pouring. And essentially the difference is more water is used... you are made to stand still or sit, and then they carry a basin... and then pour water on your head\".\nREF: BAPTISM-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-71aba375-058c.md (02:50-04:05)\n\nQ87. How does the description of John's baptism in Aenon (John 3:23) support the argument for full immersion?\nA. The text explicitly states John baptized there because there was \"much water,\" implying a full immersion was the required method.\nB. The text states John brought a large basin to sprinkle the gathered crowds.\nC. The text states John required candidates to physically drink the water to be cleansed.\nD. The text states John baptized there solely because the water was perfectly clean and sanitary.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The speaker uses John 3:23 as an argument for immersion: \"John also was baptizing in Aenon near Salim, because there was what much water there? So the need for much water... implied that a full immersion was required\".\nREF: BAPTISM-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-71aba375-058c.md (06:16-07:24)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 3:23 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+3%3A23/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Matthew 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Matthew+3%3A16/>; Acts 8:38, 39 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+8%3A38%2C+39/>; Colossians 2:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+2%3A12/>\n\nQ88. Why does the Roman Catholic Church practice infant baptism, according to their Catechism?\nA. To officially register the child's birth with the state government.\nB. Because they believe infants inherit original sin, and baptism washes it away, removing disdain and bringing them into grace.\nC. To dedicate the child's future career entirely to the church.\nD. Because it is a harmless cultural tradition with no spiritual significance.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains the Catholic view on infants: \"They believe that infants inherit original sin and baptism removes disdain and brings them into grace... Quoting from the Catechism CCC 1250, it says, born with a fallen nature. They also need the new birth in baptism\".\nREF: BAPTISM-I-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-71aba375-058c.md (10:35-12:12)\n\nQ89. What is the doctrine of \"Theosis\" held by the Eastern Orthodox Church?\nA. The belief that water supernaturally turns into blood during the act of baptism.\nB. The belief that believers are active participants of the divine life, and baptism begins this participation.\nC. The belief that infants cannot be saved under any circumstances.\nD. The belief that the Pope is infallible in all matters of faith.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: When discussing the Eastern Orthodox Church, the speaker states they believe baptism is initiatory and \"believe in something called theosis... that we as believers are participants of the divine life and that baptism begins our participation in the divine life\".\nREF: BAPTISM-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8ecea2b2-7835.md (00:16-01:37)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 2 Peter 1:4 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Peter+1%3A4/>\n\nQ90. What specific belief separates the Reformed view of baptism from the Baptist view regarding the subjects of baptism?\nA. The Reformed view insists on full immersion, while Baptists allow sprinkling.\nB. The Reformed view teaches baptism actively saves the soul, while Baptists teach it does not.\nC. The Reformed view practices infant baptism (viewing them as covenant members), whereas the Baptist view strictly requires personal faith and confession, practicing only believer's baptism.\nD. The Reformed view requires three years of baptismal classes, while Baptists require none.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: The speaker notes the Reformed view does not believe baptism saves, but they still \"believe infant baptism... infants of believing parents are baptized as covenant members.\" In contrast, the Baptist view states: \"Are infants to be baptized? No. Baptism requires personal faith and confession. Only believers are baptized\".\nREF: BAPTISM-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8ecea2b2-7835.md (07:48-10:18)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 2:38 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+2%3A38/>; Acts 8:36–38 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+8%3A36%E2%80%9338/>\n\nQ91. What was Martin Luther's core realization that sparked the Protestant Reformation?\nA. That justification does not come by paying money for indulgences or by human works, but by faith alone (\"the just shall live by faith\").\nB. That the Bible should only be read and taught in Latin.\nC. That the church urgently needed to build more monasteries to preserve knowledge.\nD. That infant baptism was entirely unbiblical and must be stopped immediately.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that Luther discovered the Catholic Church had moved from biblical positions (like selling indulgences for forgiveness). Luther read \"the just shall live by faith\" and realized that \"justification does not come by paying any money... It comes by faith\".\nREF: BAPTISM-II-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-8ecea2b2-7835.md (11:44-13:54)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Romans 1:17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Romans+1%3A17/>\n\nQ92. According to the Naked Bible Podcast reference, how did Old Testament circumcision relate to the community, providing a parallel for infant baptism arguments?\nA. It guaranteed personal salvation and immediate entry into heaven upon death.\nB. It represented adoption into the community of people who knew the true God and had access to His revelations (the oracles), rather than indicating personal faith.\nC. It proved that the individual had already made a mature, personal confession of faith.\nD. It was strictly a physical health requirement for living within the borders of Israel.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker references Michael Heiser's podcast on Colossians 2:11, explaining that circumcision \"represented that you become part of the community of people who knew the true God, had access to the true revelations of God, namely the oracles of God. It wasn't quite about personal faith\".\nREF: BAPTISM-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a7ea45f3-3f8a.md (12:05-14:09)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Colossians 2:11 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+2%3A11/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Colossians 2:12 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Colossians+2%3A12/>\n\nQ93. How did Jesus use \"thirst\" as a metaphor for salvation in John 7:37?\nA. To describe the intense physical dehydration of walking in the desert without water.\nB. To illustrate that when a person receives the Holy Spirit, nothing else defines satisfaction greater than that.\nC. To prove that physical water baptism is an absolute requirement for salvation.\nD. To demand that His followers drink only water and abstain from wine.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker lists \"thirst\" as a metaphor of salvation alongside poverty. Quoting John 7:37 (\"if anyone thirsts, let him come to me and drink\"), he explains, \"why was hunger and thirst used? Because when you receive the Holy Spirit, when you are born again, nothing else defines satisfaction greater than that\".\nREF: BAPTISM-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a7ea45f3-3f8a.md (15:14-16:19)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: John 7:37 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+7%3A37/> | Also cited in lesson segment: Ezekiel 36:25–27 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Ezekiel+36%3A25%E2%80%9327/>\n\nQ94. What contrast does John the Baptist make between his baptism and the baptism of Jesus?\nA. John baptizes with water, but Jesus baptizes with the Holy Spirit and fire.\nB. John baptizes for physical healing, while Jesus baptizes for financial blessing.\nC. John baptizes infants, while Jesus strictly baptizes adults.\nD. John's baptism saves the soul, while Jesus's baptism only empowers the believer for ministry.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The speaker quotes John the Baptist in Luke 3:16 making a clear differentiation: \"I baptize you with water. But one who is more powerful than I will come... He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and with fire\".\nREF: BAPTISM-III-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-a7ea45f3-3f8a.md (19:40-20:18)\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Luke 3:16 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Luke+3%3A16/> | Also cited in lesson segment: John 1:32–33 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/John+1%3A32%E2%80%9333/>\n\nQ95. According to historical trends mentioned in the lesson, how did the preparation for baptism change from the first century to the third century?\nA. It remained immediate upon profession of faith throughout all three centuries.\nB. It shifted from immediate baptism in the first century, to basic instruction in the second, to a formal three-year preparation in the third century.\nC. It shifted from a three-year requirement in the first century to immediate baptism in the third.\nD. Preparation was banned completely by the third century to encourage mass conversions.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker outlines the progression: \"in the first century... Immediate baptism on profession of faith... in the second century. Basic instruction was added in the Didache... in the third century, there was now more emphasis on preparation... And this formal preparation took three years\".\nREF: BAPTISM-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS--mp4-b9dcb190-9b43.md (02:24-03:37), (05:33-06:41)\n\nQ96. What does the speaker consider the only true indicator of a person's salvation?\nA. A signed certificate of water baptism.\nB. Believing loyalty (a profession of faith combined with a life that stays and acts accordingly).\nC. Perfect attendance at weekly church services.\nD. The ability to recite the Nicene Creed from memory.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: Reflecting on false converts being baptized, the speaker asserts, \"the only thing that truly points to the salvation of a person is believing loyalty... That the person makes a profession and stays\".\nREF: BAPTISM-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS--mp4-b9dcb190-9b43.md (06:41-08:02)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Hebrews 3:14 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Hebrews+3%3A14/>\n\nQ97. How does the speaker view the \"right use of means\" regarding traditions like wedding rings or church weddings?\nA. They are sinful additions to scripture that must be avoided.\nB. They are mere symbolisms that, while not explicitly commanded in scripture, have a right use of means and hold powerful significance.\nC. They are absolute biblical requirements for salvation.\nD. They are pagan rituals that the early church successfully destroyed.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker concedes that while some practices are not explicitly commanded (\"What scripture says we should wear wedding rings... we should do church weddings\"), they are \"mere symbolisms but are important\" and represent a \"right use of means\".\nREF: BAPTISM-IV-Celebration-Church-International-LMS--mp4-b9dcb190-9b43.md (12:36-14:01)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Corinthians 10:23-24 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Corinthians+10%3A23-24/>\n\nQ98. What practical standard does the speaker suggest implementing for future water baptisms to ensure the candidate's genuine conversion?\nA. Requiring them to pay a nominal baptismal fee.\nB. Requiring someone to vouch for the individual's character and profession of faith.\nC. Requiring a comprehensive written theology exam.\nD. Requiring them to fast for seven continuous days.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker states that to avoid baptizing false converts, \"We are going to set up a standard that will still reflect our core conviction. Somebody must vouch for you. Yes, I know him. He's a believer... If you call it strict, it's okay\".\nREF: BAPTISM-V-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-145d20c7-d8f1.md (00:16-01:31)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 8:36-38 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+8%3A36-38/>\n\nQ99. In the lesson's explanation of 1 Peter 3, what did the flood of Noah accomplish besides preserving humanity?\nA. It provided necessary water for post-flood agriculture.\nB. It acted as a judgment on rebel spirits (fallen angels) and their giant offspring.\nC. It washed away the original sin of the surviving animals.\nD. It created the physical boundaries of the Mediterranean Sea.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that the flood was \"actually the preservation of mankind and judgment on all those rebel spirits and their children. And so the flood judged them.\" Baptism now acts as a demonstration separating the believer from those judged spirits.\nREF: BAPTISM-V-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-145d20c7-d8f1.md (02:26-04:26)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: 1 Peter 3:18–22 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+3%3A18%E2%80%9322/>\n\nQ100. Why does the speaker reject the argument that the baptism of entire \"households\" (like the Philippian jailer's) proves infant baptism?\nA. Because the jailer was explicitly stated in scripture to have no children.\nB. Because arguing that infants were present in the household is an argument from silence and mere \"gymnastics.\"\nC. Because the jailer was a Roman soldier and couldn't legally have a family.\nD. Because the jailer's household only included his adult servants.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: The speaker refutes infant baptism by addressing the \"household\" argument: \"many of the people who argue for it argue from silence... he asked, what shall. What can I do to be saved? And he said, believe... you and your house. And so now they argue on silence from silence that if it was here in his house, they were probably infants... it's just gymnastics\".\nREF: BAPTISM-V-Celebration-Church-International-LMS-mp4-145d20c7-d8f1.md (12:33-13:31)\nBIBLE: Added from online research: Acts 16:31-34 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+16%3A31-34/>\n\nQ101. According to 1 Peter 3:21 as explained in the lesson, how does water baptism function in relation to salvation?\nA. It serves as an outward antitype and pledge of a good conscience toward God through Christ's resurrection.\nB. It magically cleanses every physical stain and guarantees salvation regardless of whether one believes the gospel.\nC. It replaces the cross by functioning as an earthly sacrifice that satisfies divine justice.\nD. It acts as an esoteric secret ceremony reserved exclusively for ordained church leaders.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: 1 Peter 3:21 clarifies that baptism corresponds to the ark saving eight souls through water—not by washing away physical dirt, but as an appeal and pledge of a good conscience toward God through the resurrection of Jesus.\nREF: BAPTISM V — 02:05–02:44\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Peter 3:20–21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+3%3A20%E2%80%9321/>\n\nQ102. What does the lesson identify as the crucial requirement before administering water baptism to a candidate?\nA. The candidate must complete a mandatory three-year course in ancient church history.\nB. The candidate must have verifiable personal faith in Christ and a witness who can vouch for their confession.\nC. The candidate must experience an angelic visitation before entering the baptismal pool.\nD. The candidate must pay a ceremonial fee to support the maintenance of the baptismal pool.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: In the lesson, water baptism is treated with reverence; candidates must have genuine, verifiable faith in Jesus and someone who can vouch that they are a believer before being immersed.\nREF: BAPTISM V — 00:34–01:06\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: Acts 8:36–38 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Acts+8%3A36%E2%80%9338/>\n\nQ103. In 1 Peter 3:19–20, what historical Old Testament event does Peter connect to the typology of baptism?\nA. The Exodus crossing of the Red Sea under the leadership of Moses.\nB. Joshua leading the Israelites across the flooded Jordan River into Canaan.\nC. Elijah crossing the Jordan River before being taken up into heaven in a chariot of fire.\nD. Noah and his family of eight souls being brought safely through the flood waters in the ark.\nANSWER: D\nEXPLANATION: Peter directly links baptism as an antitype to the days of Noah, when eight people were brought safely through the floodwaters in the ark as an advance picture of salvation through Christ's resurrection.\nREF: BAPTISM V — 01:49–02:22\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 1 Peter 3:19–21 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/1+Peter+3%3A19%E2%80%9321/>; Genesis 7:1–7 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Genesis+7%3A1%E2%80%937/>\n\nQ104. In Judges 13:19–20 as cited in the lesson, what took place when Manoah offered a sacrifice on the rock to the Lord?\nA. The offering was rejected because Manoah failed to build an altar made of hewn stones.\nB. The angel of the Lord performed a wondrous act and ascended into heaven within the altar's flame.\nC. A sudden thunderstorm extinguished the altar fire before any offering could be consumed.\nD. Two angels appeared with drawn swords to warn Manoah against naming his unborn child Samson.\nANSWER: B\nEXPLANATION: In Judges 13:19-20, when Manoah offered a young goat and grain offering upon the rock to the Lord, the angel did wondrously and ascended upward in the flame of the altar before Manoah and his wife.\nREF: HOW TO ATTRACT ANGELS V — 09:48–10:05\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: Judges 13:19–20 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/Judges+13%3A19%E2%80%9320/>\n\nQ105. How does the lesson warn believers regarding extreme teachings on financial sacrifice and giving?\nA. By explaining that the enemy uses extreme abuses to create strawman arguments that turn believers away from biblical sacrifice.\nB. By teaching that believers in the New Testament should completely abandon all financial generosity and sacrifice.\nC. By claiming that God only honors offerings given after a public display of emotional manipulation.\nD. By instructing church leaders to demand donations from university students regardless of conscience.\nANSWER: A\nEXPLANATION: The speaker explains that Satan often highlights extreme, ungodly abuses in fundraising to construct a strawman argument, deceiving believers into discarding biblical principles of genuine sacrifice and generosity.\nREF: HOW TO ATTRACT ANGELS V — 07:14–08:22\nBIBLE: Also cited in lesson segment: 2 Corinthians 9:6–8 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Corinthians+9%3A6%E2%80%938/>\n\nQ106. Why did King David refuse to drink the water brought to him from the well of Bethlehem by his three mighty men?\nA. Because the water had been poisoned by Philistine archers during the retreat.\nB. Because the water was retrieved from a cistern reserved exclusively for pagan ceremonies.\nC. Because the men had risked their lives to retrieve it, making it too sacred to consume as an ordinary drink.\nD. Because Levitical law forbade warriors from drinking water taken from occupied enemy territory.\nANSWER: C\nEXPLANATION: In 2 Samuel 23:15-17, David poured the water out before the Lord as a drink offering because his mighty men had risked their lives to bring it, illustrating that godly generosity and sacrifice must never be treated with careless disregard.\nREF: HOW TO ATTRACT ANGELS V — 07:41–08:00\nBIBLE: Confirmed in lesson and deck: 2 Samuel 23:15–17 \u003chttps://www.esv.org/verses/2+Samuel+23%3A15%E2%80%9317/>";
